{
  "raw": {
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46228": {
      "title": "Research Technician",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Research Technician Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Research Technician Apply for job Job reference: BMH-032568 Salary: UoM Grade 4 £29,588 - £32,080 per annum, depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Biology, Medicine Health Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: FBMH Technical Services Hours Per Week: 35 hours per week (1 FTE) Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 26/10/2026 Contract Duration: Fixed Term for 12 months School/Directorate: Faculty Office - Biology, Medicine and Health Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description We are seeking a motivated and collaborative individual to join our team as a Research Technician . This role offers an exciting opportunity to contribute to state-of-the-art cytoskeleton research using biochemical, biophysical and cell biological approaches within a dynamic and inclusive environment. You will be responsible for: 1) Expressing and purifying recombinant proteins using bacterial, insect and mammalian expression systems, and chromatography techniques including affinity, ion-exchange and size-exclusion chromatography. 2) Culturing and manipulating bacterial, insect and mammalian cells to support recombinant protein production and experimental studies. 3) Performing molecular biology techniques, including cloning, PCR, site-directed mutagenesis, electrophoresis and Western blotting. 4) Conducting protein biochemistry experiments to investigate protein structure, interactions and function. 5) Providing technical guidance and support to laboratory members, including training and supervision, maintaining laboratory equipment and inventories, ordering and organising reagents and supplies, and ensuring compliance with laboratory safety procedures. We welcome candidates who bring diverse perspectives, experiences, and approaches to their work. About You We encourage applications from individuals with a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. You should demonstrate: Essential Criteria: - A qualification at QCF level 6, such as a bachelor’s degree, in a relevant biological science subject; or QCF level 2 passes, such as GCSEs, in English, Mathematics and at least one science subject, supported by substantial relevant research laboratory experience. - Strong experience in protein biochemistry e.g. recombinant protein expression using bacterial and/or insect-cell-based systems and protein purification using affinity, ion-exchange and size-exclusion chromatography. - Strong analytical and quantitative image analysis skills, with the ability to interpret, evaluate and quantify scientific data effectively. - Excellent organisational and communication skills, with the ability to prioritise a variety of activities, meet deadlines, and communicate effectively and professionally with a wide range of staff, students and visitors. - Ability to work independently with minimal supervision, using initiative and maintaining a high level of accuracy, attention to detail and experimental reproducibility. Desirable Criteria: - Experience culturing and manipulating bacterial, insect and mammalian cells. - Experience in molecular biology techniques e.g. cloning, PCR and site-directed mutagenesis. - Experience with biochemical and molecular biology analysis techniques e.g. SDS-PAGE and Western blotting. - Experience using light microscopy approaches e.g. total internal reflection fluorescence microscopy (TIRF). - Strong interest in mechanistic cell biology and biochemistry. We value transferable skills and real-world experience as much as formal qualifications. Our benefits include: - Generous employer pension contribution - Christmas closure across all departments outside of annual leave and bank holidays - Purple Place employee discount platform For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits. You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here. We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy. Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Beth Lawrence Email Address: beth.lawrence@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support Jobtrain Client Support Hub This role is not eligible for Skilled Worker visa sponsorship. Applicants must demonstrate the right to work in the UK. Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46239": {
      "title": "Research Associate (Socially Responsible Earth Observation)",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Research Associate (Socially Responsible Earth Observation) Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Research Associate (Socially Responsible Earth Observation) Apply for job Job reference: SAE-032577 Salary: £37,694 to £42,254 per annum depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Science and Engineering Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Department of Civil Engineering and Management Hours Per Week: 35 Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 08/11/2026 Contract Duration: 24 months School/Directorate: School of Engineering Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description We are seeking to appoint a talented and enthusiastic Postdoctoral Research Associate (PDRA) to play a key role in delivering an exciting investment project (IrrEO) that will develop satellite-based irrigation mapping tools for governments and development actors in Sub-Saharan Africa (SSA). Earth Observation (EO) technologies are enabling agricultural mapping at increasingly large scales and fine resolutions. This brings exciting opportunities to understand complex farming systems, particularly in data-sparse smallholder farming regions. However, recent research has revealed the social risks of EO-based land monitoring. Examples include imperfect representations leading to poorly informed decision-making or biased investments, and marginalised people being made visible through EO in ways which may expose them to harm. There is an urgent need for improved social risk analysis and development of good practice guidance for EO-based agricultural mapping. As part of the IrrEO project, the PDRA will lead on developing guidance for the socially responsible development and use of EO-based agricultural mapping tools in small-scale farming systems in Sub-Saharan Africa. They will catalogue existing scholarship and frameworks on the ethical and equitable use of EO in agricultural mapping. They will also carry out document-based and qualitative interview research to identify potential risks of EO-based agricultural mapping in different socio-political and agricultural contexts, focusing particularly on risks to farmers and farming communities. Based on these two workstreams and consultation with the IrrEO partners, they will devise and share strategies for socially response EO-based agricultural monitoring. The PDRA will be an integral member of the IrrEO project team, working closely with Principal Investigator Prof. Tim Foster (Department of Civil Engineering and Management) and Co-Investigators Dr. Rose Pritchard (Global Development Institute). They will also work closely with a wider team of investigators and postdoctoral researchers at the University of Manchester involved in the IrrEO project and other aligned research on socially-just use of Earth Observation including the Just Earth Observation for Conservation project (JEOC – https://www.justearthobservation.org/) which is co-led by Pritchard and Foster. The role will involve close collaboration with academic and non-academic partners in the project’s case study countries (Kenya, Nigeria, and Ethiopia) and active engagement with UK and international partners including the World Bank and the Bill & Melinda Gates Foundation. The IrrEO project is funded by a four-year (2025-2029) investment from the Bill & Melinda Gates Foundation. Due to the nature of the funding, this post is available for a fixed term of up to 24 months with an end date of 31 August 2029. We are looking for a candidate to start in early 2027, with some flexibility over the exact start date. (Grades 6-9) Our benefits include: • Generous employer contribution pension • 29 days annual leave plus bank holidays, along with Christmas closure • Ride to work and EV car scheme available For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Prof Tim Foster, Professor of Land and Water Informatics and Director of Manchester Environmental Research Institute (MERI) Email: timothy.foster@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46104": {
      "title": "Post-Doctoral Research Associate in Cancer Genomics",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Post-Doctoral Research Associate in Cancer Genomics Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Post-Doctoral Research Associate in Cancer Genomics Apply for job Job reference: BMH-032444 Salary: £37,694 - £58,225 per annum, depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Biology, Medicine Health Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Division of Population Health, Health Services Research And Primary Care Hours Per Week: 1 FTE Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 22/10/2026 Contract Duration: 12 months School/Directorate: School of Health Sciences Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description We are seeking a motivated and collaborative individual to join our team as a post-doctoral research associate/fellow with cancer genomics research experience. This role offers an exciting opportunity to analyse genomic data and calculate prostate cancer polygenic risk scores for the GP-TEST-Pro study. This clinical study involves a multidisciplinary team of leading academics from the University of Manchester, Queen Mary University London, University College London, and the Institute for Cancer Research and the postholder will be working within a dynamic and inclusive environment. You will be responsible for: Receiving, securely storing and managing genomic data in line with University, NHS and study governance requirements. Performing genotype and imputed-data quality control, including sample-level and variant-level checks, ancestry and population-structure assessment, allele harmonisation and preparation of analysis-ready datasets. Using appropriate genetic analysis tools, such as PLINK/PLINK2 and imputation server outputs, to clean, process and analyse genomic data. Calculating and validating a 451-SNV prostate cancer polygenic risk score, including harmonisation of variants, application of published effect sizes and assessment of score distribution. Evaluating additional genomic markers or PRS approaches relevant to clinically significant prostate cancer detection. About You We encourage applications from individuals with a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. You should demonstrate the following: Essential Criteria: Have, or be about to obtain, a PhD (or equivalent) in a relevant discipline, such as genetic epidemiology, statistical genetics, bioinformatics, cancer genomics, epidemiology, data science or a related quantitative discipline Experience in cancer genomic medicine research, including hands-on analysis of genetic or genomic datasets Experience cleaning, quality-controlling and managing genotype or imputed genomic data, including pre- and post-imputation quality checks Experience calculating, validating or applying polygenic risk scores using genetic variant effect sizes and appropriate ancestry or population-structure adjustment Understanding of key genetic epidemiology concepts, including SNVs, alleles, imputation, linkage disequilibrium, ancestry, population structure and principal components Desirable Criteria: First or co-author publications in cancer detection research Experience with prostate cancer genetics, cancer risk prediction, diagnostic accuracy studies, calibration/discrimination or multivariable risk modelling Experience integrating genomic data with clinical, epidemiological, biomarker or imaging data Experience of working with PPI groups and co-investigators Expertise in cancer screening or early detection research We value transferable skills and real-world experience as much as formal qualifications. Our benefits include: • Generous employer contribution pension • 29 days annual leave plus bank holidays, along with Christmas closure • Ride to work and EV car scheme available For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Dr Samuel Merriel Email Address: Samuel.merriel@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home This role is not eligible for Skilled Worker visa sponsorship. Applicants must demonstrate the right to work in the UK. Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46186": {
      "title": "Research Fellow in Sustainable Automotive Polymers",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Research Fellow in Sustainable Automotive Polymers Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Research Fellow in Sustainable Automotive Polymers Apply for job Job reference: SAE-032527 Salary: £47,389 - £58,225 per annum depending on experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Science and Engineering Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Department of Materials Hours Per Week: Full time ( 1 FTE) Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 23/10/2026 Contract Duration: Fixed term for 26months School/Directorate: School of Natural Sciences Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description We are seeking a motivated and collaborative individual to join our team as a Research Fellow in Sustainable Automotive Polymers. This Research Fellowship position will involve working closely with The Vita Groups to develop sustainable automotive polymers, with a particular focus on sustainable foams for the transport industry. The project will critically evaluate sustainability, safety, and scalability throughout the project to enable translation into Vita pilots and prototypes. You will be responsible for: Improving the sustainability of polyurethane and alternative feedstock foams through increasing recycled content, developing carbon-negative and carbon-neutral additives and polymers, and improving recyclability. Leading the dissemination of their own research outcomes (e.g. through high-impact publications), presenting their independent work at international conferences, and technical communications with the technical team with Polestar and their collaborators. Develop and deliver their own individual programme of high-quality research through independent publications, securing additional external funding, and translating projects to industry, at an internationally leading level, within the field of sustainable polymers and related materials. Contribute to teaching within the School (at an appropriate level) and develop their teaching and supervision skills through the University’s New Academics Programme, which is accredited by the Higher Education Academy and provides an excellent grounding for transition into a full academic post. About You We encourage applications from individuals with a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. You should demonstrate: Essential Criteria: A PhD in Polymer Science, Sustainability or Circular Economy, or equivalent in a relevant scientific discipline. An established research track record, at an international level, in sustainable polymer science, including in one or more of the topics of life cycle assessment, particularly in circular economy or carbon negative materials, polymer recycling, new additives, or polymer characterisation. Well-developed technical skills with experience in designing and delivering research projects at a postdoctoral level. Demonstrable ability to provide effective leadership to small research teams. Well-developed communication and interpersonal skills to work effectively and network with a wide range of people, including industry, university staff and students at all levels, and as part of a team. Desirable Criteria: Experience in evaluating sustainability of polymers and plastics through life cycle assessment and/or technoeconomic analysis. Experience translating innovation into industry. Experience in polyurethane foams, with a particular focus on improved sustainability. Experience in developing and optimising circular pathways for automotive polymers or similar multi-materials. Our benefits include: • Generous employer contribution pension • 29 days annual leave plus bank holidays, along with Christmas closure • Ride to work and EV car scheme available For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Michael Shaver Email Address: michael.shaver@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=45928": {
      "title": "Lecturer in Pure Mathematics",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Lecturer in Pure Mathematics Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Lecturer in Pure Mathematics Apply for job Job reference: SAE-032269 Salary: £47,389 - £58,225 per annum depending on experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Science and Engineering Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Permanent Division/Team: Department of Mathematics Hours Per Week: Full time (1 FTE) Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 26/11/2026 Contract Duration: Permanent School/Directorate: School of Natural Sciences Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description Applications are invited for this Grade 7 post, to start in February 2027 or at a mutually agreed alternative date before September 2027. We are looking for mathematicians of outstanding ability who will contribute to the continued development of the Pure Mathematics Group and to the life of the Department of Mathematics. Applicants are sought in Pure Mathematics, with particular focus in Analysis and Dynamical Systems. Applications should normally be made online. Candidates should provide a full academic CV, to which they should append: (i) a research statement outlining their research record and future plans (up to 2 pages); (ii) a teaching statement outlining their teaching experience, approach and philosophy (up to 2 pages); and (iii) a statement outlining their commitment to non-discriminatory conduct and their contributions to creating inclusive environments (up to 1 page). Candidates should make sure to explain how they meet the essential and desirable criteria outlined in the person specification for the post. This information should be provided in the Supporting Information section of Jobtrain by listing each criterion as a paragraph heading and then explaining underneath how that criterion is met. You will be responsible for: undertaking high-quality research, in collaboration with colleagues in the University and other institutions as appropriate, and publishing the results in world-leading journals, books or other appropriate outlets; seeking grants and funding to support their research from relevant bodies; seeking to attract and supervise postgraduate research students and research assistants, associates and fellows; Actively contributing to delivering and enhancing the teaching programmes of Department of Mathematics at both undergraduate and postgraduate levels. contributing to the research life of the Department of Mathematics by participating in and organising research events and activities; We welcome candidates who bring diverse perspectives, experiences, and approaches to their work. About You We encourage applications from individuals with a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. You should demonstrate: Essential Criteria a PhD or equivalent research experience in mathematics or a closely related discipline; Sufficient knowledge to develop and deliver lecture courses as part of undergraduate and postgraduate degree programmes in pure mathematics; a strong record (commensurate with career stage) of original research in pure mathematics, in at least one of the following areas: Analysis and/or Dynamical Systems, evidenced by high-quality publications in internationally leading journals and appropriate esteem indicators, such as prizes and invitations to participate in research activities; evidence of potential for excellent teaching at both undergraduate and postgraduate levels; the skills to communicate effectively with staff, students and external collaborators. Desirable Criteria experience of supporting staff and students from a wide range of demographic and cultural backgrounds; a track record of excellent teaching at undergraduate and/or postgraduate level; evidence of a record of development in the content, delivery or assessment of the curriculum; experience of knowledge transfer via interaction with industry or public engagement. We value transferable skills and real-world experience as much as formal qualifications. Closing date: Tuesday 26th November 2026 Interview date: Tuesday 19th, Wednesday 20th , Thursday 21st January 2027 Our benefits include: • Generous employer contribution pension • 29 days annual leave plus bank holidays, along with Christmas closure • Ride to work and EV car scheme available For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Omar León Sánchez Email Address: omar.sanchez@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46150": {
      "title": "Lecturer (Teaching and Research) in International Business",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Lecturer (Teaching and Research) in International Business Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Lecturer (Teaching and Research) in International Business Apply for job Job reference: HUM-032487 Salary: £47,389 to £58,225 per annum, depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Humanities Location: Manchester Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Innovation Management and Policy Hours Per Week: Full Time Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 05/11/2026 Contract Duration: 2 years School/Directorate: Alliance Manchester Business School Job Description We are seeking a Lecturer in International Business for a full-time, two-year fixed-term Teaching and Research appointment. The successful candidate will contribute to undergraduate and postgraduate teaching, assessment and dissertation supervision while developing their own programme of research and publications. We welcome applications across international business and related fields from candidates whose research connects with or complements the Group’s strengths. We are open to different theoretical perspectives, methodological approaches and geographical areas of expertise. The post offers opportunities to develop research and teaching through collaboration, mentoring and participation in the Group’s academic activities. What you will get in return: Fantastic market leading Pension scheme Excellent employee health and wellbeing services including an Employee Assistance Programme Exceptional starting annual leave entitlement, plus bank holidays Additional paid closure over the Christmas period Local and national discounts at a range of major retailers As an equal opportunities employer we welcome applicants from all sections of the community regardless of age, sex, gender (or gender identity), ethnicity, disability, sexual orientation and transgender status. All appointments are made on merit. Our University is positive about flexible working – you can find out more here Hybrid working arrangements may be considered. Please be aware that due to the number of applications we unfortunately may not able to provide individual feedback on your application. Please note that we are unable to respond to enquiries, accept CVs or applications from Recruitment Agencies. Any recruitment enquiries from recruitment agencies should be directed to recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Any CV’s submitted by a recruitment agency will be considered a gift. Enquiries about the vacancy, shortlisting and interviews: Name: Professor Matthew Alford Email: matthew.alford@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries: Email: recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical support: 0161 850 2004 https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home This vacancy will close for applications at midnight on the closing date. Please see the link below for the Further Particulars document which contains the person specification criteria. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46233": {
      "title": "Research Technician",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Research Technician Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Research Technician Apply for job Job reference: BMH-032570 Salary: £29,588 to £32,080 per annum, depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Biology, Medicine Health Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: FBMH Technical Services Hours Per Week: 35 hours per week Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 22/10/2026 Contract Duration: 11 months School/Directorate: Faculty Office - Biology, Medicine and Health Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description We are seeking a motivated and collaborative individual to join our team as a Research Technician. This role offers an exciting opportunity to contribute to treating the autonomic neural pathobiology of a devastating early onset genetic urinary bladder disease within a dynamic and inclusive environment. You will be responsible for: Performing histology, immunohistochemistry, immunofluorescence, and other experiments needed to evaluate the pathology and treatment in a mouse model of urofacial syndrome (UFS), to maintain the UFS mouse colony and have extensive knowledge and excellent technical skills in gene and protein expression analysis. We welcome candidates who bring diverse perspectives, experiences, and approaches to their work. About You We encourage applications from individuals with a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. You should demonstrate: Essential Criteria: Knowledge of murine models of disease Practical experience in histology, molecular biology, immunohistochemistry and working with viral vectors such as AAVs Practical experience in project-specific laboratory techniques, such as whole tissue organ bath physiology techniques, such as myography Desirable Criteria: Practical experience of western blotting, microscopy, image analysis AND/OR physiology such as VSOP, cystometry, organ bath Experience of Bladder dissection We value transferable skills and real-world experience as much as formal qualifications. Our benefits include: (Grades 1-5) Generous employer pension contribution Christmas closure across all departments outside of annual leave and bank holidays Purple Place employee discount platform We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy. As an equal opportunities employer we welcome applicants from all sections of the community regardless of age, sex, gender (or gender identity), ethnicity, disability, sexual orientation and transgender status. All appointments are made on merit. Our University is positive about flexible working – you can find out more here Hybrid working arrangements may be considered. Please be aware that due to the number of applications we unfortunately may not able to provide individual feedback on your application. Please note that we are unable to respond to enquiries, accept CVs or applications from Recruitment Agencies. Any recruitment enquiries from recruitment agencies should be directed to People.Recruitment@manchester.ac.uk . Any CV’s submitted by a recruitment agency will be considered a gift. Please note this role is not eligible for sponsorship under the Skilled Worker route of the Points Based System. Candidates will need to be able to demonstrate their right to work in the UK in order to be eligible to take up the post. Enquiries about the vacancy, shortlisting and interviews: Name: Neil Roberts Email: neil.roberts-2@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries: Email: People.recruitment@manchester.ac.uk Technical support: Jobtrain: 0161 850 2004 https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home This vacancy will close for applications at midnight on the closing date. Please see the link below for the Further Particulars document which contains the person specification criteria. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46224": {
      "title": "Research Assistant in Mixed-Methods/Qualitative Research",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Research Assistant in Mixed-Methods/Qualitative Research - Co-production and feasibility of an intervention to enhance between-session work in low-intensity Cognitive Behavioural Therapy Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Research Assistant in Mixed-Methods/Qualitative Research - Co-production and feasibility of an intervention to enhance between-session work in low-intensity Cognitive Behavioural Therapy Apply for job Job reference: BMH-032563 Salary: £33,002 per annum Faculty/Organisational Unit: Biology, Medicine Health Location: Jean McFarlane Building, University of Manchester Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Division of Nursing, Midwifery & Social Work Hours Per Week: Part time (0.5 FTE) Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 21/10/2026 Contract Duration: Fixed Term from 1st Nov 2026 until 30th April 2029 School/Directorate: School of Health Sciences Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description This is an outstanding opportunity for a Research Assistant, with a background in mental health, psychology, health services research or a related discipline, to work within the Division of Nursing, Midwifery and Social Work at the University of Manchester under the supervision of Dr Mia Bennion and the wider research team. The post holder will be part of the Mental Health Research group, which is based within the Jean McFarlane Building. The candidate will support the aims of the NIHR Research for Patient Benefit-funded project, “Improving engagement with between-session work in NHS Talking Therapies: Testing the feasibility and acceptability of a co-produced practitioner behaviour change intervention.” This innovative project will evaluate the feasibility and acceptability of an intervention designed to support practitioner behaviours that improve patient engagement with between-session work within NHS Talking Therapies. This position offers an ideal opportunity for an enthusiastic, well-motivated individual to work as part of a collaborative multidisciplinary team consisting of researchers, NHS clinicians, and people with lived experience. The ideal candidate will have excellent research, organisational, communication and interpersonal skills, and will be able to work effectively with a degree of independence. Experience of conducting applied health or mental health research, including collecting and managing research data and working with research participants, is essential for the position. You will be responsible for: Supporting the preparation and submission of ethics applications and other necessary research approvals, study documentation and amendments. Supporting relationships with participating Talking Therapies services and community organisations, including coordinating study set-up and recruitment activities. Recruiting patients, practitioners and other relevant stakeholders and supporting informed consent and ongoing participant engagement. Organising and supporting co-production workshops with patients, practitioners and key informants to inform the development and refinement of the intervention. Collecting quantitative and qualitative research data, including questionnaires, routinely collected clinical data and semi-structured interviews with patients, practitioners and key informants. We welcome candidates who bring diverse perspectives, experiences, and approaches to their work. About You We encourage applications from individuals with a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. You should demonstrate: Essential Criteria First or upper second-class honours degrees (or equivalent) in Psychology or a relevant social science or health science discipline. Experience of conducting qualitative research, including interviews and qualitative data analysis. Experience of collecting, managing and analysing quantitative research data. Experience of contributing to mixed-methods research in health, social care or a related field. Good organisational skills, attention to detail and ability to maintain accurate research records. We value transferable skills and real-world experience as much as formal qualifications. Our benefits include: Generous employer pension contribution Christmas closure across all departments outside of annual leave and bank holidays Purple Place employee discount platform For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Dr Mia Bennion Email Address: mia.bennion@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home This role is not eligible for Skilled Worker visa sponsorship. Applicants must demonstrate the right to work in the UK. Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46161": {
      "title": "Assessment Administrator",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Assessment Administrator Cookie Policy By clicking \"Allow Cookies\", you agree to the storing of cookies on your device to analyse site usage, and assist in our marketing efforts. Cookie Policy Decline Allow cookies Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Assessment Administrator Apply for job Job reference: HUM-032500 Salary: Grade 4 - £29,588-£32,080 (depending on relevant experience) Faculty/Organisational Unit: Humanities Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Permanent Division/Team: Alliance MBS Worldwide Hours Per Week: 35 hours per week (1 FTE) Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 28/10/2026 Contract Duration: Permanent School/Directorate: Alliance Manchester Business School Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description We are looking for an organised and proactive Assessment Administrator . This role offers an exciting opportunity to support assessment administration and delivery, and the student experience across Global and MBA programmes within a dynamic and inclusive environment. You will be responsible for: Take responsibility for the administration of assessment activities across programmes, including assessment set up, assessment scheduling, submission and marking processes, academic support, assessment reviews and support for international centres and partners. Provide academic and operational support for assessment activity, including support to academic staff during assessment periods and related activities, which may include out of office hours and weekend support, and collate and circulate assessment and examination documentation. Provide information, advice and guidance on assessment and examination matters, acting as first line support for team inbox queries and stakeholders, including students, academic staff and international centres. Maintain accurate records and data quality within student records and other systems, supporting reporting, financial processes, compliance, policy implementation and management information needs. Work flexibly across the Operations team, supporting committees, surveys, feedback review, projects, continuous improvement, excellent customer service and inclusive practice across all activity. About You We welcome applications from people who are committed to excellent service provision, continuous improvement and placing the student experience at the heart of what they do. You should be able to work effectively with diverse groups and demonstrate: Essential Criteria: Relevant knowledge and experience, such as working in a demanding administrative environment with defined procedures and policies. Flexible and responsive to customer needs, with excellent communication skills and the ability to provide information, advice and guidance while maintaining confidentiality. Able to coordinate assessment and examination activities, organise workloads and contribute effectively within a large team environment. Excellent organisational, analytical and problem-solving skills, with the ability to prioritise varied work, meet deadlines and resolve issues effectively. A high level of accuracy, good digital literacy and a commitment to the University’s core values and to providing the best possible support to students, staff and customers. Desirable Criteria: Experience of working in Higher Education, particularly within assessment, examinations, student administration or programme delivery support. Experience using student records or assessment systems, supporting data quality, or preparing assessment-related reports and management information. Experience supporting assessment boards, examinations, assessment processes, surveys, projects or process improvement within a complex organisation. Our benefits include: Generous employer pension contribution Christmas closure across all departments outside of annual leave and bank holidays Purple Place employee discount platform For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits. You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here. We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy. Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Dan Nelson Email: daniel.nelson-2@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home This role is not eligible for Skilled Worker visa sponsorship. Applicants must demonstrate the right to work in the UK. Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Supporting Documents save_alt Job Description (DOCX, KB) Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46115": {
      "title": "Teaching Associate",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Teaching Associate Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Teaching Associate Apply for job Job reference: BMH-032451 Salary: £37,694 - £46,049 per annum depending on experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Biology, Medicine Health Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Division of Cell Matrix Biology and Regenerative Medicine Hours Per Week: Full time ( 1 FTE) Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 21/10/2026 Contract Duration: Fixed term for 24 months School/Directorate: School of Biological Sciences Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description The Division of Cell Matrix Biology and Regenerative Medicine invites applications for a Teaching Associate. This is a fixed-term position available for two years. The successful candidate will have a PhD (or equivalent) in a Biomedical Sciences related subject area and will have some experience of high-quality teaching or demonstrating in a related field. You will be responsible for: The postholder will deliver small group teaching such as tutorials. Support practical teaching such as year 1 and 2 practicals and 3rd year project supervision. Design and deliver lectures to support practical classes where required. Contribute to lectures for master classes. Mark assessments of various types and provide constructive feedback to students. We welcome candidates who bring diverse perspectives, experiences, and approaches to their work. About You We encourage applications from individuals with a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. You should demonstrate: Essential Criteria A PhD (or equivalent) in a Biomedical Sciences related subject area. Some experience of high-quality teaching or demonstrating in a related field. We value transferable skills and real-world experience as much as formal qualifications. Our benefits include: • Generous employer contribution pension • 29 days annual leave plus bank holidays, along with Christmas closure • Ride to work and EV car scheme available For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Professor Pat Caswell Email Address: patrick.caswell@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46106": {
      "title": "Research Associate for Manufacturing-Oriented Computational Design",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Research Associate for Manufacturing-Oriented Computational Design Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Research Associate for Manufacturing-Oriented Computational Design Apply for job Job reference: SAE-032449 Salary: £37,694 - £46,049 per annum depending on experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Science and Engineering Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Department of Mechanical and Aerospace Engineering Hours Per Week: Full time (1 FTE) Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 21/10/2026 Contract Duration: Fixed term for 18 months School/Directorate: School of Engineering Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description We are seeking a motivated and collaborative individual to join the Digital Manufacturing Lab as a Research Associate for Manufacturing-Oriented Computational Design. The project aims to develop a computational kernel to effectively generate optimised structure and toolpaths for robot-assisted additive manufacturing with dynamically changed material accumulation directions. The research is funded by The University of Manchester and the UK Engineering and Physical Sciences Research Council (EPSRC). You will be responsible for: Develop research objectives and proposals for own or joint research, with the assistance of a mentor if required. Conduct individual and collaborative research projects. Write up research work for publication. Prepare proposals and applications to external bodies, e.g. for funding and contractual purposes. Plan and manage own research activity in collaboration with others. We welcome candidates who bring diverse perspectives, experiences, and approaches to their work. About You We encourage applications from individuals with a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. You should demonstrate: Essential Criteria Have, or be about to obtain, a relevant PhD (or equivalent) in Mechanical Engineering / Industrial Engineering Specialist knowledge in computational design, advanced manufacturing, intelligent mechatronics or robotics Experience in C++ programming and mathematics formulation Excellent communication and interpersonal skills Ability to work independently and as part of a team We value transferable skills and real-world experience as much as formal qualifications. Our benefits include: • Generous employer contribution pension • 29 days annual leave plus bank holidays, along with Christmas closure • Ride to work and EV car scheme available For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Charlie Wang Email Address: charlie.wang@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
