    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46199": {
      "title": "Business Development Manager",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Business Development Manager Cookie Policy By clicking \"Allow Cookies\", you agree to the storing of cookies on your device to analyse site usage, and assist in our marketing efforts. Cookie Policy Decline Allow cookies Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Business Development Manager Apply for job Job reference: CIX-032564 Salary: £37,694 to £46,049 per annum, depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Cultural Institutions Location: Jodrell Bank, Cheshire Employment type: Permanent Division/Team: JBCE Operations Hours Per Week: Full Time Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 28/10/2026 Contract Duration: Permanent starting as soon as possible School/Directorate: Jodrell Bank Centre for Engagement Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description The Business Development Manager is a pivotal role for Jodrell Bank Centre for Engagement (JBCE), responsible for driving and maximising earned income across the centre from venue hire, groups, filming and other revenue generating initiatives at a unique, UNESCO world heritage site. The role is responsible for leading and driving all sales activity, together with the development and delivery of the centre’s business strategy which proactively generates new business and income across corporate meetings, events and private dining, together with weddings, group bookings and filming opportunities. Commercial activity will be successfully converted, planned and delivered through building strong relationships with corporate clients, agencies, private bookers and key partners, together with colleagues across JBCE. In this role, you will: Curate a \"unique\" commercial offer & experience: develop premium packages and offers which reflect Jodrell’s location and unique facilities for corporate and group bookings. Lead an inspired team: Motivate and empower our Events Coordinator and team of Casual Event Assistants to deliver professional client liaison and exceptional customer service when attending site for their booking/event. Commercial focus: Have a keen focus on commercial success, driving revenue and maximising profitability, whilst being mindful of other activity across a busy visitor and educational site. Partner for success: Collaborate with the Head of Marketing & Communications, together with the Catering & Hospitality Manager to help plan marketing and sales initiatives to showcase the facilities and catering across Jodrell Bank for bookings and events. Contribute to our strategic vision: Set and track key performance indicators for commercial business, check costs, analyse budgets, and contribute to the annual business plans for Venue Hire, group bookings and other income streams. Jodrell Bank is a vibrant centre for scientific discovery, education and inspiration. You will join a passionate team and play a key role in shaping an outstanding visitor experience. Our Values Jodrell Bank Centre for Engagement (JBCE) is a Cultural Institution of the University of Manchester. We value: Inclusivity: we support participation for diverse audiences, communities and staff, seeking to connect people with Jodrell Bank and with each other. Furthermore, we strive to create an inclusive culture by recognising each other’s differences and strengths. Integrity: we care deeply about the impact of our work, and we hold ourselves to high standards. We value honesty and integrity, ensuring that our practices are always responsible, ethical and sustainable. Empowerment: we empower our audiences, communities, our staff and our volunteers to enable confidence. Support: Our success rests on the achievements of others, and in turn we aim to provide the tools and the support needed to inspire others. All colleagues are expected to share this vision and help create a rewarding and fulfilling working environment. What you will get in return: Fantastic market leading Pension scheme Excellent employee health and wellbeing services including an Employee Assistance Programme Exceptional starting annual leave entitlement, plus bank holidays Additional paid closure over the Christmas period Local and national discounts at a range of major retailers As an equal opportunities employer we welcome applicants from all sections of the community regardless of age, sex, gender (or gender identity), ethnicity, disability, sexual orientation and transgender status. All appointments are made on merit. Our University is positive about flexible working – you can find out more here Hybrid working arrangements may be considered. Please be aware that due to the number of applications we unfortunately may not able to provide individual feedback on your application. Please note that we are unable to respond to enquiries, accept CVs or applications from Recruitment Agencies. Any recruitment enquiries from recruitment agencies should be directed to recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Any CV’s submitted by a recruitment agency will be considered a gift. Enquiries about the vacancy, shortlisting and interviews: Name: Adam Woof Email: adam.woof@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries: Email: recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical support: 0161 850 2004 https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home This vacancy will close for applications at midnight on the closing date. Please see the link below for the Further Particulars document which contains the person specification criteria. Supporting Documents save_alt CIX-032564 Business Development Manager FPs (PDF, KB) Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46139": {
      "title": "Research Associate on Pinching Antenna Systems",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Research Associate on Pinching Antenna Systems Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Research Associate on Pinching Antenna Systems Apply for job Job reference: SAE-032480 Salary: £37,694 - £46,049 per annum depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Science and Engineering Location: Manchester Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Department of Electrical and Electronic Engineering Hours Per Week: 1 FTE Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 20/10/2026 Contract Duration: 36 Months School/Directorate: School of Engineering Job Description The University of Manchester (www.manchester.ac.uk) enjoys a global reputation for its research and its innovative approach to learning, with a £1 billion investment in facilities, staff and new engineering buildings that fully opened in 2022. This builds on our tradition of success that stretches back over 180 years. We are at the forefront of the search for solutions to some of the world's most pressing challenges, with strong collaborative links with industry and public services. The Department of Electrical and Electronic Engineering (EEE) at the University of Manchester is one of the largest departments in its discipline in the UK. The Communications and Signal Processing research group has the combined strength of communication systems, microwave engineering, and signal processing. With 13 academic staff and over 70 PhD students, research associates and visitors, it is one of the largest groups in the UK specialising in mobile wireless networks, antennas and propagation, RF and microwave sensors and tomographic systems, radar, signal analysis and processing. The post is funded by a grant from the Engineering and Physical Sciences Research Council (EPSRC) on “Pinching Antenna Systems (PASS) – A New Paradigm for Wireless Network Designs”. The aim of this project is to investigate a new type of flexible-antenna systems, PASS, whose core concept is to activate pinching antennas by applying small dielectric particles, e.g., plastic pinches, onto a dielectric waveguide. A prototype demonstrated that strong LoS communication links can be established by this simple, low-cost pinching mechanism. With a stepper motor, a pinching antenna can be dynamically deployed near the receiver to establish the strong LoS link, achieving ultra-high spectral and energy efficiency. This architecture creates a new paradigm for future mobile networks and is currently one of the most active research topics in wireless communication. Purpose of the Job This post will support a recently awarded grant conducting ambitious research on the development of PASS. The scope of work ranges from the development of a theoretical framework to the designing of a practical transceiver. The goal of this project is to advance the technology readiness level of PASS for commercial adoption. In particular, these are the project objectives: Characterize the communication theoretic fundamentals of PASS; Design spectrally efficient and low-overhead channel estimation algorithms for PASS; Develop sophisticated transceiver designs to realize the full potential of PASS; Investigate two crucial applications of PASS, namely NOMA and ISAC. The project is led by the University of Manchester, and involves the University of Essex and three industrial partners. The post holder will work collaboratively with project partners to deliver high‑quality research on PASS and ensure the successful completion of all project objectives. The University actively fosters a culture of inclusion and diversity and seeks to achieve true equality of opportunity for all members of its community. The Faculty welcomes applications from all sections of the community and are committed to having a representative workforce. Across the Schools we hold Bronze and Silver Athena SWAN Awards, which recognise our commitment to equality, diversity and inclusion and particularly the advancement of women’s careers in STEM. (Grades 6-9) Our benefits include: • Generous employer contribution pension • 29 days annual leave plus bank holidays, along with Christmas closure • Ride to work and EV car scheme available For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Professor Daniel So Email: d.so@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46153": {
      "title": "ICNS Research Associate in Neuromorphic Vision Systems",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for ICNS Research Associate in Neuromorphic Vision Systems Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results ICNS Research Associate in Neuromorphic Vision Systems Apply for job Job reference: SAE-032494 Salary: £37,694 to £46,049 per annum, depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Science and Engineering Location: Manchester Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Department of Computer Science Hours Per Week: Full Time Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 20/10/2026 Contract Duration: 24 months School/Directorate: School of Engineering Job Description The Postdoctoral Research Associate (Neuromorphic Vision Systems) plays a pivotal role in designing and implementing neuromorphic vision systems, spanning the theory of pixel operation, optimisation of processing algorithms, and hardware implementation. This position involves close collaboration with interdisciplinary teams across neuroscience, engineering, and computer science to develop functional models capable of learning, storing, and detecting patterns. The role also includes contributing to demonstrator applications that showcase the practical impact of the research. Beyond technical development, the Research Associate is expected to actively contribute to the University’s research output through data analysis, publication, and dissemination via seminars, conferences, and public engagement. Additionally, the role supports the identification and development of new research directions and the preparation of proposals for both independent and collaborative projects. What you will get in return: Fantastic market leading Pension scheme Excellent employee health and wellbeing services including an Employee Assistance Programme Exceptional starting annual leave entitlement, plus bank holidays Additional paid closure over the Christmas period Local and national discounts at a range of major retailers As an equal opportunities employer we welcome applicants from all sections of the community regardless of age, sex, gender (or gender identity), ethnicity, disability, sexual orientation and transgender status. All appointments are made on merit. Our University is positive about flexible working – you can find out more here Hybrid working arrangements may be considered. Please be aware that due to the number of applications we unfortunately may not able to provide individual feedback on your application. Please note that we are unable to respond to enquiries, accept CVs or applications from Recruitment Agencies. Any recruitment enquiries from recruitment agencies should be directed to recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Any CV’s submitted by a recruitment agency will be considered a gift. Enquiries about the vacancy, shortlisting and interviews: Name: Andre Van Schaik Email: andre.vanschaik@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries: Email: recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical support: 0161 850 2004 https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home This vacancy will close for applications at midnight on the closing date. Please see the link below for the Further Particulars document which contains the person specification criteria. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46188": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research associate (neuroscience)",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Postdoctoral Research associate (neuroscience) Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Postdoctoral Research associate (neuroscience) Apply for job Job reference: BMH-032529 Salary: UoM Grade 6 £37,694 Faculty/Organisational Unit: Biology, Medicine Health Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Division of Immunology, Immunity to Infection, and Respiratory Medicine Hours Per Week: Full time (1FTE) Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 03/11/2026 Contract Duration: Fixed term for 18 months School/Directorate: School of Biological Sciences Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description We are seeking an experienced/senior postdoctoral researcher in neuroscience to explore how the immune system shapes hypothalamic circuits involved in sleep-wake cycle regulations during development and in response to inflammation. This is an exciting opportunity for a highly ambitious, competitive, and motivated individual interested in developing an innovative research programme combining immunology and neuroscience. The post holder will investigate the mechanisms involved in sculpting inhibitory and excitatory circuits in the hypothalamus, from early life through to adulthood. The successful candidate will join the Quintana Lab, based within the Lydia Becker Institute of Immunology and Inflammation and the Geoffrey Jefferson Centre for Brain Research, working within a dynamic, collaborative and inclusive research environment. You will be responsible for: To conduct original research exploring the immunological basis of hypothalamic maturation Strong abilities to conduct surgeries and behavioural studies in mice To identify and develop suitable techniques, and apparatus, for the collection and analysis of data To draft publications and prepare them for submission to refereed journals To lead on and contribute to writing bids for research grants We welcome candidates who bring diverse perspectives, experiences, and approaches to their work. About You We encourage applications from individuals with a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. You should demonstrate: Essential Criteria: Hold a PhD in neuroscience, behavioural sciences, sleep, or related disciplines. Demonstrable experience through high-impact scientific outputs as first and/or corresponding author. Extensive and up-to-date theoretical and practical knowledge in neuroscience, electrophysiology, behaviour, sleep, in vivo genetic manipulations, and related skills. Proven ability to lead project and teams as demonstrated by publications as first-authors and/or demonstrable deliverables within an industry setting. Excellent interpersonal and communication skills and ability to work with colleagues at all levels. Desirable Criteria: Experience with patent applications. Experience working in industrial R&D. Demonstrable track-record of high impact outputs as first author. We value transferable skills and real-world experience as much as formal qualifications. Our benefits include: Generous employer contribution pension 29 days annual leave plus bank holidays, along with Christmas closure Ride to work and EV car scheme available For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Dr. Juan F. Quintana Email Address: juan.quintana@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support Jobtrain Client Support Hub Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46187": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research associate",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Postdoctoral Research associate Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Postdoctoral Research associate Apply for job Job reference: BMH-032528 Salary: UoM Grade 6 £39,906 Faculty/Organisational Unit: Biology, Medicine Health Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Division of Immunology, Immunity to Infection, and Respiratory Medicine Hours Per Week: Full time (1 FTE) Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 03/11/2026 Contract Duration: Fixed term for 9 months School/Directorate: School of Biological Sciences Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description We are seeking an experienced postdoctoral research associate to lead an ongoing clinical study exploring the immunological basis of sleep disorders. This is an exciting opportunity for someone interested in developing translational research whilst also maintaining their ability to conduct basic research in neuroimmunology. The postholder will work collaboratively with partners at the NHS and industrial collaborators to develop this research. The role is based within the Quintana Lab, part of the Lydia Becker Institute of Immunology and Inflammation and the Geoffrey Jefferson Centre for Brain Research. The successful candidate will benefit from a rich network of national and international collaborators, ranging from basic scientists to clinical partners, within a research environment that values curiosity, passion, inclusion, humility and teamwork. You will be responsible for: To conduct original research exploring the immunological basis of sleep disorders To identify and develop suitable techniques, and apparatus, for the collection and analysis of data To draft publications and prepare them for submission to refereed journals To submit publications to refereed journals To lead on and contribute to writing bids for research grants We welcome candidates who bring diverse perspectives, experiences, and approaches to their work. About You We encourage applications from individuals with a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. You should demonstrate: Essential Criteria: Hold a PhD in immunology (or related fields) Extensive and demonstrable experience in B cell biology through first-author publications Extensive and demonstrable industrial experience in Biosciences and/or industrial R&D Extensive and up-to-date theoretical and practical knowledge in immunoparasitology Excellent technical skills in immunoparasitology and in vivo models of infection Proven ability to lead project and teams as demonstrated by publications as first-author and/or demonstrable deliverables within an industry setting Desirable Criteria: Experience working with African trypanosomes in mice at PhD level Experience working in industrial R&D Demonstrable track-record of high impact outputs as first author We value transferable skills and real-world experience as much as formal qualifications. Our benefits include: • Generous employer contribution pension • 29 days annual leave plus bank holidays, along with Christmas closure • Ride to work and EV car scheme available For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Dr. Juan F. Quintana Email Address: juan.quintana@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support Jobtrain Client Support Hub Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=45955": {
      "title": "AVID Research and Business Engagement (RBE) Officer",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for AVID Research and Business Engagement (RBE) Officer Cookie Policy By clicking \"Allow Cookies\", you agree to the storing of cookies on your device to analyse site usage, and assist in our marketing efforts. Cookie Policy Decline Allow cookies Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results AVID Research and Business Engagement (RBE) Officer Apply for job Job reference: SAE-032296 Salary: £37,694 to £46,049 per annum, depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Science and Engineering Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Department of Materials Hours Per Week: Full Time Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 19/10/2026 Contract Duration: until 31 March 2030 School/Directorate: School of Natural Sciences Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description The purpose of the role is to support businesses in the Greater Manchester region and beyond to accelerate innovation in advanced materials systems. The RBE Officer will play a crucial role in engaging businesses on projects, both in the provision of advice and in managing the transition to assessment and through translating innovation and connecting regional businesses to national supply chain needs. Key advanced materials themes include, but are not limited to advanced fibres, sustainable polymers, digital manufacturing and materials for the hydrogen economy. The role will have considerable focus also on supporting the operational delivery of the Sustainable Materials Manufacturing Centre (SMMC), engaging prospective tenants and users, coordinating visits and onboarding activities, and developing a pipeline of businesses that can access SMMC facilities, expertise and innovation programmes to support innovation and growth across the Atom Valley cluster. The post holder will work closely with the wider University research Institutes and centres (i.e. Royce/GEIC/Sustainable Materials Innovation Hub) and consortium partners’ technical and research teams to support businesses primarily SMEs throughout their journey. The RBE Officer will be line managed by the Business Development Manager at the Henry Royce Institute and will report also to the Principal Investigator on the grant. The role will be primarily based at the Sustainable Materials and Manufacturing Centre (SMMC), currently under construction at the Atom Valley Innovation District in Rochdale, with occasional travel to the UoM campus and partner sites as appropriate. Until the opening of the SMMC in mid-2027, the role will be based at the UoM campus. This is an exciting opportunity to play a key support role at the intersection of academia, industry and local government, helping to accelerate innovation and enable inclusive economic growth. What you will get in return: Fantastic market leading Pension scheme Excellent employee health and wellbeing services including an Employee Assistance Programme Exceptional starting annual leave entitlement, plus bank holidays Additional paid closure over the Christmas period Local and national discounts at a range of major retailers As an equal opportunities employer we welcome applicants from all sections of the community regardless of age, sex, gender (or gender identity), ethnicity, disability, sexual orientation and transgender status. All appointments are made on merit. Our University is positive about flexible working – you can find out more here Hybrid working arrangements may be considered. Please be aware that due to the number of applications we unfortunately may not able to provide individual feedback on your application. Please note that we are unable to respond to enquiries, accept CVs or applications from Recruitment Agencies. Any recruitment enquiries from recruitment agencies should be directed to recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Any CV’s submitted by a recruitment agency will be considered a gift. Enquiries about the vacancy, shortlisting and interviews: Name: Michelle Ledward, Business Development Manager or Jill Brown, Programme Manager Email: Michelle.ledward@manchester.ac.uk or jill.brown@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries: Email: recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical support : 0161 850 2004 https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home This vacancy will close for applications at midnight on the closing date. Please see the link below for the Further Particulars document which contains the person specification criteria. Supporting Documents save_alt SAE-032296 - AVID Research and Business Engagement (RBE) Officer FPs (PDF, KB) Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46119": {
      "title": "Vice-Dean and Head of the School&#39;s: Medical Sciences &amp; Health Sciences",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Vice-Dean and Head of the School&#39;s: Medical Sciences &amp; Health Sciences Cookie Policy By clicking \"Allow Cookies\", you agree to the storing of cookies on your device to analyse site usage, and assist in our marketing efforts. Cookie Policy Decline Allow cookies Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Vice-Dean and Head of the School's: Medical Sciences & Health Sciences Apply for job Job reference: BMH-032454 Salary: Competitive Faculty/Organisational Unit: Biology, Medicine Health Location: Manchester Employment type: Permanent Division/Team: Not specified Hours Per Week: FTE Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 15/11/2026 Contract Duration: Initial five year term, with the possibility of extension School/Directorate: Multiple Schools Job Description Help shape the future of health, discovery and education at Manchester. Two Schools. Two leadership opportunities. Vice-Dean and Head of the School of Medical Sciences Vice-Dean and Head of the School of Health Sciences The University of Manchester is seeking two outstanding academic leaders to join the Faculty of Biology, Medicine and Health (FBMH) as Vice-Dean and Head of School, leading either the School of Medical Sciences or the School of Health Sciences. These are significant leadership opportunities at a pivotal moment for our university, our faculty and the wider health and higher education sectors. As one of the UK's largest and most comprehensive faculties of its kind, FBMH brings together world leading research, outstanding education and powerful partnerships across healthcare, industry and communities. Through Manchester 2035, we are advancing knowledge, improving lives and delivering meaningful social, economic and environmental impact through research, education and innovation. Lead with purpose As Vice-Dean and Head of School, you will provide visible, inclusive and values-led leadership to your School, setting strategic direction and creating the conditions for colleagues and students to thrive. You will lead your School's contribution to the ambitions of FBMH and the University, driving excellence in research, education, innovation, knowledge exchange, public engagement and social responsibility. Working across disciplines and organisational boundaries, you will help shape solutions to some of the most pressing health and societal challenges of our time. As a member of the Faculty Executive and the University's extended leadership community, you will influence institutional strategy and decision making. Working closely with the Dean, Faculty colleagues and the Director of School Operations, you will lead change, align priorities and build a sustainable future for your School. You will also strengthen relationships across the NHS, Greater Manchester's health and care system, industry, government, civic organisations and academic partners, creating new opportunities for collaboration, innovation and impact. What you will bring You will be an accomplished academic leader with a strong track record of achievement and experience of leading within a complex research, education or healthcare environment. Academic distinction and credibility. Strategic vision and the ability to translate ambition into delivery. Experience of leading change and developing inclusive, high-performing cultures. Strong financial, operational and organisational leadership capability. The ability to inspire, engage and develop colleagues and students. A collaborative approach and commitment to partnership working. Above all, you will be a leader who builds trust, enables others to succeed and brings people together behind a shared ambition. Join Manchester. Shape what comes next. These appointments offer a rare opportunity to lead ambitious Schools within a globally recognised Faculty and to help shape the future of health, discovery and education at one of the world's leading universities. If you are inspired by the opportunity to make a lasting difference through academic leadership, we would welcome your application. Please note: These are separate appointments for the School of Medical Sciences and the School of Health Sciences. Please indicate in your cover letter if you would like to be considered for a specific School or for both. A shared Candidate Brochure applies and can be found below, with candidates assessed in the context of the School they would lead. Panel interviews are due to take place on Monday 30th November. Candidate Brochure - UoM FBMH Vice-Dean & Head of Schools Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Rosie Harmshaw Email Address: talent.attraction@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home This vacancy will close for applications at midnight on the closing date. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46157": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate in Mixed-Signal Chip Design",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Postdoctoral Research Associate in Mixed-Signal Chip Design Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Postdoctoral Research Associate in Mixed-Signal Chip Design Apply for job Job reference: SAE-032497 Salary: £37,694 - £46,049 per annum depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Science and Engineering Location: Manchester Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Department of Electrical and Electronic Engineering Hours Per Week: 35 Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 20/10/2026 Contract Duration: 30 months School/Directorate: School of Engineering Job Description The University of Manchester is inviting applications for a Post-Doctoral Research Associate to lead the design of next-generation vision sensors. This role focuses on developing mixed-signal integrated circuits (ICs) for advanced imaging and edge computing applications, exploring novel approaches to integrating sensing and pixel-level processing. The research involves full-custom chip design using state-of-the-art EDA tools and fabrication of analogue and mixed-signal circuits. You will explore new transistor-level circuits through simulations and layout design, while also contributing to the back-end physical implementation of digital subsystems. The work addresses practical aspects of system implementation, including chip tape-out, post-silicon laboratory bring-up, system integration, and building practical technology demonstrations of the fabricated silicon. This position is embedded within a long-standing research programme on vision sensors and neuromorphic computing circuits in the laboratory of Prof Piotr Dudek at the University of Manchester. The role is directly associated with the EPSRC-funded project on \"On-sensor Computer Vision,\" carried out in collaboration with Imperial College London, University of Bristol, and industrial partners. Our benefits include: Generous employer contribution pension, 29 days annual leave plus bank holidays, along with Christmas closure, Ride to work and EV car scheme available. For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Piotr Dudek Email Address: p.dudek@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46072": {
      "title": "Teaching, Learning and Student Experience Coordinator (Student Support and Wellbeing)",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Teaching, Learning and Student Experience Coordinator (Student Support and Wellbeing) Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Teaching, Learning and Student Experience Coordinator (Student Support and Wellbeing) Apply for job Job reference: HUM-032417 Salary: £33,002 to £36,636 pro rata per annum, depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Humanities Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Permanent Division/Team: School of Arts, Languages and Cultures Administration Hours Per Week: 0.5 FTE  17.5 hrs per week Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 26/10/2026 Contract Duration: Permanent School/Directorate: School of Arts, Languages and Cultures Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description We are seeking a motivated and collaborative individual to join our team as a Teaching, Learning Student Experience Coordinator (Student Support and Wellbeing). We are looking for a Teaching, Learning and Student Experience Coordinator to join our Student Support and Wellbeing team within the School of Arts, Languages and Cultures. This is an excellent opportunity for an organised, proactive and student focused professional to play a key role in supporting student wellbeing, academic engagement and success. You will coordinate a team delivering frontline support services and work closely with students, academic colleagues and specialist support services to ensure students receive effective and timely support. About the role Working in a busy and rewarding environment, you will: Lead and coordinate a team providing student support and wellbeing services. Support students with a range of issues, ensuring they are signposted to appropriate University support services. Coordinate key student support processes, including mitigating circumstances, fitness to study and student engagement monitoring. Act as Disability Coordinator for the School, working closely with the Disability Advisory and Support Service (DASS). Develop effective working relationships with academic and professional services colleagues across the University. Use data and insight to support service delivery and continuous improvement. Contribute to a culture of excellent customer service, innovation and student centred practice. About you We are looking for someone with: Experience of working in student support, student administration, higher education or a comparable customer-focused environment. Experience of supervising or coordinating the work of others. Excellent interpersonal and communication skills, with the ability to build positive relationships with a wide range of stakeholders. Strong organisational skills and the ability to manage competing priorities in a complex environment. A compassionate and professional approach when dealing with sensitive student matters. A commitment to delivering excellent service and improving the student experience. Strong digital skills and confidence working with a range of systems and data. We welcome candidates who bring diverse perspectives, experiences, and approaches to their work. What you will get in return: Fantastic market leading Pension scheme Excellent employee health and wellbeing services including an Employee Assistance Programme Exceptional starting annual leave entitlement, plus bank holidays Additional paid closure over the Christmas period Local and national discounts at a range of major retailers We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . As an equal opportunities employer we welcome applicants from all sections of the community regardless of age, sex, gender (or gender identity), ethnicity, disability, sexual orientation and transgender status. All appointments are made on merit. Our University is positive about flexible working – you can find out more here Hybrid working arrangements may be considered. Please be aware that due to the number of applications we unfortunately may not able to provide individual feedback on your application. Please note that we are unable to respond to enquiries, accept CVs or applications from Recruitment Agencies. Any recruitment enquiries from recruitment agencies should be directed to recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Any CV’s submitted by a recruitment agency will be considered a gift. Enquiries about the vacancy, shortlisting and interviews: Name: Sarah Thompson Email: sarah.thompson-2@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries: Email: recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical support: 0161 850 2004 https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home This vacancy will close for applications at midnight on the closing date. Please see the link below for the Further Particulars document which contains the person specification criteria. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46096": {
      "title": "Senior Technician (Mechatronics)",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Senior Technician (Mechatronics) Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Senior Technician (Mechatronics) Apply for job Job reference: SAE-032476 Salary: £33,002 to £36,636 per annum, depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Science and Engineering Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Permanent Division/Team: FSE Technical Services Hours Per Week: 35 Hours Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 26/10/2026 Contract Duration: Permanent School/Directorate: Faculty Office - Science and Engineering Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description This role offers an exciting opportunity to play a key part in supporting Undergraduate students in the area of mechatronics. within the Faculty of Science and Engineering. Working as a Senior Technician within technical platform, you will apply your technical expertise to support practical student projects and student society activities. Through design and development you will help create the facilities, equipment and technical solutions that enable discovery, learning and innovation. Your contribution will directly enhance the student experience, support academic excellence, and help deliver sector leading teaching support. As Technical Staff, you are part of Professional Services. Together, all technical staff who work within the Faculty of Science and Engineering (FSE) form FSE Technical Operations. FSE Technical Operations is organised into five key functional areas: • Research • Teaching • Infrastructure & Facilities • Workshops • Computing, Robotics & AI Teaching - The teaching technical team provides high quality technical advice and support to students of all years of education. The team are led by Technical Specialists (TTS) who are responsible for managing and coordinating technical support for teaching in each of their specific disciplines. This includes all related teaching space, associated equipment and ensuring all Health and Safety obligations are met. The team support a host of taught units within each department. These can be based in computer clusters, bespoke labs or other shared areas.. The team works closely with Timetabling, Discipline Heads of Education and laboratory tutors to advise on experiment design and the technical resource required for its delivery. The role will be part of the Makerspace team. The team is made up of 3 dedicated technicians and is supported by technical teams from other areas. The team support 3rd and 4th year group and individual projects and societies including Robosoc, Giant foamboard aircraft, UAV and ManSEDS. What you will get in return: Fantastic market leading Pension scheme Excellent employee health and wellbeing services including an Employee Assistance Programme Exceptional starting annual leave entitlement, plus bank holidays Additional paid closure over the Christmas period Local and national discounts at a range of major retailers As an equal opportunities employer we welcome applicants from all sections of the community regardless of age, sex, gender (or gender identity), ethnicity, disability, sexual orientation and transgender status. All appointments are made on merit. Our University is positive about flexible working – you can find out more here Please be aware that due to the number of applications we unfortunately may not able to provide individual feedback on your application. Please note that we are unable to respond to enquiries, accept CVs or applications from Recruitment Agencies. Any recruitment enquiries from recruitment agencies should be directed to recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Any CV’s submitted by a recruitment agency will be considered a gift. Enquiries about the vacancy, shortlisting and interviews: Name: John Bramwell Email: John.Bramwell@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries: Email: recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical support: 0161 850 2004 https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home This vacancy will close for applications at midnight on the closing date. Please see the link below for the Further Particulars document which contains the person specification criteria. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46140": {
      "title": "Senior Technician (EEE (Teaching))",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Senior Technician (EEE (Teaching)) Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Senior Technician (EEE (Teaching)) Apply for job Job reference: SAE-032473 Salary: £33,002 to £36,636 per annum, depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Science and Engineering Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Permanent Division/Team: FSE Technical Services Hours Per Week: 35 hours per week Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 30/10/2026 Contract Duration: Permanent School/Directorate: Faculty Office - Science and Engineering Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description The Senior Teaching Technician will provide specialist technical support for practical teaching across Electrical and Electronic Engineering, working collaboratively with technical and academic colleagues to prepare, maintain and improve laboratories, equipment and learning activities. Through expert guidance, problem-solving and the promotion of safe and effective working practices, the role will help students build practical engineering skills and confidence. This is an opportunity to make a visible difference to the quality, reliability and inclusivity of the student learning experience while contributing to the continued development of high-quality teaching facilities. As Technical Staff, you are part of Professional Services. Together, all technical staff who work within the Faculty of Science and Engineering (FSE) form FSE Technical Operations. FSE Technical Operations is organised into five key functional areas: Research Teaching Infrastructure & Facilities Workshops Computing, Robotics & AI Within FSE Technical Operations, you will belong to the Teaching area. Teaching - The teaching technical team provides high-quality technical advice and support to students of all years of education. They also support academic staff in the diverse range of areas within the Faculty of Science and Engineering. The team are led by several Teaching Technical Specialists (TTS) who are responsible for managing and coordinating technical support for teaching in each of their specific disciplines. This includes all related teaching space, associated equipment and ensuring all Health and Safety obligations are met. The team support a host of taught units within each department. These can be based in computer clusters, bespoke labs or other shared areas. They also support student projects, student societies and field trips in the areas of Civil engineering, Aero and Earth Science. The team works closely with Timetabling, Discipline Heads of Education and laboratory tutors to advise on experiment design and the technical resource required for its delivery. Within the Teaching area, you will be part of the Department of Electronic and Electrical Engineering The EEE Teaching Technical Team is a collaborative team of six technicians who provide specialist technical support for undergraduate practical teaching, student projects, academic staff and research activities across Electrical and Electronic Engineering. Reporting to and working closely with the Teaching Technical Specialist, the team plays a key role in the delivery of high-quality teaching and research by preparing, maintaining and developing laboratory equipment, instrumentation, components and teaching facilities. The team supports students during practical activities and project work, provides technical guidance to academics and researchers, and contributes to the safe, effective and efficient operation of teaching and research environments. Collectively, the team brings expertise in electronics, printed circuit board design, construction and repair, microcontrollers and embedded systems, fault-finding, equipment maintenance and safe laboratory practice. Responsibilities include technical project support, diagnostic analysis, equipment management, production of bespoke project hardware, and the provision of specialist training and guidance to students and staff. The team also supports department specific activities including PCB manufacture and repair, embedded systems projects, technical student societies and engineering design competitions. The successful candidate will join an experienced and supportive team committed to sharing knowledge, continuously improving facilities and processes, and enhancing the quality, safety and reliability of the practical learning environment. Through supporting laboratory-based teaching, undergraduate design projects and research activities, the team helps students develop the practical engineering skills required for academic and professional success. As an equal opportunities employer we welcome applicants from all sections of the community regardless of age, sex, gender (or gender identity), ethnicity, disability, sexual orientation and transgender status. All appointments are made on merit. Our University is positive about flexible working – you can find out more here Please be aware that due to the number of applications we unfortunately may not able to provide individual feedback on your application. Please note that we are unable to respond to enquiries, accept CVs or applications from Recruitment Agencies. Any recruitment enquiries from recruitment agencies should be directed to recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Any CV’s submitted by a recruitment agency will be considered a gift. Enquiries about the vacancy, shortlisting and interviews: Name: Kerry Plant Email: kerry.plant@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries: Email: recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical support: 0161 850 2004 https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home This vacancy will close for applications at midnight on the closing date. Please see the link below for the Further Particulars document which contains the person specification criteria Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46084": {
      "title": "Research Associate in Materials Chemistry",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Research Associate in Materials Chemistry Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Research Associate in Materials Chemistry Apply for job Job reference: SAE-032427 Salary: UoM Grade 6 £37,694 - £46,049 per annum depending on experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Science and Engineering Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Department of Chemistry Hours Per Week: Full time (1FTE) Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 31/10/2026 Contract Duration: Fixed term for 15 months School/Directorate: School of Natural Sciences Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description We are seeking a motivated and collaborative individual to join our team as a Research Associate within the Department of Chemistry, School of Natural Sciences. This exciting research project aims to produce ultra-thin polymer-based membranes and fluidic chips with polymers of intrinsic porosity (PIMs), to study molecular and ionic permeation for ionic gating, ionic memory applications, and molecular separations. You will be expected to publish academic papers and contribute towards advanced new concepts. You will be responsible for: Conduct original research and collaborate within the group and internationally as per the project needs Develop research objectives and proposals for own or joint research, with the assistance of a mentor if required. Conduct individual and collaborative research projects. Write up research work for publication. Training students and others in skills appropriate to the project. We welcome candidates who bring diverse perspectives, experiences, and approaches to their work. About You We encourage applications from individuals with a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. You should demonstrate: Essential Criteria A PhD (or be about to obtain) in electrochemistry, polymer science or membranes with experimental work in chemistry, materials science or chemical engineering. A strong publication record in internationally peer-reviewed journals and an ability to write scientific reports and contribute to writing research papers. Experience of working with electrochemical measurements, electrical source meters, precision electrical meters, patch-clamp amplifiers, neuromorphic measurements. Significant experience of nanofabrication, measurement and characterization of ultrathin membranes, 2D materials van der Waals heterostructures. Excellent communication and interpersonal skills. Desirable Criteria Expertise in electrochemistry. Experience of working in nanofluidics/membrane technologies. Experience in clean room micro/nanofabrication and working with 2D materials devices. Experience of sequencing measurements with biomolecules. We value transferable skills and real-world experience as much as formal qualifications. Our benefits include: Generous employer contribution pension 29 days annual leave plus bank holidays, along with Christmas closure Ride to work and EV car scheme available For more information, please see the University of Manchester Benefits page. You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working arrangements. We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Dr Ashok Keerthi or Prof Radha Boya Email: ashok.keerthi@manchester.ac.uk or radha.boya@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support People.Recruitment@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46124": {
      "title": "Project Manager",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Project Manager Cookie Policy By clicking \"Allow Cookies\", you agree to the storing of cookies on your device to analyse site usage, and assist in our marketing efforts. Cookie Policy Decline Allow cookies Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Project Manager Apply for job Job reference: PSX-032459 Salary: £47,389 - £58,225 per annum depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Professional Services Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Permanent Division/Team: Projects Team Hours Per Week: 35 Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 22/10/2026 Contract Duration: Permanent School/Directorate: Directorate of Estates and Facilities Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description The Role: As a Project Manager within the Minor Works Team, you will lead the delivery of a varied portfolio of capital projects across the University estate, taking responsibility from initial planning and development through to successful completion. Acting as the University’s client representative, you will manage multiple projects and workstreams, coordinating consultants, contractors, stakeholders, priorities and risks to ensure delivery against agreed scope, programme, budget and quality requirements. You will work closely with colleagues and stakeholders across the University, providing effective project leadership, maintaining strong project controls and ensuring emerging risks and issues are managed or escalated appropriately through established governance arrangements. Your Responsibilities Will Include: Act as the University’s client representative, leading assigned capital projects from feasibility and planning through to successful completion and handover. Manage multiple projects and workstreams, coordinating priorities, dependencies, risks, programme and budget to achieve agreed outcomes. Maintain effective project governance and controls, including programmes, budgets, forecasts, risk registers, change control and performance reporting. Lead and coordinate consultants and contractors, supporting procurement and appointments and actively managing external team performance throughout delivery. Build effective relationships with project sponsors and stakeholders, providing clear advice on delivery options, risks, programme and budget and escalating key issues where required. Ensure projects are delivered safely, compliantly and sustainably, meeting relevant legislation, CDM and Building Regulations, University requirements and sustainability objectives. About You: Degree and/or relevant professional qualification in construction, engineering, surveying, architecture or project management. Experience managing capital, infrastructure, refurbishment or construction projects within a complex organisational environment. Experience managing multiple concurrent projects or workstreams, coordinating priorities, dependencies, risks and delivery activity. Strong understanding of project management and delivery, including procurement, contracts, budgets, forecasting, risk, governance and reporting. Strong stakeholder management, communication and influencing skills, with the ability to manage competing priorities and work effectively with internal and external stakeholders. What You’ll Get: 29 days annual leave, plus bank holidays and December closure days Generous pension scheme Flexible and hybrid working Access to a wide range of staff benefits, including discounts, wellbeing support, and development programmes Explore full benefits: www.manchester.ac.uk/connect/jobs/benefits-working-here For informal enquiries please contact the Talent Attraction Team talent.attraction@manchester.ac.uk Supporting Documents save_alt Project Manager Job Description (PDF, KB) Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46123": {
      "title": "Senior Project Manager",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Senior Project Manager Cookie Policy By clicking \"Allow Cookies\", you agree to the storing of cookies on your device to analyse site usage, and assist in our marketing efforts. Cookie Policy Decline Allow cookies Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Senior Project Manager Apply for job Job reference: PSX-032458 Salary: £59,966 - £71,566 per annum depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Professional Services Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Permanent Division/Team: Projects Team Hours Per Week: 35 Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 22/10/2026 Contract Duration: Permanent School/Directorate: Directorate of Estates and Facilities Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description The Role: As a senior member of the Estates and Facilities Major Projects team, you will lead the delivery of complex, high value and strategically important capital projects and programmes across the University estate. Working with a high degree of autonomy, you will take projects from planning through to completion, managing multiple priorities, risks, budgets and stakeholders to ensure successful delivery against time, cost, quality, safety and sustainability objectives. You will act as a trusted adviser to senior stakeholders, providing expert professional and commercial advice, managing consultants and contractors, and resolving complex delivery challenges. You will also play a key role in strengthening project and programme governance, controls and ways of working across Major Projects, helping ensure the University’s capital investment delivers long term value. Your Responsibilities Will Include: Provide autonomous senior client side leadership for complex, high value and strategically important capital projects and programmes, from initiation and brief development through delivery, handover and operational use. Take ownership of multiple concurrent projects and programmes, managing interdependencies, risks, resources, approvals, constraints and stakeholder impacts to achieve agreed scope, time, cost, quality and safety outcomes. Own robust financial and project controls, including significant budgets, cashflow forecasts, schedules, risk registers, change control and performance reporting, providing clear delivery confidence to senior stakeholders. Provide authoritative commercial and professional leadership, shaping procurement and contracting strategies and managing consultant and contractor appointments, performance, cost risk, change and claims to secure value for money. Lead senior stakeholder engagement across academic, professional services and operational areas, managing competing priorities and translating complex technical, commercial and programme information into clear recommendations and decisions. Provide professional leadership to multidisciplinary teams, consultants and contractors, setting performance standards, challenging underperformance and intervening where delivery is off track to agree and implement recovery actions. About You: Substantial experience of leading complex, high value and strategically important capital projects and programmes within a large, multi stakeholder organisation. Proven ability to manage standalone projects and programmes of multiple interdependent workstreams, including planning, dependencies, risks, governance, reporting and delivery to agreed outcomes. Demonstrable experience operating with a high degree of autonomy and professional judgement, advising senior stakeholders and governance forums on complex project, programme, commercial and delivery matters. Excellent knowledge of construction procurement, contract administration and commercial management, including NEC contracts and effective management of consultant and contractor performance. Highly developed stakeholder management, influencing, analytical and communication skills, with the ability to interpret complex information, manage competing priorities and provide clear recommendations in high risk or uncertain situations. What You Will Get: 29 days annual leave, plus bank holidays and December closure days Generous pension scheme Flexible and hybrid working Access to a wide range of staff benefits, including discounts, wellbeing support, and development programmes Explore full benefits: www.manchester.ac.uk/connect/jobs/benefits-working-here For informal enquiries please contact the Talent Attraction Team talent.attraction@manchester.ac.uk Supporting Documents save_alt Senior Project Manager Job Description (PDF, KB) Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46144": {
      "title": "Junior Sous Chef - Central Production Kitchen",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Junior Sous Chef - Central Production Kitchen Cookie Policy By clicking \"Allow Cookies\", you agree to the storing of cookies on your device to analyse site usage, and assist in our marketing efforts. Cookie Policy Decline Allow cookies Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Junior Sous Chef - Central Production Kitchen Apply for job Job reference: PSX-032479 Salary: £31,408 per annum Faculty/Organisational Unit: Professional Services Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Permanent Division/Team: Hospitality and Events Hours Per Week: 40 Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 21/10/2026 Contract Duration: Permanent School/Directorate: Directorate of Estates and Facilities Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description Overall Purpose Working under the direction of the Sous Chef, the postholder will support the preparation, production, packing and dispatch of consistently high-quality food for Hospitality services, events and outlets. The role combines skilled food production with the day-to-day coordination of colleagues on shift, translating production plans, recipes and service requirements into safe, efficient and timely kitchen activity while promoting high standards, teamwork and continuous improvement. The postholder will coordinate an allocated section or production activity, check product quality and consistency, and help ensure food is cooled, stored, labelled, packed and dispatched safely and on time. They will also support colleagues and deputise for the Sous Chef on agreed operational matters. About the Team The role is based within the Hospitality Central Production team at the Central Production Unit, Oxford Road, Manchester. The team produces food for catered conferences and events, lunches, dinners, banqueting, weddings, cafés and other food outlets across the University. The operation is focused on food safety, service excellence, teamwork, sustainability and the consistent delivery of high-quality food. Catering at the University encompasses a broad and diverse range of business areas – including retail outlets, catered halls, student bars, conferencing, events and hospitality. There are many benefits to working at the University: • 26 days holiday plus Bank Holidays • Generous pension scheme with generous employer contribution • A strong commitment to personal and career development, as well as wellbeing and mental health support • Annual salary review To find out more about staff benefits and working within catering at the University, visit our catering staff website: www.umc-limited.co.uk We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Eddie MaKuwaza – Executive Head Chef Email Address: eddie.makuwaza@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Supporting Documents save_alt PSX-032479 Junior Sous Chef - Central Production Kitchen FP's (PDF, KB) Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46143": {
      "title": "Research Associate in Indoor Air Quality",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Research Associate in Indoor Air Quality Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Research Associate in Indoor Air Quality Apply for job Job reference: SAE-032478 Salary: UoM Grade 6 £37,694 - £46,049 per annum depending on experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Science and Engineering Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Not specified Hours Per Week: Full time (1 FTE) Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 25/10/2026 Contract Duration: Fixed term for 42 months School/Directorate: School of Natural Sciences Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description We are seeking a motivated and collaborative individual to join our team as a Research Associate in Indoor Air Quality . This role offers an exciting opportunity to contribute to the Retrofit Advanced Materials & Manufacturing Innovation Centre (RAMMIC), a major collaborative research and innovation programme supported through the UKRI Local Innovation Partnership Fund. RAMMIC brings together Greater Manchester capabilities in building performance, advanced materials, indoor air quality, acoustics, environmental sustainability assessment, and innovation support to establish a collaborative innovation ecosystem for smart and sustainable retrofit products and processes. You will be responsible for: Develop and lead research examining indoor air quality within retrofit environments at the Energy House. Design experimental protocols for evaluating retrofit products, materials, ventilation systems and other interventions. Undertake measurements in Energy House facilities and other project locations. Deploy, operate and maintain appropriate sensor systems and research-grade atmospheric instrumentation. Work closely with the Research Associate in Sustainable Materials to coordinate experiments and integrate indoor air-quality and material-sustainability evidence. We welcome candidates who bring diverse perspectives, experiences, and approaches to their work. About You We encourage applications from individuals with a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. You should demonstrate: Essential Criteria Have, or be about to obtain, a PhD or equivalent qualification in atmospheric science, or a very closely related discipline. Specialist knowledge of atmospheric composition measurements and their application to indoor or outdoor air quality. Demonstrable experience of designing and undertaking atmospheric measurement studies. Experience of quantitative data processing, quality assurance, analysis and interpretation. Understanding of the physical and chemical processes influencing indoor air quality. Desirable Criteria Experience of indoor air-quality measurements in homes, test buildings or other occupied environments. Experience of online mass spectrometry, including Chemical Ionisation Mass Spectrometry, or other advanced gas- or particle-phase measurement techniques. Knowledge of emissions from building materials, consumer products or retrofit interventions. Experience of investigating ventilation, damp, mould related indoor air-quality problems. Experience of controlled intervention studies or building-performance experiments. We value transferable skills and real-world experience as much as formal qualifications. Our benefits include: Generous employer contribution pension 29 days annual leave plus bank holidays, along with Christmas closure Ride to work and EV car scheme available For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits. You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working arrangements. We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Thomas Bannan Email Address: thomas.bannan@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support Jobtrain Client Support Hub Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46054": {
      "title": "RESEARCH ASSOCIATE IN SOLID-STATE NMR SPECTROSCOPY",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for RESEARCH ASSOCIATE IN SOLID-STATE NMR SPECTROSCOPY Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results RESEARCH ASSOCIATE IN SOLID-STATE NMR SPECTROSCOPY Apply for job Job reference: SAE-032399 Salary: £37,694 - £46,049 per annum depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Science and Engineering Location: Manchester Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Department of Chemical Engineering Hours Per Week: Full time (1 FTE) Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 31/10/2026 Contract Duration: Fixed term for 36 months School/Directorate: School of Engineering Job Description * Interviews are expected to take place at the end of November or early December. * Please submit a covering letter and CV as a merged document (must be under 1MB). We are seeking a motivated and collaborative individual to join our team as a Research Associate in Solid-State NMR Spectroscopy . This Postdoctoral Research Associate (PDRA) position is part of a new £7m EPSRC funded Programme Grant that brings together researchers from the Universities of Durham, Leeds and Manchester investigating the formation of Molecular Solid Solutions (MoSS). The project will develop and apply cutting-edge techniques in crystal synthesis, characterisation and modelling to generate new understanding of MoSS, with applications across pharmaceuticals, agrochemicals and food industries. Based within the Department of Chemical Engineering, you will work closely with researchers across the consortium and have opportunities to collaborate, attend network events and undertake research placements. You will be responsible for: Conduct individual and collaborative research projects according to the overall needs of the research program. Develop research objectives and proposals for own or joint research. Write up research work for publication. Continually update knowledge and understanding in field or specialism. Translate knowledge of advances in the subject area into research activity. We welcome candidates who bring diverse perspectives, experiences, and approaches to their work. About You We encourage applications from individuals with a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. You should demonstrate: Essential Criteria: A PhD in the physical sciences or related subject. Experience in implementing a wide range of advanced solid-state NMR experiments. Experience with multinuclear solid-state NMR spectroscopy (e.g. 1H, 2H, 13C, 15N, 17O, 19F and other nuclei as appropriate). Good data analysis skills, including processing and simulation of NMR data. Ability to work both independently and collaboratively as a member of a multi-disciplinary team. Desirable Criteria: Practical experience running a DNP MAS NMR spectrometer and associated experiments. Practical experience with computational modelling (e.g. spin dynamics simulations, molecular dynamics, DFT). Experience correlating NMR data with structural information derived from diffraction or computational methods. Experience in the characterisation of molecular solids and/or crystalline organic materials. Experience with complementary characterisation methods such as PXRD, DSC, TGA, microscopy, Raman, IR spectroscopy, or solid-form analysis. Our benefits include: • Generous employer contribution pension • 29 days annual leave plus bank holidays, along with Christmas closure • Ride to work and EV car scheme available For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Dr Daniel Lee Email Address: daniel.lee@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support Jobtrain Client Support Hub Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=45779": {
      "title": "Teaching, Learning and Student Experience Coordinator (Programmes and Curriculum (ITET))",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Teaching, Learning and Student Experience Coordinator (Programmes and Curriculum (ITET)) Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Teaching, Learning and Student Experience Coordinator (Programmes and Curriculum (ITET)) Apply for job Job reference: HUM-032182 Salary: £33,002 - £36,636 per annum (depending on relevant experience) Faculty/Organisational Unit: Humanities Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Permanent Division/Team: SEED Administration Hours Per Week: 35 hours Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 22/10/2026 Contract Duration: Permanent School/Directorate: School of Environment, Education & Development Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description We are seeking a motivated and collaborative individual to join our team as a Teaching, Learning and Student Experience Coordinator (Teacher Education). This role offers an exciting opportunity to contribute and support all our placement activities for our students in our Initial Teacher Education (ITE) programmes. The role includes managing a small team, liaising with students, academic and professional services teams, partner schools and external bodies (including the Department for Education (DfE) and Ofsted) within a dynamic and inclusive environment. You will be responsible for: Providing line management for the Initial Teacher Education team. Coordinating the disbursal of student bursaries, travel awards, mentor and school payments and ensuring accurate records are maintained for both internal and external audits, and Ofsted inspections. Supporting the DfE census, ensuring all trainee records are included and bursary information is accurately recorded. Maintaining accurate records of students and placements are kept in line with university policies and procedures. We welcome candidates who bring diverse perspectives, experiences, and approaches to their work. About You We encourage applications from individuals with a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. You should demonstrate: Essential Criteria: Ability to supervise and motivate staff. Excellent communication skills; numeracy skills (demonstrating both accuracy and attention to detail); and organisational skills. Ability to solve problems creatively, with experience of contributing to the development and improvement of systems and procedures. A flexible team player with the ability to support colleagues. Desirable Criteria: Experience of working in an ITE or education-related administrative environment. Experience of working with external regulatory or compliance bodies. We value transferable skills and real-world experience as much as formal qualifications. Our benefits include: Generous employer pension contribution Christmas closure across all departments outside of annual leave and bank holidays Purple Place employee discount platform For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Catriona Fraser Email Address: catriona.fraser@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=45951": {
      "title": "R&amp;I Projects Team Administrator",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for R&amp;I Projects Team Administrator Cookie Policy By clicking \"Allow Cookies\", you agree to the storing of cookies on your device to analyse site usage, and assist in our marketing efforts. Cookie Policy Decline Allow cookies Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results R&I Projects Team Administrator Apply for job Job reference: BMH-032293 Salary: £29,588 - £32,080  per annum depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Biology, Medicine Health Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Permanent Division/Team: Faculty Office - BMH - Administration Hours Per Week: 35 Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 21/10/2026 Contract Duration: N/A School/Directorate: Faculty Office - Biology, Medicine and Health Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description Overall Purpose The Faculty and University of Manchester regard the development and implementation of an effective research strategy for Biology, Medicine and Health as central and crucial to its continuing success. This post is held within the Research & Innovation Projects Team. The team provides a professional service to support the development and implementation of the Faculty’s research and innovation strategy. It works closely with the academic community to deliver this strategy. About the Team The Research & Innovation Projects Team supports the Faculty by: • Coordinating FBMH internationalisation teaching and research partnerships and collaboration opportunities • Running the Faculty Fellowship Academy, helping researchers gain externally-funded fellowships, from doctoral studies to senior levels • Working closely alongside the Faculty Research Support Services team, which provides operational support for administrative and financial aspects of research grant management. • Supporting the formation and ongoing management of research networks by coordinating meetings, workshops and large events that help collaborations grow and prosper. This is in addition to the planning, coordination and management of projects and initiatives • Providing strategic and administrative support to the Vice Dean for Research and Innovation, Head of Research and Business Engagement and the Research Development and Innovation Manager Our benefits include: (Grades 1-5) Generous employer pension contribution Christmas closure across all departments outside of annual leave and bank holidays Purple Place employee discount platform For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . This role is not eligible for Skilled Worker visa sponsorship. Applicants must demonstrate the right to work in the UK. We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Annette Barber Email Address: annette.barber@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Supporting Documents save_alt BMH-032293 R&I Projects Team Administrator FP's (PDF, KB) Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46049": {
      "title": "Technical Specialist (High Voltage)",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Technical Specialist (High Voltage) Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Technical Specialist (High Voltage) Apply for job Job reference: SAE-032416 Salary: £37,694 - £46,049 per annum depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Science and Engineering Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Permanent Division/Team: FSE Technical Services Hours Per Week: 35 hours Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 21/10/2026 Contract Duration: Permanent School/Directorate: Faculty Office - Science and Engineering Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description The Technical Specialist (High Voltage) enables world-class research and teaching in high-voltage engineering. Through the provision of specialist technical expertise and support within the high-voltage Platform, the role helps create an environment where complex experimental activities can be undertaken effectively and with confidence. By supporting the development of new knowledge, technologies and research capabilities, the post holder contributes to the University's research excellence, student experience, innovation ambitions and wider societal impact. • Support the work of the high-voltage Platform within the Faculty of Science and Engineering. The primary focus of this role will be providing support for the development and delivery of our activities in high-voltage engineering. • Be expected to play a key role in ensuring our experimental facilities run efficiently. They will be expected to have a close and supportive working relationship with the platform lead, academic staff, and researchers de-livering research projects. • Will need to collaborate, cooperate and work effectively with other platforms utilising facilities and Plat-forms such as Mechanical Testing, Optical and Electronic, Advanced Manufacturing, Workshops and Surface via the PPMS booking system. (Grades 6-9) Our benefits include: • Generous employer contribution pension • 29 days annual leave plus bank holidays, along with Christmas closure • Ride to work and EV car scheme available For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Ramy Afia – Senior Technical Specialist Email Address: ramy.afia@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk T echnical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46062": {
      "title": "Research Associate",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Research Associate Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Research Associate Apply for job Job reference: BMH-032413 Salary: £37,694 - £41,064 per annum depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Biology, Medicine Health Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Division of Cardiovascular Sciences Hours Per Week: 35 Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 20/10/2026 Contract Duration: 36 Months School/Directorate: School of Medical Sciences Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description This is an outstanding opportunity for a Postdoctoral Research Associate with a background in cardiovascular biology, cell biology, molecular biology, mitochondrial biology or a related discipline to work within the Kitmitto Research Group under the supervision of Professor Ashraf Kitmitto. The post holder will be part of a multidisciplinary research team based within the Division of Cardiovascular Sciences at The University of Manchester. The successful candidate will support the aims of a British Heart Foundation-funded research project grant to advance our work showing that mitochondrial quality control pathways are impaired during the early stages of type 2 diabetes. This innovative project will define the molecular mechanisms leading to defects in mitochondrial structure-function and the development of diabetic heart failure, evaluate novel therapeutic strategies, and investigate potential biomarkers and molecular signatures for patient stratification. This position offers an ideal opportunity for an enthusiastic and highly motivated scientist to work within a collaborative and interdisciplinary environment, also working with Professors Elizabeth Carwright and Delvac Oceandy. The ideal candidate will have excellent experimental, organisational and communication skills, and will be capable of working both independently and as part of a multidisciplinary team. Experience of cardiomyocyte biology, in-vivo small animal model work and /or mammalian cell culture, molecular and cell biology, and mechanistic investigation of signalling pathways using genetic and/or pharmacological approaches is essential. Experience in the analysis of mitochondrial function, advanced imaging and therapeutic intervention studies would be advantageous. Successful candidates may be subject to pre-employment screening carried out on our behalf by a third party. The offer of employment will be dependent on the successful candidate passing that screening. Whilst you will be required to provide express consent at a later stage, by continuing with your application now you acknowledge that you are aware that such screening will take place, and agree to take part in the process. (Grades 6-9) Our benefits include: • Generous employer contribution pension • 29 days annual leave plus bank holidays, along with Christmas closure • Ride to work and EV car scheme available For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Ashraf Kitmitto Email Address: ashraf.kitmitto@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46063": {
      "title": "Research Associate on the Mechanics of Floating Structures",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Research Associate on the Mechanics of Floating Structures Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Research Associate on the Mechanics of Floating Structures Apply for job Job reference: SAE-032408 Salary: £37,694 - £46,049 per annum, depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Science and Engineering Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Department of Physics & Astronomy Hours Per Week: 1 FTE Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 27/10/2026 Contract Duration: 7 months School/Directorate: School of Natural Sciences Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description We are seeking a motivated and collaborative individual to join our team as a Post-doctoral Research Associate. This role offers an exciting opportunity to contribute to research on the mechanics of floating structures. The role will focus on building mathematical models of fluid-structure interactions, developing new experimental activities and analysing data, and will take place within a dynamic and inclusive research environment. You will be responsible for * Performing experiments on floating structures. * Acquiring & analysing experimental data and interpreting findings. * Using mathematical techniques to model fluid-structure interactions. * Writing up research work for publication. * Working with colleagues on joint projects We welcome candidates who bring diverse perspectives, experiences, and approaches to their work. About You We encourage applications from individuals with a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. You should demonstrate: Essential Criteria: * Hold or be about to obtain a PhD (or equivalent) in physics, engineering, chemistry or applied mathematics. * Strong problem-solving skills, and eagerness to learn new skills. * Ability to work collaboratively within a group and, also, to work independently. * Excellent written and oral communication skills. Desirable Criteria: * Demonstrable experience researching fluid-structure interactions. * Demonstrable experience in mechanical modelling. * Demonstrable experience performing experiments on soft and/or slender structures. * Analytical understanding of elastic buckling (in)stabilities. We value transferable skills and real-world experience as much as formal qualifications. Our benefits include: • Generous employer contribution pension • 29 days annual leave plus bank holidays, along with Christmas closure • Ride to work and EV car scheme available For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Finn Box Email Address: finn.box@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46142": {
      "title": "Research Associate in Sustainability Assessment in Retrofitssociate in Sustainability Assessment in Retrofit",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Research Associate in Sustainability Assessment in Retrofitssociate in Sustainability Assessment in Retrofit Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Research Associate in Sustainability Assessment in Retrofitssociate in Sustainability Assessment in Retrofit Apply for job Job reference: SAE-032477 Salary: £37,694 to £46,049 per annum, depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Science and Engineering Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Department of Materials Hours Per Week: Full Time Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 26/10/2026 Contract Duration: 42 months School/Directorate: School of Natural Sciences Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description This Research Associate position forms part of the Retrofit Advanced Materials & Manufacturing Innovation Centre (RAMMIC), a major collaborative research and innovation programme supported through the UKRI Local Innovation Partnership Fund. Led by the University of Salford’s Energy House Labs, RAMMIC brings together Greater Manchester capabilities in building performance, advanced materials, indoor air quality, acoustics, environmental sustainability assessment, and innovation support. Its purpose is to establish a collaborative innovation ecosystem for smart and sustainable retrofit products and processes. RAMMIC will work with businesses to overcome technical and commercial barriers to retrofit innovation. Research and innovation activities will make use of the Energy House Labs facilities, including existing and newly developed test houses in controlled environment warehouses. The programme will evaluate new products and services under controlled conditions, generate reliable evidence of their performance and environmental effects, quantify environmental impacts across marketable comparators, and support promising innovations towards commercialisation and wider deployment. The programme will address challenges associated with the building envelope, heating and cooling systems, ventilation, material selection, commissioning, indoor air quality and the assessment of retrofit performance. What you will get in return: Fantastic market leading Pension scheme Excellent employee health and wellbeing services including an Employee Assistance Programme Exceptional starting annual leave entitlement, plus bank holidays Additional paid closure over the Christmas period Local and national discounts at a range of major retailers As an equal opportunities employer we welcome applicants from all sections of the community regardless of age, sex, gender (or gender identity), ethnicity, disability, sexual orientation and transgender status.  All appointments are made on merit. Our University is positive about flexible working – you can find out more here Hybrid working arrangements may be considered. Please be aware that due to the number of applications we unfortunately may not able to provide individual feedback on your application. Please note that we are unable to respond to enquiries, accept CVs or applications from Recruitment Agencies. Any recruitment enquiries from recruitment agencies should be directed to recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Any CV’s submitted by a recruitment agency will be considered a gift. Enquiries about the vacancy, shortlisting and interviews: Name: Michael Shaver Email: michael.shaver@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries: Email: recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical support: 0161 850 2004 https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home This vacancy will close for applications at midnight on the closing date. Please see the link below for the Further Particulars document which contains the person specification criteria. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=45911": {
      "title": "Head of School Finance (Snr Business Partner)",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Head of School Finance (Snr Business Partner) Cookie Policy By clicking \"Allow Cookies\", you agree to the storing of cookies on your device to analyse site usage, and assist in our marketing efforts. Cookie Policy Decline Allow cookies Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Head of School Finance (Snr Business Partner) Apply for job Job reference: PSX-032259 Salary: £47,389 - £58,225 per annum depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Professional Services Location: Manchester Employment type: Permanent Division/Team: Financial Analysis and Reporting Hours Per Week: 35 Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 16/10/2026 Contract Duration: N/A School/Directorate: Directorate of Finance Job Description Overall Purpose of the Job The Head of School/Directorate Finance is a member of the Directorate of Finance working under the guidance of the Deputy Director of Faculty Finance. You will work alongside Heads of School/Directors/Senior Managers as a business partner to provide operational and strategic support on all financial matters. You will be responsible for managing the financial planning, budgeting, forecasting and reporting processes for your area of responsibility. This will include analysis of performance and highlighting remedial action where required. You will develop and continuously improve management information to assist School/Directorate budget holders. You will be customer focused, flexible in your approach, and work in conjunction with other finance colleagues, covering for and relating with them in order to develop and enhance your role and the service delivered by the Directorate of Finance. (Grades 6-9) Our benefits include: • Generous employer contribution pension • 29 days annual leave plus bank holidays, along with Christmas closure • Ride to work and EV car scheme available For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Vicky Skinner Email Address: victoria.skinner@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Supporting Documents save_alt PSX-032259 HEAD OF SCHOOL FINANCE (SNR BUSINESS PARTNER) FP'S (PDF, KB) Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=45981": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate in Polymeric Materials Modelling",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Postdoctoral Research Associate in Polymeric Materials Modelling Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Postdoctoral Research Associate in Polymeric Materials Modelling Apply for job Job reference: SAE-032320 Salary: £37,694 - £46,049 per annum depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Science and Engineering Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Department of Materials Hours Per Week: Full Time (1 FTE) Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 26/10/2026 Contract Duration: 24 Months School/Directorate: School of Natural Sciences Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description A senior postdoctoral position is available for an ambitious individual to carry out the molecular modelling of cellulose oligomers investigating the self-assembly of the polymer in the plant cell wall. The senior postdoctoral assistant will also assist another postdoctoral researcher in the day-to-day supervisorial activities for Eichhorn’s wider group of diverse PhD and MSc students, including project supervision, health and safety, and assistance with the drafting of manuscripts. The successful applicant will undertake a fundamental study of the assembly of cellulose, using a unique approach of cellulose oligomers that have surfactant-like properties. There have many recent attempts to synthesize cellulose analogues in the laboratory, through self-assembly methods using enzymatic produced oligomeric forms. This work will pave the way for the practical assembly of cellulose oligomeric forms by screening large arrays of structures with different moieties and charges. To effectively do this, the postdoctoral researcher will combine machine learning, molecular dynamics simulations and high performance computing (Isambard AI). Applicants must have a PhD in an appropriate area of computational chemistry or a related discipline and experience in the molecular modelling of polymers, polysaccharides, and preferably cellulose. Informal enquiries are welcome and should be made to Steve Eichhorn (Email: s.j.eichhorn@bristol.ac.uk). You will be responsible for: The conducting of high-quality and publishable research in the area of the molecular modelling of cellulose and related materials e.g. other plant cell wall polymers. This will involve the implementation, execution and analysis of advanced molecular simulations, the use of machine learning techniques and deployment on state-of-the-art high performance computing platforms. Through interaction with the PI and other members of the team, contributing proactively to the development, progression and execution of the project Analysing and interpreting data and make a significant input to the scientific direction of the project Preparation and presentation of regular reports on research progress We welcome candidates who bring diverse perspectives, experiences, and approaches to their work About You We encourage applications from individuals with a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. You should demonstrate: Essential Criteria: Ph.D. in computational chemistry, computational materials science or a relevant discipline Proven research skills Scientific scripting / programming Experience in molecular dynamics simulations of polymers Desirable Criteria: Molecular modelling and simulation experience during PhD Ability to present complex information effectively to a range of audiences Experience of manipulating and visualizing large datasets We value transferable skills and real-world experience as much as formal qualifications. Our benefits include: • Generous employer contribution pension • 29 days annual leave plus bank holidays, along with Christmas closure • Ride to work and EV car scheme available For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Professor Steve Eichhorn Email Address: s.j.eichhorn@bristol.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home Applications close at midnight on the closing date Further particulars (with person specification) linked below Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46091": {
      "title": "Student Finance Administrator – University Funded Schemes",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Student Finance Administrator – University Funded Schemes Cookie Policy By clicking \"Allow Cookies\", you agree to the storing of cookies on your device to analyse site usage, and assist in our marketing efforts. Cookie Policy Decline Allow cookies Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Student Finance Administrator – University Funded Schemes Apply for job Job reference: PSX-032435 Salary: £29,588 to £32,080 per annum, depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Professional Services Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Permanent Division/Team: Student and Academic Services Hours Per Week: 35 hours per week Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 22/10/2026 Contract Duration: Permanent School/Directorate: Directorate for the Student Experience Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description Overall Purpose of the Job: Be responsible for the administration of activities for the assigned area; supervise and co-ordinate the work of the team, acting as a point of reference. Provide information, advice and guidance to stakeholders. Work flexibly as required across the University, supporting delivery of University and local priorities as a single cross-University team. Maintain own data management, the student record and data quality for all related areas of delivery. Maintain and update system records with reference to current data protection legislation and provide reports. Assist with the planning, organisation and delivery of events; organise and service meetings. Act in accordance with and promote University policies and requirements at all times. Deliver excellent customer service and play an active role in communities of practice. Assist with the delivery and monitoring of projects and activities. Be part of the collaborative student finance team taking ownership of student finance enquiries within specified service level agreements. Process Hardship Fund applications and other University funding applications in a timely manner and in line with guidance and allocated budget. Undertake administrative and financial processes for schemes requiring evaluation by a panel of staff; undertake administration of loans in respect of US students, having detailed knowledge of regulations governing the US Loan Program and private US loans. Have knowledge of the administration of Veteran's Affairs (VA) benefits in respect of US military veterans and/or their children. Generate requests for refund, investigate and resolve as appropriate; communicate with current and prospective students regarding their loans; prepare and submit online bi-monthly enrolment reports to National Student Loan Database System (NSLDS). Please note, under the new immigration rules this role is not eligible for sponsorship under the Skilled Worker route of the Points Based System. Candidates will need to be able to demonstrate their right to work in the UK in order to be eligible to take up the post. What you will get in return: Fantastic market leading Pension scheme Excellent employee health and wellbeing services including an Employee Assistance Programme Exceptional starting annual leave entitlement, plus bank holidays Additional paid closure over the Christmas period Local and national discounts at a range of major retailers As an equal opportunities employer we welcome applicants from all sections of the community regardless of age, sex, gender (or gender identity), ethnicity, disability, sexual orientation and transgender status. All appointments are made on merit. Our University is positive about flexible working – you can find out more here Hybrid working arrangements may be considered. Please be aware that due to the number of applications we unfortunately may not able to provide individual feedback on your application. Please note that we are unable to respond to enquiries, accept CVs or applications from Recruitment Agencies. Any recruitment enquiries from recruitment agencies should be directed to People.Recruitment@manchester.ac.uk . Any CV’s submitted by a recruitment agency will be considered a gift. Enquiries about the vacancy, shortlisting and interviews: Name: Taibah Khan Email: taibah.khan@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries: Email: People.recruitment@manchester.ac.uk Technical support: Jobtrain: 0161 850 2004 https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home This vacancy will close for applications at midnight on the closing date. Please see the link below for the Further Particulars document which contains the person specification criteria. Supporting Documents save_alt PSX-032435 - Student Finance Administrator FPs (PDF, KB) Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=45982": {
      "title": "KTP Associate in Development of Low-carbon Bricks",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for KTP Associate in Development of Low-carbon Bricks Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results KTP Associate in Development of Low-carbon Bricks Apply for job Job reference: SAE-032321 Salary: £37,694 to £46,049 per annum, depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Science and Engineering Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Department of Civil Engineering and Management Hours Per Week: Full Time Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 21/10/2026 Contract Duration: 34 months School/Directorate: School of Engineering Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description This is an exciting opportunity to play a leading role in delivering a high-impact 34-month Knowledge Transfer Partnership (KTP) project with the University of Manchester (UoM) and Michelmersh Brick UK Ltd., focused on decarbonising clay brick manufacturing. The project aims to develop and validate a sustainable bio-based alternative to fossil derived body fuel for use in premium clamp fired products, supporting the transition to lower carbon manufacturing. The KTP Associate will coordinate activity across manufacturing operations, technical teams, academic researchers and external partners, translating research into practical solutions that can be implemented at scale within a live production environment. The role will involve planning and leading trials, ensuring product quality, process stability and supply resilience are maintained throughout. Part funded through Innovate UK’s KTP programme, this role offers a unique opportunity to deliver meaningful industrial and environmental impact while developing technical leadership, project management and innovation delivery experience. The Associate will be based primarily at Michelmersh’s Freshfield Lane site near Haywards Heath, Sussex, with periods spent at UoM to support collaboration and access specialist research facilities. (The Associate’s time in the lab in Manchester is likely to be split across 3 trips of 2-3 months duration each.) Provisional interview dates: 3rd and 6th of November Due to the nature of the funding, KTP Associates who have already completed a KTP are not eligible to apply. What you will get in return: Fantastic market leading Pension scheme Excellent employee health and wellbeing services including an Employee Assistance Programme Exceptional starting annual leave entitlement, plus bank holidays Additional paid closure over the Christmas period Local and national discounts at a range of major retailers As an equal opportunities employer we welcome applicants from all sections of the community regardless of age, sex, gender (or gender identity), ethnicity, disability, sexual orientation and transgender status. All appointments are made on merit. Our University is positive about flexible working – you can find out more here Hybrid working arrangements may be considered. Please be aware that due to the number of applications we unfortunately may not able to provide individual feedback on your application. Please note that we are unable to respond to enquiries, accept CVs or applications from Recruitment Agencies. Any recruitment enquiries from recruitment agencies should be directed to recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Any CV’s submitted by a recruitment agency will be considered a gift. Enquiries about the vacancy, shortlisting and interviews: Name: Meini Su Email: meini.su@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries: Email: recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical support: 0161 850 2004 https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home This vacancy will close for applications at midnight on the closing date. Please see the link below for the Further Particulars document which contains the person specification criteria. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46012": {
      "title": "Research Associate in Mathematical Biomedicine",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Research Associate in Mathematical Biomedicine Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Research Associate in Mathematical Biomedicine Apply for job Job reference: BMH-032414 Salary: £37,694 to £46,049 per annum, depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Biology, Medicine Health Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Division of Integrative Physiology Hours Per Week: Full Time Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 23/10/2026 Contract Duration: 33 months School/Directorate: School of Medical Sciences Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description We are excited to offer this opportunity to a highly motivated Research Associate to join an ambitious new team working in the field of Digital Endocrinology. The successful applicant will conduct original research aimed at translating understanding of endocrine function into precision medicine applications. The research will use data from wearable device technologies to characterise variability in circadian hormone profiles in humans, uncovering fundamental insights about endocrine rhythms that can be used to support the diagnosis and management of endocrine conditions. The position is funded through a UKRI Future Leaders Fellowship awarded to Dr Eder Zavala: “Developing a mathematical and computational framework for digital endocrinology”. The work will build on hormonal time series data collected at unprecedented time resolution in healthy humans and in patients, including studies in real life settings. We are seeking to appoint a creative and enthusiastic individual who will lead on computational data analysis projects that interrogate the mechanisms behind hormonal rhythm misalignment. The research will be closely integrated with mathematical models that predict dynamic responses to perturbations (e.g., stressors, sleep disruptions, diagnostic tests), as well as the long-term changes that occur during disease. A key challenge of your work will be accounting for the variability in hormonal rhythms observed across individuals. To characterise this variability, you will take advantage of unique in its kind datasets and advanced mathematical and computational methods and help develop personalised models. The practical element of your project will be based on, but not limited to, time series analysis, network analysis, Bayesian inference, Machine Learning, as well as computational simulation of mathematical models written in terms of differential equations. In addition to leading their own research projects, the appointed candidate will have the opportunity to contribute to the projects of PhD students in the group, as well as with an international team of collaborators including clinicians, computer scientists, and mathematicians. The team is dynamic and ambitious as well as being welcoming and supportive. The applicant should have high proficiency with scientific computer programming in Python, Julia, R and/or MATLAB, parameter estimation methods and HPC simulations. Experience with dynamical systems theory and mathematical modelling using ODEs and/or DDEs is desirable. The vacancy is for a full-time post available from 1st February 2027 until 31st December 2029. What you will get in return: Fantastic market leading Pension scheme Excellent employee health and wellbeing services including an Employee Assistance Programme Exceptional starting annual leave entitlement, plus bank holidays Additional paid closure over the Christmas period Local and national discounts at a range of major retailers As an equal opportunities employer we welcome applicants from all sections of the community regardless of age, sex, gender (or gender identity), ethnicity, disability, sexual orientation and transgender status. All appointments are made on merit. Our University is positive about flexible working – you can find out more here Hybrid working arrangements may be considered. Please be aware that due to the number of applications we unfortunately may not able to provide individual feedback on your application. Please note that we are unable to respond to enquiries, accept CVs or applications from Recruitment Agencies. Any recruitment enquiries from recruitment agencies should be directed to recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Any CV’s submitted by a recruitment agency will be considered a gift. Enquiries about the vacancy, shortlisting and interviews: Name: Dr Eder Zavala (UKRI Future Leaders Fellow; Senior Lecturer in Mathematical Biomedicine) Email: eder.zavala@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries: Email: recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical support: 0161 850 2004 https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home This vacancy will close for applications at midnight on the closing date. Please see the link below for the Further Particulars document which contains the person specification criteria. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=45459": {
      "title": "Integrated Designer",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Integrated Designer Cookie Policy By clicking \"Allow Cookies\", you agree to the storing of cookies on your device to analyse site usage, and assist in our marketing efforts. Cookie Policy Decline Allow cookies Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Integrated Designer Apply for job Job reference: CIX-032351 Salary: UoM Grade 5 £33,002 - £36,636 pro rata depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Cultural Institutions Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Permanent Division/Team: Whitworth Art Gallery Administration Hours Per Week: 21 hours per week (0.6 FTE) Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 22/10/2026 Contract Duration: Permanent School/Directorate: Whitworth Art Gallery Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description *CV, Cover letter & portfolio of own work required. When submitting your application, please upload as one document under 1MB. .We are seeking a motivated and collaborative individual to join our team as a Designer at The Whitworth . This role offers an exciting opportunity to create high-quality visual design for the Whitworth’s exhibitions, programmes and communications across print and digital platforms. The role will support the delivery of marketing campaigns, digital content and audience engagement activity by producing compelling visual assets that reflect the Whitworth’s identity and align with both our and The University of Manchester’s brand guidelines. You will be responsible for: Produce creative design solutions for exhibition campaigns, digital content, social media, print collateral and promotional materials. Prepare artwork for print and digital production, ensuring files meet technical specifications and brand standards. Design visual assets that support storytelling and audience engagement across multiple platforms. Support the delivery of exhibition and programme campaigns by producing design assets for web, email, social media and print. Work with colleagues across curatorial, programming and engagement teams to interpret briefs and deliver effective design solutions. We welcome candidates who bring diverse perspectives, experiences, and approaches to their work. About You We encourage applications from individuals with a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. You should demonstrate: Essential Criteria: Demonstrate strong design skills with experience producing visual content across both print and digital formats. Have experience using Adobe Creative Suite (particularly InDesign, Illustrator and Photoshop). Demonstrate strong typography, layout and visual communication skills. Be able to interpret design briefs and translate them into effective visual solutions. Show a commitment to equality, diversity and inclusion and to representing diverse audiences through visual communications. Desirable Criteria: Experience working in a cultural, arts, museum or higher education environment. Experience producing design for digital and social media platforms. Experience creating simple motion graphics or video assets. Familiarity with asset management systems or digital publishing platforms. Experience working with external suppliers such as printers or photographers. We value transferable skills and real-world experience as much as formal qualifications. Our benefits include: Generous employer pension contribution Christmas closure across all departments outside of annual leave and bank holidays Purple Place employee discount platform For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits. You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working. We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy. Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Rakhi Sinha Jones Email Address: rakhi.sinhajones@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support Jobtrain Client Support Hub This role is not eligible for Skilled Worker visa sponsorship. Applicants must demonstrate the right to work in the UK. Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Supporting Documents save_alt CIX-032351 JD (PDF, KB) Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=45843": {
      "title": "Teaching, Learning and Student Experience Assistant (Programmes & Curriculum)",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Teaching, Learning and Student Experience Assistant (Programmes &amp; Curriculum) Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Teaching, Learning and Student Experience Assistant (Programmes & Curriculum) Apply for job Job reference: BMH-032190 Salary: £27,319 - £28,778 per annum depending on experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Biology, Medicine Health Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Permanent Division/Team: School of Biological Sciences Admin Hours Per Week: 35 Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 12/10/2026 Contract Duration: N/A - Permanent School/Directorate: School of Biological Sciences Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description Overall Purpose Providing clerical and administrative support for teaching, learning and student administration within the Programmes and Curriculum team. Working accurately, flexibly and collaboratively, the role supports effective services, reliable student records and clear communication. The post contributes to an excellent student experience and to continuous improvement across Professional Services. About the Team The Programmes and Curriculum team sits within the School of Biological Sciences in the Faculty of Biology, Medicine and Health. The team supports teaching, learning and student administration for students and academic colleagues. The role reports to a Teaching and Learning Coordinator and works collaboratively across School and University boundaries to deliver local and University priorities as part of a single Professional Services team. Key Accountabilities • Providing administrative support for teaching, learning and student administration, as directed by the Teaching, Learning and Student Experience Coordinator. • Responding as a first point of contact for routine enquiries, providing clear information and guidance, and escalating matters when appropriate. • Maintaining accurate student records, managing data responsibly and supporting high standards of data quality. • Preparing correspondence and maintaining accurate records and documentation for local processes. • Supporting financial administration, including placing orders for goods and services in line with University procedures. • Organising meetings, room bookings and catering, preparing and circulating papers, and taking minutes where required. • Supporting the delivery of projects, team activities, policies and procedures, with a focus on continuous improvement. • Producing appropriate management information, data and analysis to support the work of the team. • Delivering excellent and inclusive customer service to students, colleagues and other stakeholders. • Working flexibly and collaboratively across teams, contributing to communities of practice and helping to create a joined-up student experience. • Following and promoting University requirements relating to health and safety, equality, diversity and inclusion, information governance and financial procedures. Our benefits include: (Grades 1-5) Generous employer pension contribution Christmas closure across all departments outside of annual leave and bank holidays Purple Place employee discount platform For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Charlotte Jenkins Email Address: charlotte.jenkins@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home This role is not eligible for Skilled Worker visa sponsorship. Applicants must demonstrate the right to work in the UK. Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46036": {
      "title": "Senior Technician (Civil and Mechanical Engineering)",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Senior Technician (Civil and Mechanical Engineering) Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Senior Technician (Civil and Mechanical Engineering) Apply for job Job reference: SAE-032381 Salary: £33,002 - £36,636 per annum, depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Science and Engineering Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Permanent Division/Team: FSE Technical Services Hours Per Week: 35 hours per week (1 FTE) Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 27/10/2026 Contract Duration: Permanent School/Directorate: Faculty Office - Science and Engineering Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description We are seeking a motivated and collaborative individual to join our team as a Senior Technician (Civil and Mechanical Engineering). This role offers an exciting opportunity to provide expert technical support across our Fluids, Heavy Structures, Dynamics, Impact/Ballistics, Geotechnical and Concrete Processing laboratories. Using your technical knowledge and problem-solving skills, you will support safe, efficient and high-quality teaching, research and testing activities within a dynamic and inclusive environment. You will be responsible for: To work closely with undergraduate/postgraduate students to support them in conducting experimental work in the areas of heavy structures, dynamics, fluids and testing equipment. This will include supporting them in setting up test rigs and measurement chains, conducting experiments and validating test results. Demonstrating and supporting delivery of practical classes as part of taught programs. Including but not limited to Mechanical and Aerospace Engineering and Civil Engineering and Management 4th Year and PGT Mechanical Engineering Group Design Projects, IMechE grand challenges and Design, Manufacture and Test modules. To train or instruct other staff and students in the use of equipment and it’s servicing as required and within the limits of technical competence. Working collaboratively with colleagues and stakeholders, help resolve technical challenges, maintain and develop facilities and equipment, and identify opportunities to improve our services. Supervise, motivate and develop junior staff and University of Manchester technical apprentices. We welcome candidates who bring diverse perspectives, experiences, and approaches to their work. About You We encourage applications from individuals with a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. You should demonstrate: Essential Criteria A qualification in a science or engineering-related subject area e.g. degree, HND, HNC, OR significant work experience in a relevant technical, engineering or scientific role Experience in fabrication and testing methods used in the application of Civil and Mechanical Engineering. Demonstrable experience in engineering elements like functionality, replicability, and costs in relation to the design and how they are applied in the completion of engineering projects Extensive knowledge in the use of workshop machine tools necessary, for fabrication and sample preparation, including: lathes, CNC milling, grinding, cutting, grinding, polishing and drilling machines An aptitude for training and mentoring students from a diverse range of backgrounds Desirable Criteria Demonstrable experience in the operation of furnace control systems, data loggers, and the application of fire engineering practice Experience of strain gauging and mechanical property testing of concrete, composite and metallic systems Experience of working with specialist IT packages/software such as LabCup, PPMS and CAD/CAM for example, SolidWorks Knowledge of teaching and research operations gained from within a Higher Education setting Line management / supervisory experience We value transferable skills and real-world experience as much as formal qualifications Our benefits include: Generous employer pension contribution Christmas closure across all departments outside of annual leave and bank holidays Purple Place employee discount platform For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Jacob Skelly Email Address: jacob.skelly@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home This role is not eligible for Skilled Worker visa sponsorship. Applicants must demonstrate the right to work in the UK Applications close at midnight on the closing date Further particulars (with person specification) linked below Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=45984": {
      "title": "PGR Operations Officer (Recruitment &amp; Admissions)",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for PGR Operations Officer (Recruitment &amp; Admissions) Cookie Policy By clicking \"Allow Cookies\", you agree to the storing of cookies on your device to analyse site usage, and assist in our marketing efforts. Cookie Policy Decline Allow cookies Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results PGR Operations Officer (Recruitment & Admissions) Apply for job Job reference: BMH-032324 Salary: £37,694 to £46,049 per annum, depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Biology, Medicine Health Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Permanent Division/Team: FBMH Research and Business Engagement Support Services Hours Per Week: Full Time Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 16/10/2026 Contract Duration: Permanent School/Directorate: Faculty Office - Biology, Medicine and Health Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description Interviews will be held on Monday 2 November. This role leads the delivery of postgraduate research (PGR) recruitment and admissions activity within the Doctoral Academy. The postholder contributes to the development of recruitment strategy and works closely with the PGR Services Manager and with the Marketing, Planning and Finance teams to support delivery against student number targets. The postholder manages the Doctoral Academy’s Recruitment and Admissions Team, providing operational oversight of policy, processes, reporting, and compliance. They are responsible for ensuring the effective management of all admissions and selection processes. Working collaboratively with senior stakeholders, the postholder oversees the allocation of studentship funding, liaising with recruiting supervisors, industry partners, and colleagues in Finances. The postholder also plays a key role in enhancing the applicant experience, ensuring a high-quality, consistent approach to recruitment and admissions delivery. More broadly, they contribute to a culture of excellence and continuous improvement across Professional Services, supporting a flexible and collaborative approach that places the postgraduate researcher experience at the centre of all activity. Colleagues will be expected to work flexibly across the University, taking on assignments and roles as required, underpinned by the principles of business partnering and collaborative working. This approach supports the delivery of both University-wide and local priorities, with colleagues operating as part of a single, cross-University team. Assignments may include work across: PGR progression and support; PGR programmes; PGR funding and programmes. What you will get in return: Fantastic market leading Pension scheme Excellent employee health and wellbeing services including an Employee Assistance Programme Exceptional starting annual leave entitlement, plus bank holidays Additional paid closure over the Christmas period Local and national discounts at a range of major retailers As an equal opportunities employer we welcome applicants from all sections of the community regardless of age, sex, gender (or gender identity), ethnicity, disability, sexual orientation and transgender status. All appointments are made on merit. Our University is positive about flexible working – you can find out more here Hybrid working arrangements may be considered. Please be aware that due to the number of applications we unfortunately may not able to provide individual feedback on your application. Please note that we are unable to respond to enquiries, accept CVs or applications from Recruitment Agencies. Any recruitment enquiries from recruitment agencies should be directed to recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Any CV’s submitted by a recruitment agency will be considered a gift. Enquiries about the vacancy, shortlisting and interviews: Name: Tasleem Hanif Email: tasleem.hanif@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries: Email: recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical support: 0161 850 2004 https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home This vacancy will close for applications at midnight on the closing date. Please see the link below for the Further Particulars document which contains the person specification criteria. Supporting Documents save_alt BMH-032324 - PGR Operations Officer (Recruitment & Admissions) FPs (PDF, KB) Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=45926": {
      "title": "Accreditation &amp; Rankings Manager",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Accreditation &amp; Rankings Manager Cookie Policy By clicking \"Allow Cookies\", you agree to the storing of cookies on your device to analyse site usage, and assist in our marketing efforts. Cookie Policy Decline Allow cookies Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Accreditation & Rankings Manager Apply for job Job reference: HUM-032280 Salary: £47,389 - £58,225 per annum depending on experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Humanities Location: Manchester Employment type: Permanent Division/Team: Alliance MBS Administration Hours Per Week: Full Time (1 FTE) Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 20/10/2026 Contract Duration: Permanent School/Directorate: Alliance Manchester Business School Job Description We are seeking a motivated and collaborative individual to join our team as an Accreditation & Rankings Manager. The Accreditation & Rankings Manager has a key strategic and operational role within Alliance Manchester Business School. They support the Head of School, Director of School Operations and Partnership Director in the development and implementation of the School’s accreditation strategy and reaccreditation cycle as well as the School’s strategic approach to external rankings along with their operational delivery. You will be responsible for: Reporting to the Partnership Director and supporting the Head of School and the Director of School Operations in the development, implementation, execution and monitoring of the School’s strategy, policies, procedures and priorities relating to external accreditation standards, ensuring a key aim of continuous improvement is embedded across the School To take overall responsibility for the consistent delivery of the reaccreditation cycle, including writing five yearly submissions, external mid-term progress reports and annual progress reports for each Standard across the School’s activities To manage and coordinate all peer review visits, including all preparations, correspondence, continuous data requirements and delivery of post accreditation actions Lead on the annual Business School Rankings cycle, act as secretary to the Strategic Rankings Committee and work with the Partnership Director and other key stakeholders providing analysis and insight to support strategic decision making in relation to rankings Lead on operational delivery of the rankings strategy, leading cross functional groups to coordinate our rankings submissions and communications We welcome candidates who bring diverse perspectives, experiences, and approaches to their work. About You We encourage applications from individuals with a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. You should demonstrate: Essential Criteria: A degree or equivalent professional experience in a relevant field Knowledge of business school accreditations and rankings Development of large, complex reports, presenting at senior levels, managing a range of stakeholders and leading and motivating teams without line authority Strong relationship management, stakeholder engagement, communication, facilitation and influencing skills Exceptional writing skills, critical thinking and the ability to understand data Desirable Criteria: Wider knowledge of the business school landscape both in the UK and internationally Project management, setting and achieving agreed deadlines Evidence of delivering continuous improvement Digitally literate, comfortable with a range of IT tools We value transferable skills and real-world experience as much as formal qualifications. Our benefits include: • Generous employer contribution pension • 29 days annual leave plus bank holidays, along with Christmas closure • Ride to work and EV car scheme available For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Chris Garnett Email Address: chris.garnett@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home This role is not eligible for Skilled Worker visa sponsorship. Applicants must demonstrate the right to work in the UK. Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Supporting Documents save_alt HUM-032280 - Accreditation & Rankings Manager - FPs (PDF, KB) Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=45861": {
      "title": "Collections Engagement Officer",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Collections Engagement Officer Cookie Policy By clicking \"Allow Cookies\", you agree to the storing of cookies on your device to analyse site usage, and assist in our marketing efforts. Cookie Policy Decline Allow cookies Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Collections Engagement Officer Apply for job Job reference: PSX-032209 Salary: UoM Grade 5 £33,002 - £36,636 pro rata depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Professional Services Location: Ahmed Iqbal Ullah RACE Centre The University of Manchester, Manchester Central Library, 3rd Floor, St Peter’s Square, Manchester Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Special Collections Hours Per Week: 28 hours per week (0.8 FTE) Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 01/11/2026 Contract Duration: Fixed term from 1 February 2027 (or earlier if available), until Monday 8 November 2027 School/Directorate: The University of Manchester Library Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description We are seeking a motivated and collaborative individual to join the team as a Collections Engagement Officer on a fixed-term contract providing maternity cover. Based within the Ahmed Iqbal Ullah RACE Centre , you will work to increase knowledge, value and use of the Centre’s rich library and archive collections through inclusive and creative engagement activities. While the role has a natural focus on academic audiences, you will work with schools and young people, community organisations and other cultural and heritage sector organisations, and the general public. The role requires someone who can embed racial and social justice principles in creative collections-based engagement work. Contract details This is a 0.8 FTE role (28 hours per week). The contract will start on 1 February 2027 (though an earlier start is possible, dependent on the selected candidate's availability) and will end on Monday 8 November 2027. Interviews will take place on Monday 23 November and Friday 27 November 2026 (afternoons). Interview questions will be provided in advance. You will be responsible for: - Increasing knowledge, value and use of the Ahmed Iqbal Ullah RACE Centre's library and archive collections through inclusive and creative engagement activities. - Working with academic audiences, schools and young people, community organisations, cultural and heritage sector organisations, and the general public. - Embedding racial and social justice principles in creative collections-based engagement work. - Developing and maintaining meaningful relationships and partnerships that support engagement with the Centre’s collections. - Supporting the Centre’s mission to make Global Majority history, heritage and culture more accessible through community partnerships and inclusive practice. We welcome candidates who bring diverse perspectives, experiences, and approaches to their work. About You We encourage applications from individuals with a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. You should demonstrate: Key criteria include the following: Essential Criteria: - Experience of embedding anti-racism and social justice in engagement activities. - Experience of working with collections. - Ability to develop and deliver inclusive and creative engagement activities. - Experience of working with a range of audiences and stakeholders. - Commitment to the values of anti-racism, community partnership and inclusive practice. Desirable Criteria: - Experience working within a library, archive, heritage or cultural organisation. - Experience of working with schools and young people. - Experience of community engagement and partnership working. - Knowledge of Global Majority histories, migration, refugeeism and race equality issues. - Experience of working in a hybrid environment across multiple locations. Please see the attached document for the full Person Specification. Our benefits include: • Generous employer contribution pension • 29 days annual leave plus bank holidays, along with Christmas closure • Ride to work and EV car scheme available For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Maya Sharma, Head of the Ahmed Iqbal Ullah RACE Centre & Education Trust Email Address: maya.sharma@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support JobTrain Support Applications close at midnight on Sunday 1 November 2026. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Please note: To be shortlisted for interview you will need to demonstrate experience of embedding anti-racism and social justice in engagement activities, and experience of working with collections. Supporting Documents save_alt PSX-032209 Collections Engagement Officer FPs (PDF, KB) Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=45810": {
      "title": "Divisional Administrator",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Divisional Administrator Cookie Policy By clicking \"Allow Cookies\", you agree to the storing of cookies on your device to analyse site usage, and assist in our marketing efforts. Cookie Policy Decline Allow cookies Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Divisional Administrator (Redeployees Only) Apply for job Job reference: BMH-032159 Salary: £29,588 - £32,080 per annum depending on experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Biology, Medicine Health Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Division of Pharmacy and Optometry Hours Per Week: 35 hours per week (1 FTE) Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 21/10/2026 Contract Duration: Fixed term for 12 months School/Directorate: School of Health Sciences Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description We are seeking a motivated, organised and collaborative individual to join our Division of Pharmacy and Optometry as a Divisional Administrator. This role provides comprehensive and proactive PA, administrative, operational, people services and finance support to staff and activities across the Division. You will help ensure the smooth running of day-to-day divisional activity by coordinating meetings, events, recruitment and administrative processes, while working professionally with colleagues and stakeholders across the University. Please note that the earliest start date for this post is Monday 2nd November 2026 You will be responsible for: Providing proactive PA and administrative support, including diary coordination, meeting arrangements, agenda preparation, minute taking and following up agreed actions. Coordinating divisional meetings, committees, seminars, visitor programmes and events, ensuring logistics, materials and related arrangements are in place. Supporting recruitment and people-related administration, including selection arrangements, interview coordination, appointments, onboarding and induction activity. Supporting finance and procurement administration, including purchasing, expenses, invoice-related activity, payment forms and liaison with finance colleagues. Maintaining effective communication with academic, research, professional services and external stakeholders, signposting queries appropriately and escalating matters where required. We welcome candidates who bring diverse perspectives, experiences, and approaches to their work. About You We encourage applications from individuals with a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. You should demonstrate the following essential criteria: Essential Criteria Experience of working in an administrative, customer-facing role and providing effective PA support, including diary management and meeting coordination. Experience of supporting meetings, committees or events, including arranging logistics, preparing documentation, taking minutes and following up actions. Excellent written and verbal communication skills, with the ability to work professionally with a wide range of colleagues and stakeholders. Strong organisational skills, with the ability to prioritise competing tasks, meet deadlines, maintain accuracy and resolve routine problems effectively. Good IT skills, particularly in Microsoft 365 applications, with a proactive, flexible and collaborative approach to supporting colleagues and responding to changing priorities. We value transferable skills and real-world experience as much as formal qualifications. Our benefits include: Generous employer pension contribution Christmas closure across all departments outside of annual leave and bank holidays Purple Place employee discount platform For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Paula Lee Email Address: paula.lee@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home This role is not eligible for Skilled Worker visa sponsorship. Applicants must demonstrate the right to work in the UK. Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Supporting Documents save_alt Divisional Administrator - G4 - JD BMH-032159 (PDF, KB) Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=45636": {
      "title": "Research Associate in Spatiotemporal Causal Inference",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Research Associate in Spatiotemporal Causal Inference Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Research Associate in Spatiotemporal Causal Inference Apply for job Job reference: BMH-031990 Salary: £37,694 - £46,049 per annum depending on experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Biology, Medicine Health Location: Paterson Building Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Division of Cancer Sciences Hours Per Week: Full time (1 FTE) Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 22/10/2026 Contract Duration: Fixed-term for up to 34 months, ending no later than 30th September 2029 School/Directorate: School of Medical Sciences Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description This is an outstanding opportunity for a Research Associate, with a background in statistics, biostatistics, data science, computer science, applied mathematics, or a related quantitative discipline, to work within the reirradiation research team under the supervision of Dr Vasquez Osorio. The post holder will be part of the Radiotherapy Related Research (RRR) group, which is based at the Paterson Building in Withington. The candidate will support the aims of Dr Vasquez Osorio’s UKRI Future Leaders Fellowship on Advancing Reirradiation and Treatment Outcome Understanding with Robust Dose Mapping and Causal Inference. This innovative project will study how advanced spatiotemporal causal inference methods can be developed and applied to complex radiotherapy datasets to understand the relationship between radiation dose and treatment outcomes, enabling improved evidence-based decision-making for patients undergoing reirradiation. This position offers an ideal opportunity for an enthusiastic, well-motivated individual to work as part of a sociable multidisciplinary team consisting of physicists, computer scientists, clinicians, radiographers, and imaging experts. The ideal candidate will have excellent interpersonal, communication, programming, and organisational skills, and will be able to work with minimal supervision. Experience of advanced statistical modelling, causal inference methods, and programming for the analysis of complex datasets is essential for the position. You will be responsible for: developing research objectives and proposals for own or joint research, with the assistance of a mentor if required. conducting individual and collaborative research projects. writing up research work for publication. communicating complex information, orally, in writing and electronically. using creativity to analyse and interpret research data and draw conclusions on the outcomes. We welcome candidates who bring diverse perspectives, experiences, and approaches to their work. About You We encourage applications from individuals with a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. You should demonstrate: Essential Criteria having, or being about to obtain, a relevant PhD (or equivalent). experience in advanced statistical modelling and/or causal inference methods. ability to develop or adapt novel computational or statistical methods (beyond application of existing techniques). strong programming skills (e.g. Python and/or R). knowledge of radiotherapy, cancer research, medical imaging, or clinical data analysis. Desirable Criteria experience analysing complex biomedical, healthcare, or spatiotemporal datasets. experience with spatiotemporal methods. track record of presenting at national and international conferences. We value transferable skills and real-world experience as much as formal qualifications. Our benefits include: • Generous employer contribution pension • 29 days annual leave plus bank holidays, along with Christmas closure • Ride to work and EV car scheme available For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Dr Eliana Vasquez Osorio / Dr Ruth Forman Email Address: Eliana.vasquezosorio@manchester.ac.uk / ruth.forman@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=45470": {
      "title": "Research Associate in vivo imaging",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Research Associate in vivo imaging Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Research Associate in vivo imaging Apply for job Job reference: BMH-031830 Salary: £37,694 - £46,049 per annum depending on experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Biology, Medicine Health Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Division of Cardiovascular Sciences Hours Per Week: Full Time (1 FTE) Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 31/10/2026 Contract Duration: Fixed Term for 36 months School/Directorate: School of Medical Sciences Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description This is an outstanding opportunity for a Research Associate with expertise in pre-clinical in-vivo cerebrovascular imaging, vascular physiology or neurovascular coupling to join the Microvascular@Manchester group under the supervision of Adam Greenstein, Ingo Schiessl, Kira Shaw and Harry Pritchard. The post holder will be based within the Division of Cardiovascular Sciences and the Geoffrey Jefferson Brain Research Centre at the University of Manchester. The position is funded through the Manchester NaVigate Partnership, a major collaborative programme investigating how impaired cerebral blood flow contributes to cognitive decline and dementia. The project will determine how vascular amyloid-β(1-40) alters vascular smooth muscle calcium signaling, arterial constriction and cerebral blood flow, and whether these mechanisms can be targeted therapeutically. The successful candidate will lead studies using state-of-the-art pre- clinical in vivo imaging approaches, including multiphoton microscopy and optical imaging methods, to assess pial artery function, vascular smooth muscle calcium signaling and cerebral blood flow in mouse models relevant to Alzheimer’s disease. Complementary approaches will include behavioural assessment, histology, immunohistochemistry and physiological analysis. This position offers an excellent opportunity for an ambitious and highly motivated researcher to lead an exciting translational project within a multidisciplinary academic-industry collaboration. The ideal candidate will have strong experimental and analytical skills, experience in preclinical vascular imaging, and a passion for understanding mechanisms underlying neurovascular dysfunction in dementia. The successful applicant will have opportunities to publish, present internationally, develop collaborations and contribute to future grant applications within The University of Manchester. (Grades 6-9) Our benefits include: • Generous employer contribution pension • 29 days annual leave plus bank holidays, along with Christmas closure • Ride to work and EV car scheme available For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Successful candidates may be subject to pre-employment screening carried out on our behalf by a third party. The offer of employment will be dependent on the successful candidate passing that screening. Whilst you will be required to provide express consent at a later stage, by continuing with your application now you acknowledge that you are aware that such screening will take place, and agree to take part in the process. Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Dr. Ingo Schiessl Email: i.schiessl@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Please see Further Particulars and Person Specification below Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=34459": {
      "title": "Lecturer in Digital Engineering for Fusion Energy",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Lecturer in Digital Engineering for Fusion Energy – (Teaching and Research) Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Lecturer in Digital Engineering for Fusion Energy – (Teaching and Research) Apply for job Job reference: SAE-030868 Salary: £47,389 - £58,225 per annum depending on experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Science and Engineering Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Permanent Division/Team: Department of Mechanical and Aerospace Engineering Hours Per Week: Full time (1 FTE) Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 31/10/2026 Contract Duration: Permanent School/Directorate: School of Engineering Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description We are seeking an exceptional candidate to join the Department of Mechanical and Aerospace Engineering as a Lecturer in Digital Engineering for Fusion Energy. The role-holder will drive forward research and teaching in digital engineering for fusion energy, developing and delivering the computational tools needed to meet the formidable engineering challenges this exciting field will face over the coming decades. You will be responsible for: Development and delivery of internationally excellent research in one or more of the following topics, from an engineering perspective: multiphysics modelling and simulation of fusion in-vessel components (breeder blanket, first wall and divertor); development of digital twins, high-performance computing and AI/machine learning for fusion design and operation; verification, validation and uncertainty quantification for fusion safety and licensing; development of computational engineering tools for fusion materials modelling and structural integrity assessment Undertaking teaching duties in areas related to modelling & simulation, mechanics, structures & engineering design, (including but not limited to assessment, academic advising and project supervision) within the University on undergraduate and postgraduate courses. Embedding social responsibility and environmental sustainability within teaching and research practice. About You Essential Criteria Educated to PhD level or equivalent in a topic closely linked to digital engineering for fusion energy, most likely via undergraduate degree in engineering, maths or physics. Proven track record in one of the areas of digital engineering for fusion energy described in the above section. Excellent communication skills and evidence of developing/delivering successful collaboration with other academic researchers. Commitment to contributing improvements to advance our inclusive, equal and fair working environment. Desirable Criteria Experience with or capability for establishing meaningful links with industrial partners which are directly or indirectly relevant to the fusion or nuclear energy sector. Our benefits include: • Generous employer contribution pension • 29 days annual leave plus bank holidays, along with Christmas closure • Ride to work and EV car scheme available For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Lee Margetts Email Address: lee.margetts@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46170": {
      "title": "ESRC IAA RESEARCH ASSOCIATE",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for ESRC IAA RESEARCH ASSOCIATE Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results ESRC IAA RESEARCH ASSOCIATE Apply for job Job reference: HUM-032511 Salary: £37,694 per annum Faculty/Organisational Unit: Humanities Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Management Sciences and Marketing Hours Per Week: 35 Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 30/10/2026 Contract Duration: 12 (subject to start date) School/Directorate: Alliance Manchester Business School Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description This is an exciting opportunity for an ambitious graduate with the ability and confidence to work on a research project for developing eXplainable AI-powered Decision Models (XAI-DMs) for Insurance Under-writing, which is funded by ESRC IAA to The University of Manchester (UoM) together with Protector In-surance as industrial partner. The University of Manchester is looking to recruit a decision scientist, artificial intelligence (AI) modeller, system scientist to undertake this project, which is based on the research conducted by UoM experts over many years in areas of decision science, systems science, AI and Data Science to develop the Evidential Reasoning (ER) theory, including the Maximum Likelihood Evidential Reasoning (MAKER) framework for probabilistic reasoning with imperfect data, the ER rule for combination of uncertain evidence acquired from imperfect data and human judgment, the ER approach for multiple criteria decision making under uncertainty, and the Belief Rule Base (BRB) methodology for probabilistic system modelling and intrinsi-cally explainable machine learning funded by many EPSRC grants. The RA position will provide the successful candidate with a unique opportunity to take the lead on a strategic project, aimed at translating the ER theory and industrial domain knowledge to develop new XAI-DMs for Insurance Underwriting and to integrate them into existing working practices for Protector Insur-ance, which will be supported by the specialists of the company. The RA will therefore be faced with the challenge of understanding both the theory and the practical applications of decision modelling, data an-alytics, probabilistic inference, XAI decision modelling and evidence-based decision making to support the development of the XAI-DMs; and translating the technical know-how into a form that can be easily un-derstood and used by users within Protector Insurance. Candidates will require a minimum 2:1 with doctoral research experience in Systems Science, Decision Science, Operational Research, Data Analytics, Artificial Intelligence, or Applied Statistics with Decision, or a PhD degree in the-afore mentioned or related subject areas, with evidence of a strong grounding in probabilistic data analysis and reasoning, XAI decision modelling, decision analysis, multiple criteria assessment, and software development in Python or equivalent. This post is funded through an ESRC IAA award together with Protector Insurance, a UK Government scheme intended to promote sustained and mutually beneficial relationships between universities and industry. Based at Alliance Manchester Business School and Protector Insurance, the successful candidate will work directly with the supervisors from the University and with other researchers and practitioners from Pro-tector Insurance. (Grades 6-9) Our benefits include: • Generous employer contribution pension • 29 days annual leave plus bank holidays, along with Christmas closure • Ride to work and EV car scheme available For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Prof Jian-Bo Yang; Email: jian-bo.yang@manchester.ac.uk Tel: 0161 3063427 Prof Dong-Ling Xu; Email: ling.xu@manchester.ac.uk Tel: 0161 2750941 General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46263": {
      "title": "Research Associate in EPR and Magnetism of Molecular Spin Qubits",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Research Associate in EPR and Magnetism of Molecular Spin Qubits Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Research Associate in EPR and Magnetism of Molecular Spin Qubits Apply for job Job reference: SAE-032615 Salary: £37,694 - £46,049 per annum depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Science and Engineering Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Department of Chemistry Hours Per Week: 1 FTE Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 18/11/2026 Contract Duration: 30 months School/Directorate: School of Natural Sciences Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description A 2.5-year Postdoctoral Research Associate position is available for an outstanding and ambitious scientist to study transition metal molecular spin qubits using pulse EPR spectroscopy and related methods. This position, funded by an UKRI grant managed by Dr Floriana Tuna (https://research.manchester.ac.uk/en/persons/floriana.tuna/), involves close collaboration with the University of Rhode Island (Huh group) and Brown University (Robinson group) under an UK-US research partnership to explore quantum information science concepts in metal organic systems. As part of the project, the successful applicant will interact closely with experts at the UK National EPR Facility at Manchester (Prof David Collison, Prof Eric McInnes) and access state-of-the-art instrumentation for cw and pulse EPR (1-34 GHz), rapid scan, light-induced EPR, and SQUID magnetometry (https://www.chemistry.manchester.ac.uk/epr/). Recent publications relevant to the project: J. Am .Chem. Soc. 2024, 146, 15000; Nat. Commun. 2019, 10, 3330; Chem. Sci. 2024, 15, 3003, Chem.Eur.J. 2026, e71619, Inorg. Chem. 2026, 65, 6544. Purpose of the Job The role holder is expected to characterise the electronic structure and coherence properties of a series of d-block metal complexes to a standard suitable for publication, using pulse EPR and magnetometry, and to supervise junior researchers in the research group. Candidates should have or expect to obtain a PhD qualification in chemistry, physics, or a closely related field. They should have experience in performing advanced spectroscopic and/or magnetic measurements. Experience of EPR spectroscopy and handling and measuring highly air-sensitive samples, using glove boxes, d/f block chemistry, and being adept at a variety of characterisation techniques and calculations, are all highly desirable. The successful candidate will be a highly capable, motivated and independent researcher who is able to manage a group of PhD and undergraduate students working on a variety of spectroscopy and magnetism projects. You will be part of a larger research team working across synthesis, measurements and calculations to characterise qubits and establish the electronic structures of paramagnetic complexes. You will be expected to take a leading role in the research group, including coordination with the Huh group at Rhode Island University involved in chemical syntheses, organising group meetings, and presenting findings at international conferences. The start date is 1 February 2027, or soon after, and the position is available for up to 2.5 years. The Department of Chemistry is strongly committed to promoting equality and diversity, including the Athena SWAN Charter for gender equality in higher education. The School of Natural Sciences holds a Silver Award, which recognises their good practice in relation to gender; including flexible working arrangements, family-friendly policies, and support to allow staff to achieve a good work-life balance. We particularly welcome applications from women for this post, who are currently under-represented at this grade. Appointment will always be made on merit. For further information, please visit: https://www.manchester.ac.uk/connect/jobs/equality-diversity-inclusion/awards/athena-swan/ The University will actively foster a culture of inclusion and diversity and will seek to achieve true equality of opportunity for all members of its community. (Grades 6-9) Our benefits include: • Generous employer contribution pension • 29 days annual leave plus bank holidays, along with Christmas closure • Ride to work and EV car scheme available For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Dr Floriana Tuna Email: floriana.tuna@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46258": {
      "title": "Executive Director of Communications, Marketing and Engagement",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Executive Director of Communications, Marketing and Engagement Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Executive Director of Communications, Marketing and Engagement Apply for job Job reference: PSX-032598 Salary: Competitive Faculty/Organisational Unit: Professional Services Location: Manchester Employment type: Permanent Division/Team: Not specified Hours Per Week: FTE Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 15/11/2026 Contract Duration: Permanent School/Directorate: Directorate of Comms, Marketing & Student Recruitment Job Description Executive Director of Communications, Marketing and Engagement Shape our voice. Strengthen our influence. Lead our transformation. The University of Manchester is seeking an exceptional communications, marketing and engagement leader to help shape our next chapter. Our Manchester 2035 strategy, From Manchester for the World, sets a bold direction for the University. This is an opportunity to bring our ambitions to life: strengthening our reputation, connecting with audiences in new ways and ensuring that our research, education, innovation and contribution to society are understood and recognised around the world. The opportunity Working in close partnership with the President and Vice-Chancellor and senior leaders across the University, you will be our senior professional adviser on communications, engagement and reputation. You will bring external perspective, audience insight and sound judgement to important decisions, helping the University anticipate opportunities, navigate complex issues and build influence. Your portfolio will bring together internal and external communications, student and University marketing, media relations, public affairs, brand, campaigns, social media and creative services. You will develop an integrated approach that strengthens our distinctive identity, attracts outstanding students and colleagues, and builds meaningful relationships with our communities and partners. A central priority will be transforming the directorate. You will shape its structure, capabilities and ways of working, bringing colleagues through change and enabling central and local teams to deliver effectively together. Alongside this, you will champion the practical, responsible use of digital platforms, data, automation and AI to strengthen campaigns, improve audience engagement and modernise how we work. About you You will bring substantial senior leadership experience in communications, marketing, corporate or external affairs, reputation or a related discipline, with a track record of delivering strategies that have strengthened organisational profile and engagement. You will have worked with chief executives, boards or senior leadership teams as a trusted adviser and will be confident operating in complex, highly scrutinised environments. An outstanding communicator and relationship builder, you will know how to secure support across organisational boundaries and turn shared ambition into delivery. We are looking for an inclusive, collaborative leader with experience of transforming multidisciplinary functions, developing talented teams and building new capabilities. Technologically confident, you will bring evidence of successful digital and social media campaigns and an informed understanding of how emerging technologies can deliver measurable impact. Your experience may come from higher education or another complex, knowledge-intensive or internationally focused organisation. Higher education experience is welcome but is not essential. Why Manchester? You will have an extraordinary foundation to build on: research and academic expertise that changes lives, a globally recognised name, deep connections to our city and region, and a diverse international community. For a leader with ambition, creativity and a commitment to public purpose, this is an opportunity to shape how the University connects with the world and to make a lasting contribution to its future. Find out more Please see the link to the candidate brochure and job description, attached below, for further information about the role and selection criteria. For informal enquiries or to arrange an exploratory conversation with Patrick Hackett, Registrar, Secretary and Chief Operating Officer, or Duncan Ivison, President and Vice-Chancellor, please contact Vicky Roth at talent.attraction@manchester.ac.uk . We welcome applications from people of all backgrounds and experiences. If you require reasonable adjustments at any stage of the recruitment process, please contact talent.attraction@manchester.ac.uk so that we can discuss how best to support you. For technical support please call 0161 850 2004 or visit https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home This vacancy will close for applications at midnight on the closing date. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46149": {
      "title": "Chair/Professor in International Business (Teaching and Research)",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Chair/Professor in International Business (Teaching and Research) Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Chair/Professor in International Business (Teaching and Research) Apply for job Job reference: HUM-032488 Salary: Competitive Faculty/Organisational Unit: Humanities Location: Manchester Employment type: Permanent Division/Team: Innovation Management and Policy Hours Per Week: 35 Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 13/11/2026 Contract Duration: N/A School/Directorate: Alliance Manchester Business School Job Description Chair/Professor in International Business The role: We are seeking an internationally recognised scholar for a permanent, full-time Chair in International Business. The successful candidate will advance an ambitious programme of research, provide intellectual leadership and contribute to excellent research-led teaching. They will work with the Head of Group and colleagues to develop the Group’s research, mentor scholars at different career stages, attract external funding and supervise doctoral researchers. We welcome applications across international business and related fields from scholars whose work complements or extends the Group’s strengths. We are open to different theoretical perspectives, methodological approaches and geographical areas of expertise. Candidates should demonstrate a clear contribution to international business scholarship and a commitment to a collegial academic community. Overall Purpose of the Job: The overall purpose of the role is to provide academic leadership in International Business at AMBS through world-leading research, excellent research-led teaching, and leadership within the International Business Group, the Innovation, Management and Policy Division and the wider School. In particular, the post holder will be expected to: Conduct and publish world-leading research in international business in the highest-quality international peer-reviewed journals, and make a significant contribution to the School’s submission to the Research Excellence Framework (REF). Provide intellectual leadership to the International Business Group, helping to shape and deliver its research strategy and fostering collaboration within the Group, across AMBS and the University, and with external partners. Lead or co-lead applications for significant external research funding from UK, European and international funders. Mentor and support the development of colleagues, particularly early- and mid-career researchers. Attract and supervise high-quality doctoral researchers and contribute to the Group’s doctoral training activities. Design and deliver excellent research-led teaching in international business at undergraduate, postgraduate, MBA and/or executive education levels, and contribute to curriculum and programme development. Engage with business, policy and civil society audiences to generate impact from research, and enhance the School’s visibility and reputation in the international business community, including through editorial and learned-society leadership. Undertake leadership and citizenship roles within the Group, Division and School as required. Our benefits include: • Generous employer contribution pension • 29 days annual leave plus bank holidays, along with Christmas closure • Ride to work and EV car scheme available For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Head of International Business Group Email: matthew.alford@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46213": {
      "title": "Digital Preservation and Systems Manager",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Digital Preservation and Systems Manager Cookie Policy By clicking \"Allow Cookies\", you agree to the storing of cookies on your device to analyse site usage, and assist in our marketing efforts. Cookie Policy Decline Allow cookies Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Digital Preservation and Systems Manager Apply for job Job reference: PSX-032553 Salary: £37,694 - £46,049 per annum, depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Professional Services Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Permanent Division/Team: AI and Ideas Adoption Hours Per Week: 35 Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 30/10/2026 Contract Duration: Permanent School/Directorate: The University of Manchester Library Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description The University of Manchester Library is seeking a motivated and collaborative Digital Preservation and Systems Manager to join the Digitisation and Digital Preservation Team within Digital Reach, Directorate of AI and Ideas Adoption. You will lead the technical transformation of our digital collection systems, helping unique physical and digital collections become accessible, sustainable resources that inspire research, enrich teaching and connect wider communities with cultural heritage. This work supports the Manchester 2035 strategy by advancing the foundation Organised for Success: One University through cross-University collaboration, and the leap Digital Inside and Out through resilient, connected and user-focused digital services. The role is also an exemplar of the One Library Working anchor set out in the library’s Imagine2035 strategy, connecting expertise, systems and services across organisational boundaries to deliver shared priorities. Reporting to the Head of Digital Reach, you will provide technical leadership across digitisation, digital preservation, discovery, metadata interoperability and learning resources. You will work with Library colleagues, IT Services, the University’s cultural institutions and suppliers to ensure that platforms and services are robust, sustainable and aligned with Library priorities. You will be responsible for: Leading the technical transformation of digital collection systems to improve discovery, access and long-term preservation. Managing systems, infrastructure and services that support digitisation, preservation and access. Providing specialist advice on digitisation, digital preservation, metadata, digital asset management and application support. Improving metadata quality, interoperability and integration, and delivering technical projects into operational service. Leading supplier relationships and managing and developing a small specialist team. We welcome candidates who bring diverse perspectives, experiences and approaches to their work. About You We encourage applications from people with a wide range of backgrounds and experience. Essential Criteria Strong knowledge of digitisation, metadata standards, digital object management and digital preservation approaches. Experience supporting digitised collections, technical projects and applications that manage metadata and digital objects. Ability to import and export digital objects and metadata, and to work with legacy formats and systems. A logical, service-oriented approach, with excellent problem-solving, communication and technical documentation skills. Experience managing a small team or providing day-to-day supervision and support. Desirable Criteria Experience developing, or working with others to develop, front-end interfaces to digital content. A relevant honours degree or professional qualification in library, information, computing or digital scholarship; equivalent professional experience is equally valued. You will also demonstrate commitment to accessibility, copyright, data protection, safeguarding, ethical practice, inclusion, health and safety, continuous improvement and service excellence. Our benefits include: Generous employer pension contribution 29 days’ annual leave plus bank holidays and Christmas closure Ride to Work and electric vehicle schemes For more information, see University of Manchester Benefits and our Flexible and Hybrid Working information . This role is categorised as fully hybrid, normally involving some campus working. We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace difference, diversity and debate, and support the lawful exchange and testing of ideas. Read our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Gail Millin-Chalabi: Gail.Millin-Chalabi@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support Jobtrain support Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars, including the full person specification, are linked below. Supporting Documents save_alt Digital Preservation and Systems Manager FPs (PDF, KB) Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46300": {
      "title": "Research Associate In Proton Therapy Detector Development",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Research Associate In Proton Therapy Detector Development Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Research Associate In Proton Therapy Detector Development Apply for job Job reference: BMH-032638 Salary: £38,448 per annum Faculty/Organisational Unit: Biology, Medicine Health Location: The Christie NHS Foundation Trust, Withington, Manchester Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Division of Cancer Sciences Hours Per Week: Full time (1 FTE) Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 27/10/2026 Contract Duration: Fixed term from 13th December 2026 to 31st March 2027 (or earlier if candidate is available) School/Directorate: School of Medical Sciences Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description The University of Manchester is seeking to appoint a Research Associate to contribute to a short-term translational pilot project focused on the development and evaluation of a novel time-of-flight detector system for in-vivo range verification in proton beam therapy. Based within the Division of Cancer Sciences and working closely with collaborators at The Christie Hospital NHS Foundation Trust, the post offers an excellent opportunity to undertake experimental work in the proton research facility, analyse data to support validation of the technology and contribute to project delivery, dissemination of findings and outputs arising from the pilot study. You should hold, or be close to completing, a PhD in physics, medical physics, engineering or a closely related discipline, with relevant experimental research experience in detectors, instrumentation or radiation physics. You should also be able to demonstrate strong analytical and data handling skills, experience of independent experimental work and the ability to contribute effectively within a multidisciplinary research team. Experience in proton therapy, fast timing techniques, Monte-Carlo methods or clinical research environments would be advantageous. This fixed-term post offers access to specialist proton therapy research infrastructure and close links to one of the UK’s leading cancer centres. The University of Manchester is committed to equality, diversity and inclusion, and welcomes applications from all sections of the community. You will be responsible for: Design, construct and optimise a prototype time-of-flight based range probe integrating ultra-fast silicon detectors and timing electronics. Plan and undertake experimental measurements in the proton therapy research facility to characterise detector performance, timing resolution and calibration against proton range. Carry out validation studies using appropriate phantom models and compare findings with established clinical systems and detectors. Analyse and interpret experimental data to assess system precision, uncertainty and performance against project objectives. Work collaboratively with academic, clinical and technical colleagues across the University of Manchester and The Christie NHS Foundation Trust to support delivery of the project. We welcome candidates who bring diverse perspectives, experiences, and approaches to their work. About You We encourage applications from individuals with a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. You should demonstrate: Essential Criteria PhD (or near completion) in physics, medical physics, engineering or a closely related discipline. Experience in experimental research involving detectors, instrumentation or radiation physics. Experience of designing and conducting experiments independently. Experience of analysing complex data sets and interpreting results. Ability to work independently and collaboratively across disciplines. Desirable Criteria Experience of working with time-of-flight measurement techniques or fast timing electronics. Experience of working with Monte-Carlo simulation tools. Experience of in-vivo range verification research or related translational detector development. We value transferable skills and real-world experience as much as formal qualifications. Our benefits include: • Generous employer contribution pension • 29 days annual leave plus bank holidays, along with Christmas closure • Ride to work and EV car scheme available For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Dr Michael Taylor, Senior Lecturer in Proton Therapy Physics Email Address: m.j.taylor@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Watch: The University of Manchester – our new strategy to 2035 Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46252": {
      "title": "Infrastructure Analyst",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Infrastructure Analyst Cookie Policy By clicking \"Allow Cookies\", you agree to the storing of cookies on your device to analyse site usage, and assist in our marketing efforts. Cookie Policy Decline Allow cookies Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Infrastructure Analyst Apply for job Job reference: PSX-032590 Salary: £33,002 - £36,636 per annum depending on experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Professional Services Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Permanent Division/Team: IT Services Research IT Hours Per Week: Full time Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 29/10/2026 Contract Duration: Permanent School/Directorate: Directorate of IT Services Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description We are seeking a motivated and collaborative individual to join our team as a Infrastructure Analyst. This role offers an exciting opportunity to contribute to Provide practical support to users of our computational platforms (HPC/HTC) via a ticketing system and in-person/via video, with guidance from the rest of the team (65%); Help the team deliver face-to-face training courses (15%); Assist with the maintenance of our on-site computational platforms (HPC/HTC) (20%). You will be responsible for: Configures the hardware/software environment as required by the system being integrated. Installs or removes hardware and/or software, using supplied installation instructions and tools; follows agreed standards, including those for electrical work. Agrees the timing of the work with those affected, e.g. users, operations management, including, where appropriate, hand-over to client. Conducts tests of the hardware and/or software affected using supplied test procedures and diagnostic tools. Helps to resolve problems and faults, and corrects malfunctions, calling on help from more experienced colleagues if required. Documents results in accordance with agreed procedures. Carries out required collection of information and records, including using network management systems and appropriate performance analysis equipment to monitor installation performance against agreed service levels. Takes action on known or moderately complex problems, advising superiors and specialists only when their attention is required. Reports details of all hardware/software items that have been installed and removed so that configuration management records can be updated. We welcome candidates who bring diverse perspectives, experiences, and approaches to their work. About You We encourage applications from individuals with a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. You should demonstrate: Essential Criteria: University IT Portfolio: Knowledge of systems, applications, technologies, and equipment in use within the University as appropriate Operational/Service Architecture: Knowledge of the IT infrastructure (hardware, databases, operating systems, local area networks etc.) and the IT applications and service processes used within own organisation.Safe Installation Practice: The knowledge and ability to install and maintain hardware systems in a way that ensures they operate within their planned specification and are safe to use by those authorised to work with them. Progress Reporting: Methods and techniques for reporting progress and financial conformance against an agreed plan. Equipment Installation: Installation, testing and commissioning of supplier specific equipment and software in all legitimate operational environments. Desktop Software: The use of everyday desktop software. Desirable Criteria: Networking and Communications: The planning and management of the interaction between two or more networking systems, computers or other \"intelligent\" devices. Programming Languages: A set of codes and syntax (supported by software tools) which enables the unambiguous translation of specified functionality into \"source code\" for the creation of computer programs. Flexibility: Taking account of new information or changed circumstances and modifying understanding of a problem or situation accordingly. Corporate, Industry and Professional Standards: Specific standards associated with the IT practitioner's current role. Specialist Equipment Interfacing: Knowledge of interfaces between IT and associated specialist equipment (e.g. microscopes, language labs, translation labs, OCR Readers, Spectrometers, etc.) We value transferable skills and real-world experience as much as formal qualifications. Our benefits include: Generous employer pension contribution Christmas closure across all departments outside of annual leave and bank holidays Purple Place employee discount platform For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Pen Richardson Email Address: pen.richardson@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home This role is not eligible for Skilled Worker visa sponsorship. Applicants must demonstrate the right to work in the UK. Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Supporting Documents save_alt PSX-032590 Infrastructure Analyst (PDF, KB) Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46276": {
      "title": "Reader Services Assistant",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Reader Services Assistant Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Reader Services Assistant Apply for job Job reference: PSX-032611 Salary: £27,319 - £28,778 Faculty/Organisational Unit: Professional Services Location: John Rylands Library Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Special Collections Hours Per Week: 35 Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 29/10/2026 Contract Duration: 12 School/Directorate: The University of Manchester Library Job Description The Library's Special Collections are among the richest holdings of rare books, manuscripts, archives, maps and visual collections in the world and are an outstanding resource, spread across three sites: The Ahmed Iqbal Ullah Race Centre, the University’s Main Library and The John Rylands Library. We deliver an extensive programme of research on the collections, in collaboration with academic colleagues at the University. As a Reader Service Assistant, you will be part of a busy team providing a welcoming and informative service to customers using Special Collections, both in person and remotely. The role includes facilitating research by dealing with enquiries, locating and retrieving material across sites, invigilating our two reading rooms and promoting the use of Special Collections within the library and the wider community. You will also help to widen access to our collections by hosting remote consultations via video call. This job includes covering Saturday and Thursday evening opening hours as part of a rota. The University of Manchester Library is one of only five National Research Libraries and the third largest academic library in the UK. Our vast and rich collections (both print and digital) help us to deliver a world class library and information service for the University. We sit at the heart of the University experience for both learning and research and are fully committed to widening access to our expertise, collections and services to researchers, local schools, and others in the community. Hours & Place of Work Normal working hours are 35 hours per week. This post is based primarily at The John Rylands Library and The Special Collections Reading Room at Main Library. However, all Library employees are required to work at any Library site, as necessary. You will be responsible for: Responding to enquiries Following processes and procedures to make bookings Locating material using collections management systems and digital catalogues Issuing material and providing handling guidance to researchers Delivering remote Research Room appointments over video call using a visualiser We welcome candidates who bring diverse perspectives, experiences, and approaches to their work. About You We encourage applications from individuals with a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. You should demonstrate: Essential Criteria Excellent digital literacy, especially Microsoft Office packages, including Outlook, Excel, and Word, and online collaboration tools such as MS Teams Customer service: enquiry management and interpreting customer needs Experience of working well within a team Attention to detail: consistent application of standards and implementation of policies and procedures Be educated to GCSE Level standard or equivalent including Maths and English Desirable Criteria Handling Special Collections materials (e.g. rare books, maps, manuscripts, archives, art and visual collections) Appreciation of conservation and security requirements of rare and valuable material Having undertaken customer service training We value transferable skills and real-world experience as much as formal qualifications. Our benefits include: Generous employer pension contribution Christmas closure across all departments outside of annual leave and bank holidays Purple Place employee discount platform For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Imogen Durant Email Address: imogen.durant@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home This role is not eligible for Skilled Worker visa sponsorship. Applicants must demonstrate the right to work in the UK. Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Find out more about our future in The University of Manchester – our new strategy to 2035 . Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46242": {
      "title": "Platform Engineer",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Platform Engineer Cookie Policy By clicking \"Allow Cookies\", you agree to the storing of cookies on your device to analyse site usage, and assist in our marketing efforts. Cookie Policy Decline Allow cookies Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Platform Engineer Apply for job Job reference: PSX-032581 Salary: £38,448 to £46,970 per annum depending on experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Professional Services Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Permanent Division/Team: Campus Technology Services Hours Per Week: Full time Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 11/11/2026 Contract Duration: Permanent School/Directorate: Directorate of IT Services Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description About IT Services IT Services at the University of Manchester is a vibrant and fast-moving department, we focus on delivering excellent customer service and quality services for our staff, students and researchers. The Team The role is within the Platform Engineering Team, the key team developing our cloud platforms and supporting migration to cloud through reusable Infrastructure as Code libraries. Our Platform Engineers design, build and maintain the delivery infrastructure, systems and processes underpinning technology transformation engagements and lead the adoption of modern platforms and ways of working. The Role We’re looking for a Platform Engineer who has a broad understanding of cloud, and a deep understanding of at least one major cloud platform. You will collaborate closely with the wider Platform Engineering Team, Cloud Architects and colleagues in Research to deliver exciting digital programmes in the education sector. The University has a clearly defined IT digital roadmap and as a cloud expert, you'll work in the Platform Engineering team alongside developers, architects, UX designers, analysts and Scrum Master to deliver DevOps CI/CD, automation and cloud infrastructure. You’ll work especially closely with our colleagues in Research, helping to define and develop self-service cloud infrastructure, supporting AI/ML implementations and accelerating the pace of moving from ideation to active research. We encourage continuous learning, so as well as the opportunity to work with some of the brightest and best in the industry, and evaluate the latest technologies, we’ll encourage online training for obtaining Cloud certifications. Even if you don’t meet all the criteria, we encourage you to apply as we can develop you into the role using a training plan and career path. The Person You’ll have the expertise and confidence to lead the definition and support of the Azure, AWS delivery platform in complex engagements, typically working in multi-disciplinary teams. The successful candidate will need to be able to demonstrate the below skills and experience: Hands on with any of Oracle OCI, Azure or AWS Automating delivery of Infrastructure as Code (IaC) via Terraform Code Repository Management (e.g. GitLab, GitHub, Bitbucket) Deep understanding of CI/CD pipeline Scripting with one or more of the following: PowerShell, Python, Golang Understanding of architecture design and documentation Understanding of Security Compliance PCI DSS, ISO, Cyber Essentials, NIST Excellent communication and written skills. Desirable Criteria Familiarity with collaboration suites i.e. Atlassian products (Jira, Confluence) Experience of building automation/integrations with Service NOW. Key stakeholder management and collaborative working with wider teams. What You Can Expect in Return Our diverse job opportunities all include a top benefits package that includes many features that are hard to find in the private sector: Generous annual leave allowance, including Christmas/New Year closure; Pension scheme membership to provide benefits for you and your family; Well-being programme with counselling, fitness and leading sports facilities; Learning and development opportunities; Season ticket loans for public transport; Cycle to Work Scheme; Workplace nursery scheme; Staff recognition schemes; Staff discounts on a range of products and services including travel and high street savings. In fact, there are so many benefits available you can see more of them by clicking here. Find out more about our organisation and values: IT Services: About us and ITS Practice Charter When completing the additional information section of your application, please ensure you make reference to the job description and person specification above, as this will form an integral part of the shortlisting process. As an equal-opportunity employer, we welcome applicants from all sections of the community regardless of age, sex, gender (or gender identity), ethnicity, disability, sexual orientation and transgender status. All appointments are made on merit. Our University is positive about flexible working – you can find out more here Hybrid working arrangements may be considered. Please be aware that due to the number of applications we unfortunately may not able to provide individual feedback on your application. Please note that we are unable to respond to enquiries, accept CVs or applications from Recruitment Agencies. Any recruitment enquiries from recruitment agencies should be directed to recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Any CV’s submitted by a recruitment agency will be considered a gift. Enquiries about the vacancy, shortlisting and interviews: Please contact our IT Resource Management Team Email: it.resource@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries: Email: recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical support: 0161 850 2004 https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home This vacancy will close for applications at midnight on the closing date. Please see the link below for the Further Particulars document which contains the person specification criteria. Supporting Documents save_alt PSX-032581 Platform Engineer Fps (PDF, KB) Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46231": {
      "title": "Research Associate in Health Economics",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Research Associate in Health Economics Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Research Associate in Health Economics Apply for job Job reference: BMH-032571 Salary: £38,448 to £46,970 per annum, depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Biology, Medicine Health Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Division of Population Health, Health Services Research And Primary Care Hours Per Week: Full Time Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 28/10/2026 Contract Duration: 12 months School/Directorate: School of Health Sciences Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description We wish to appoint a Research Associate in Health Economics to join the Health Organisation, Policy and Economics (HOPE) research group at The University of Manchester. The successful candidate will contribute to a School for Primary Care Research (SPCR) funded grant exploring the impact of continuity of care on GP work life satisfaction, co-led by Dr Emma McManus and Dr Laura Jefferson. The post is available for 12 months in the first instance. The contract of employment will be held by, and the post will be located at, The University of Manchester. The successful applicant will be under the line management of Dr Emma McManus and will also work closely with Dr Michael Anderson and Dr Sean Urwin, as well as the wider SPCR project team comprising of: Dr Laura Jefferson, Dr Mhorag Goff and Dr Patrick Birch. You will be based in the Division of Population Health, Health Services Research and Primary Care, in the Faculty of Biology, Medicine and Health. This role will offer an excellent opportunity to join a team delivering high-impact, policy-relevant research using large national, linked datasets. Working as part of a wider team, the post holder will also develop experience working as part of a mixed methods research project and build knowledge of primary care workforce issues. What you will get in return: Fantastic market leading Pension scheme Excellent employee health and wellbeing services including an Employee Assistance Programme Exceptional starting annual leave entitlement, plus bank holidays Additional paid closure over the Christmas period Local and national discounts at a range of major retailers As an equal opportunities employer we welcome applicants from all sections of the community regardless of age, sex, gender (or gender identity), ethnicity, disability, sexual orientation and transgender status. All appointments are made on merit. Our University is positive about flexible working – you can find out more here Hybrid working arrangements may be considered. Please be aware that due to the number of applications we unfortunately may not able to provide individual feedback on your application. Please note that we are unable to respond to enquiries, accept CVs or applications from Recruitment Agencies. Any recruitment enquiries from recruitment agencies should be directed to recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Any CV’s submitted by a recruitment agency will be considered a gift. Enquiries about the vacancy, shortlisting and interviews: Name: Emma Mcmanus Email: emma.mcmanus@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries: Email: recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical support: 0161 850 2004 https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home This vacancy will close for applications at midnight on the closing date. Please see the link below for the Further Particulars document which contains the person specification criteria. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46173": {
      "title": "Research Technician",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Research Technician Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Research Technician Apply for job Job reference: BMH-032513 Salary: £29,588 - £32,080 pro rata, per annum, depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Biology, Medicine Health Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: FBMH Technical Services Hours Per Week: 28 hours per week (0.8 FTE) Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 28/10/2026 Contract Duration: 2 years School/Directorate: Faculty Office - Biology, Medicine and Health Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description The Byron Lab within the School of Biological Sciences wish to appoint a laboratory-based Research Technician to contribute to an exciting project exploring the regulation of nuclear function by actin-associated adhesion proteins. We seek a motivated and collaborative molecular cell biologist to investigate how regulation of the cytoskeleton at the nucleus controls cell behaviour and gene expression. During this position, the post-holder will undertake a range of molecular and cell biological activities, including use of recombinant DNA technology, RT-PCR, cell imaging and biochemical and proteomic techniques to characterise cytoskeletal and nuclear protein localisation and function in mammalian cells, including skin cancer cells. You will be responsible for: Carrying out and assisting staff and students with standard experimental protocols. Supporting the smooth running of laboratories, including general housekeeping duties. Collating and storing accurate records using paper- and computer-based systems, and analysing and preparing data for inclusion in presentations and publications. Contributing to the group’s progression by providing feedback and input to experimental design and development. Carrying out risk assessments for the preparation and use of reagents, chemicals, equipment and procedures by self and others in the research group. We welcome candidates who bring diverse perspectives, experiences, and approaches to their work. About You We encourage applications from individuals with a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. You should demonstrate: Essential Criteria: A qualification at QCF level 6, such as a bachelor’s degree, in a relevant biological science subject; or QCF level 2 passes, such as GCSEs, in English, Mathematics and at least one science subject, supported by substantial relevant research laboratory experience. Practical experience in a range of relevant specialised techniques, including molecular biology, cell culture, immunoprecipitation, western blotting, brightfield and fluorescence microscopy, PCR and RT-qPCR. Knowledge of, and compliance with, relevant Health and Safety regulation, the Data Protection Act, Standard Operating Procedures and risk assessments, including COSHH risk assessments. Excellent written and oral communication skills, with the ability to deal effectively and politely with staff, students and visitors. Excellent organisational skills, with the ability to prioritise a variety of work activities in a busy environment and meet strict deadlines. Desirable Criteria: Practical experience in one or several of the following laboratory techniques: histology, immunohistochemistry, live-cell imaging, super-resolution imaging, image analysis, site-directed mutagenesis, retroviral transduction, sample preparation for mass spectrometry, sample preparation for gene sequencing. Good statistical skills. Experience of working with animal and/or ex vivo and/or in vitro models of disease. Willingness to attend and contribute to research group and staff meetings. We value transferable skills and real-world experience as much as formal qualifications. Our benefits include: Generous employer pension contribution Christmas closure across all departments outside of annual leave and bank holidays Purple Place employee discount platform For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits. You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here. We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy. Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Dr Adam Byron Email address: adam.byron@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home This role is not eligible for Skilled Worker visa sponsorship. Applicants must demonstrate the right to work in the UK. Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46264": {
      "title": "Virtualisation Engineer",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Virtualisation Engineer Cookie Policy By clicking \"Allow Cookies\", you agree to the storing of cookies on your device to analyse site usage, and assist in our marketing efforts. Cookie Policy Decline Allow cookies Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Virtualisation Engineer Apply for job Job reference: PSX-032602 Salary: £37,694 - £46,049 per annum depending on experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Professional Services Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Permanent Division/Team: Campus Technology Services Hours Per Week: Full time (1 FTE) Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 10/11/2026 Contract Duration: Permanent School/Directorate: Directorate of IT Services Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description The Role We’re looking for an experienced engineer who has a broad understanding of VMware, and a deep understanding of at least one major supporting operating system. You will collaborate closely with the wider engineering & development teams, architects and colleagues in Research to deliver exciting digital programmes in the education sector. The University has a clearly defined IT digital roadmap and as an experienced Senior engineer, you'll work in the Virtualisation Engineering team alongside developers, architects, UX designers, analysts and product owners to deliver automation and VMWARE with opportunities to deliver an OpenStack infrastructure. You’ll work especially closely with our colleagues in Research, helping to define and develop self-service infrastructure, supporting implementations and accelerating the pace of moving from ideation to active research. We encourage continuous learning, so as well as the opportunity to work with some of the brightest and best in the industry, and evaluate the latest technologies, we’ll encourage online training for obtaining certifications. Even if you don’t meet all the criteria, we encourage you to apply as we can develop you into the role using a training plan and career path. The Person You’ll have the expertise and confidence to lead the definition, delivery and support of enterprise infrastructure and hybrid platforms in complex environments, typically working in multi-disciplinary teams. Essential Criteria The successful candidate will need to be able to demonstrate the below skills and experience: Strong experience supporting VMware or OpenStack and hybrid infrastructure environments Administration and support of Linux and Windows Server operating systems Scripting skills using technologies such as PowerShell, Python or Bash Experience with infrastructure automation and Infrastructure as Code tools such as Terraform or OpenTofu and Ansible Knowledge of enterprise virtualisation, storage and networking technologies Experience with code repository management tools. Understanding of platform security and compliance frameworks Excellent communication, stakeholder engagement and technical documentation skills Desirable Criteria Experience with monitoring and logging platforms such as Zabbix & Grafana or similar Experience integrating infrastructure platforms with ServiceNow or similar ITSM tooling Familiarity with Atlassian products such as Jira and Confluence Strong collaborative working and stakeholder management skills . Find out more about our organisation and values: IT Services: About us and ITS Practice Charter When completing the additional information section of your application, please ensure you make reference to the job description and person specification above, as this will form an integral part of the shortlisting process. Our benefits include: • Generous employer contribution pension • 29 days annual leave plus bank holidays, along with Christmas closure • Ride to work and EV car scheme available For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Nisar Khan Email Address: Nisar.khan@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Supporting Documents save_alt PSX-032602 - Virtualisation Engineer (PDF, KB) Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46257": {
      "title": "CLINICAL RESEARCH ASSOCIATE",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for CLINICAL RESEARCH ASSOCIATE Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results CLINICAL RESEARCH ASSOCIATE Apply for job Job reference: BMH-032597 Salary: 54,499 - 67,325 pro rata per annum Faculty/Organisational Unit: Biology, Medicine Health Location: Manchester Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Division of Psychology, Communication and Human Neuroscience Hours Per Week: 0.6 FTE / 21 Hours per week Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 27/10/2026 Contract Duration: Fixed term for 12 Months School/Directorate: School of Health Sciences Job Description We are seeking a motivated and collaborative individual to join our team as a Clinical Research Associate. This fixed-term, part-time role will undertake innovative studies of the causes of schizophrenia and related disorders, including studies testing the role of excitation-inhibition balance in psychotic disorders using cutting-edge fMRI techniques, EEG biomarkers and other experimental medicine work. You will be responsible for: Recruitment and clinical assessment of participants with psychosis, including using clinical rating scales Organising and acquiring imaging (MRI and EEG) and other measures Ensuring high quality research governance across projects Helping analyse, interpret and publish the results of projects Ensuring effective collaborative relationships with other research workers on the project, as well as with local clinical services and researchers on other related projects. We welcome candidates who bring diverse perspectives, experiences, and approaches to their work. About You We encourage applications from individuals with a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. You should demonstrate: Essential Criteria Full GMC registration with valid license to practice (candidates without this will not be considered further) Experience of assessing patients with psychotic disorders in UK or similar healthcare settings Ambitious, enthusiastic, responsible, flexible and able to work independently to deadlines Ability to work in a team with excellent written and oral communication skills Evidence of strong interest and aptitude for research, particularly in a relevant area (e.g. psychopharmacology, neuroimaging, psychosis) Desirable Criteria Training and/or experience with relevant psychiatric rating and/or neuroimaging measures Experience of acquisition and analysis of relevant neuroimaging data (e.g. PET, MRI, EEG, MEG) Experience of working with patients with psychosis in research or other relevant settings Knowledge of drug treatments in psychosis particularly in psychosis Publications and/or conference abstracts in relevant area (such as psychosis (SIRS)/psychiatry/ psychopharmacology) We value transferable skills and real-world experience as much as formal qualifications. Our benefits include: • Generous employer contribution pension • 29 days annual leave plus bank holidays, along with Christmas closure • Ride to work and EV car scheme available For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Stephen Kaar Email Address: stephen.kaar@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46007": {
      "title": "Senior Technician (Polymer Processing, Characterisation and Synthesis)",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Senior Technician (Polymer Processing, Characterisation and Synthesis) Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Senior Technician (Polymer Processing, Characterisation and Synthesis) Apply for job Job reference: SAE-032352 Salary: £33,002 - £36,636 per annum depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Science and Engineering Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: FSE Technical Services Hours Per Week: 35 Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 30/10/2026 Contract Duration: 36 months School/Directorate: Faculty Office - Science and Engineering Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description Overall Purpose This role is based in the Royce Hub Building, a £105m investment containing £200m of equipment. Part of Royce’s mission is to provide access to the latest facilities to the UK Materials Science community and to catalyse industrial collaboration and exploitation of materials research, so, alongside supporting internal access to the equipment from within the University of Manchester, the role holder will also be working on external projects for both industrial and academic partners. The Polymer Characterisation, Processing and Synthesis Core Facility builds upon the innovative, creative, practical, and research capabilities of our enterprising technical staff. The facility aims to accelerate interdisciplinary research and teaching by recognising the intrinsic synergies within the experimental workflows of Natural Sciences and Engineering, while also supporting the Royce Chemical Materials Discovery theme. We are seeking a Senior Technician who will be primarily responsible for the operation and maintenance of instrumentation for physical characterisation techniques e.g. Differential Scanning Calorimetry (DSC), Rheology, Mechanical testing and Dynamic mechanical analysis (DMA). The role will also involve supporting the core facility team in delivering spectroscopic capabilities, such as FTIR, fluorimetry and Ellipsometry. Our benefits include: (Grades 1-5) Generous employer pension contribution Christmas closure across all departments outside of annual leave and bank holidays Purple Place employee discount platform For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Dr Nicola Jones Senior Technical Specialist (Polymer Characterisation, Processing and Synthesis) Email Address: Nicola.jones-4@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=45948": {
      "title": "NSEC Project Manager",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for NSEC Project Manager Cookie Policy By clicking \"Allow Cookies\", you agree to the storing of cookies on your device to analyse site usage, and assist in our marketing efforts. Cookie Policy Decline Allow cookies Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results NSEC Project Manager Apply for job Job reference: SAE-032289 Salary: £37,694 - £46,049 per annum depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Science and Engineering Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Thomas Ashton Institute Hours Per Week: 35 Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 10/11/2026 Contract Duration: Fixed term until 31/1/2029 School/Directorate: FSE Research Institutes Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description Overall Purpose The Network of Security & Excellence (NSEC) project manager will manage, facilitate and enhance growth of the network. This role essentially brings together the management of various work packages, tasks and stakeholder engagement/communications activities to deliver NSEC. Alongside the Building a secure and resilient world research and coordination hub (SALIENT) and programme manager, you will need to define key deliverables with deadlines and some that will be ongoing in support of securing long-term funding, advocacy and outcomes for the network. You will work alongside key leaders in both SALIENT (Hull and Manchester Universities) and ACE (Home Office) and will engage with a broad set of stakeholders across UK Government, Academia and Industry. The successful candidate must be capable of achieving at least the Baseline Personnel Security Standard (BPSS), other security clearance may be required, and be committed to the British values of democracy, rule of law, respect and tolerance, individual liberty and the seven principles of public life. Applicants should ensure they use the additional information section of the application to evidence how they meet the essential criteria. About the Team NSEC is a multidisciplinary community of academics with interests in supporting the UK’s national security and resilience needs. NSEC enables the effective coordination of stakeholders and initiatives to enhance cohesion and accelerate collaborations. This includes; • Academics who are keen to collaborate and engage with complex challenges for maximum impact. • Problem owners in government who are seeking solutions to some of the UK’s most intractable societal challenges. • Industry and third sector organisations aiming to enhance capabilities through academic collaboration. SALIENT is a UKRI-funded research and coordination hub, that also administers devolved funding calls and is led by The University of Manchester. SALIENT and NSEC sit within the Thomas Ashton Institute at The University of Manchester. SALIENT is focused on addressing national security and resilience challenges through interdisciplinary research collaboration. Flexible, hybrid and job share applications will be considered. More information can be found at https://nsec.uk/ (Grades 6-9) Our benefits include: • Generous employer contribution pension • 29 days annual leave plus bank holidays, along with Christmas closure • Ride to work and EV car scheme available For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Victoria Turner Email Address: victoria.turner@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Supporting Documents save_alt SAE-032289 NSEC Project Manager FP's (PDF, KB) Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46160": {
      "title": "Programme Assistant (Assessments)",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Programme Assistant (Assessments) Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Programme Assistant (Assessments) Apply for job Job reference: HUM-032499 Salary: £27,319 to £28,778 per annum, depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Humanities Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Permanent Division/Team: MBA and Global Programmes Hours Per Week: 35 Hours per week (1 FTE) Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 30/10/2026 Contract Duration: Permanent School/Directorate: Alliance Manchester Business School Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description The key aspect of this role is to provide accurate, responsive administrative support for assessment and progression activities across the MBA & Global Programmes, helping students move successfully through each stage of their academic journey. The successful candidate will achieve this by maintaining high-quality student records, assisting with processing marks and progression data, supporting Examination Boards and disciplinary panels, responding to enquiries, and working closely with colleagues across programme delivery and student services. By combining excellent organisation and attention to detail with proactive, inclusive customer service, you will help ensure fair outcomes, smooth programme delivery and a positive student experience. This is an ideal opportunity for someone who enjoys varied work, collaborative problem-solving and making a visible difference to students in a dynamic, internationally focused environment. A flexible, adaptable and proactive approach is vital given the blended/distance learning nature of the programmes that are delivered and the challenges that can arise as a result. Based within the MBA & Global Programmes team at Alliance Manchester Business School, the role operates in a collaborative environment supporting the delivery of flexible, blended and distance-learning programmes. Within the assessment and progression team, you will assist with the team across the Global, Full Time and Financial management programmes. This involves working closely with colleagues in programme managed & delivery, student support, scheduling and academic teams. The post helps coordinate consistent and responsive support across the student journey. As part of the wider University of Manchester, the role contributes to shared standards, policies and continuous improvement, connecting School-level activity with the University’s commitment to an inclusive, high- quality student experience. What you will get in return: Fantastic market leading Pension scheme Excellent employee health and wellbeing services including an Employee Assistance Programme Exceptional starting annual leave entitlement, plus bank holidays Additional paid closure over the Christmas period Local and national discounts at a range of major retailers As an equal opportunities employer we welcome applicants from all sections of the community regardless of age, sex, gender (or gender identity), ethnicity, disability, sexual orientation and transgender status. All appointments are made on merit. Our University is positive about flexible working – you can find out more here Hybrid working arrangements may be considered. Please be aware that due to the number of applications we unfortunately may not able to provide individual feedback on your application. Please note that we are unable to respond to enquiries, accept CVs or applications from Recruitment Agencies. Any recruitment enquiries from recruitment agencies should be directed to People.Recruitment@manchester.ac.uk . Any CV’s submitted by a recruitment agency will be considered a gift. Please note this role is not eligible for sponsorship under the Skilled Worker route of the Points Based System. Candidates will need to be able to demonstrate their right to work in the UK in order to be eligible to take up the post. Enquiries about the vacancy, shortlisting and interviews: Name: Daniel Nelson Email: daniel.nelson-2@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries: Email: People.recruitment@manchester.ac.uk Technical support: Jobtrain: 0161 850 2004 https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home This vacancy will close for applications at midnight on the closing date. Please see the link below for the Further Particulars document which contains the person specification criteria. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46003": {
      "title": "Regular Giving Officer",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Regular Giving Officer Cookie Policy By clicking \"Allow Cookies\", you agree to the storing of cookies on your device to analyse site usage, and assist in our marketing efforts. Cookie Policy Decline Allow cookies Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Regular Giving Officer (Redeployees Only) Apply for job Job reference: PSX-032523 Salary: £33,002 to £36,636 per annum, depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Professional Services Location: Hybrid Working on Campus/Remote Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Development and Alumni Hours Per Week: 35 hours per week Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 30/10/2026 Contract Duration: until 31 December 2027 School/Directorate: Division of Development and Alumni Relations Job Description As Regular Giving Officer, you’ll play a key role in delivering one of the UK’s most ambitious mass fundraising programmes. In this role, you’ll bring energy, creativity and a proactive mindset to help grow our programme. You’ll work closely with the Head of Regular Giving and colleagues across the Division to develop and implement new ideas, respond to emerging opportunities, and ensure our fundraising is targeted, data-driven and impactful. You’ll create and deliver tailored supporter journeys, using segmentation, training and systems to ensure every donor receives a personalised experience that reflects their relationship with the University. You’ll also play a key part in ensuring our activity is compliant with fundraising legislation and best practice, and that we’re using our systems and data in the most effective way. This is a hands-on role with real scope to make a difference. You’ll be involved in planning and delivering campaigns, analysing performance, and reporting on results to senior colleagues. You’ll need to be flexible – including occasional evening and weekend work – and confident working across multiple channels to meet the needs of the programme. With a supportive team, a compelling case for support, and a programme that’s growing in reach and ambition, this is a fantastic opportunity for a detail-oriented, goal-driven fundraiser. What you will get in return: Fantastic market leading Pension scheme Excellent employee health and wellbeing services including an Employee Assistance Programme Exceptional starting annual leave entitlement, plus bank holidays Additional paid closure over the Christmas period Local and national discounts at a range of major retailers As an equal opportunities employer we welcome applicants from all sections of the community regardless of age, sex, gender (or gender identity), ethnicity, disability, sexual orientation and transgender status. All appointments are made on merit. Our University is positive about flexible working – you can find out more here Hybrid working arrangements may be considered. Please be aware that due to the number of applications we unfortunately may not able to provide individual feedback on your application. Please note that we are unable to respond to enquiries, accept CVs or applications from Recruitment Agencies. Any recruitment enquiries from recruitment agencies should be directed to recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Any CV’s submitted by a recruitment agency will be considered a gift. Enquiries about the vacancy, shortlisting and interviews: Name: Robyn Masson Email: robyn.masson@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries: Email: recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical support: 0161 850 2004 https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home This vacancy will close for applications at midnight on the closing date. Please see the link below for the Further Particulars document which contains the person specification criteria. Supporting Documents save_alt PSX-032523 Regular Giving Officer FPs (PDF, KB) Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46165": {
      "title": "Sport Facilities Officer",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Sport Facilities Officer Cookie Policy By clicking \"Allow Cookies\", you agree to the storing of cookies on your device to analyse site usage, and assist in our marketing efforts. Cookie Policy Decline Allow cookies Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Sport Facilities Officer Apply for job Job reference: PSX-032506 Salary: £37,694 to £46,049 per annum depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Professional Services Location: Manchester Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Residential and Sport Services Division Hours Per Week: 35 Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 02/11/2026 Contract Duration: 12 months School/Directorate: Directorate for the Student Experience Job Description Interview Date: Thursday 12th November. The University of Manchester has over 16,000 students and staff taking part in organised sport and physical activity through the University. The University recognises the importance of an active lifestyle in both the experiences that these opportunities provide our audiences as well as the positive impact that they have on their wellbeing. Delivery of our sport and physical activity strategy is through UoM Sport which is based across our campuses primarily in Fallowfield at the Armitage Sports Centre and in Manchester University Students’ Union on Oxford Road. Our activities take place across the city utilising a range of city facilities in supporting our community being active and promoting physical activity opportunities through our facilities and programmes. The University is therefore looking to appoint a Sport Facilities Officer to manage the work of the Facilities Team and create strategies that aim to increase our physical activity in our university sport facilities, enhance the student experience through our facilities team and create long lasting partnerships both internally and externally which help embed UoM Sport Facilities at the heart of our student, staff and wider community. The successful candidate will take responsibility for overseeing our sport facilities as well as our Active Manchester offer, identifying gaps in our provision and delivering new ideas and initiatives to meet our key performance indicators along with key colleagues from across the team. The role holder will also be a keen team player, with the ability to both supervise staff as well as identify and build relationships with internal and external stakeholders to ensure we provide a full pathway of opportunities to our students, staff and local community where relevant. Specifically, the role holder will also be required to oversee the delivery of regional and national training camps from our facilities as well as supporting our internal offer to students and staff at the university. We are therefore looking for an enthusiastic and engaging individual to join UoM Sport to support our students and staff being more physically active in our programmes and facilities. The University of Manchester values a diverse workforce and welcomes applications from all sections of the community. (Grades 6-9) Our benefits include: • Generous employer contribution pension • 29 days annual leave plus bank holidays, along with Christmas closure • Ride to work and EV car scheme available For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Andrew Miller, Business Operations Manager Email: andrew.miller@manchester.ac.uk Tel: 0161 529 4203 General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Supporting Documents save_alt PSX-032506 Sport Facilities Officer FP's (PDF, KB) Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46221": {
      "title": "Whitworth Curatorial Fellow",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Whitworth Curatorial Fellow Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Whitworth Curatorial Fellow Apply for job Job reference: CIX-032561 Salary: £27,319 per annum Faculty/Organisational Unit: Cultural Institutions Location: Manchester Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Whitworth Art Gallery Administration Hours Per Week: 35 hours per week Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 01/11/2026 Contract Duration: 18 months School/Directorate: Whitworth Art Gallery Job Description We are looking for an individual with a genuine interest in the visual arts, a willingness to learn and get involved in a variety of activities, and a desire to develop a long-term career as a curator. The Whitworth Curatorial Fellow is supported by the Frieze x Deutsche Bank Emerging Curators Fellowship. This is a paid traineeship, which aims to widen access to curatorial careers in the visual arts and support the professional development of an emerging curator. We particularly encourage applications from people from Global Majority backgrounds, who remain underrepresented in the UK cultural sector, alongside others from backgrounds underrepresented in museums and galleries. A key focus of the role will be the Whitworth's Musgrave Kinley Outsider Art Collection, the only significant collection of its kind held by a UK institution. Comprising more than 1,200 works by over 120 artists from Europe, the USA and beyond, it includes works by artists who developed their practices outside formal art education and gallery structures, including neurodivergent and working-class artists. The collection brings together practices historically described as 'Outsider Art'. Building on previous research into the collection, the Fellow will develop knowledge of its artists and histories and explore questions around terminology, representation and community engagement. They will contribute to research, interpretation and a planned exhibition, considering how artists' voices and perspectives can inform the ways their work is documented, displayed and shared with audiences. The Fellow will also gain wider curatorial experience within the Whitworth's Collections and Exhibitions Team, working across its historic, modern and contemporary art, textiles and wallpaper collections. They will have opportunities to develop practical curatorial skills and work with colleagues across the gallery's values-led and innovative transcultural programme. The Whitworth Curatorial Fellow traineeship is open to applicants who are UK nationals or have the right to work in the UK. This is a fixed-term post of 18 months, and the University is unable to offer visa sponsorship for this role. The role is open to applicants based in Northern England (Cheshire, Cumbria, County Durham, East Yorkshire, Greater Manchester, Lancashire, Merseyside, North Lincolnshire and North East Lincolnshire, Northumberland, North Yorkshire, South Yorkshire, Tyne and Wear and West Yorkshire). What you will get in return: Fantastic market leading Pension scheme Excellent employee health and wellbeing services including an Employee Assistance Programme Exceptional starting annual leave entitlement, plus bank holidays Additional paid closure over the Christmas period Local and national discounts at a range of major retailers As an equal opportunities employer we welcome applicants from all sections of the community regardless of age, sex, gender (or gender identity), ethnicity, disability, sexual orientation and transgender status. All appointments are made on merit. Our University is positive about flexible working – you can find out more here Hybrid working arrangements may be considered. Please be aware that due to the number of applications we unfortunately may not able to provide individual feedback on your application. Please note that we are unable to respond to enquiries, accept CVs or applications from Recruitment Agencies. Any recruitment enquiries from recruitment agencies should be directed to People.Recruitment@manchester.ac.uk . Any CV’s submitted by a recruitment agency will be considered a gift. Please note this role is not eligible for sponsorship under the Skilled Worker route of the Points Based System. Candidates will need to be able to demonstrate their right to work in the UK in order to be eligible to take up the post. Enquiries about the vacancy, shortlisting and interviews: Name: Christo Kefalas Email: christo.kefalas@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries: Email: People.recruitment@manchester.ac.uk Technical support: Jobtrain: 0161 850 2004 https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home This vacancy will close for applications at midnight on the closing date. Please see the link below for the Further Particulars document which contains the person specification criteria. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46098": {
      "title": "Scheduling Manager (Interim)",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Scheduling Manager (Interim) Cookie Policy By clicking \"Allow Cookies\", you agree to the storing of cookies on your device to analyse site usage, and assist in our marketing efforts. Cookie Policy Decline Allow cookies Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Scheduling Manager (Interim) Apply for job Job reference: PSX-032445 Salary: £47,389 - £58,225 per annum depending on experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Professional Services Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Student and Academic Services Hours Per Week: Full time (1 FTE) Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 29/10/2026 Contract Duration: Fixed term for 12 months School/Directorate: Directorate for the Student Experience Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description We are seeking a motivated and collaborative individual to join our team as a Scheduling Manager. You will provide leadership and direction to the Scheduling and Assessment team, ensuring the accurate and timely production of timetables for scheduled learning opportunities and centrally timetabled summative assessment. You will also provide advice, support and guidance to colleagues across the University and strategic advice to senior leaders. You will be responsible for: Lead teams to deliver consistent, sustainable teaching and assessment scheduling, ensuring excellent services to students across the organisation; in line with agreed standards and in response to changing needs. Lead on and oversee the provision of advice and guidance to University colleagues, including PS/Faculty/Schools-based colleagues, on key issues concerning teaching and assessment scheduling including external compliance, sector expertise and sharing best practice. Lead the implementation, evaluation and monitoring of institution-wide strategy and policy at a local level, and lead the development of local procedures as necessary. Lead, organise, motivate and direct the work of the team, delegating responsibility appropriately to ensure objectives are met efficiently and operating effectively. To be a member of the management team, contributing to operational planning, visions and values and providing effective leadership. Work with senior management and key stakeholders to maintain and review a scheduling strategy across the university, covering scheduling and assessment delivery that contributes to the University's strategic vision. We welcome candidates who bring diverse perspectives, experiences, and approaches to their work. About You We encourage applications from individuals with a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. You should demonstrate: Essential Criteria Significant experience, knowledge and understanding of strategic and operational leadership for teaching and assessment scheduling. Extensive specialist knowledge and experience within timetabling and in the use and management of scheduling software within the HE sector. Proven record in effectively leading and managing a team with evidence of setting and achieving clear objectives and performance goals. Able to present complex, and often specialised, material in a clear and concise manner, with the ability to communicate complex, technical approaches to a ‘lay’ audience. Excellent communication and interpersonal skills to engage and influence colleagues and work collaboratively across organisational boundaries in the pursuit of university strategies and their implementation at a local level. We value transferable skills and real-world experience as much as formal qualifications. Our benefits include: • Generous employer contribution pension • 29 days annual leave plus bank holidays, along with Christmas closure • Ride to work and EV car scheme available For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Chris Mason Email Address: chris.mason@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Supporting Documents save_alt TLD_G7-Scheduling-Manager-Interim_OCT26 (PDF, KB) Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46041": {
      "title": "Senior Technician (Civil and Mechanical Engineering)",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Senior Technician (Civil and Mechanical Engineering) Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Senior Technician (Civil and Mechanical Engineering) Apply for job Job reference: SAE-032382 Salary: £33,002 - £36,636 per annum depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Science and Engineering Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Permanent Division/Team: FSE Technical Services Hours Per Week: 35 hours Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 28/10/2026 Contract Duration: Permanent School/Directorate: Faculty Office - Science and Engineering Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description Overall Purpose The Senior Technician (Civil and Mechanical Engineering) will provide expert technical support across our Fluids, Heavy Structures, Dynamics, Impact/Ballistics, Geotechnical and Concrete Processing laboratories. Using their technical knowledge and problem-solving skills, they will support safe, efficient and high-quality teaching, research and testing activities. Working collaboratively with colleagues and stakeholders, they will help resolve technical challenges, maintain and develop facilities and equipment, and identify opportunities to improve our services. This is an opportunity to make a real difference across a diverse range of specialist engineering environments while sharing expertise and helping the wider team deliver its goals. About the Team As Technical Staff, you are part of Professional Services. Together, all technical staff who work within the Faculty of Science and Engineering (FSE) form FSE Technical Operations. FSE Technical Operations is organised into five key functional areas: • Research • Teaching • Infrastructure & Facilities • Workshops • Computing, Robotics & AI Within FSE Technical Operations, you will belong to the research area. Research - The Research Technical Teams use their specialist skills and experience to support academic and industrial research across the Faculty. As the largest part of our technical workforce, they work across a diverse range of disciplines, projects and facilities, providing expert technical input, maintaining research infrastructure, and enabling high‑quality experimental work. They train researchers and students in the latest techniques and methods, contribute to taught activities where specialist expertise is required, and play a central role in ensuring that complex research environments operate safely, effectively and to professional standards. Within the research area, you will be part of the Design, Fabrication and Testing (DF&T) Core Facility Team. Design, Fabrication and Testing Team The Design, Fabrication and Testing (DFT) Team is a multidisciplinary technical team within the Faculty of Science and Engineering’s Research Technical Operations community. Made up of 14 technical professionals, the team supports world-class research, teaching and industry engagement across a broad range of engineering disciplines. It manages and operates specialist facilities in hydrodynamics, concrete processing, heavy structures, geotechnical engineering, composites, static and dynamic mechanical testing, dynamics and high energy impact testing. The team provides practical technical support to academics, researchers, students and external partners. This includes developing experimental programmes, designing and manufacturing test specimens and campaigns, operating specialist equipment, collecting data and maintaining research infrastructure. DFT team members are encouraged to share knowledge, learn from one another and contribute their individual skills and experience. Each person strengthens the team, whether through specialist expertise, day-to-day operational support, developing new capabilities or mentoring colleagues. Together, these contributions help the DFT Team deliver a high quality, responsive and professional technical service. Our benefits include: (Grades 1-5) Generous employer pension contribution Christmas closure across all departments outside of annual leave and bank holidays Purple Place employee discount platform For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Jacob Skelly (Technical Specialist – Civil & Mechanical Engineering) Email Address: jacob.skelly@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46023": {
      "title": "Drama Technician",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Drama Technician Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Drama Technician Apply for job Job reference: HUM-032398 Salary: UoM Grade 4  £29,588 - £32,080 per annum depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Humanities Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Permanent Division/Team: Humanities Dean's Office Hours Per Week: 35 hours per week (1 FTE) Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 27/10/2026 Contract Duration: Permanent School/Directorate: Faculty Office - Humanities Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description We are seeking a motivated and collaborative individual to join our team as a Drama Technician. This role offers an exciting opportunity to contribute to teaching, performance and research within the Martin Harris Centre for Music, Drama and Film. Working as part of the Humanities Technical Operations Team, the post holder will support the safe and efficient operation of specialist facilities, equipment and performance technologies. The role will contribute to the smooth running, maintenance and organisation of the Centre’s technical spaces, while providing technical support and training to staff, students, visitors and external users. You will be responsible for: - Providing technical support for teaching, performance and research and across the Martin Harris Centre. - Setting up, using and supporting a range of performance technologies, equipment and software for teaching, performances, conferences and events. - Programming lighting, sound and visuals using appropriate technologies and platforms for theatre performances and events. - Supporting the safe and efficient operation of theatres, teaching rooms, studios and associated technical areas. - Contributing to the maintenance, repair, inspection, storage and cataloguing of technical equipment, consumables and materials. We welcome candidates who bring diverse perspectives, experiences, and approaches to their work. About You We encourage applications from individuals with a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. You should demonstrate: You should demonstrate: - Relevant technical theatre qualification, equivalent vocational training, or practical experience in a relevant technical environment. - Experience of providing technical support for theatre performance, rehearsal, or event activity. - Experience of rigging and programming lighting for theatre and events, ideally using ETC Eos. - Experience of rigging and programming sound and visuals for theatre and events, ideally using Qlab. - Ability to identify and solve technical problems, including equipment fault diagnosis, troubleshooting and basic maintenance. - Experience of training, inducting or demonstrating safe equipment use, processes or procedures to staff, students, visitors or external users. - Experience of supporting users with different levels of technical knowledge, providing routine advice and escalating issues where necessary. - Practical technical theatre skills, including confidence with lighting, sound, video, recording or related performance technologies. - Ability to work effectively as part of a technical team and collaborate with colleagues across the University. - Willingness to continue developing technical knowledge and to share knowledge with others. We value transferable skills and real-world experience as much as formal qualifications. Our benefits include: - Generous employer pension contribution - Christmas closure across all departments outside of annual leave and bank holidays - Purple Place employee discount platform For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits. You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working arrangements. We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Tom Pearce Email Address: tom.pearce@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support Jobtrain Client Support Hub This role is not eligible for Skilled Worker visa sponsorship. Applicants must demonstrate the right to work in the UK. Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=45678": {
      "title": "Senior Sport Centre Assistant",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Senior Sport Centre Assistant Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Senior Sport Centre Assistant Apply for job Job reference: PSX-032482 Salary: £27,319 - £28,778 Faculty/Organisational Unit: Professional Services Location: Manchester Employment type: Permanent Division/Team: Campus Life Hours Per Week: 35 1 FTE Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 27/10/2026 Contract Duration: N/A School/Directorate: Directorate for the Student Experience Job Description We are seeking a motivated and collaborative individual to join UoM Sport as a Senior Sport Centre Assistant. You will support the safe and efficient operation of the Armitage Sports Centre, helping to ensure that indoor and outdoor sports facilities are clean, safe and fit for purpose. You will supervise colleagues on site, respond to operational priorities and provide excellent customer service in a busy, multi-purpose sport and leisure environment. You will be responsible for: Undertake key holding responsibilities ensuring the safe and efficient operation of the sports facilities in line with centre opening times and wider operational need through regular site walks. To supervise the work of sport operations colleagues on site and delegate tasks accordingly in line with daily operational priorities. To undertake & delegate maintenance tasks relating to indoor and outdoor sports facilities and equipment to ensure they are fit for purpose and to agreed standards. To undertake regular facility checks, monitoring all areas and reporting any faults or issues on the facility management system. Provide excellent customer service at all times. We welcome candidates who bring diverse perspectives, experiences, and approaches to their work. About You We encourage applications from individuals with a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. You should demonstrate: Essential Criteria A thorough understanding of health and safety, through appropriate training and practice, within sport and leisure facilities. Proven experience of working in and providing services at indoor and outdoor sport facilities and venues Experience of supervising and leading teams in a sport related environment. Good communication skills, demonstrating an ability to deal effectively with a wide range of people. Good organisational skills, and able to prioritise work in a busy environment. We value transferable skills and real-world experience as much as formal qualifications. Our benefits include: Generous employer pension contribution Christmas closure across all departments outside of annual leave and bank holidays Purple Place employee discount platform For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . For informal enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews: Name: Andy Brown Email: andy.brown-3@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home This role is not eligible for Skilled Worker visa sponsorship. Applicants must demonstrate the right to work in the UK. Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46217": {
      "title": "Student Service, Support and Development Manager",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Student Service, Support and Development Manager Cookie Policy By clicking \"Allow Cookies\", you agree to the storing of cookies on your device to analyse site usage, and assist in our marketing efforts. Cookie Policy Decline Allow cookies Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Student Service, Support and Development Manager Apply for job Job reference: BMH-032558 Salary: UoM Grade 7 £47,389 - £58,225 per annum depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Biology, Medicine Health Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Permanent Division/Team: Not specified Hours Per Week: Full time (1 FTE) Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 27/10/2026 Contract Duration: Permanent School/Directorate: School of Biological Sciences Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description We are seeking a motivated and collaborative individual to join our team as a Student Service, Support and Development Manager within the School of Biological Sciences, Faculty of Biology, Medicine and Health. This is an exciting opportunity to lead the school-based student services team, ensuring students receive consistent, high-quality information, advice and welfare support throughout their journey. Working closely with academic, Professional Services and Directorate for the Student Experience colleagues, you will support a joined-up student experience and contribute to wider University priorities around service excellence, inclusion, student success and continuous improvement. You will be responsible for: - To lead teams to deliver consistent, sustainable support and expertise, ensuring excellent services to students across organisation; in line with agreed standards and in response to changing needs. - To lead, organise, motivate and direct the work of the team, delegating responsibility to the appropriate level, ensuring that its objectives are met and it is operating efficiently and effectively. - To effectively manage the Student Service, Support and Development Team, including management of the school-based hub. - To develop, manage and have responsibility for the student communication and engagement framework within the School and ensure suitable co-ordination and execution across all areas of the School. - To manage the development and delivery of appropriate welcome, induction, graduation and related activities for all students across the departments. We welcome candidates who bring diverse perspectives, experiences, and approaches to their work. About You We encourage applications from individuals with a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. You should demonstrate: Essential Criteria: - Significant experience, knowledge and understanding of information, advice and student support. - Proven record in effectively leading and managing a team with evidence of setting and achieving clear objectives and performance goals. - Evidence of experience of working in a customer focused environment and delivering exceptional customer service to stakeholders. - Excellent communication and interpersonal skills to engage and influence colleagues and work collaboratively across organisational boundaries in the pursuit of University strategies and their implementation at a local level. Excellent analytical and problem-solving skills. Desirable Criteria: - Knowledge and understanding of how IT systems can support and enhance service delivery, with knowledge and experience of IT systems relevant to the role. - Experience of policy development and implementation. - Capacity to think in strategic terms and a willingness to exercise leadership including leading and managing significant cultural and organisational change. - Strong negotiation and relationship building skills, the ability to influence others outside the direct sphere of control, and experience of matrix working. - The ability to represent the University externally. We value transferable skills and real-world experience as much as formal qualifications. Our benefits include: • Generous employer contribution pension • 29 days annual leave plus bank holidays, along with Christmas closure • Ride to work and EV car scheme available For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits. You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working arrangements online. We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Kerry Mycock, Head of Teaching and Learning (SBS) PA: Lucy Woodburn Email Address: SBSSchooloffice@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support People.Recruitment@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support Jobtrain Client Support Hub Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Supporting Documents save_alt BMH-032558 - Student Services Support and Development Manager - FPs (PDF, KB) Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46185": {
      "title": "Admissions Co-ordinator (Functional)",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Admissions Co-ordinator (Functional) Cookie Policy By clicking \"Allow Cookies\", you agree to the storing of cookies on your device to analyse site usage, and assist in our marketing efforts. Cookie Policy Decline Allow cookies Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Admissions Co-ordinator (Functional) Apply for job Job reference: PSX-032537 Salary: £33,002 to £36,636 per annum, depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Professional Services Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Permanent Division/Team: Student and Academic Services Hours Per Week: 35 hours per week Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 26/10/2026 Contract Duration: Permanent School/Directorate: Directorate for the Student Experience Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description The Student Admissions Team in the Directorate for the Student Experience supports admissions colleagues in the Faculties and Doctoral Academies to process applications fairly, consistently and transparently. The team is responsible for the process, systems, policy, governance and training that underpins the admissions service. Overall Purpose of the Job To co-ordinate the delivery of functional support for the technologies used for admissions processing and supervise the work of the Admissions Assistants in the delivery of first line support to admissions staff across the University. To support colleagues to embed locally and University-wide a culture of excellent service provision and continuous improvement, placing the student experience at the heart of what we do, with support for a single PS team working flexibly across organisational boundaries. To support senior colleagues in the delivery of technical/functional support for admissions staff. What you will get in return: Fantastic market leading Pension scheme Excellent employee health and wellbeing services including an Employee Assistance Programme Exceptional starting annual leave entitlement, plus bank holidays Additional paid closure over the Christmas period Local and national discounts at a range of major retailers As an equal opportunities employer we welcome applicants from all sections of the community regardless of age, sex, gender (or gender identity), ethnicity, disability, sexual orientation and transgender status. All appointments are made on merit. Our University is positive about flexible working – you can find out more here Hybrid working arrangements may be considered. Please be aware that due to the number of applications we unfortunately may not able to provide individual feedback on your application. Please note that we are unable to respond to enquiries, accept CVs or applications from Recruitment Agencies. Any recruitment enquiries from recruitment agencies should be directed to recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Any CV’s submitted by a recruitment agency will be considered a gift. Enquiries about the vacancy, shortlisting and interviews: Name: Suzi Wright Email: suzi.wright@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries: Email: recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical support: 0161 850 2004 https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home This vacancy will close for applications at midnight on the closing date. Please see the link below for the Further Particulars document which contains the person specification criteria. Supporting Documents save_alt PSX-032537 Admissions Co-ordinator FPs (PDF, KB) Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46148": {
      "title": "Multimedia Content Producer",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Multimedia Content Producer Cookie Policy By clicking \"Allow Cookies\", you agree to the storing of cookies on your device to analyse site usage, and assist in our marketing efforts. Cookie Policy Decline Allow cookies Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Multimedia Content Producer Apply for job Job reference: PSX-032484 Salary: £37,694 to £46,049 per annum, depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Professional Services Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Permanent Division/Team: Not specified Hours Per Week: Full Time Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 26/10/2026 Contract Duration: Permanent School/Directorate: Directorate of Comms, Marketing & Student Recruitment Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description Working closely with the President and Vice-Chancellor's Office, CMSR internal communications teams, and senior stakeholders across the University, the postholder will develop engaging multimedia content that supports the University's strategic priorities and major initiatives. They will translate complex messages into accessible and compelling stories that build understanding, trust and engagement with internal and external audiences. A key focus of the role is supporting leadership communications, executive engagement and strategic campaigns through the creation of high-quality content across video, photography, podcasts, and digital channels. Creative is an in-house creative team within the Directorate of Communications, Marketing and Student Recruitment (CMSR), delivering creative solutions that support the University's Strategy to 2035. Working closely with colleagues across CMSR and the wider University, the team creates impactful design, content and campaigns that strengthen the University's reputation, brand and engagement with key audiences. Alongside your application, please provide a link in your supporting information to a digital reel showcasing your work. What you will get in return: Fantastic market leading Pension scheme Excellent employee health and wellbeing services including an Employee Assistance Programme Exceptional starting annual leave entitlement, plus bank holidays Additional paid closure over the Christmas period Local and national discounts at a range of major retailers As an equal opportunities employer we welcome applicants from all sections of the community regardless of age, sex, gender (or gender identity), ethnicity, disability, sexual orientation and transgender status. All appointments are made on merit. Our University is positive about flexible working – you can find out more here Hybrid working arrangements may be considered. Please be aware that due to the number of applications we unfortunately may not able to provide individual feedback on your application. Please note that we are unable to respond to enquiries, accept CVs or applications from Recruitment Agencies. Any recruitment enquiries from recruitment agencies should be directed to recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Any CV’s submitted by a recruitment agency will be considered a gift. Enquiries about the vacancy, shortlisting and interviews: Name: Kirsty Smith Email: kirsty.smith@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries: Email: recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical support: 0161 850 2004 https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home This vacancy will close for applications at midnight on the closing date. Please see the link below for the Further Particulars document which contains the person specification criteria. Supporting Documents save_alt PSX-032484 Multimedia Content Producer FPs (PDF, KB) Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=45989": {
      "title": "Domestic Assistant",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Domestic Assistant Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Domestic Assistant Apply for job Job reference: PSX-032328 Salary: £25,249 - £25,804 pro rata per annum depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Professional Services Location: Manchester Employment type: Permanent Division/Team: Campus Life Hours Per Week: 14 hours per week (part time) Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 27/10/2026 Contract Duration: Permanent School/Directorate: Directorate for the Student Experience Job Description The University of Manchester has over 16,000 students and staff taking part in organised sport and physical activity through the University. The University recognises the importance of an active lifestyle in both the experiences that these opportunities provide our audiences as well as the positive impact that they have on their wellbeing. Delivery of our sport and physical activity strategy is through UoM Sport which is based across our campuses primarily in Fallowfield at the Armitage Sports Centre and in Manchester University Students’ Union on Oxford Road. Our activities take place across the city utilising a range of city facilities in supporting our community being active and promoting physical activity opportunities through our facilities and programmes. As part of the Sport operations team and in liaison with the Sport Coordinator (Operations), the successful candidate will be responsible for the day-to-day standards of the facility and a high-quality experience for all users. The university therefore is looking to appoint a highly motivated and enthusiastic person to work as a Domestic Assistant. You will be responsible for the following: To ensure that all facility areas are maintained in a safe, clean, and hygienic condition and to agreed cleaning standards To carry out a range of domestic tasks within routine and established processes Cleaning of public areas e.g. corridors, kitchens, bath/shower rooms, toilets, changing rooms, stairs, sport activity rooms, conference areas Safe operation of electrical powered and hand-held tools and cleaning equipment in a standardised way and where appropriate to achieve the required tasks as listed above, following standard safety and hygiene procedures as agreed To monitor any damage to premises, equipment, fittings or furnishings, take appropriate action to assist in preventing accident or health hazards, and to report any such issues through the established reporting processes We welcome candidates who bring diverse perspectives, experiences, and approaches to their work. About You We encourage applications from individuals with a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. You should demonstrate the following: Essential Criteria: Experience and knowledge of domestic processes and skills to enable, with training, the role to be carried out successfully to agreed standards A sound basic knowledge of health and safety at work procedures Good interpersonal skills Flexible and adaptable towards a busy and pressured work environment Be self-motivated Desirable Criteria: A good understanding of health and safety issues from appropriate training and practice – including COSHH, Manual Handling and Personal Protective Equipment We value transferable skills and real-world experience as much as formal qualifications. Our benefits include: Generous employer pension contribution Christmas closure across all departments outside of annual leave and bank holidays Purple Place employee discount platform For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Luke Skelding Email Address: Luke.Skelding@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home This role is not eligible for Skilled Worker visa sponsorship. Applicants must demonstrate the right to work in the UK Applications close at midnight on the closing date Further particulars (with person specification) linked below Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=45979": {
      "title": "Digital Content Officer",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Digital Content Officer Cookie Policy By clicking \"Allow Cookies\", you agree to the storing of cookies on your device to analyse site usage, and assist in our marketing efforts. Cookie Policy Decline Allow cookies Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Digital Content Officer Apply for job Job reference: CIX-032350 Salary: £33,002 to £36,636 pro rata per annum, depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Cultural Institutions Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Permanent Division/Team: Whitworth Art Gallery Administration Hours Per Week: 21 hours per week Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 04/11/2026 Contract Duration: Permanent School/Directorate: Whitworth Art Gallery Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description The Whitworth is part of The University of Manchester and one of the UK’s leading university art galleries, connecting art, people and ideas across Manchester and beyond. The Digital Content Officer will plan, create and deliver high-quality digital content across the Whitworth’s channels, with a particular focus on social media, video, photography, website and newsletters. You will create engaging, accessible and visually compelling content that brings the Whitworth’s exhibitions, collections, public programmes and civic work to life. Working closely with colleagues across Curatorial, Civic Engagement and Education, and Audiences and Communications, you will identify stories, capture rich content, and translate them into platform-ready outputs that build connection with audiences locally, nationally and internationally. This is a practical role suited to a confident content creator who is comfortable filming, editing and producing work independently, while contributing to agreed communications plans. Applicants need to include their cover letter and a link to their Portfolio when uploading your CV. What you will get in return: Fantastic market leading Pension scheme Excellent employee health and wellbeing services including an Employee Assistance Programme Exceptional starting annual leave entitlement, plus bank holidays Additional paid closure over the Christmas period Local and national discounts at a range of major retailers As an equal opportunities employer we welcome applicants from all sections of the community regardless of age, sex, gender (or gender identity), ethnicity, disability, sexual orientation and transgender status. All appointments are made on merit. Our University is positive about flexible working – you can find out more here Hybrid working arrangements may be considered. Please be aware that due to the number of applications we unfortunately may not able to provide individual feedback on your application. Please note that we are unable to respond to enquiries, accept CVs or applications from Recruitment Agencies. Any recruitment enquiries from recruitment agencies should be directed to recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Any CV’s submitted by a recruitment agency will be considered a gift. General enquiries: Email: recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical support: 0161 850 2004 https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home This vacancy will close for applications at midnight on the closing date. Please see the link below for the Further Particulars document which contains the person specification criteria. Supporting Documents save_alt CIX-032350 Digital Content Officer FPs (PDF, KB) Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46009": {
      "title": "Volunteer Coordinator",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Volunteer Coordinator Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Volunteer Coordinator Apply for job Job reference: CIX-032358 Salary: £29,588 - £32,080 pro rata, per annum, depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Cultural Institutions Location: Jodrell Bank, Cheshire Employment type: Permanent Division/Team: JBCE Education Hours Per Week: 17.5 hours per week (0.5 FTE) Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 29/10/2026 Contract Duration: permanent School/Directorate: Jodrell Bank Centre for Engagement Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description Join us at an exciting time for Jodrell Bank Centre for Engagement, part of The University of Manchester, as we continue to grow our work as a world-leading destination for science, heritage, culture and public engagement. Home to the iconic Lovell Telescope, a UNESCO World Heritage Site, the award-winning First Light Pavilion and with a vibrant programme of education, special events and visitor experiences, Jodrell Bank welcomes thousands of visitors and learners each year. The Volunteer Programme was launched through our NLHF-funded First Light Project and now supports a cohort of more than 60 individuals who contribute to activities across the site, enriching the visitor experience. As Volunteer Coordinator, you will play a key role in developing, coordinating and supporting this welcoming, inclusive and rewarding volunteer programme. You will help volunteers contribute meaningfully across a range of activities, including visitor welcome, hosting, gardens, public engagement, events and work experience placements, while ensuring they feel valued, informed and supported throughout their volunteering journey. This is a permanent, part-time role, working 0.5 FTE, equivalent to 17.5 hours per week. The role is based at Jodrell Bank and will involve some evening and weekend work to support the Centre’s visitor, events and engagement programme. Jodrell Bank does not currently have a public transport link, so the post-holder will need access to personal transport. This is primarily an on-site role with occasional flexibility for home working. About the Role We are seeking a proactive, highly organised and people-focused Volunteer Coordinator to strengthen and develop volunteer engagement across Jodrell Bank Centre for Engagement. You will act as the main point of contact for volunteers, supporting them from recruitment and induction through to scheduling, training, supervision, ongoing communication and recognition. You will work closely with colleagues across Visitor Services, Public Programming, Education, Engagement and Operations to identify volunteer opportunities, coordinate volunteer involvement and ensure volunteers are effectively integrated into the work of the Centre. The role will also lead on the Work Experience Programme, working closely with the Engagement Team to coordinate placements, schedules and administration. You will help ensure opportunities are attractive, accessible and well managed, supporting a positive experience for volunteers, students, colleagues and visitors. Jodrell Bank is an active research, heritage and visitor site, and the role requires sensitivity to the unique nature of the environment, including the need to balance volunteering activity with conservation, public engagement and the operational requirements of a radio astronomy site. What you will be doing: • Develop and maintain volunteering opportunities across Jodrell Bank Centre for Engagement, working with colleagues to identify meaningful roles that support visitor welcome, engagement activities, events, gardens, work experience and wider organisational priorities. • Produce and maintain clear volunteer role descriptions, agreements, schedules and supporting information, ensuring expectations are well understood by volunteers and colleagues. • Promote and recruit volunteers through a range of channels, including volunteer databases, email, community networks and social media. • Interview prospective volunteers and complete the necessary administration relating to recruitment, appointment and induction. • Coordinate volunteer and work experience schedules, ensuring appropriate cover for daily activities, special events and large-scale programmes. • Act as the main point of contact for volunteers, providing ongoing support, guidance, communication and encouragement. • Ensure volunteers are fully inducted, appropriately trained and confident in carrying out their roles. • Assess volunteer training needs and work with colleagues to arrange appropriate training, briefings and development opportunities. • Maintain accurate volunteer records, assignments, availability and skills information, ensuring Team Kinetic is kept up to date as the volunteer management system. • Coordinate work experience placements, including advertising opportunities, managing applications and producing schedules in liaison with the Education and Engagement Team. • Support volunteers to feel integrated, valued and recognised as part of the Jodrell Bank team. • Administer volunteer travel expenses and support practical arrangements, including dedicated spaces for volunteer rest and refreshment. • Develop relationships with partner organisations and local community groups to widen access to volunteering opportunities. • Support the development of inclusive, accessible volunteering and work experience programmes that reflect the diversity of the communities Jodrell Bank serves. • Collect feedback from volunteers, conduct exit interviews and use insight to support continual improvement. What We’re Looking For • Experience of working with volunteers or community groups and/or personal experience as a volunteer. • Experience of working in a heritage site, science centre, museum, visitor attraction, cultural venue or comparable public-facing environment. • Experience of working with a diverse range of people from local communities. • Experience of recruiting people through a range of channels. • Strong organisational skills, with the ability to prioritise a varied workload, meet deadlines and coordinate people, schedules and activities in a busy environment. • Excellent administrative skills, with the ability to develop and maintain effective systems. • Working knowledge of databases and Microsoft Office, particularly Excel. • A friendly, encouraging and approachable manner, with an ability to build positive relationships with volunteers, colleagues, partners and visitors. • Excellent communication skills, including the ability to listen attentively, respond constructively and communicate clearly with a wide range of people. • The ability to coordinate and work as part of a team, demonstrating flexibility and a willingness to support colleagues. • An interest in or curiosity about the visitor attraction sector, heritage, science and culture. • A strong understanding of why people volunteer and what supports a rewarding volunteer experience. • Awareness of the sensitivities involved in balancing volunteering activities with heritage conservation, visitor experience and the working operation of an active science research facility. • A commitment to equality, diversity, inclusion, wellbeing and the University’s values. It would also be helpful if you have: • Experience of working in partnership with community organisations. • Coaching, supervision or training experience. • A strong understanding of the voluntary sector. • Experience of developing volunteering programmes that support community-focused audience development. Why Work at the University of Manchester? Join a world-class institution that values diversity, equality, enquiry and challenge in the workplace. At Jodrell Bank Centre for Engagement, you will be part of a committed and creative team working at the intersection of science, heritage, culture and public engagement. You will help volunteers contribute to an inspiring place with a unique history, an active research environment and a growing role as one of the University’s cultural institutions. The University of Manchester is committed to providing a supportive and inclusive environment where all employees can thrive. We welcome applications from all backgrounds and look forward to welcoming the next member of our team to Jodrell Bank Centre for Engagement. Our benefits include: • Generous employer pension contribution • Christmas closure across all departments outside of annual leave and bank holidays • Purple Place employee discount platform For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits. You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here. We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy. Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Julia Riley Email: julia.c.riley@manchester,ac,uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars, including the full person specification, are linked below. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=45987": {
      "title": "Principal Architects",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Principal Architects Cookie Policy By clicking \"Allow Cookies\", you agree to the storing of cookies on your device to analyse site usage, and assist in our marketing efforts. Cookie Policy Decline Allow cookies Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Principal Architects Apply for job Job reference: PSX-032327 Salary: £75,000 - £95,987 Faculty/Organisational Unit: Professional Services Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Enterprise and Business Architecture Hours Per Week: 35 Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 25/10/2026 Contract Duration: 24 School/Directorate: Directorate of IT Services Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description Shape the future architecture of one of the UK's leading universities. The University of Manchester is seeking two Principal Architects to help shape and deliver the architectural direction of a major portfolio of change and transformation activity. This is an opportunity to influence enterprise-wide initiatives, contribute to long-term technology strategy and support the delivery of the University's ambitions through effective architecture planning, governance and collaboration. As a senior member of the Enterprise and Business Architecture team, you will work alongside executive sponsors, business leaders, technology teams and delivery partners to ensure strategic objectives are translated into clear architecture roadmaps and achievable change plans. You will contribute to investment discussions, help shape future demand and support informed decision-making across the University. What you'll be doing • Shaping architecture strategy, planning and roadmap development across a major University value stream. • Developing and maintaining architecture blueprints, future-state visions and transition plans that support delivery of the University's strategic goals. • Building effective partnerships with stakeholders and acting as a trusted adviser on architectural, technological and strategic matters. • Working with colleagues across programmes and projects to provide architectural guidance and ensure alignment with enterprise architecture principles, standards and roadmaps. • Identifying opportunities for simplification, reuse and continuous improvement while helping teams navigate risks, dependencies and complexity. • Supporting architecture governance and assurance activities, helping stakeholders understand and navigate key decision-making processes. • Sharing insight on emerging technologies and future opportunities that could enhance teaching, research and professional services. • Coaching, developing and supporting Business Service Architects and Business Analysts aligned to the portfolio. About you We're looking for someone who enjoys bringing people together to solve complex challenges and can balance long-term thinking with practical delivery. You'll be comfortable building relationships across a large organisation and helping colleagues make informed choices in a collaborative environment. You will bring: • Significant experience in enterprise architecture, architecture leadership, technology strategy or a related discipline within a large and complex organisation. • A strong understanding of business architecture, applications, data, integration, infrastructure and security considerations. • Experience working with senior stakeholders and providing clear, evidence-based recommendations to support strategic outcomes. • A track record of developing architecture roadmaps, target states and transformation plans. • Experience of architecture governance, assurance and design decision-making. • Excellent communication skills and the ability to explain complex concepts to both technical and non-technical audiences. • Experience supporting, developing and enabling high-performing teams. Professional certifications such as TOGAF, BCS Architecture, cloud architecture certifications or experience with architecture modelling frameworks such as ArchiMate would be advantageous. Why join Manchester? The University of Manchester is one of the world's leading universities, recognised for its pioneering research, outstanding teaching and global impact. You'll have the opportunity to work on complex, organisation-wide challenges, contributing to the technology landscape that supports our students, staff and researchers for years to come. We recognise that strong candidates may not meet every requirement listed. If you're excited about the opportunity and believe your skills and experience are relevant, we'd encourage you to apply. We welcome applications from people with a variety of career backgrounds and value collaboration, curiosity, continuous learning and different perspectives alongside technical expertise. Our benefits • Generous employer contribution pension • 29 days annual leave plus bank holidays and Christmas closure • Ride to Work and Electric Vehicle schemes • Access to a wide range of learning and development opportunities General enquiries about the role Lewis Burne Talent.attraction@manchester.ac.uk This role is not eligible for Skilled Worker visa sponsorship. Applicants must demonstrate the right to work in the UK. Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Supporting Documents save_alt Principal Architect Candidate Brief (PDF, KB) Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=45875": {
      "title": "Teaching, Learning and Student Experience Assistant (Assessment and Progression)",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Teaching, Learning and Student Experience Assistant (Assessment and Progression) Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Teaching, Learning and Student Experience Assistant (Assessment and Progression) Apply for job Job reference: HUM-032221 Salary: £27,319 - £28,778 per annum depending on experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Humanities Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Permanent Division/Team: Alliance MBS Administration Hours Per Week: 35 hours per week (1 FTE) Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 29/10/2026 Contract Duration: Permanent School/Directorate: Alliance Manchester Business School Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description We are seeking a motivated and collaborative individual to join our team as a Teaching, Learning and Student Experience Assistant (Assessment and Progression) within Alliance Manchester Business School. This role offers an exciting opportunity to provide clerical and administrative support for assessment and progression activity, contributing to the effective delivery of teaching, learning and student administration across the academic cycle. You will be responsible for: Providing administrative support for Teaching, Learning and Student Experience and Assessment and Progression activities. Acting as a first point of contact for routine student administration and assessment-related enquiries, escalating matters where appropriate. Supporting assessment processes including exam paper preparation, marks transfer, checking and publication of student results, bulk processes and tracking documentation. Assisting with Summary Disciplinary Panels, Examination Boards, meetings and committees, including arrangements, papers and minute taking where required. Maintaining accurate student records and documentation across University systems, supporting data quality and service excellence. We welcome candidates who bring diverse perspectives, experiences, and approaches to their work. About You We encourage applications from individuals with a wide range of administrative, customer service and student support experience. You should be organised, accurate and confident working with information, systems and people in a busy service environment, with a strong commitment to confidentiality, inclusion and the student experience. Essential Criteria: Experience of working with administrative processes in a service environment. Good oral and written communication skills, with the ability to provide information and guidance professionally. Strong organisational skills, with the ability to prioritise work and maintain accuracy in a busy environment. Good numeracy, attention to detail and confidence using IT systems to maintain records and documentation. Understanding of confidentiality, data quality, equality, diversity and inclusion, and customer-focused service delivery. Desirable Criteria: Familiarity with higher education student administration, assessment and progression processes. Experience of supporting committees, panels, examination boards or similar meeting activity. Experience of working in a customer service environment. Relevant administrative, customer service or higher education training or qualifications. We value transferable skills and real-world experience as much as formal qualifications. Our benefits include: Generous employer pension contribution Christmas closure across all departments outside of annual leave and bank holidays Purple Place employee discount platform For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Luke Robinson Email Address: luke.robinson-2@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home This role is not eligible for Skilled Worker visa sponsorship. Applicants must demonstrate the right to work in the UK. Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=45194": {
      "title": "Research Fellowship in Human AI-Teaming",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Research Fellowship in Human AI-Teaming Cookie Policy By clicking \"Allow Cookies\", you agree to the storing of cookies on your device to analyse site usage, and assist in our marketing efforts. Cookie Policy Decline Allow cookies Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Research Fellowship in Human AI-Teaming Apply for job Job reference: HUM-031918 Salary: £37,694 - £46,049 per annum depending on experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Humanities Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: SoSS Research Centres Hours Per Week: Full Time (1 FTE) Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 30/10/2026 Contract Duration: Fixed Term for 20 months School/Directorate: School of Social Sciences Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description Applications are invited for a research fellowship focused upon how people and AI can and should work together in a collaborative partnership.  The fellowship is jointly hosted by the University of Manchester, Manchester Metropolitan University and the University of Salford, and supported by the UK Government.  The fellowship is initially through until March 2028, with the possibility of an extension. Applicants are requested to submit a brief research proposal addressing the topic of human-AI collaboration with their application. If successful they will be required to revise this proposal, in consultation with colleagues from UK Government, both to address government priorities and to improve the fit of the proposal with the demands of the National Security context, where advances in AI capability could give significant efficiency gains but must be balanced against a strong regulatory regime. Some of the research will assume a standard academic form and fellows will be expected to pursue publication in relevant academic journals, subject to approval from the government sponsor.  Some may be classified and intended for use by UK Government and their partner organisations only. Fellows will therefore gain experience of two very different research environments and of negotiating the complexities of working between them. The Fellow will have a base within one of the partner universities.  They will have mentors both within their University and UK Government, to offer guidance and support.  Mentors from the organisations will work together to ensure coherence and consistency in their advice and support.  Fellows will be required to be in Manchester several days a week and so are expected either to be located in the North-West or willing to relocate. Applicants should have completed or at least submitted a relevant PhD and have expertise in computer science, AI, machine learning and/or an intersection of those fields.  This expertise should be explained in a letter of application and demonstrated in the abovementioned proposal, which short-listed candidates will be expected to present at interview. The fellow must be eligible and willing to apply for national security clearance at a minimum level of Enhanced Security Clearance (eSC) and a preferred level of Enhanced Developed Vetting (eDV), making them subject to the provisions of the Official Secrets Act (which includes restrictions on travel to certain countries).  UK nationals currently resident in the UK will normally be eligible, but all applicants should carefully consider their eligibility before applying for the fellowship. Information about vetting and clearance can be found on the UK Government Security Clearance website. The employer reserves the right to terminate a fellowship in the event of a loss of security clearance. Our benefits include: • Generous employer contribution pension • 29 days annual leave plus bank holidays, along with Christmas closure • Ride to work and EV car scheme available For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Email Address: bsfellowships@manchester.ac.uk . General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Supporting Documents save_alt HUM-031918 - Postdoctoral Research Fellowship in Human AI-Teaming (PDF, KB) Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46330": {
      "title": "Research Associate in Chemical Biology",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Research Associate in Chemical Biology Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Research Associate in Chemical Biology Apply for job Job reference: SAE-032677 Salary: £38,448 - £46,970 per annum depending on experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Science and Engineering Location: - Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Department of Chemistry Hours Per Week: 1 FTE Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 09/11/2026 Contract Duration: 14 months School/Directorate: School of Natural Sciences Job Description This PDRA position will be based in the Manchester Institute of Biotechnology (MIB) under the supervision of Dr Yi Jin, Wellcome Trust Sir Henry Dale Fellow. The candidate will conduct research in the laboratories and manage the Jin group. They will lead the design, development and implementation of the experiments to study a bacterial pathway called sulfoglycolysis, as well as plasmid design and cloning, enzyme production and purification, and BSL2 organism handling. Whilst a number of aspects of this project have been established and implemented in the group, an essential part of this role will be to communicate and further develop the project with other group members and collaborators. This post will be last part of 6-year long project and bring a conclusion by publishing the impactful results in high-impact journals. About the Team MIB is a cross-disciplinary research centre that brings together more than 400 researchers with expertise and skills supporting industrial, environmental and clinical biotechnology, including chemistry; chemical biology; engineering biology; biochemical-engineering; materials science; synthetic biology; advanced analytics and computer science. The Jin group is located in Manchester Institute of Biotechnology (MIB), currently consisted of 7 PhD students. Her group has expertise in fluorine chemistry, structural biology and mechanistic enzymology enables her to drive transformative research into the enzymes and pathways involved in bacterial persistence and antibiotic resistance. This role is funded by the Wellcome Trust Fellowship, involving working in both MIB and Michael Smith Building. (Grades 6-9) Our benefits include: • Generous employer contribution pension • 29 days annual leave plus bank holidays, along with Christmas closure • Ride to work and EV car scheme available For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Yi Jin Email Address: yi.jin@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://knowledge.jobtrain.co.uk/jobseeker [knowledge.jobtrain.co.uk] Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46328": {
      "title": "Research Impact Associate: Evidencing the Research Impact of Public Engagement Activities",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Research Impact Associate: Evidencing the Research Impact of Public Engagement Activities Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Research Impact Associate: Evidencing the Research Impact of Public Engagement Activities Apply for job Job reference: SAE-032704 Salary: £38,448 to £46,970 per annum depending on experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Science and Engineering Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: FSE Research & Business Services Hours Per Week: Full Time Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 16/11/2026 Contract Duration: 28 months School/Directorate: Faculty Office - Science and Engineering Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description The Faculty of Science and Engineering is seeking a Research Impact Associate with a background in qualitative and quantitative evaluation to strengthen how we evidence the difference our research-led public engagement makes. Working closely with the Associate Dean for REF, academic teams and professional services colleagues, the candidate will design and apply robust, creative approaches to evaluating changes in audience understanding and behaviour. There work will help researchers demonstrate impact, improve future engagement and support the development of high-quality impact case studies for the Research Excellence Framework (REF) 2028. Expectations of the role The postholder would design and apply quantitative and qualitative methods to evaluate the impact of research-led public engagement on public and industrial stakeholders. Working in collaboration with academic and professional services teams, the postholder would help plan stakeholder engagement, coordinate evaluation activity and establish clear project deliverables. They would also be involved in the evaluation of high value activities, assessing both the outcomes of the activities and the effectiveness of different approaches. You will establish frameworks for managing project deliverables and support colleagues to articulate research impact through high-quality reports, communications and REF impact cases. You will also prepare presentations and publications on methods and findings, contribute to strategic discussions with senior stakeholders, and develop training that enables researchers to integrate effective impact evaluation into their projects. This is an opportunity to shape emerging practice in research impact evaluation and help better articulate the impact of public-engagement activities on external communities. You will be encouraged to share successful approaches across the faculty and to publish and present your work to wider academic audiences. Candidate skills You will bring a PhD or equivalent relevant experience, together with experience of developing and evaluating quantitative and qualitative stakeholder engagement methods. You will be confident building effective professional relationships with varied audiences and able to manage competing deadlines in a changing environment. Strong analytical and communication skills are essential. You should be able to evaluate complex qualitative evidence and present clear conclusions in writing and verbally. You will also understand the practical, ethical and data protection considerations involved in public engagement activity. Awareness of research impact assessment, including the role of impact within REF and research funding, is required. Experience in higher education, delivering training, assessing research impact, developing public engagement activity or publishing research methods and findings would be advantageous, as would a degree or equivalent experience in the social sciences. What you will get in return: Fantastic market leading Pension scheme Excellent employee health and wellbeing services including an Employee Assistance Programme Exceptional starting annual leave entitlement, plus bank holidays Additional paid closure over the Christmas period Local and national discounts at a range of major retailers As an equal opportunities employer we welcome applicants from all sections of the community regardless of age, sex, gender (or gender identity), ethnicity, disability, sexual orientation and transgender status. All appointments are made on merit. Our University is positive about flexible working – you can find out more here Hybrid working arrangements may be considered. Please be aware that due to the number of applications we unfortunately may not able to provide individual feedback on your application. Please note that we are unable to respond to enquiries, accept CVs or applications from Recruitment Agencies. Any recruitment enquiries from recruitment agencies should be directed to recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Any CV’s submitted by a recruitment agency will be considered a gift. Enquiries about the vacancy, shortlisting and interviews: Name: Tim Stallard Email: Tim.Stallard@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries: Email: recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical support: 0161 850 2004 https://knowledge.jobtrain.co.uk/jobseeker [knowledge.jobtrain.co.uk] This vacancy will close for applications at midnight on the closing date. Please see the link below for the Further Particulars document which contains the person specification criteria. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46200": {
      "title": "Research Technician in Hereditary Prostate Cancer",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Research Technician in Hereditary Prostate Cancer Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Research Technician in Hereditary Prostate Cancer Apply for job Job reference: BMH-032543 Salary: £27,865 per annum depending on experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Biology, Medicine Health Location: Oglesby Cancer Research Building,  555 Wilmslow Road, M20 4GJ Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Division of Cancer Sciences Hours Per Week: 35 hours per week (1 FTE) Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 09/11/2026 Contract Duration: Fixed term for 24 Months School/Directorate: School of Medical Sciences Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description We are seeking a motivated and collaborative individual to join our team as a Research Technician. This role offers an exciting opportunity to contribute to a Career Acceleration Fellowship Award within the Translational Oncogenomics lab at the CRUK MI and University of Manchester. The successful candidate will join the Translational Oncogenomics team within the Manchester Cancer Research Centre, Division of Cancer Sciences at the University of Manchester. Working directly with the Fellowship-award holder and head of the host University of Manchester Translational Oncogenomics team, the focus of this role is to provide technical support as directed in a range of project specific activities within a dynamic and inclusive environment. You will be responsible for: running experiments and maintaining cell stocks data analysis and maintaining accurate records of laboratory work using, maintaining, and teaching the use of research associated equipment assist team members in ensuring that staff and students operate safe working practices, ensuring health and safety compliance within the laboratories. We welcome candidates who bring diverse perspectives, experiences, and approaches to their work. About You We encourage applications from individuals with a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. You should demonstrate: Essential Criteria A qualification at QCF level 6 (such as a bachelor’s degree) in a relevant biological science subject. OR QCF level 2 passes (such as GCSE’s) in English and Mathematics and at least one Science subject and some relevant experience of working in a research laboratory. Competent computer skills Desirable Criteria Some practical experience in one or several of the following laboratory techniques: Cell culture, molecular biology (e.g., cloning with standard bacterial approaches), western blotting. Experience of working with Microsoft Access packages. Knowledge of DNA damage and Repair processes and DNA Replication. Knowledge of Prostate Cancer Biology We value transferable skills and real-world experience as much as formal qualifications. Our benefits include: Generous employer pension contribution Christmas closure across all departments outside of annual leave and bank holidays Purple Place employee discount platform For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Peter Reid Email Address: Peter.Reid@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home This role is not eligible for Skilled Worker visa sponsorship. Applicants must demonstrate the right to work in the UK. Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46277": {
      "title": "Research Technician",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Research Technician Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Research Technician Apply for job Job reference: BMH-032614 Salary: £30,180 - £31,861 per annum depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Biology, Medicine Health Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: FBMH Technical Services Hours Per Week: 1.0 FTE (100%) Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 20/11/2026 Contract Duration: 18 months School/Directorate: Faculty Office - Biology, Medicine and Health Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description We are seeking an enthusiastic, organised, and highly committed laboratory-based Research Technician to join the Gago´s Lab based at the Manchester Fungal Infection Group on a newly funded Academy of Medical Sciences research programme. This role offers an exciting opportunity to contribute to cutting-edge work at the interface of infection biology and translational research. This Academy of Medical Sciences-funded position is full-time (1.0 FTE) for 18 months. This innovative project will study the fundamental mechanisms of polymicrobial interactions in the lungs. The Research Technician will independently perform grant-funded experiments, contributing to experimental design, consultation with other researchers, data analysis, presentation and reporting. These will include cell culture (primary cultures and immortalised cell lines), culturing of microorganisms (fungi and viruses), immunoassays (e.g. ELISA, western blot), imaging and transcriptomic analyses. The Research Technician will also contribute to the maintenance, use and teaching of research associated equipment. The post holder will assist team members in ensuring that staff and students operate safe working practices, ensuring health and safety compliance within the laboratory. Applicants will preferably have knowledge and experience in establishing and maintaining primary cell cultures and microbiological techniques. Experience of mammalian cell culture is essential for this position. Our benefits include: (Grades 1-5) Generous employer pension contribution Christmas closure across all departments outside of annual leave and bank holidays Purple Place employee discount platform For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . This role is not eligible for Skilled Worker visa sponsorship. Applicants must demonstrate the right to work in the UK. We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Simone Ward Email Address: simone.ward@manchester.ac.uk Name: Dr Sara Gago Email: sara.gago-2@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46341": {
      "title": "Senior Network Engineer",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Senior Network Engineer Cookie Policy By clicking \"Allow Cookies\", you agree to the storing of cookies on your device to analyse site usage, and assist in our marketing efforts. Cookie Policy Decline Allow cookies Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Senior Network Engineer Apply for job Job reference: PSX-032673 Salary: £48,337 - £59,390 per annum depending on experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Professional Services Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Campus Technology Services Hours Per Week: Full time Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 09/11/2026 Contract Duration: 18 months School/Directorate: Directorate of IT Services Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description The University of Manchester The University of Manchester is a truly global institution with a reputation for education and innovation that resonates across the world, offering a wide range of courses and boasting outstanding facilities in the centre of Manchester. About IT Services IT Services at the University of Manchester is a vibrant and fast-moving department, we focus on delivering excellent customer service and quality services for our staff, students and researchers. Find out more about our organisation and values: IT Services: About us and ITS Practice Charter Benefits of working at The University of Manchester The team The Network team sits within the core area of CTS in IT Services, providing underpinning network services and support to our customers. Our network team work closely with all parts of IT Services and throughout the University to ensure our services are consistently meeting and adapting to the changing needs of the University. The University of Manchester is large and steeped in history and heritage, as such our IT landscape is broad and complex. From cultural institutions such as Jodrell Bank, to state-of-the-art teaching and research facilities such as the new Engineering Buildings and the National Graphene Institute, the scope of the services we provide are wide-ranging. The role The University of Manchester operates one of the largest single site campus networks in the UK and is currently undergoing a major refresh of all its LAN, Data Centre and DDI infrastructure. After a period of working with a managed service partner, ITS have made the decision to bring services and in-house. A strategic decision that puts our network infrastructure directly in our hands, enabling delivery of rapid, tailored support that aligns perfectly with our unique business requirements. This role provides an exciting opportunity for a skilled and experienced network engineer to join the University and work as a subject matter expert within the team. The role will suit an experienced engineer with a breadth of networking knowledge and experience covering LAN/WAN/Wireless and Data Centre network design and operation. The role will also include developing our network policy and DDI environment to help us migrate to a more agile, automated, identity driven network service. Working within an expanding and evolving team, you will provide Subject Matter Expertise (SME) across our technology stack (HPE, Cisco & Arista) to assure that any designs and deployments are agreed with our network architect prior to implementation and that they are fit-for-purpose and adhere to our strategic principles. Specific Role Requirements It is important that candidates have experience of both design, delivery and operations of enterprise class networking and backend services and can think innovatively to identify opportunities and to add value. In return, you will receive exposure and experience in the latest HPE Aruba and Arista technologies and have an opportunity to work alongside both an experienced University network team and our strategic Network partner to contribute towards the development and ongoing level 3 support of one the UK’s largest, single campus, software defined network rollouts. The person The successful candidate will possess some of the following experience: Network (LAN and wireless) design, operation and troubleshooting experience either with Cisco or Aruba technology, preferably demonstrated by professional level certifications in either Cisco or Aruba networking. Experience of working in a service support function, dealing with customers to resolve issues and answer their questions. Experience of working with 3rd party suppliers to define, design, deliver and operate successful solutions. Ability to quickly assess network issues, identify the likely root cause and see technical issues through to a successful conclusion. Strong experience in LAN and wireless networking with an excellent knowledge of network protocols, routing (BGP and OSPF) and switching. Strong experience in data centre network technologies and their application. Experience of HPE and Arista networking and F5 load balancers is desirable. Good experience in network security tooling, firewalls, IPS, remote access solutions to support our Cyber Security team with their operations. Analytical with strong attention to detail – able to identify and articulate issues to both technical and non-technical audiences Ability to work to tight timescales without sacrificing quality When addressing the person specification criteria in your application for this role, please ensure you make reference to the specific role requirements above. (Grades 6-9) Our benefits include: • Generous employer contribution pension • 29 days annual leave plus bank holidays, along with Christmas closure • Ride to work and EV car scheme available For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Warren Mittoo Email: Warren.Mittoo@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Supporting Documents save_alt PSX-032673 Senior Network Engineer FP's (PDF, KB) Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46075": {
      "title": "Assistant Technician (Clinical Teaching)",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Assistant Technician (Clinical Teaching) Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Assistant Technician (Clinical Teaching) Apply for job Job reference: BMH-032421 Salary: £27,319 - £28,778 Faculty/Organisational Unit: Biology, Medicine Health Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Permanent Division/Team: FBMH Technical Services Hours Per Week: 35 Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 09/11/2026 Contract Duration: N/A School/Directorate: Faculty Office - Biology, Medicine and Health Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description We are looking to appoint a full-time Assistant Technician to support practical teaching within Audiology. The role will be based in the Manchester Centre for Audiology and Deafness (ManCAD), in the Ellen Wilkinson Building. The postholder will be responsible for setting up the wide range of audiological equipment and consumables required to deliver the laboratory-based teaching sessions. The postholder will be responsible for ensuring that the teaching rooms are always fit for purpose, including any housekeeping duties required to maintain the high standards for students training for clinical practice. The postholder will be expected to have a good background in science and a willingness to learn new techniques. This is an opportunity for a motivated and organized individual with an interest in physics, hearing, and sound, with a relevant background to provide technical support. An understanding of the technical and scientific principles of Audiology would be an advantage but is not essential as training will be given. Following a recent review of our Technical Services, all technical roles in support of Teaching are designed to be flexible and agile. As such, this role is advertised in support of Audiology Teaching, but you could be asked to move to support another Clinical Teaching area or work within the wider teaching technical structure on a short- or long-term basis. You will be responsible for: Supporting the preparation and set-up of clinical, simulation and practical teaching spaces, equipment and materials for scheduled classes, outreach activity and events. Preparing and checking scientific equipment, instruments, consumables and personal protective equipment for teaching sessions. Acting as a point of contact for staff and students reporting technical issues, escalating matters appropriately so that problems are resolved quickly. Supporting compliance with health and safety requirements, including risk assessments, COSHH, manual handling, PAT testing, safe working procedures and use of PPE. Working flexibly across Faculty technical services, learning new techniques and supporting other areas where service need requires. We welcome candidates who bring diverse perspectives, experiences, and approaches to their work. About You We encourage applications from individuals with a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. You should demonstrate: Essential Criteria Knowledge of laboratory or clinical teaching techniques and procedures, with an understanding of relevant workplace health and safety requirements including risk assessments, COSHH, manual handling, PPE and basic equipment safety. Experience of working in a relevant technical, laboratory, clinical teaching or practical support environment, or demonstrable transferable experience gained through study or work. Good practical, organisational and problem-solving skills, with attention to detail and the ability to follow procedures accurately. A qualification in a science-related subject area, such as a degree, HND or HNC, or equivalent relevant technical experience. Willingness to work flexibly across Faculty areas where service need requires and to undertake work outside regular office hours, including weekends, where necessary. Desirable Criteria Experience of preparing equipment, materials, teaching spaces or resources for practical activity, events or service delivery. We value transferable skills and real-world experience as much as formal qualifications. Our benefits include: Generous employer pension contribution Christmas closure across all departments outside of annual leave and bank holidays Purple Place employee discount platform For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Anthony Steel Email Address: anthony.steel@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home This role is not eligible for Skilled Worker visa sponsorship. Applicants must demonstrate the right to work in the UK. Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Watch: The University of Manchester – our new strategy to 2035 Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46070": {
      "title": "Senior Technician (Aerospace)",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Senior Technician (Aerospace) Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Senior Technician (Aerospace) Apply for job Job reference: SAE-032429 Salary: £33,662 to £37,369 per annum, depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Science and Engineering Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Permanent Division/Team: FSE Technical Services Hours Per Week: 35 hours Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 06/11/2026 Contract Duration: Permanent School/Directorate: Faculty Office - Science and Engineering Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description The Assessment & Progression Officer will lead a team and manage delivery of the School’s undergraduate and postgraduate taught assessment and progression activity. Through effective leadership, expert interpretation of policy and regulations, accurate management of student data and close collaboration with academic and Professional Services partners, the postholder will ensure that assessments, examination boards, progression decisions, external examining and complex student cases are managed consistently and effectively. The role will contribute to robust academic governance, regulatory compliance, service improvement and a positive experience for students and colleagues. The Assessment & Progression team sits within Teaching, Learning and Student Experience (TLSE) and is responsible for delivering a wide range of assessment, progression and student records activities that support undergraduate and postgraduate taught programmes across the School of Medical Sciences. Working in a complex and professionally regulated environment, the team collaborates closely with academic staff, professional services colleagues, external examiners, Faculty and University partners to ensure the effective administration of assessments, examination boards, student progression and academic governance processes. The role plays an important part in the wider University community by helping to maintain academic standards, support regulatory compliance, enhance the student experience and contribute to continuous improvement in teaching and learning services. What you will get in return: Fantastic market leading Pension scheme Excellent employee health and wellbeing services including an Employee Assistance Programme Exceptional starting annual leave entitlement, plus bank holidays Additional paid closure over the Christmas period Local and national discounts at a range of major retailers As an equal opportunities employer we welcome applicants from all sections of the community regardless of age, sex, gender (or gender identity), ethnicity, disability, sexual orientation and transgender status. All appointments are made on merit. Our University is positive about flexible working – you can find out more here Hybrid working arrangements may be considered. Please be aware that due to the number of applications we unfortunately may not able to provide individual feedback on your application. Please note that we are unable to respond to enquiries, accept CVs or applications from Recruitment Agencies. Any recruitment enquiries from recruitment agencies should be directed to recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Any CV’s submitted by a recruitment agency will be considered a gift Enquiries about the vacancy, shortlisting and interviews: Name: Denise Grehan Email: denise.grehan@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries: Email: recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical support: 0161 850 2004 https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home This vacancy will close for applications at midnight on the closing date. Please see the link below for the Further Particulars document which contains the person specification criteria. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46347": {
      "title": "Director of Reward, Benefits & Recognition",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Director of Reward, Benefits &amp; Recognition Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Director of Reward, Benefits & Recognition Apply for job Job reference: PSX-032683 Salary: Competitive salary Faculty/Organisational Unit: Professional Services Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Permanent Division/Team: Reward, Benefits and Pensions Hours Per Week: Full Time (1 FTE) Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 22/11/2026 Contract Duration: Permanent School/Directorate: People Directorate Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description Director of Reward, Benefits & Recognition We are seeking an experienced and collaborative Director of Reward, Benefits & Recognition to lead our Reward, Benefits and Recognition Centre of Expertise. This is an opportunity to shape a modern, inclusive and competitive reward offer for one of the UK’s leading universities, helping us attract, retain and recognise outstanding people while delivering value, fairness and agility. You will be responsible for: Leading an evidence-led reward strategy that is competitive regionally, nationally and internationally Continually improving our benefits and recognition offer, strengthening our employee value proposition and leading the University’s pensions service Using robust data, market intelligence and clear KPIs to address pay gaps, equal pay and wider reward outcomes Evolving job evaluation, job families and role architecture, while leading an effective and inclusive annual performance review cycle Building strong relationships across the University and sector, leading high-performing teams and providing senior governance groups with high-quality insight and advice About You We encourage applications from individuals with a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. You should demonstrate: Essential Criteria: Senior-level reward leadership experience in a large, complex and unionised organisation, covering strategy, policy, benchmarking, job frameworks and benefits. A track record of turning strategy into measurable improvements while balancing long-term priorities with excellent operational delivery Strong people leadership and the ability to build capable, inclusive and high-performing teams Credibility and influence with senior stakeholders, supported by excellent communication, negotiation and relationship-management skills. Experience of organisational transformation, with sound commercial, budget and financial management capability, and a commitment to equality, diversity, inclusion and the University’s values Our benefits include: • Generous employer contribution pension • 29 days annual leave plus bank holidays, along with Christmas closure • Ride to work and EV car scheme available For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Lewis Burne Email Address: talent.attraction@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support talent.attraction@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support For technical support please call 0161 850 2004 or visit https://knowledge.jobtrain.co.uk/jobseeker Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Please see the link to the candidate brochure and job description, attached below, for further information about the role and selection criteria. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46288": {
      "title": "Research Associate in Particle Physics on ATLAS",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Research Associate in Particle Physics on ATLAS Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Research Associate in Particle Physics on ATLAS Apply for job Job reference: SAE-032629 Salary: £38,448 - £46,970 per annum depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Science and Engineering Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Department of Physics & Astronomy Hours Per Week: 35 Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 19/11/2026 Contract Duration: Fixed term for 36 Months from 1st Jan 2027 or shortly thereafter School/Directorate: School of Natural Sciences Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description The Particle Physics group at the University of Manchester has a vacancy for a Research Associate to work on the ATLAS experiment at the CERN LHC. The Manchester ATLAS group consists of seven academic members of staff, twelve research staff and around 20 PhD students. The Manchester group has a strong involvement in many areas of ATLAS physics analysis, including studies of the Higgs boson’s interactions, measurements of high-energy electroweak processes (VBS/VBF, multiboson production), top quark physics, and searches for new phenomena. The Manchester group is also heavily involved ATLAS detector operations and performance, with long-standing contributions to the ATLAS trigger and data acquisition system, jet and flavour tagging performance, and the Phase-II upgrade. The postdoctoral position is funded by the Leverhulme Trust and will be hosted by the Manchester ATLAS group. The overall goal of the project is to study the joint polarisation of weak boson pairs that are produced via the vector boson scattering process and decay via the semileptonic channel. New jet reconstruction and tagging algorithms will be developed and calibrated to aid the polarisation measurements. There may be opportunities to take part in phenomenology studies relevant to HL-LHC and FCC. Applicants must have a PhD or equivalent in particle physics by the time of starting the position. The successful candidate will have experience in one or more of the following areas of particle physics: data analysis, event generator modelling, trigger, detector operations, or combined detector performance. You should be able to work independently and collaboratively, communicate research effectively and organise work to agreed timescales. The full essential and desirable criteria are set out in the accompanying Job Description and Person Specification. The appointment will be from 1st January 2027, or as soon as possible thereafter. The appointment will initially be for a period of three years. The successful candidate will be based in Manchester and will make frequent visits to CERN. Application and enquiries Along with your application, please submit a single file under 1 MB containing your CV, publication list and a one-page research statement. Three letters of recommendation should be sent to andrew.pilkington@manchester.ac.uk by two days after the application deadline. The Department of Physics & Astronomy is strongly committed to promoting equality and diversity, including the Athena SWAN Charter for gender equality in higher education. The Department holds a Silver Award which recognises their good practice in relation to gender; including flexible working arrangements, family-friendly policies, and support to allow staff to achieve a good work-life balance. We positively welcome applications from women for this post. Appointment will always be made on merit. For further information, please visit: https://www.physics.manchester.ac.uk/connect/social-responsibility/ (Grades 6-9) Our benefits include: • Generous employer contribution pension • 29 days annual leave plus bank holidays, along with Christmas closure • Ride to work and EV car scheme available For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Prof. Andrew Pilkington Email Address: andrew.pilkington@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46335": {
      "title": "Research Assistant Protein Synthesis and Cancer",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Research Assistant Protein Synthesis and Cancer Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Research Assistant Protein Synthesis and Cancer Apply for job Job reference: BMH-032666 Salary: £33,662 per annum, depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Biology, Medicine Health Location: Oglesby Cancer Research Building,  555 Wilmslow Road, M20 4GJ Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Division of Cancer Sciences Hours Per Week: Full Time Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 04/11/2026 Contract Duration: 12 Months from 1 January 2027 School/Directorate: School of Medical Sciences Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description We are seeking a motivated and collaborative individual to join our team as a Research Assistant. This role offers an exciting opportunity to contribute to join the Protein Synthesis and Cancer group at the University of Manchester. The project is funded by a pilot grant from Bowel Cancer UK. You will perform complex cell and molecular studies using models of colorectal cancer with the aim of improving our knowledge and treatment of the disease, working within a dynamic and inclusive environment. You will be responsible for: Conducting individual and collaborative research. Use initiative and creativity to identify areas for research and develop research methods. Use creativity to analyse and interpret data and draw conclusions on the outcomes. Clearly communicate complex information when required (orally, in writing and electronically). Have an understanding of and commitment to promote the University’s policies and procedures to support and promote Equality & Diversity We welcome candidates who bring diverse perspectives, experiences, and approaches to their work. About You We encourage applications from individuals with a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. You should demonstrate: Essential Criteria: Have, or be about to obtain, a Masters (or equivalent) in cancer or molecular biology Specialist knowledge in cell culture and complex molecular biology methods Ability to communicate in both written and oral formats Excellent communication and interpersonal skills Desirable Criteria: Experience of researching the molecular biology of colorectal cancer Ability to develop techniques as required for individual projects We value transferable skills and real-world experience as much as formal qualifications. What you will get in return: Fantastic market leading Pension scheme Excellent employee health and wellbeing services including an Employee Assistance Programme Exceptional starting annual leave entitlement, plus bank holidays Additional paid closure over the Christmas period Local and national discounts at a range of major retailers As an equal opportunities employer we welcome applicants from all sections of the community regardless of age, sex, gender (or gender identity), ethnicity, disability, sexual orientation and transgender status. All appointments are made on merit. Our University is positive about flexible working – you can find out more here Hybrid working arrangements may be considered. Please be aware that due to the number of applications we unfortunately may not able to provide individual feedback on your application. Please note that we are unable to respond to enquiries, accept CVs or applications from Recruitment Agencies. Any recruitment enquiries from recruitment agencies should be directed to recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Any CV’s submitted by a recruitment agency will be considered a gift. Enquiries about the vacancy, shortlisting and interviews: Name: Dr John Knight Email: john.knight@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries: Email: recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical support: 0161 850 2004 https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home This vacancy will close for applications at midnight on the closing date. Please see the link below for the Further Particulars document which contains the person specification criteria. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46327": {
      "title": "Research Associate in Computational Fluid Mechanics",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Research Associate in Computational Fluid Mechanics Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Research Associate in Computational Fluid Mechanics Apply for job Job reference: SAE-032662 Salary: £38,448 to £41,885 per annum, depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Science and Engineering Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Department of Mechanical and Aerospace Engineering Hours Per Week: Full Time Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 18/11/2026 Contract Duration: 30 months School/Directorate: School of Engineering Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description An exciting opportunity is available for a postdoctoral Research Associate to join a recently funded Leverhulme Trust project investigating air entrainment and bubble formation by plunging jets in complex fluids. The project is motivated by a recently observed and unexpected fluid-mechanical phenomenon involving plunging jets in non-Newtonian fluids. It aims to understand the physical mechanisms governing the interaction between the jet, the free surface and the entrained air, and how these are influenced by the rheological properties of the fluid. The research will combine numerical simulations with complementary experimental and theoretical investigations to develop a fundamental understanding of these flows. This project sits at the intersection of fluid mechanics, complex fluids and interfacial phenomena. It offers the chance to contribute to ambitious, curiosity-driven research aimed at understanding a phenomenon that has not previously been described in the scientific literature. The successful candidate will play a leading role in the project’s numerical programme, developing and deploying new algorithms, and carrying out simulations of jets, bubbles and air entrainment in complex fluids. The work will involve developing new algorithms for high-fidelity adaptive multiphase fluid simulations, deploying these in in-house and open-source code bases, conducting a campaign of numerical experiments, and contributing to publications and conference presentations. The post holder will work closely with other academic investigators and project partners, including researchers undertaking complementary experimental/theoretical studies. This is an excellent opportunity for someone with a background in numerical method development for fluid mechanics who is excited by fundamental research and wants to help shape an emerging area of study from an early stage. Applicants should have, or be close to completing, a PhD (or equivalent) in fluid mechanics, physics, engineering, or a closely related discipline. Experience in developing, deploying and analysing new numerical methods and algorithms is essential. Experience in areas such as multiphase flows, rheology, non-Newtonian fluids, or mesh-free numerical methods would be an advantage, but we welcome applicants with a strong numerical background and enthusiasm to develop new skills. The School is committed to Athena SWAN principles to promote women in science; the School’s website documenting activity in this area can be found at: https://www.manchester.ac.uk/connect/jobs/equality-diversity-inclusion/awards/athena-swan/ The University of Manchester values a diverse workforce and welcomes applications from all sections of the community. What you will get in return: Fantastic market leading Pension scheme Excellent employee health and wellbeing services including an Employee Assistance Programme Exceptional starting annual leave entitlement, plus bank holidays Additional paid closure over the Christmas period Local and national discounts at a range of major retailers Our University is positive about flexible working – you can find out more here Hybrid working arrangements may be considered. Please be aware that due to the number of applications we unfortunately may not able to provide individual feedback on your application. Please note that we are unable to respond to enquiries, accept CVs or applications from Recruitment Agencies. Any recruitment enquiries from recruitment agencies should be directed to recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Any CV’s submitted by a recruitment agency will be considered a gift. Enquiries about the vacancy, shortlisting and interviews: Name: Dr Jack King Email: jack.king@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries: Email: recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical support: 0161 850 2004 https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home This vacancy will close for applications at midnight on the closing date. Please see the link below for the Further Particulars document which contains the person specification criteria. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46298": {
      "title": "Research Associate",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Research Associate Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Research Associate Apply for job Job reference: SAE-032636 Salary: £38,448 - £46,970 per annum depending on experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Science and Engineering Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Department of Civil Engineering and Management Hours Per Week: Full time (1 FTE) Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 03/11/2026 Contract Duration: Fixed term for 12 months School/Directorate: School of Engineering Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description We are seeking a highly motivated Research Associate to join an interdisciplinary research project examining how lessons from industrial incidents can be more effectively transferred across sectors. The project spans multiple high-hazard and safety-critical industries (e.g. construction, oil and gas, aviation, healthcare, nuclear) and aims to develop and validate a practical, cross-sectoral framework for learning from incidents. You will be responsible for: Conduct a comprehensive cross-sectoral systematic review of academic literature, grey literature, regulatory reports, and industry guidance related to learning from incidents and accidents. Analyse existing incident reporting, learning, and feedback mechanisms across multiple sectors, identifying transferable practices, gaps, and limitations. Design and conduct qualitative research, including semi-structured interviews with industry practitioners, regulators, and safety professionals. Synthesise qualitative and quantitative findings into a structured, evidence-based framework for intersectoral learning from incidents. Work closely with project partners, including industry forums, regulators, and professional bodies, to ensure relevance and applicability of outputs. We welcome candidates who bring diverse perspectives, experiences, and approaches to their work. About You We encourage applications from individuals with a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. You should demonstrate: Essential Criteria PhD (or near completion) in a relevant discipline, such as safety science, engineering management, risk management, human factors / applied psychology, organisational studies, systems engineering or related fields. Proven experience in qualitative research, including interview design and thematic analysis, with strong proficiency in qualitative data analysis software such as NVivo or ATLAS.ti. Experience in using quantitative methods, survey design and analysis, and software packages, such as SPSS, Mplus, or R. Strong analytical and synthesis skills, with the ability to integrate evidence across disciplines and sectors. Excellent written and verbal communication skills, with experience producing reports and/or academic publications. Desirable Criteria Experience researching and learning from incidents, accident investigation, or safety management systems. Familiarity with socio-technical safety frameworks (e.g. STAMP, FRAM, Safety-I/SafetyII). Experience working with industry or regulatory bodies in applied research contexts. Experience with mixed-methods research. Evidence of publications in safety, risk, or management journals. We value transferable skills and real-world experience as much as formal qualifications. Our benefits include: Generous employer contribution pension 29 days annual leave plus bank holidays, along with Christmas closure Ride to work and EV car scheme available For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Saeed Reza Mohandes Email Address: saeedreza.mohandes@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. The University of Manchester – our new strategy to 2035 video Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46271": {
      "title": "Post Doctoral Research Associate in Maternal Cardiovascular Health",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Post Doctoral Research Associate in Maternal Cardiovascular Health Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Post Doctoral Research Associate in Maternal Cardiovascular Health Apply for job Job reference: BMH-032613 Salary: £38,448 - £46,970 per annum, depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Biology, Medicine Health Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Division of Developmental Biology and Medicine Hours Per Week: 35 Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 09/11/2026 Contract Duration: 42 months School/Directorate: School of Medical Sciences Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description We are seeking a motivated and collaborative individual to join our team as a Post Doctoral Research Associate in maternal cardiovascular health. This role offers an exciting opportunity to contribute to a programme of preclinical research investigating mechanisms linking hypertensive disorders of pregnancy (e.g. preeclampsia) to long-term cerebrovascular risk and testing novel treatment strategies to improve long-term brain health. This work will run in parallel to ongoing human studies in the Maternal and Fetal Health Research Centre and contribute to our goal of improving women’s health through translational research, within a dynamic and inclusive environment. You will be responsible for: Conducting a programme of preclinical research Analysing and interpreting research data and drawing conclusions on the outcomes Reporting on research outcomes to the wider study team Contributing to research student supervision We welcome candidates who bring diverse perspectives, experiences, and approaches to their work. About You We encourage applications from individuals with a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. You should demonstrate: Essential Criteria: Applicants must have a Home Office personal licence and experience of planning and delivering in vivo studies Relevant laboratory experience in the field of pregnancy research and/or cardiovascular research and/or neuroscience research Ability to work to a high standard with good reproducibility of results. Ability to plan, organise and undertake a complex workload with multiple and competing priorities, with minimal supervision. Desirable Criteria: Strong journal publication record Record of supervision of undergraduate and postgraduate students We value transferable skills and real-world experience as much as formal qualifications. Our benefits include: • Generous employer contribution pension • 29 days annual leave plus bank holidays, along with Christmas closure • Ride to work and EV car scheme available For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Dr Elizabeth Cottrell Email Address: elizabeth.cottrell@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home This role is not eligible for Skilled Worker visa sponsorship. Applicants must demonstrate the right to work in the UK. Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46249": {
      "title": "Research Technician",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Research Technician Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Research Technician Apply for job Job reference: BMH-032620 Salary: £33,002 - £36,636 Faculty/Organisational Unit: Biology, Medicine Health Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Division of Developmental Biology and Medicine Hours Per Week: Full time (1 FTE) Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 02/11/2026 Contract Duration: Fixed term for 42 months School/Directorate: School of Medical Sciences Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description We are seeking a motivated and collaborative individual to join our team as a Research Technician . This role offers an exciting opportunity to support a programme of preclinical research investigating mechanisms linking hypertensive disorders of pregnancy, such as preeclampsia, to cardiovascular and cerebrovascular risk and testing novel treatment strategies to improve long-term health. This work will run alongside ongoing human studies in the Maternal and Fetal Health Research Centre and contribute to improving women’s health through translational research. You will be responsible for: Contribute to a programme of research by organising, conducting and reporting on research outcomes, as directed by the Principal Investigators. Contribute to the analysis and presentation of data using appropriate systems and methods for inclusion within publications and presentations. Supervise and advise staff and students on experimental protocols, equipment use, induction and safe working practices. Manage the efficient and safe operation of the Group’s Laboratory areas. Monitoring both that the activity and personnel are appropriate for the area. Ensure systems are in place for monitoring general consumable stock levels and procurement of consumables and equipment. We welcome candidates who bring diverse perspectives, experiences, and approaches to their work. About You We encourage applications from individuals with a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. You should demonstrate: Essential Criteria: Extensive, relevant and specific laboratory experience in the field of pregnancy-related research. A Home Office personal licence and experience of planning and delivering in vivo studies, ideally in both mice and rats. A breadth and depth of knowledge in a wide range of specialised techniques including: preclinical study design, molecular biology, histology, immunohistochemistry, vascular biology (wire myography). Ability to plan, organise and undertake a complex workload with multiple and competing priorities, without supervision. A qualification at QCF level 6 (such as a bachelor’s degree) in a relevant biological science subject OR QCF level 2 passes (such as GCSEs) in English and Mathematics and at least one Science subject and extensive relevant experience of working in a research laboratory. Desirable Criteria: No specific desirable criteria have been identified for this role. We value transferable skills and real-world experience as much as formal qualifications. Our benefits include: Generous employer pension contribution Christmas closure across all departments outside of annual leave and bank holidays Purple Place employee discount platform For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Email Address: General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support JobTrain Support Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46010": {
      "title": "Admissions Assistant",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Admissions Assistant Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Admissions Assistant Apply for job Job reference: BMH-032349 Salary: £27,319 - £28,778 per annum depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Biology, Medicine Health Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: FBMH Teaching, Learning and Student Experience Hours Per Week: 35 hours per week (1 FTE) Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 27/10/2026 Contract Duration: Fixed term until 29th June 2027 School/Directorate: Faculty Office - Biology, Medicine and Health Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description We are seeking motivated and collaborative individuals to join our team as an Admissions Assistants. This role offers an exciting opportunity to contribute to the Faculty Teaching, Learning and Student Experience team and support the delivery of a professional, fair and consistent admissions service within a dynamic and inclusive environment. You will be responsible for: - Responding to enquiries, maintaining applicant records, and escalating more complex matters as appropriate. - Making standard admissions decisions and process course applications in line with specified criteria, including invitation to interview. - Assisting with the delivery of admissions and conversion activities such as open days, interview days, Confirmation and Clearing, post-offer conversion activities, and admissions networks. - Undertaking the administration of core admissions processes such as CAS, ATAS, and Occupational Health Questionnaires in line with statutory and professional requirements and University regulations. We welcome candidates who bring diverse perspectives, experiences, and approaches to their work. About You We encourage applications from individuals with a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. You should demonstrate: Essential Criteria: - Experience of working with administrative processes. - Good oral and written communication skills with an ability to deal with a wide range of people. - Flexible and responsive to customer needs and able to provide information and guidance, whilst maintaining confidentiality. - Good organisational skills, and able to prioritise work in a busy environment and meet strict deadlines. - Experience of working in a team environment with the ability to be flexible and provide support for colleagues. - Good numeracy skills, attention to detail and the ability to identify and resolve issues effectively. - Digital literacy, with a familiarity with standard IT packages. - A commitment to the University’s core values and to the provision of the best possible support to our students, staff and customers. We value transferable skills and real-world experience as much as formal qualifications. Our benefits include: Generous employer pension contribution Christmas closure across all departments outside of annual leave and bank holidays Purple Place employee discount platform For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Chelsea Woods Email Address: Chelsea.woods@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home This role is not eligible for Skilled Worker visa sponsorship. Applicants must demonstrate the right to work in the UK. Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46269": {
      "title": "Collections Assistant",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Collections Assistant Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Collections Assistant Apply for job Job reference: PSX-032607 Salary: £30,180 to £32,722 pro rata, per annum, depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Professional Services Location: John Rylands Library Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Special Collections Hours Per Week: 21 hours per week (Part Time) Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 06/11/2026 Contract Duration: 12 months School/Directorate: The University of Manchester Library Job Description Are you interested in helping people discover and use remarkable historic collections at the University of Manchester Library? Join our Special Collections team as a Collections Assistant and help us care for and widen access to rare books, manuscripts, archives, maps and visual materials. You will contribute to a wide range of activities, from cataloguing, accessioning and preservation activities to supporting researchers, teaching sessions, exhibitions and public engagement. We are looking for a practical and adaptable team member with relevant Special Collections experience, strong attention to detail and enthusiasm for making Special Collections accessible to a wide range of audiences. The University of Manchester Library is one of only five National Research Libraries and the third largest academic library in the UK. Our vast and rich collections (both print and increasingly digital) help us to deliver a world-class library and information service for the University of Manchester. While our primary objective is to meet the learning, research and teaching needs of University members, we are also fully committed to widening access to our services to individual researchers, local schools and others in the regional community. The UML’s Special Collections are among the richest holdings of rare books, manuscripts, archives, maps and visual collections in the world. They span five millennia and include virtual every medium ever used for written communications, from cuneiform clay tablets to email archives. The collections are a superb resource for research, teaching and learning and the Library has a vibrant academic engagement programme. What you will get in return: Fantastic market leading Pension scheme Excellent employee health and wellbeing services including an Employee Assistance Programme Exceptional starting annual leave entitlement, plus bank holidays Additional paid closure over the Christmas period Local and national discounts at a range of major retailers As an equal opportunities employer we welcome applicants from all sections of the community regardless of age, sex, gender (or gender identity), ethnicity, disability, sexual orientation and transgender status. All appointments are made on merit. Our University is positive about flexible working – you can find out more here Hybrid working arrangements may be considered. Please be aware that due to the number of applications we unfortunately may not able to provide individual feedback on your application. Please note that we are unable to respond to enquiries, accept CVs or applications from Recruitment Agencies. Any recruitment enquiries from recruitment agencies should be directed to People.Recruitment@manchester.ac.uk . Any CV’s submitted by a recruitment agency will be considered a gift. Please note this role is not eligible for sponsorship under the Skilled Worker route of the Points Based System. Candidates will need to be able to demonstrate their right to work in the UK in order to be eligible to take up the post. Enquiries about the vacancy, shortlisting and interviews: Name: Clare Baker Email: clare.baker@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries: Email: People.recruitment@manchester.ac.uk Technical support: Jobtrain: 0161 850 2004 https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home This vacancy will close for applications at midnight on the closing date. Please see the link below for the Further Particulars document which contains the person specification criteria. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46215": {
      "title": "Senior Technician (Machinist)",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Senior Technician (Machinist) Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Senior Technician (Machinist) Apply for job Job reference: SAE-032556 Salary: UoM Grade 5 £33,002 - £36,636 per annum depending on experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Science and Engineering Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Permanent Division/Team: FSE Technical Services Hours Per Week: 35 hours per week (1 FTE) Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 04/11/2026 Contract Duration: Permanent School/Directorate: Faculty Office - Science and Engineering Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description We are seeking a motivated and collaborative individual to join our team as a Senior Technician (Machinist). The role provides specialist machining and mechanical engineering support that enables students, researchers and academic colleagues to turn designs and ideas into safe, accurate and reliable components, assemblies and experimental equipment. Through this work, you will make a direct contribution to excellent teaching, innovative research and the development of practical engineering skills across the Faculty. You will be responsible for: Manufacture components and assemblies using a wide range of machine tools, to the standard of precision required by the end user Effectively demonstrate the operation equipment to students within practical classes and related facilities Provide technical guidance and design advice to end users in the construction of new experimental rigs in all areas of FSE Supervise, motivate and develop junior staff and University of Manchester technical apprentices Ensure the delivery of service excellence and customer satisfaction. We welcome candidates who bring diverse perspectives, experiences, and approaches to their work. About You We encourage applications from individuals with a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. You should demonstrate: Essential Criteria Significant work experience in a relevant technical, engineering or scientific role, OR a qualification in a science or engineering-related subject area e.g. degree, HND, HNC. Specialised knowledge in the use of machine tools/equipment necessary to undertake the duties required for the post: Conventional lathes and/or milling machines CNC Lathes and/or turning centres CNC milling machines and/or machining centres An ability to support the teaching and learning of students through the effective demonstration of the operation of equipment within practical classes Experience of using CAD/CAM software An ability to understand and interpret engineering drawings Desirable Criteria Experience of carrying out practical classes for students. Knowledge and experience of the service and repair of laboratory equipment. Knowledge of teaching and research operations gained from within a Higher Education setting. Line management / supervisory experience. We value transferable skills and real-world experience as much as formal qualifications. Our benefits include: Generous employer pension contribution Christmas closure across all departments outside of annual leave and bank holidays Purple Place employee discount platform For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Kieran Nolan Email Address: kieran.nolan@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home This role is not eligible for Skilled Worker visa sponsorship. Applicants must demonstrate the right to work in the UK. Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46250": {
      "title": "Associate Director of Estates Partnering",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Associate Director of Estates Partnering Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Associate Director of Estates Partnering Apply for job Job reference: PSX-032589 Salary: £85,417 - £105,011 per annum depending on experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Professional Services Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Permanent Division/Team: Estates Partnering Team Hours Per Week: Full time (1 FTE) Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 17/11/2026 Contract Duration: Permanent School/Directorate: Directorate of Estates and Facilities Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description We are seeking an exceptional estates leader to join the Directorate of Estates & Facilities as Associate Director of Estates Partnering, leading the strategic partnering function across Faculties, Professional Services, cultural institutions and external partners. The Role: Reporting to the Director of Estates Partnering, you will provide senior strategic leadership across the Estates Partnering function, ensuring that institutional priorities are translated into integrated estates plans, service strategies and delivery programmes that support the University’s Manchester 2035 vision, financial sustainability and operational resilience. You will play a central role in shaping and delivering the University’s Integrated Infrastructure Plan, leading senior colleague engagement, estate planning, service performance, prioritisation and delivery assurance across a highly complex environment. As a member of the Directorate Senior Leadership Team, you will act as a key interface between Estates & Facilities and senior academics and Professional Services, ensuring that estate services, space, maintenance and investment programmes enable world-leading teaching, research, innovation and student experience. You Will: Lead the strategic development and delivery of Estates Business Partnering services across Faculties, cultural institutions, Professional Services and external partners Translate institutional priorities into clear, evidence-based estates plans, service strategies and capital, maintenance, space and infrastructure priorities Build trusted relationships with senior colleagues, providing authoritative advice, constructive challenge and clear escalation routes Oversee service standards, KPIs, governance and continuous improvement, ensuring responsive, consistent and customer-focused delivery Provide strategic oversight of project prioritisation, briefing and delivery assurance across capital, revenue, minor works and long-term maintenance programmes Provide senior stewardship of staffing, budgets, business cases and operational resources, maintaining effective governance, risk management and value for money Lead, coach and develop high-performing partnering teams, fostering collaboration, inclusion, accountability and professional growth Why Join Us: This is an opportunity to play a pivotal role in shaping how one of the UK’s largest and most ambitious university estates responds to academic, operational and strategic priorities. You will help lead a modern, trusted and evidence-led partnering function that connects institutional ambition with effective estate planning and delivery. You will join a forward-looking Directorate at a time of significant investment, transformation and strategic development, working with senior leaders across the University to strengthen services, improve estate performance and support teaching, research, innovation and civic engagement. What will you get in return: Fantastic market leading Pension scheme Excellent employee health and wellbeing services including an Employee Assistance Programme Exceptional starting annual leave entitlement, plus bank holidays Additional paid closure over the Christmas period Local and national discounts at a range of major retailers Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews please contact: talent.attraction@manchester.ac.uk Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46209": {
      "title": "Teaching, Learning & Student Experience Assistant (Student Support & Wellbeing)",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Teaching, Learning &amp; Student Experience Assistant (Student Support &amp; Wellbeing) Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Teaching, Learning & Student Experience Assistant (Student Support & Wellbeing) Apply for job Job reference: FOBM-032548 Salary: £27,865 to £29,354 per annum, depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Biology, Medicine Health Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: School of Medical Sciences Admin Hours Per Week: 35 hours per week Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 27/10/2026 Contract Duration: 12 months School/Directorate: School of Medical Sciences Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description Overall, Purpose of the Job: To provide clerical and administrative support for key activities of the Student Support & Wellbeing Team. To deliver excellent service provision and support colleagues with continuous improvement, placing the student experience at the heart of what we do with support for a single Professional Services (PS) team working flexibly across organisational boundaries. The postholder will play a key role in supporting the wellbeing and development of our students by delivering high quality administrative and front-line support within the SMS Student Support Hub and Teaching, Learning and Student Experience Team. This includes (but is not limited to): acting as the first point of contact for student support and wellbeing queries, signposting students to appropriate University services and further support, supporting the planning and delivery of wellbeing events and campaigns, involvement in associated student experience and support-related administrative processes and tasks. This is a student facing role requiring a compassionate and proactive individual who is committed to delivering an excellent student experience. We welcome applications from anyone with strong administrative and communication skills, as well as a genuine interest in supporting student wellbeing. Applicants should be confident using Microsoft 365 applications, able to work collaboratively in a team, and committed to upholding the University’s values of inclusivity and student-centred service. Appointment Notes: Colleagues will be required to work on a rota basis The timing and frequency of support may vary dependent on agreed opening hours and resource demands for the service Colleagues will be required to work on the Student Support Hub on a rota basis This role offers hybrid working, with the necessity for three days per week working on campus, and the expectation of certain days/periods per year which will require full time working on campus, (Welcome week, graduation days, etc) What will you get in return: Fantastic market leading Pension scheme Excellent employee health and wellbeing services including an Employee Assistance Programme Exceptional starting annual leave entitlement, plus bank holidays Additional paid closure over the Christmas period Local and national discounts at a range of major retailers As an equal opportunities employer we welcome applicants from all sections of the community regardless of age, sex, gender (or gender identity), ethnicity, disability, sexual orientation and transgender status. All appointments are made on merit. Our University is positive about flexible working – you can find out more here Please be aware that due to the number of applications we are unfortunately not able to provide individual feedback on your application. Please note that we are unable to respond to enquiries, accept CVs or applications from Recruitment Agencies. Any recruitment enquiries from recruitment agencies should be directed to people.talent@manchester.ac.uk Any CV’s submitted by a recruitment agency will be considered a gift. Please note this role is not eligible for sponsorship under the Skilled Worker route of the Points Based System. Candidates will need to be able to demonstrate their right to work in the UK in order to be eligible to take up the post. Enquiries about the vacancy, shortlisting and interviews: Name: Cerys Jones Email: cerys.jones@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries: Email: recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical support: https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home This vacancy will close for applications at midnight on the closing date. Please see the link below for the Further Particulars document which contains the person specification criteria. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46238": {
      "title": "Finance Assistant Accounts Payable(Redeployees Only)",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Finance Assistant Accounts Payable Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Finance Assistant Accounts Payable (Redeployees Only) Apply for job Job reference: PSX-032576 Salary: UoM Grade 3 £27,319 - £28,778 pro rata depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Professional Services Location: Hybrid Working on Campus/Remote Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Directorate of Finance Hours Per Week: 35 hours per week (1 FTE) Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 03/11/2026 Contract Duration: Fixed term for 12 months School/Directorate: Directorate of Finance Job Description The Accounts Payable Office is part of Transactional Services based in the Finance Directorate. The office is responsible for processing, checking, monitoring and payment of transactions on the University’s financial ledgers. Finance Assistants will each be part of a small team within the Accounts Payables Office, reporting to a Team Leader. It is anticipated that all Finance Assistants will rotate between each of the three teams within the office being input of invoices, resolutions and helpdesk. What you will get in return: Fantastic market leading Pension scheme Excellent employee health and wellbeing services including an Employee Assistance Programme Exceptional starting annual leave entitlement, plus bank holidays Additional paid closure over the Christmas period Local and national discounts at a range of major retailers As an equal opportunities employer we welcome applicants from all sections of the community regardless of age, sex, gender (or gender identity), ethnicity, disability, sexual orientation and transgender status. All appointments are made on merit. Our University is positive about flexible working – you can find out more here Hybrid working arrangements may be considered. Please be aware that due to the number of applications we unfortunately may not able to provide individual feedback on your application. Please note that we are unable to respond to enquiries, accept CVs or applications from Recruitment Agencies. Any recruitment enquiries from recruitment agencies should be directed to People.Recruitment@manchester.ac.uk . Any CV’s submitted by a recruitment agency will be considered a gift. Please note this role is not eligible for sponsorship under the Skilled Worker route of the Points Based System. Candidates will need to be able to demonstrate their right to work in the UK in order to be eligible to take up the post. Enquiries about the vacancy, shortlisting and interviews: Name: Jacqueline Lorenzelli Email: Jacqueline.Lorenzelli@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries: Email: People.recruitment@manchester.ac.uk Technical support: Jobtrain Client Support Hub This vacancy will close for applications at midnight on the closing date. Please see the link below for the Further Particulars document which contains the person specification criteria Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46141": {
      "title": "Head of Finance (Education Development)",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Head of Finance (Education Development) Cookie Policy By clicking \"Allow Cookies\", you agree to the storing of cookies on your device to analyse site usage, and assist in our marketing efforts. Cookie Policy Decline Allow cookies Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Head of Finance (Education Development) Apply for job Job reference: PSX-032471 Salary: £47,389 - £58,225 per annum depending on experience, with a £4000 market supplement Faculty/Organisational Unit: Professional Services Location: Manchester Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Financial Accounting Hours Per Week: 35 hours per week (1 FTE) Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 02/11/2026 Contract Duration: Fixed term for 12 months School/Directorate: Directorate of Finance Job Description We are seeking a motivated and collaborative individual to join our team as Head of Finance (Education Development). This role will work alongside the Head of Education Development and the Director of International Development as Finance Business Partner to provide operational and strategic support on all financial matters. You will be responsible for: Oversee monthly management accounts across two finance systems to tight deadlines, reviewing and challenging results. Develop and present insightful management information, KPIs and financial analysis for numerous audiences. Lead, develop and manage a professional UK finance team and provide indirect line management of finance and administrative staff in international centres to meet Education Development objectives and strategy. Review UoMW Ltd cash flow requirements to ensure international centres can operate within available cash reserves and meet obligations as they fall due. We welcome candidates who bring diverse perspectives, experiences, and approaches to their work. About You We encourage applications from individuals with a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. You should demonstrate: Essential Criteria A recognised CCAB professional accountancy qualification or equivalent. Experience of working with complex international financial processes and multiple currencies. Experienced in leading, managing and developing teams with the ability to think in strategic terms and exercise leadership. Strong analytical skills to enable production of meaningful financial reporting and convert data to management information easily understood by nonaccountants. Excellent communication and interpersonal skills to build networks and to engage and influence internal and external colleagues. Desirable Criteria Experience and knowledge of higher education in the UK and internationally is desirable. We value transferable skills and real-world experience as much as formal qualifications. Our benefits include: • Generous employer contribution pension • 29 days annual leave plus bank holidays, along with Christmas closure • Ride to work and EV car scheme available For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Philip Pearson Email Address: philip.pearson@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Watch: The University of Manchester – our new strategy to 2035 Supporting Documents save_alt PSX-032471 Head of Finance (Educational Development) (new) (PDF, KB) Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46182": {
      "title": "Senior Technician (Mass Spectrometry & Separations)",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Senior Technician (Mass Spectrometry &amp; Separations) Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Senior Technician (Mass Spectrometry & Separations) Apply for job Job reference: SAE-032524 Salary: UoM Grade 5 £33,002 - £36,636 per annum depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Science and Engineering Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: FSE Technical Services Hours Per Week: 35 hours Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 02/11/2026 Contract Duration: Approximately 24 months – precise contract length will be dependent on start date. Contract end date is fixed as Friday 29th September 2028 School/Directorate: Faculty Office - Science and Engineering Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description We are seeking a motivated and collaborative individual to join our team as a Technical Specialist (Mass Spectrometry & Separations). This role offers an exciting opportunity to join the Mass Spectrometry & Separations team and provide technical support to research and teaching within the School of Chemistry and the wider University. You will be responsible for the oversight and usage of a range of analytical instrumentation including but not limited to, liquid chromatography-mass spectrometry, gas chromatography-mass spectrometry and matrix assisted laser desorption ionisation-mass spectrometry. You will work as part of a team to provide timely provision of analytical results and interpretation thereof, as a fully serviced analytical facility. You will be responsible for: - Ensure the delivery of an excellent mass spectrometry service provision Inc. accurate mass (ESI, APCI), MALDI-MS. - Operation, calibration and routine maintenance of a range of analytical techniques including but not limited to chromatography systems and hyphenated mass spectrometers. - Liaison with academic and research staff including the interpretation and reporting of accurate and reliable data. - Supervise, motivate and develop junior staff and University of Manchester technical apprentices. - Implement, comply with and monitor all relevant health and safety requirements in the workplace in order for the University to meet its legislative requirements and duty of care to all staff, students and visitors. We welcome candidates who bring diverse perspectives, experiences, and approaches to their work. About You We encourage applications from individuals with a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. You should demonstrate: Essential Criteria: - A qualification in a science or engineering-related subject area e.g. degree, HND, HNC, OR significant work experience in a relevant technical, engineering or scientific role. - Substantial hands-on experience with mass spectrometry and chromatography equipment. - Substantial knowledge of chemical nomenclature and experience of interpreting complex data. - Ability to lead and deliver technical operations. - Excellent knowledge, understanding and experience of implementing and applying relevant health and safety requirements in a science and engineering environment (e.g. risk assessments, operating procedures, COSHH assessments). Desirable Criteria: - Knowledge of teaching and research operations gained from within a Higher Education setting. - Line management / supervisory experience. We value transferable skills and real-world experience as much as formal qualifications. Our benefits include: - Generous employer pension contribution - Christmas closure across all departments outside of annual leave and bank holidays - Purple Place employee discount platform For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Contract: Approximately 24 months. Precise contract length will be dependent on start date. Contract end date is fixed as Friday 29 September 2028. Working arrangements: 35 hours per week. Due to the in-person requirements of this role, hybrid working is not feasible. Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Dr Katherine Hollywood, Senior Technical Specialist Mass Spectrometry & Separations Email Address: Katherine.Hollywood@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support Jobtrain Client Support Hub This role is not eligible for Skilled Worker visa sponsorship. Applicants must demonstrate the right to work in the UK. Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46163": {
      "title": "Histology Technician",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Histology Technician Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Histology Technician Apply for job Job reference: BMH-032503 Salary: £29,588 - £32,080 per annum depending on experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Biology, Medicine Health Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: FBMH Technical Services Hours Per Week: 21 hours per week (0.6 FTE) Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 02/11/2026 Contract Duration: Fixed term for 24 months School/Directorate: Faculty Office - Biology, Medicine and Health Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description We are seeking a motivated and collaborative individual to join our team as a Technician – Manchester Brain Bank. The role holder will provide specialist technical support for the Manchester Brain Bank, enabling the safe, compliant and effective processing, storage and use of human tissue for diagnostic neuropathology and research. Working across histology, immunohistochemistry, molecular testing, quality assurance and laboratory governance, the post holder will help ensure that high-quality, clinically and pathologically annotated tissue and associated data are available to researchers within The University of Manchester, across the UK and internationally. You will be responsible for: Receiving, processing, cataloguing, storing and distributing human tissue samples in line with Human Tissue Authority licensing requirements, University policies and approved procedures. Undertaking specialist histological and immunohistochemical staining to support diagnostic neuropathological assessment and approved research projects. Performing and supporting basic molecular testing, including APOE genotyping and method optimisation for DNA extraction from archived tissue. Maintaining accurate sample, testing, staining and laboratory activity records to support traceability, quality assurance and reporting. Supporting the day-to-day operation of the laboratory, including equipment maintenance, stock control, reagent preparation, ordering, housekeeping and safe storage of materials. About You Essential Criteria Degree-level qualification, or equivalent experience, in biomedical science, biological sciences, molecular biology, biomedical research or a closely related discipline. Practical laboratory experience in histology, immunohistochemistry, molecular testing or a closely related biomedical research setting. Sound understanding of the Human Tissue Act 2004, Human Tissue Authority Codes of Practice and governance requirements for the storage and use of human tissue for research. Excellent attention to detail and commitment to accurate record keeping, sample traceability, quality assurance and reproducible laboratory practice. Ability to optimise laboratory techniques, troubleshoot technical issues and adapt protocols to improve reliability, efficiency and responsible tissue use. Desirable Criteria Experience of molecular techniques such as APOE genotyping and DNA extraction from frozen, fixed or archived tissue. Familiarity with digital pathology platforms, image analysis software or their application to neuropathological assessment or research workflows. Our benefits include: Generous employer pension contribution Christmas closure across all departments outside of annual leave and bank holidays Purple Place employee discount platform For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Caroline Grimshaw Email Address: caroline.grimshaw@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home This role is not eligible for Skilled Worker visa sponsorship. Applicants must demonstrate the right to work in the UK. Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46082": {
      "title": "Senior Technician (Aerospace Teaching)",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Senior Technician (Aerospace Teaching) Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Senior Technician (Aerospace Teaching) Apply for job Job reference: SAE-032428 Salary: £33,002 to £36,636 per annum, depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Science and Engineering Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Permanent Division/Team: FSE Technical Services Hours Per Week: 35 hours Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 06/11/2026 Contract Duration: Permanent School/Directorate: Faculty Office - Science and Engineering Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description The Senior Technician (Aerospace Teaching) provides specialist technical advice, practical support and day-to-day coordination across Aerospace teaching spaces. Working with the Teaching Technical Specialist and the wider technical team, the role prepares, operates, maintains and demonstrates equipment used in practical classes, supports students and staff, and helps plan resources and improvements. The role makes a direct difference to the student experience by ensuring that Aerospace teaching is safe, reliable, inclusive and delivered to a consistently high standard. As Technical Staff, you are part of Professional Services. Together, all technical staff who work within the Faculty of Science and Engineering (FSE) form FSE Technical Operations. FSE Technical Operations is organised into five key functional areas: • Research • Teaching • Infrastructure & Facilities • Workshops • Computing, Robotics & AI Within FSE Technical Operations, you will belong to the Teaching area. Teaching - The teaching technical team provides high quality technical advice and support to students of all years of education. They also support academic staff in the diverse range of areas within the Faculty of Science and Engineering. The team are led by several Teaching Technical Specialists (TTS) who are responsible for managing and coordinating technical support for teaching in each of their specific disciplines. This includes all related teaching space, associated equipment and ensuring all Health and Safety obligations are met. The team support a host of taught units within each department. These can be based in computer clusters, bespoke labs or other shared areas. They also support student projects, student societies and field trips in the areas of Civil engineering, Aero and Earth Science. The team works closely with Timetabling, Discipline Heads of Education and laboratory tutors to advise on experiment design and the technical resource required for its delivery. Within the Teaching area, you will be part of the Aerospace Teaching Technical Team. The Aerospace Teaching Technical Team supports practical teaching, student projects, student societies and field activities across Aerospace Engineering. The team prepares and maintains specialist facilities including internal combustion and jet engine test rigs, fluids and smoke laboratories, flight simulators and field events. Team members work closely with academic staff, laboratory tutors, timetabling colleagues and students to plan practical delivery, provide technical guidance and ensure that equipment and teaching spaces are safe, ready and fit for purpose. The team operates as a collective, with members cross-trained across key activities to provide resilience and continuity of support. While all technicians are able to support the wider Aerospace teaching portfolio, individuals are encouraged to develop and lead in areas aligned with their particular skills, experience and technical strengths. What you will get in return: Fantastic market leading Pension scheme Excellent employee health and wellbeing services including an Employee Assistance Programme Exceptional starting annual leave entitlement, plus bank holidays Additional paid closure over the Christmas period Local and national discounts at a range of major retailers As an equal opportunities employer we welcome applicants from all sections of the community regardless of age, sex, gender (or gender identity), ethnicity, disability, sexual orientation and transgender status. All appointments are made on merit. Our University is positive about flexible working – you can find out more here Please be aware that due to the number of applications we unfortunately may not able to provide individual feedback on your application. Please note that we are unable to respond to enquiries, accept CVs or applications from Recruitment Agencies. Any recruitment enquiries from recruitment agencies should be directed to recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Any CV’s submitted by a recruitment agency will be considered a gift. Enquiries about the vacancy, shortlisting and interviews: Name: Natalie Parish Email: Natalie.Parish@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries: Email: recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical support: 0161 850 2004 https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home This vacancy will close for applications at midnight on the closing date. Please see the link below for the Further Particulars document which contains the person specification criteria. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46018": {
      "title": "BSF Business Administrator",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for BSF Business Administrator Cookie Policy By clicking \"Allow Cookies\", you agree to the storing of cookies on your device to analyse site usage, and assist in our marketing efforts. Cookie Policy Decline Allow cookies Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results BSF Business Administrator Apply for job Job reference: BMH-032361 Salary: £33,002 - £36,636 per annum depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Biology, Medicine Health Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Permanent Division/Team: FBMH Research and Innovation Hours Per Week: 35 Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 09/11/2026 Contract Duration: Permanent School/Directorate: FBMH Research and Innovation Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description Overall Purpose The primary purpose of this role is to provide efficient, high-quality administrative and transactional financial support to the Biological Services Facility (BSF) and to foster positive relations with internal and external stakeholders. A critical dimension of the post involves providing administrative oversight and data management during the facility’s transition to the new MCMS animal management system, alongside ensuring robust day-to-day financial operations, billing, and reporting. About the Team The post sits within the Core Technology Platforms (CTP) operational framework within the Faculty of Biology, Medicine and Health. The role directly supports the BSF Senior Leadership Team—comprising the Strategic Director, Operations Manager, Compliance and Licensing Manager, Named Training and Competency and 3Rs Manager, and the Aquatics, Occupational Health, Safety and Wellbeing Manager— providing central coordination to modernize facility workflows and maintain compliance and service excellence across regulated scientific facilities. Our benefits include: (Grades 1-5) Generous employer pension contribution Christmas closure across all departments outside of annual leave and bank holidays Purple Place employee discount platform For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Vicky Taylor Email: Vicky.Taylor-2@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Supporting Documents save_alt BMH-032361 BSF Business Administrator FP's (PDF, KB) Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=45848": {
      "title": "Post Management Assistant",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Post Management Assistant Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Post Management Assistant Apply for job Job reference: PSX-032202 Salary: £30,180 - £32,722 per annum, depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Professional Services Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: People Services Hours Per Week: 35 hours Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 27/10/2026 Contract Duration: 9 months School/Directorate: People Directorate Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description The University of Manchester is seeking a detail‑focused and customer‑centred Post Management Assistant to support the accurate creation, maintenance and governance of Positions within our People & OD system, Oracle Fusion HCM. This role is central to ensuring our organisational structure, reporting lines and Position data remain reliable, up to date and aligned with Finance systems and University policy. Working within the People Systems team, you will act as a key point of contact for colleagues across People & OD, Finance and wider University stakeholders. You will help maintain the integrity of Position information, support seamless updates to Position and structures, and contribute to the development of our Employee and Manager Self‑Service offering. About You We welcome applications from individuals with a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. You should demonstrate: Strong organisational and administrative skills, with the ability to manage competing priorities. Excellent communication skills, both written and verbal, with confidence engaging with colleagues at all levels. A detail‑oriented approach and the ability to maintain high levels of accuracy. Experience using standard IT packages and ideally experience with Oracle Fusion HCM, ResourceLink or similar HR/Payroll systems. The ability to extract, manipulate and interpret data using reporting tools such as Power BI. An understanding of data protection principles and their practical application. A commitment to continuous improvement and delivering a customer‑focused service. The ability to work both independently and collaboratively within a team. We value transferable skills and real‑world experience as much as formal qualifications. What you’ll do: Provide frontline support via telephone and email to colleagues across the University. Set up and maintain Positions on Oracle Fusion HCM, ensuring appropriate authorisation and timely updates. Work closely with Employment Services to ensure Position changes are processed accurately and efficiently. Liaise with Finance colleagues and budget holders to maintain accurate Position funding information and ensure finance codes are active ahead of payroll deadlines. Support shared protocols between People & OD and Finance to ensure alignment of Position data across systems. Maintain Position‑to‑Position reporting structures and support organisational unit changes and restructures. Produce regular data quality reports, investigate anomalies and resolve issues relating to funding/activity codes or incorrect Position selection. Support HESA data requirements through monthly housekeeping and ensuring Position information meets reporting standards. Assist in developing and improving Position management processes, documentation and stakeholder communications. Attend meetings with senior stakeholders to review staff lists, reporting structures and data accuracy. Contribute to the continuous development of the Systems function and promote equality, diversity and inclusion in all aspects of your work. Undertake ad hoc duties commensurate with the role. This is a hands‑on role for someone who enjoys working with data, systems and people. You’ll play a key part in maintaining the integrity of our organisational structure and supporting colleagues across People & OD and Finance. Our benefits include: Generous employer pension contribution Christmas closure across all departments outside of annual leave and bank holidays Purple Place employee discount platform For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Kish Lad Title: Head of People Technology Experience Email Address: kish.lad@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home This role is not eligible for Skilled Worker visa sponsorship. Applicants must demonstrate the right to work in the UK. Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=45579": {
      "title": "Research Technician (neuro-oncology)",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Research Technician (neuro-oncology) Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Research Technician (neuro-oncology) Apply for job Job reference: BMH-031935 Salary: UoM Grade 4  £30,180 - £32,722 pro rata depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Biology, Medicine Health Location: Manchester Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: FBMH Technical Services Hours Per Week: 35 hours per week (1 FTE) Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 03/11/2026 Contract Duration: Fixed term for 12 months School/Directorate: Faculty Office - Biology, Medicine and Health Job Description We are excited to offer this opportunity for a Research Technician to deliver high-quality technical support across neuro-oncology research, including managing clinical samples and maintaining laboratory infrastructure. The postholder will work independently and collaboratively, applying technical expertise, careful data management and regulatory compliance to support safe and efficient research operations. By enabling robust workflows and supporting researchers and clinical studies, the role will help generate reliable data and advance translational research that aims to improve patient outcomes. This is a 1.0 FTE fixed-term role for 12 months, primarily based at Salford Royal Hospital and working across University and NHS environments, with 1–2 days per month at the Paterson Building. The postholder will contribute to clinical trials and translational research focused on improving the diagnosis, monitoring and treatment of brain tumours, and must be able to start at 7am when patient sample collections are scheduled. About the Team This role sits within a collaborative translational neuro-oncology research network at the University of Manchester and the Geoffrey Jefferson Research Centre, working across academic and clinical settings. The postholder will work with Professor Petra Hamerlik, Dr Gerben Borst and Dr Claire O’Leary, supporting research that connects patient recruitment, sample collection and advanced biomarker analysis. What you will get in return: Market-leading pension scheme Employee health and wellbeing services, including an Employee Assistance Programme Generous annual leave, plus bank holidays Additional paid closure over the Christmas period Local and national retail discounts As an equal opportunities employer, we welcome applicants from all sections of the community. All appointments are made on merit. Our University is positive about flexible working. Hybrid working may be considered. We cannot respond to enquiries, accept CVs or applications from recruitment agencies. Recruitment agency enquiries should be directed to People.Recruitment@manchester.ac.uk . Any CVs submitted by an agency will be considered a gift. This role is not eligible for sponsorship under the Skilled Worker route. Candidates must demonstrate their right to work in the UK. Enquiries about the vacancy, shortlisting and interviews: Name: Petra Hamerlik, Chair of Translational NeuroOncology Email: petra.hamerik@manchester.ac.uk Technical support: https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home This vacancy closes at midnight on the closing date. Please see the Further Particulars document for the person specification criteria. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=34238": {
      "title": "Research Associate",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Research Associate Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Research Associate Apply for job Job reference: BMH-030650 Salary: £37,694 per annum Faculty/Organisational Unit: Biology, Medicine Health Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Division of Musculoskeletal and Dermatological Science Hours Per Week: Full Time Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 02/11/2026 Contract Duration: 24 months School/Directorate: School of Biological Sciences Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description The Rice Lab within the Division of Musculoskeletal and Dermatological Sciences wish to appoint a laboratory-based Research Associate with mass spectrometry-based proteomics experience to an exciting project exploring the effect of collagen glycosylation on cartilage health in the context of osteoarthritis. We seek a highly motivated postdoctoral mass spectrometrist/proteomics expert with cell biology experience. During this position, the post-holder will undertake a range of molecular and cell biological activities, including use of CRISPR-Cas9, primary cell culture, and western blotting, followed by downstream LC-MS/MS to characterise the chondrocyte and extracellular matrix proteome. The successful candidate would be expected to perform experiments independently and to work alongside the Principal Investigator to conceptualise studies, design experiments and publish their findings in appropriate journals. The role will involve contributing to a team-science approach in the laboratory, supporting other team members and trainees intellectually and practically and working collaboratively with internal and external collaborators. This will be a highly collaborative project with the proteomics laboratory of Professor Matthias Trost at the University of Manchester. The successful candidate would be expected to be curious and rigorous, with demonstrable knowledge of cell biology relevant to the research focus of the laboratory and substantial hands-on experience with mass spectrometry platforms. What you will get in return: · Fantastic market leading Pension scheme · Excellent employee health and wellbeing services including an Employee Assistance Programme · Exceptional starting annual leave entitlement, plus bank holidays · Additional paid closure over the Christmas period · Local and national discounts at a range of major retailers As an equal opportunities employer we welcome applicants from all sections of the community regardless of age, sex, gender (or gender identity), ethnicity, disability, sexual orientation and transgender status. All appointments are made on merit. Our University is positive about flexible working. Hybrid working arrangements may be considered. Please be aware that due to the number of applications we are unfortunately not able to provide individual feedback on your application. Please note that we are unable to respond to enquiries, accept CVs or applications from Recruitment Agencies. Any recruitment enquiries from recruitment agencies should be directed to recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Any CV’s submitted by a recruitment agency will be considered a gift. Enquiries about the vacancy, shortlisting and interviews: Name: Dr Sarah Rice Email: sarah.rice@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries: Email: recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical support: https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home Interviews will take place on the 23rd and 24th of February. This vacancy will close for applications at midnight on the closing date. Please see the link below for the Further Particulars document which contains the person specification criteria. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46343": {
      "title": "Research Impact Associate",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Research Impact Associate Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Research Impact Associate Apply for job Job reference: SAE-032703 Salary: £38,448 - £46,970 per annum depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Science and Engineering Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: FSE Research & Business Services Hours Per Week: 1 FTE Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 10/11/2026 Contract Duration: 28 months School/Directorate: Faculty Office - Science and Engineering Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description The Faculty of Science and Engineering is seeking a Research Impact Associate with a background in surveying and evaluation to develop innovative approaches for evidencing the reach and significance of open-source research resources. Working closely with the Associate Dean for REF, academic colleagues and Professional Services teams, you will explore how the impact of non-standard research outputs—including software, datasets, and regulatory or design standards—can be identified and demonstrated among external users with whom the University may not have an established relationship. You will work with the impact team and academic case authors to strengthen the evidence underpinning prospective impact case studies for the Research Excellence Framework (REF) 2028 and inform the faculty’s longer-term research impact strategy. Expectations of the role You will design and apply qualitative and quantitative research techniques to understand how open-access resources are used and the difference they make to external, non-academic users. You will work with academics and external stakeholders to develop appropriate engagement and data-collection strategies, coordinate project activity, assess the effectiveness of different approaches, and translate findings into practical recommendations. You will establish frameworks for managing project deliverables and support colleagues to articulate research impact through high-quality reports, communications and REF impact cases. You will also prepare presentations and publications on methods and findings, contribute to strategic discussions with senior stakeholders, and develop training that enables researchers to integrate effective impact evaluation into their projects. This is an opportunity to shape emerging practice in research impact evaluation and help demonstrate how open-source research creates value beyond the University. You will be encouraged to share successful approaches across the faculty and to publish and present your work to wider academic audiences. Candidate skills You will hold a PhD or have equivalent relevant experience. You will bring experience of developing and evaluating remote or in-person quantitative and qualitative data-collection approaches, alongside a track record of building effective professional relationships with external audiences. You will be able to manage multiple deadlines and shifting priorities, work independently while contributing effectively to a team, and communicate complex methods and findings clearly to different audiences. Strong analytical skills are essential, including the ability to evaluate and present outcomes from complex quantitative datasets. You should also have an awareness of research impact assessment in the context of REF and its relationship to research funding. Experience of working in higher education, developing training or external engagement activities, managing stakeholder engagement in line with GDPR and ethical requirements, evidencing impact, or publishing research methods and findings would be advantageous. (Grades 6-9) Our benefits include: • Generous employer contribution pension • 29 days annual leave plus bank holidays, along with Christmas closure • Ride to work and EV car scheme available For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Tim Stallard Email: Tim.Stallard@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative suppor t recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://knowledge.jobtrain.co.uk/jobseeker [knowledge.jobtrain.co.uk] Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46369": {
      "title": "RESEARCH FELLOW IN ARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE AND MACHINE LEARNING FOR SCIENTIFIC DISCOVERY",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for RESEARCH FELLOW IN ARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE AND MACHINE LEARNING FOR SCIENTIFIC DISCOVERY Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results RESEARCH FELLOW IN ARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE AND MACHINE LEARNING FOR SCIENTIFIC DISCOVERY Apply for job Job reference: SAE-032705 Salary: £48,337 - £59,390 per annum depending on experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Science and Engineering Location: Manchester Institute of Biotechnology Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Department of Chemistry Hours Per Week: Full time (1 FTE) Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 10/11/2026 Contract Duration: Fixed term for 24 months School/Directorate: School of Natural Sciences Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description The Chica Research Group, based within the Manchester Institute of Biotechnology and the Department of Chemistry at the University of Manchester, develops computational and experimental approaches for protein engineering and enzyme design. We are seeking an outstanding researcher with expertise in artificial intelligence, machine learning, computer science, applied mathematics, statistics, or a related quantitative discipline to join our highly interdisciplinary research environment. The successful candidate will work closely with computational and experimental researchers to develop and apply modern data-driven approaches to challenges in biomolecular design and engineering. Prior experience in chemistry, biology, or protein science is advantageous but not required. This position offers an exciting opportunity to contribute to the development of next-generation computational approaches for scientific discovery while helping to expand AI and machine-learning capabilities within the research group. Overall Purpose of the Job The postholder will develop and apply machine-learning and artificial-intelligence approaches to challenges in protein engineering and enzyme design. Working closely with researchers across the group, the successful candidate will analyse complex biological, chemical, and experimental datasets; develop predictive models and computational tools; and identify opportunities where machine learning and artificial intelligence can accelerate scientific discovery. The role offers substantial scope for methodological innovation and provides an opportunity to contribute to the future development of AI-enabled research within the group. You will be responsible for: Developing and applying machine-learning approaches to challenges in protein engineering and enzyme design. Analysing, integrating, and modelling large biological, chemical, and experimental datasets. Designing, implementing, training, and evaluating predictive machine-learning models. Developing computational tools, workflows, software, and methodologies that support data-driven scientific discovery and expand the group's computational capabilities. Providing specialist expertise, guidance, training, and support in machine learning, data science, and artificial intelligence across the research group. Collaborating with researchers from diverse disciplinary backgrounds to identify and address scientific challenges using AI and machine-learning approaches. Working closely with postdoctoral researchers, postgraduate students, and other team members on computational and experimental projects. Contributing to collaborative research projects, publications, conference presentations, and funding applications. Maintaining high standards of software development, documentation, and reproducible research. Contributing to the strategic development of the group's computational and AI research activities. Keeping abreast of developments in artificial intelligence, machine learning, data science, and scientific computing. We are particularly interested in applicants who: Enjoy working in highly collaborative and interdisciplinary research environments. Are motivated to develop innovative computational approaches to scientific challenges. Are enthusiastic about learning new technologies and methods and applying them to frontier research problems. Our benefits include: • Generous employer contribution pension • 29 days annual leave plus bank holidays, along with Christmas closure • Ride to work and EV car scheme available For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: David Leys Email Address: david.leys@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46227": {
      "title": "Research Assistant",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Research Assistant Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Research Assistant Apply for job Job reference: BMH-032565 Salary: £33,662 - £37,369 per annum depending on experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Biology, Medicine Health Location: Manchester Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Division of Medical Education Hours Per Week: 35 hours per week (1 FTE) Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 10/11/2026 Contract Duration: Fixed term for 4 months School/Directorate: School of Medical Sciences Job Description The posts will support the evaluation of the Manchester University NHS Foundation Trust (MFT) Live Well Hubs (LWH) programme and the post holder will be part of the Public Health and Community Medicine Group in the Division of Medical Education in the School of Medical Sciences working with MFT and delivery partners working across the city. Our benefits include: Generous employer pension contribution Christmas closure across all departments outside of annual leave and bank holidays Purple Place employee discount platform For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits. You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here. We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy. Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Arpana Verma Email Address: arpana.verma@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home This role is not eligible for Skilled Worker visa sponsorship. Applicants must demonstrate the right to work in the UK. Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46272": {
      "title": "Assistant Registrar (Maternity Cover)",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Assistant Registrar (Maternity Cover) Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Assistant Registrar (Maternity Cover) Apply for job Job reference: CIX-032622 Salary: £29,588 - £32,080 per annum depending on experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Cultural Institutions Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Whitworth Art Gallery Administration Hours Per Week: 35 hours per week (1 FTE) Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 10/11/2026 Contract Duration: Fixed term for 9 months School/Directorate: Whitworth Art Gallery Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description We are seeking an Assistant Registrar (Maternity Cover) to join the Collections and Exhibitions team at the Whitworth. You will support the work of the Gallery Registrar, help coordinate arrangements for all inbound and outbound loans, and support the planning and delivery of a diverse and ambitious programme of exhibitions including national and international touring projects, and artist projects. You will be responsible for: Support the Gallery Registrar with inbound and outbound loans, ensuring paperwork is completed accurately and on time. Maintain accurate, up-to-date records, including project files, reporting documents and EMu. Coordinate contracts, insurance, packing and transport for incoming exhibitions and outbound loans from the collection. Support national and international touring exhibitions, and artist projects. Liaise directly with lenders and the Collection Care and Access Team in relation to display specifications and packing, including documenting necessary information. We welcome candidates who bring diverse perspectives, experiences, and approaches to their work. About You We encourage applications from individuals with a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. You should demonstrate: Essential Criteria Demonstrable experience working with registrar or exhibition administration tasks within a museum or gallery. Excellent organisational skills with the ability to process information promptly, to act decisively and within tight timeframes. A collaborative and methodical approach and proven attention to detail. Excellent interpersonal, written, and verbal communication skills. A degree in History of Art, Visual Culture, Cultural Studies, History or other related discipline or demonstrable equivalent experience. Desirable Criteria Knowledge of one or more foreign languages. Experience of EMu or equivalent collections management system. Post-graduate qualification in Museum Studies or another relevant subject. An enthusiasm for textiles, wallpaper, historic, modern and contemporary art and artists. We value transferable skills and real-world experience as much as formal qualifications. Our benefits include: Generous employer pension contribution Christmas closure across all departments outside of annual leave and bank holidays Purple Place employee discount platform For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Jamilla Briggs Email Address: jamilla.e.briggs@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home This role is not eligible for Skilled Worker visa sponsorship. Applicants must demonstrate the right to work in the UK. Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Watch: From Manchester for the world – our new strategy to 2035 Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=45711": {
      "title": "Dalton Research Fellow",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Dalton Research Fellow Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Dalton Research Fellow Apply for job Job reference: SAE-032063 Salary: £48,337 - £59,390 per annum depending on experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Science and Engineering Location: Manchester Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Dalton Nuclear Institute Hours Per Week: Full Time (1 FTE) Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 21/12/2026 Contract Duration: 48 Months School/Directorate: FSE Research Institutes Job Description We are seeking outstanding scientists and engineers at an early stage in their academic careers to join our team as a Dalton Research Fellow. Fellows should show a high level of innovation and ambition in their ideas, and want to develop potentially transformative, high-impact research. The Fellowship is an excellent stepping stone to establishing an academic career in nuclear science or engineering. You will be responsible for: Plan, develop and conduct world-leading research Win external funding for research Supervise postgraduate and postdoctoral researchers Engage with the nuclear sector and form positive working relationship between and across external academic, industrial and policy stakeholders Actively contribute to the Dalton nuclear community (e.g. engage in Dalton outreach activities, Dalton early career researcher development and networking, and/or contribute to the Dalton policy group) We welcome candidates who bring diverse perspectives, experiences, and approaches to their work. About You We encourage applications from individuals with a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. You should demonstrate: Essential Criteria: A relevant PhD (or equivalent qualification/experience) awarded prior to commencing the appointment Ability to develop a coherent world-leading research programme Ability to plan and build a team and deliver outstanding results Strong understanding of the importance of the impact of your research programme on the nuclear sector - either industrial partners or government stakeholders Excellent communication and interpersonal skills We value transferable skills and real-world experience as much as formal qualifications. Our benefits include: • Generous employer contribution pension • 29 days annual leave plus bank holidays, along with Christmas closure • Ride to work and EV car scheme available For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Philip Edmondson and Clint Sharrad Email Address: philip.edmondson@manchester.ac.uk ; clint.a.sharrad@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=45701": {
      "title": "Dalton Research Fellow",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Dalton Research Fellow Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Dalton Research Fellow Apply for job Job reference: SAE-032060 Salary: £48,337 - £59,390 per annum depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Science and Engineering Location: Manchester Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Dalton Nuclear Institute Hours Per Week: Full Time (1 FTE) Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 21/12/2026 Contract Duration: 48 Months School/Directorate: FSE Research Institutes Job Description We are seeking outstanding scientists and engineers at an early stage in their academic careers to join the flagship Dalton Nuclear Research Fellowship Scheme. Fellows should show a high level of innovation and ambition in their ideas, and want to develop potentially transformative, high-impact research. Each Fellowship is for an initial four-year period and is an excellent stepping stone to establishing an academic career in nuclear science or engineering. You will be responsible for: Plan, develop and conduct world-leading research Win external funding for research Supervise postgraduate and postdoctoral researchers Engage with the nuclear sector and form positive working relationship between and across external academic, industrial and policy stakeholders Actively contribute to the Dalton nuclear community (e.g. engage in Dalton outreach activities, Dalton early career researcher development and networking, and/or contribute to the Dalton policy group) We welcome candidates who bring diverse perspectives, experiences, and approaches to their work. About You We encourage applications from individuals with a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. You should demonstrate: Essential Criteria A relevant PhD (or equivalent qualification/experience) awarded prior to commencing the appointment Ability to develop a coherent world-leading research programme Ability to plan and build a team and deliver outstanding results Strong understanding of the importance of the impact of your research programme on the nuclear sector – either industrial partners or government stakeholders Excellent communication and interpersonal skills We value transferable skills and real-world experience as much as formal qualifications. Our benefits include: Generous employer contribution pension 29 days annual leave plus bank holidays, along with Christmas closure Ride to work and EV car scheme available For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Applications should be submitted via the application form together with the following supporting documents noting the maximum page numbers for each document: 1. Curriculum Vitae (2 pages) 2. Please also upload your Research Proposal (2 pages), Academic Impact Statement (1 page) and Statement of Strategic Fit (1 page). These three documents must be combined into a single pdf (4 pages in total) Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Names: Prof. Phil Edmondson and Prof. Clint Sharrad Email addresses: philip.edmondson@manchester.ac.uk ; clint.a.sharrad@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=34668": {
      "title": "Research Associate in Learning-based Predictive Control of Multi-Energy Systems",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Research Associate in  Learning-based Predictive Control of Multi-Energy Systems Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Research Associate in  Learning-based Predictive Control of Multi-Energy Systems Apply for job Job reference: SAE-031051 Salary: £37,694 - £46,049 per annum, depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Science and Engineering Location: Manchester Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Department of Electrical and Electronic Engineering Hours Per Week: 1 FTE Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 10/11/2026 Contract Duration: 18 months School/Directorate: School of Engineering Job Description Overall, Purpose of the Job We are looking for one Research Associate to work on the development, implementation and testing of predictive control algorithms for the optimal coordination of available multi-energy resources and network infrastructure and thus support and improve the operation of the energy networks and the performance and the sustainability of the urban energy system. Learning-based techniques (e.g., deep neural networks) will be used to automatically learn the system dynamics and the modelling errors, as well as to obtain an automatic tuning of the cost parameters/constraints or approximators of the control law. This will alleviate the modelling complexities and the online computational requirements of the control algorithms and provide them with learning, self-regulating and adaptive capabilities. The appointed Research Associate will collaborate with researchers from other UK universities working around multi-energy networks and multi-energy technologies, focusing on their modelling, optimisation and control, within the EPSRC-funded project Supergen Energy Network Hub. This research will contribute to provide leadership, research and networking for the energy networks and systems community to grow and come together to develop a deeper understanding of the interconnected and interdependent energy network infrastructure, e.g. electricity, heat, gas, hydrogen, as well as of the urban energy systems. Please note this post will also require UK travel to collaborating universities, as well as opportunities to present research at leading international conferences, as well as the ability to interact effectively with researchers from other disciplines and industrial partners. The post duration is likely to be extended by a time depending on the available funds and the performance. What will you get in return: Fantastic market leading Pension scheme Excellent employee health and wellbeing services including an Employee Assistance Programme Exceptional starting annual leave entitlement, plus bank holidays Additional paid closure over the Christmas period Local and national discounts at a range of major retailers As an equal opportunity employer, we welcome applicants from all sections of the community regardless of age, sex, gender (or gender identity), ethnicity, disability, sexual orientation and transgender status. All appointments are made on merit. Our university is positive about flexible working – you can find out more here Hybrid working arrangements may be considered. Please be aware that due to the number of applications we are unfortunately not able to provide individual feedback on your application. Please note that we are unable to respond to enquiries, accept CVs or applications from Recruitment Agencies. Any recruitment enquiries from recruitment agencies should be directed to people.talent@manchester.ac.uk Any CV’s submitted by a recruitment agency will be considered a gift. Enquiries about the vacancy, shortlisting and interviews: Name: Professor Alessandra Parisio Email: Alessandra.parisio@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries: Email: recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical support: https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home This vacancy will close for applications at midnight on the closing date. Please see the link below for the Further Particulars document which contains the person specification criteria. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46047": {
      "title": "Research Associate: Digital Twinning and In Silico Trials",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Research Associate: Digital Twinning and In Silico Trials Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Research Associate: Digital Twinning and In Silico Trials Apply for job Job reference: SAE-032389 Salary: UoM Grade 6 £37,694 - £46,049 per annum depending on experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Science and Engineering Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Department of Computer Science Hours Per Week: Full time (1 FTE) Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 11/11/2026 Contract Duration: Fixed term for 36 months School/Directorate: School of Engineering Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description We are seeking a motivated and collaborative individual to join our team as a Research Associate: Digital Twinning and In Silico Trials. This role offers an exciting opportunity to contribute to the University of Manchester's contribution to Federated Data Science UK, a national consortium building a federated network of secure health-data environments. You will lead the scientific delivery of its flagship use case, \"Accelerating Innovation – Transforming Medical Device Development Through In Silico Trials\", scaling in silico trials of cardiovascular and cerebrovascular medical devices to very large virtual-patient populations built from real-world imaging and electronic health record data. The opportunity is to help define the specifications and evaluation standards for running in silico device trials on national data infrastructure, accelerating the development, approval and adoption of safer medical devices. You will be responsible for: Leading the scientific delivery of the in silico trials use case, scaling the existing FD-PASS intracranial aneurysm device trial cohort more than a hundred-fold using digital twins built from federated imaging and electronic health record data, and informing device selection through head-to-head comparative in silico trials. Developing automated, efficient and reproducible workflows for image-based modelling and simulation of the cardiovascular and cerebrovascular systems, with and without implanted devices, suitable for deployment across federated secure data environments. Defining the use cases, technical specifications and data and compute requirements for in silico device trials and translating these into concrete requirements for national federated research infrastructure, including its interoperability and provenance tooling. Designing and delivering evaluation and benchmarking frameworks, including model credibility (verification, validation and uncertainty quantification), to assess device safety, efficacy and failure modes across virtual populations. Developing generative and statistical anatomical models, deep-learning models of flow fields, and person-specific physiological boundary conditions derived from real-world clinical and socioeconomic data, to build virtual populations for structural heart, aortic, carotid and cerebrovascular applications. We welcome candidates who bring diverse perspectives, experiences, and approaches to their work. About You We encourage applications from individuals with a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. You should demonstrate: Essential Criteria: A PhD (or equivalent research experience) in computational cardiovascular and/or cerebrovascular modelling, biomechanics, medical image analysis or a closely related field. Domain expertise in digital twinning and in silico trials (in silico medicine): the use of computational modelling and simulation for the design, evaluation and regulatory assessment of medical devices, including the construction of virtual patient (digital twin) cohorts. Advanced proficiency in finite element analysis and/or computational fluid dynamics for cardiovascular applications, using tools such as ANSYS (CFX, Fluent), COMSOL, OpenFOAM or FEniCS, with in-depth experience of both commercial and open-source simulation software for in silico trials and cardiovascular research. Strong software development skills in Python, with familiarity with C/C++ or MATLAB, for developing efficient algorithms in computational science and engineering. Excellent communication skills, able to explain complex technical information to varied audiences including clinical, regulatory and public partners and the scientific community, both in writing and in person. We value transferable skills and real-world experience as much as formal qualifications. Our benefits include: Generous employer contribution pension 29 days annual leave plus bank holidays, along with Christmas closure Ride to work and EV car scheme available For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Professor Alejandro Frangi Email Address: alejandro.frangi@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support Jobtrain Client Support Hub Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46230": {
      "title": "Research Assistant",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Research Assistant Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Research Assistant Apply for job Job reference: BMH-032566 Salary: UoM Grade 5  £33,662 - £37,369 pro rata depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Biology, Medicine Health Location: Manchester Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Division of Medical Education Hours Per Week: 0.9FTE Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 04/11/2026 Contract Duration: Fixed term for 4 months School/Directorate: School of Medical Sciences Job Description The posts will support the evaluation of the Manchester University NHS Foundation Trust (MFT) Live Well Hubs (LWH) programme and the post holder will be part of the Public Health and Community Medicine Group in the Division of Medical Education in the School of Medical Sciences working with MFT and delivery partners working across the city. Our benefits include: Generous employer pension contribution Christmas closure across all departments outside of annual leave and bank holidays Purple Place employee discount platform For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits. You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here. We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy. Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Arpana Verma Email Address: arpana.verma@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home This role is not eligible for Skilled Worker visa sponsorship. Applicants must demonstrate the right to work in the UK. Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46154": {
      "title": "Research Associate in Perceptions of Marine Carbon Removal",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Research Associate in Perceptions of Marine Carbon Removal Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Research Associate in Perceptions of Marine Carbon Removal Apply for job Job reference: HUM-032490 Salary: £38,448 to £40,704 per annum, depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Humanities Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Geography Hours Per Week: Full Time Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 04/11/2026 Contract Duration: From 1 February 2027 until 31 January 2030 School/Directorate: School of Environment, Education & Development Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description We are looking to appoint an outstanding Research Associate to undertake research on responsible innovation and societal engagement as part of the Measurement And Reporting for responsible marine CO2 removal (MAR-CO2) project, co-funded by UKRI and Horizon Europe. The post holder will undertake a range of research tasks in collaboration with the international and multidisciplinary research team, relating to stakeholder and public engagement on the conditions for responsible marine carbon dioxide removal (mCDR) and its monitoring, reporting and verification (MRV). These tasks include semi-structured interviews with mCDR experts and stakeholders, deliberative focus groups with members of the public, and an international citizen survey. The post holder will produce a range of deliverables and academic articles for publication in peer-reviewed academic journals. The job description and person specification are attached. Please read these carefully as they will be used in our shortlisting and interview processes. PLEASE ENSURE YOU UPLOAD YOUR CV Interviews will be held on the afternoon of either 18th or 19th November 2026 What you will get in return: Fantastic market leading Pension scheme Excellent employee health and wellbeing services including an Employee Assistance Programme Exceptional starting annual leave entitlement, plus bank holidays Additional paid closure over the Christmas period Local and national discounts at a range of major retailers As an equal opportunities employer we welcome applicants from all sections of the community regardless of age, sex, gender (or gender identity), ethnicity, disability, sexual orientation and transgender status. All appointments are made on merit. Our University is positive about flexible working – you can find out more here Hybrid working arrangements may be considered. Please be aware that due to the number of applications we unfortunately may not able to provide individual feedback on your application. Please note that we are unable to respond to enquiries, accept CVs or applications from Recruitment Agencies. Any recruitment enquiries from recruitment agencies should be directed to recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Any CV’s submitted by a recruitment agency will be considered a gift. Enquiries about the vacancy, shortlisting and interviews: Name: Robert Bellamy Email: robert.bellamy@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries: Email: recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical support: 0161 850 2004 https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home This vacancy will close for applications at midnight on the closing date. Please see the link below for the Further Particulars document which contains the person specification criteria. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46076": {
      "title": "Senior Technician (Optometry Teaching)",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Senior Technician (Optometry Teaching) Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Senior Technician (Optometry Teaching) Apply for job Job reference: BMH-032425 Salary: UoM Grade 5 £33,002 - £36,636 per annum depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Biology, Medicine Health Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Permanent Division/Team: FBMH Technical Services Hours Per Week: 35 Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 11/11/2026 Contract Duration: Permanent School/Directorate: Faculty Office - Biology, Medicine and Health Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description We are seeking a motivated and collaborative individual to join our team as a Senior Technician (Optometry) . This role offers an exciting opportunity to support the delivery of high-quality practical teaching and clinical simulation activity within the Optometry facility, providing high-level technical and operational support, and hands-on assistance to students, academic staff, colleagues and external users. The role holder will help ensure the Optometry facility is safe, well maintained, compliant with relevant health and safety and General Optical Council requirements, and able to support an excellent student and patient-facing learning experience. You will be responsible for: - Providing senior technical support across the Optometry facility, including specialist advice, preparation, troubleshooting and assistance for students, academic staff, colleagues and external users. - Supporting the Technical Specialist (Clinical Teaching) in the day-to-day management, planning, maintenance and safe operation of Optometry teaching spaces, equipment and materials. - Supervising and supporting junior technical team members, including contributing to induction, training, probation support, pastoral support and day-to-day task coordination. - Deputising for the Technical Specialist (Clinical Teaching) when required and supporting effective coordination of teaching technical support and service provision. - Maintaining clean, tidy and safe facility areas in line with health and safety requirements, standard operating procedures, risk assessments and local procedures. We welcome candidates who bring diverse perspectives, experiences, and approaches to their work. About You We encourage applications from individuals with a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. You should demonstrate: Essential Criteria: - A qualification in a science-related subject area, such as a degree, HND or HNC, or equivalent extensive relevant experience in a technical or scientific role. - Understanding of Optometry as a taught subject and the requirements of specialist Optometry teaching spaces. - Knowledge of practical teaching, clinical simulation and/or laboratory facility operations within a science, healthcare or related environment. - Experience of working in a similar laboratory, clinical simulation, teaching practical or technical environment. - Strong organisational and time management skills, with the ability to plan, prioritise and manage a varied workload to meet deadlines. Desirable Criteria: - Awareness of General Optical Council requirements, Quality in Optometry processes and the importance of compliance within Optometry teaching provision. - Experience supporting Optometry teaching activity, such as maintaining trial lens sets, disinfecting contact lenses or supporting specialist optometry equipment. - Experience troubleshooting specialist laboratory, clinical simulation or analytical equipment, methods and practical delivery issues. - Experience supervising, supporting or training staff, trainees, apprentices or students. - Excellent oral and written communication skills, with the ability to communicate effectively with staff, students, external users and colleagues across the University. We value transferable skills and real-world experience as much as formal qualifications. Our benefits include: - Generous employer pension contribution - Christmas closure across all departments outside of annual leave and bank holidays - Purple Place employee discount platform For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits. You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working arrangements. We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Anthony Steel Email Address: anthony.steel@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support Jobtrain Client Support Hub This role is not eligible for Skilled Worker visa sponsorship. Applicants must demonstrate the right to work in the UK. Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=45976": {
      "title": "Teaching, Learning and Student Experience (TLSE) Administrator (Assessment and Progression)(Redeployees Only)",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Teaching, Learning and Student Experience (TLSE) Administrator (Assessment and Progression) Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Teaching, Learning and Student Experience (TLSE) Administrator (Assessment and Progression) (Redeployees Only) Apply for job Job reference: SAE-032316 Salary: £30,180 - £32,722 per annum, depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Science and Engineering Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Permanent Division/Team: School of Engineering Administration Hours Per Week: 35 Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 11/11/2026 Contract Duration: Permanent School/Directorate: School of Engineering Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description We are seeking a motivated and collaborative individual to join our team as a Teaching, Learning and Student Experience Administrator – Assessment and Progression. This role offers an exciting opportunity to support the effective administration of assessment and student records, helping us provide a consistent, high-quality service that places the student experience at the heart of our work within a dynamic and inclusive environment. You will be responsible for: Administering assessment and progression activities, including exam-paper preparation, marking processes, mitigating circumstances outcomes, results publication and exam-board decisions. Maintaining accurate student and assessment records, data quality and system updates in line with data-protection requirements. Servicing Examination Boards and Summary Disciplinary Panels, and supporting assessment activity in online learning environments such as Canvas. Providing clear information, advice and guidance to students, academic colleagues and other stakeholders while maintaining confidentiality. Supervising and co-ordinating work activities, contributing to standardised processes, service excellence and continuous improvement across teams. We welcome candidates who bring diverse perspectives, experiences, and approaches to their work. About You We encourage applications from individuals with a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. You should demonstrate: Essential Criteria: Relevant knowledge or experience of teaching and learning and/or student administrative policies and procedures. Experience of working flexibly and collaboratively in a team within a large organisation. Excellent communication and customer-service skills, with the ability to provide accurate advice and maintain confidentiality. Strong organisational skills, with the ability to prioritise varied work, meet strict deadlines and maintain a high level of accuracy. The ability to supervise or co-ordinate work activities, solve problems effectively and use digital systems confidently. Desirable Criteria: Experience of assessment and progression administration, including examination boards, results or mitigating circumstances. Experience of student-record systems or online learning environments, such as Campus Solutions or Canvas. We value transferable skills and real-world experience as much as formal qualifications. No specific educational qualification is required. Our benefits include: Generous employer pension contribution Christmas closure across all departments outside of annual leave and bank holidays Purple Place employee discount platform For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Madeleine Edden Email Address: madeleine.edden@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home This role is not eligible for Skilled Worker visa sponsorship. Applicants must demonstrate the right to work in the UK. Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46402": {
      "title": "Research Associate In Organic Synthesis",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Research Associate In Organic Synthesis Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Research Associate In Organic Synthesis Apply for job Job reference: SAE-032736 Salary: £38,448 - £46,970 per annum depending on experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Science and Engineering Location: Manchester Institute of Biotechnology Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Department of Chemistry Hours Per Week: Full time (1 FTE) Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 12/11/2026 Contract Duration: Fixed term for 12 months months School/Directorate: School of Natural Sciences Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description We are seeking a motivated and collaborative individual to join our team as a Research Associate in Organic Synthesis, working on carbohydrate skeletal editing within the Miller research group at the Manchester Institute of Biotechnology. The post is tenable for 12 months from 1 January 2027, with the possibility of extension for a further two years. Prior experience in carbohydrate chemistry is not essential. You will be responsible for: Design, plan and carry out high-quality research in organic synthesis and methodology development, establishing new transformations for the skeletal editing of sugar scaffolds. Develop and optimise synthetic transformations, investigate reaction scope and limitations, and isolate and fully characterise reaction products using appropriate analytical and spectroscopic techniques. Work as part of our diverse research team and collaborate accordingly. Be responsible for project management, including report writing. We welcome candidates who bring diverse perspectives, experiences, and approaches to their work. About You We encourage applications from individuals with a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. You should demonstrate: Essential Criteria: You must hold a PhD in organic chemistry with a research focus on organic synthesis and/or synthetic methodology development. Evidence of developing innovative synthetic methods or experimental protocols, including the ability to devise, optimise and troubleshoot organic reactions. An ability to work independently and collaboratively as part of a team, and a flexible approach to dealing with research problems as they arise. Excellent interpersonal and communication skills (both written and oral). Desirable Criteria: We value transferable skills and real-world experience as much as formal qualifications. Our benefits include: • Generous employer contribution pension • 29 days annual leave plus bank holidays, along with Christmas closure • Ride to work and EV car scheme available For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Prof. Gavin Miller Email Address: gavin.miller@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Watch: From Manchester for the world – our new strategy to 2035 . Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46349": {
      "title": "Research Fellow",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Research Fellow – Wellcome UK Social Media Restrictions Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Research Fellow – Wellcome UK Social Media Restrictions Apply for job Job reference: HUM-032680 Salary: £47,389 - £51,753 per annum depending on experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Humanities Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Education Hours Per Week: Full time (1 FTE) Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 12/11/2026 Contract Duration: Fixed term for 36 months School/Directorate: School of Environment, Education & Development Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description We are seeking a motivated and collaborative individual to join our team as a Research Fellow – Wellcome UK Social Media Restrictions. You will have the opportunity to work as a Research Fellow on a Wellcome-funded project to evaluate the impact of the upcoming UK social media restrictions on young people’s mental health and wellbeing. You will be responsible for: Coordinating participant recruitment and data collection for the ecological momentary assessment and interviews, with the support of other team members; Performing qualitative and/or quantitative analyses along with other team members; Coordinating and preparing outputs relating to analyses along with other team members (including evidence briefings, peer-reviewed academic articles, blogs, infographics, etc.); Coordinating liaison with stakeholders and the steering committee to support the smooth running of the study; Managing junior staff; We welcome candidates who bring diverse perspectives, experiences, and approaches to their work. About You We encourage applications from individuals with a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. You should demonstrate: Essential Criteria: Holds a PhD in a relevant field (e.g. psychology, epidemiology, public health, economics, social sciences); Demonstrable skills in advanced quantitative analysis (e.g. causal inference, longitudinal, EMA data) and/or qualitative data analysis (e.g. thematic analysis); Demonstrable skills in the use of statistical software/programming language (e.g., R, Stata, MPlus) and/or qualitative software (e.g., NVivo); Demonstrable experience with managing research projects or substantial project workstreams/strands; Demonstrable experience with managing/supervising and supporting staff; Desirable Criteria: Experience of working within, or in close partnership with, policy-making bodies, government departments, and/or non-governmental organizations; Experience of natural-experiment and/or quasi-experimental research, including causal inference approaches such as difference-in-differences, interrupted time-series or related designs. We value transferable skills and real-world experience as much as formal qualifications. Our benefits include: • Generous employer contribution pension • 29 days annual leave plus bank holidays, along with Christmas closure • Ride to work and EV car scheme available For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Margarita Panayiotou Email Address: margarita.panayiotou@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Watch: From Manchester for the world – our new strategy to 2035 Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46370": {
      "title": "Research Assistant in Mammalian Cell Culture",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Research Assistant in Mammalian Cell Culture Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Research Assistant in Mammalian Cell Culture Apply for job Job reference: SAE-032707 Salary: UoM Grade 5 £33,662 - £37,369 per annum depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Science and Engineering Location: Manchester Institute of Biotechnology Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Department of Chemical Engineering Hours Per Week: 35 hours per week (1 FTE) Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 11/11/2026 Contract Duration: Fixed term for 5 months School/Directorate: School of Engineering Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description Join us as a Research Assistant in Mammalian Cell Culture (Grade 5) to help develop practical alternatives to animal-derived serum. This full-time, fixed-term post lasts 5 months. You will join Prof Alan Dickson’s research group at the Manchester Institute of Biotechnology, working closely with Dr Zeynep Betts and an industry partner. You will be responsible for: - Maintaining mammalian cell cultures (such as Chinese hamster ovary cells) using sterile technique and agreed protocols. - Adapting cells to grow in different supplements. - Measuring cell growth, viability and nutrient use, and the amount and quality of protein produced where relevant. - Recording, analysing and presenting data clearly and accurately, keeping full laboratory records. - Liaising with the industry partner, including sharing results and materials and attending project meetings. We welcome candidates who bring diverse perspectives, experiences, and approaches to their work. About You We encourage applications from individuals with a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. You should demonstrate: Essential Criteria: - Degree in biological sciences or chemical engineering or a related subject. - Understanding of mammalian cell biology and the principles of cell culture. - Hands-on experience of sterile technique for handling cells (ideally mammalian cells). - Accurate record keeping and data analysis. - Clear written and spoken communication. We value transferable skills and real-world experience as much as formal qualifications. Our benefits include: - Generous employer pension contribution - Christmas closure across all departments outside of annual leave and bank holidays - Purple Place employee discount platform For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Alan Dickson Email Address: alan.dickson@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home This role is not eligible for Skilled Worker visa sponsorship. Applicants must demonstrate the right to work in the UK. Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Watch: From Manchester for the world – our new strategy to 2035 Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
