    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=45866": {
      "title": "Teaching Learning and Student Experience Administrator (Curriculum and Programmes)",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Teaching Learning and Student Experience Administrator (Curriculum and Programmes) Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Teaching Learning and Student Experience Administrator (Curriculum and Programmes) Apply for job Job reference: SAE-032250 Salary: £29,588 - £32,080 per annum, depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Science and Engineering Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: School of Engineering Administration Hours Per Week: 35 hours per week (1 FTE) Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 13/11/2026 Contract Duration: Fixed term for 24 months School/Directorate: School of Engineering Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description We are seeking a motivated and collaborative individual to join our team as a Teaching and Learning Administrator. This role offers an exciting to join This role offers an exciting opportunity to contribute to delivering a high-quality, student-centred service within a dynamic and inclusive environment. In this varied role, you’ll support the delivery of teaching, learning and student support activities within the area of Curriculum and Programmes across the School of Engineering. You’ll work closely with colleagues across Professional Services and academic teams. You will be responsible for: The administration of Teaching and Learning and Student Administration activities within Curriculum and Programmes Team. The administration of required activities in the delivery of student timetables, including annual data gathering of course unit information; allocation of students and change requests. Updating multiple area of Campus Solutions using PEAM functionality including programme structures, programme enrolments, course catalogue. Maintaining accurate student records ensuring updates made within specified deadlines. Support the administration of all required activities within the School Curriculum and Programmes team, including quality assurances process, registration, enrolment, student changes and graduation. We welcome candidates who bring diverse perspectives, experiences, and approaches to their work. About You We encourage applications from individuals with a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. You should demonstrate: Essential Criteria: Experience of working administration in a team environment within a large-scale organisation. Excellent organisational skills, able to prioritise a wide variety of work activities and meet strict deadlines. Good analytical and problem-solving skills with the ability to identify and resolve issues effectively. Able to supervise staff and co-ordinate work activities. Oral and written communication skills with an ability to deal with a wide range of people with a high level of accuracy and attention to detail. Desirable Criteria: Good digital literacy skills with experience of University software packages, or willingness to be trained in appropriate systems. Our benefits include: Generous employer pension contribution Christmas closure across all departments outside of annual leave and bank holidays Purple Place employee discount platform For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Loraine McKoy Email Address: Loraine.mckoy@Manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home This role is not eligible for Skilled Worker visa sponsorship. Applicants must demonstrate the right to work in the UK. Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46412": {
      "title": "Research Technician",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Research Technician Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Research Technician Apply for job Job reference: BMH-032744 Salary: £33,662 - £36,320 per annum depending on experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Biology, Medicine Health Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: FBMH Technical Services Hours Per Week: 35 hours per week (1 FTE) Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 13/11/2026 Contract Duration: Fixed term for 36 months School/Directorate: Faculty Office - Biology, Medicine and Health Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description We are seeking a Research Technician (Grade 5) to join the Kulathu Lab in the School of Biological Sciences, Faculty of Biology, Medicine and Health. This senior technical post combines hands-on research in ubiquitin biology and protein biochemistry with responsibility for the day-to-day running of the laboratory. You will be responsible for: Managing and delivering pilot research programmes and contributing significantly to main research projects by organising, maintaining and reporting on research outcomes as directed by the Principal Investigator. Contributing to experimental design, data analysis, presentation and reporting for inclusion in publications, presentations and wider research outputs. Providing supervision, advice and support to staff, students and laboratory users, including training, development, induction and guidance on experimental protocols and safe working practices. Managing the efficient and safe operation of laboratory areas, ensuring activity, equipment use and personnel are appropriate for the space and aligned with local requirements. We welcome candidates who bring diverse perspectives, experiences, and approaches to their work. About You We encourage applications from individuals with a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. You should demonstrate: Essential Criteria: A qualification at QCF level 6, such as a bachelor’s degree, in a relevant biological science subject, or QCF level 2 passes in English, Mathematics and at least one science subject with extensive relevant research laboratory experience. Relevant and specific laboratory experience in the field of biochemistry and protein science. Familiarity with ubiquitin biology, protein quality control or related areas of cell biology. Desirable Criteria: Generation and characterisation of knockout and knock in cell lines using CRISPR/Cas9 gene editing techniques (Desirable). We value transferable skills and real-world experience as much as formal qualifications. Our benefits include: Generous employer pension contribution Christmas closure across all departments outside of annual leave and bank holidays Purple Place employee discount platform For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Yogesh Kulathu Email Address: yogesh.kulathu@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home This role is not eligible for Skilled Worker visa sponsorship. Applicants must demonstrate the right to work in the UK. Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Watch: From Manchester for the world – our new strategy to 2035 Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46409": {
      "title": "Research Associate in Ubiquitin Signalling",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Research Associate in Ubiquitin Signalling Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Research Associate in Ubiquitin Signalling Apply for job Job reference: BMH-032741 Salary: £38,448 to £41,885 per annum,  depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Biology, Medicine Health Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Division of Molecular and Cellular Function Hours Per Week: Full Time Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 13/11/2026 Contract Duration: 36 months School/Directorate: School of Biological Sciences Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description Please upload a single PDF document containing your CV and cover letter. This is a fantastic opportunity for a postdoctoral Research Associate, with a background in protein biochemistry, molecular biology or molecular recognition, to work within the Kulathu group under the supervision of Professor Yogesh Kulathu. The post holder will be part of the Kulathu group, which is based at the University of Manchester. How do different ubiquitin signals direct different cellular outcomes? We investigate how ubiquitin and ubiquitin-like signalling coordinate cellular quality control, combining innovative tools with biochemical, structural and cellular approaches. The candidate will support the aims of a BBSRC funded research programme to develop precision tools to dissect ubiquitin signalling. This innovative project will study how polyubiquitin chains of different linkages encode cellular signals. You will develop a nanobody toolkit to recognise these chains and enable new experiments in ubiquitin biology. Your work will connect nanobody selection and engineering with protein biochemistry, quantitative binding assays and cellular applications. Working with colleagues in structural biology, proteomics and cell biology, you will investigate molecular recognition and develop approaches to detect, visualise and interrogate ubiquitin signalling. There will be scope to shape the toolkit’s applications, pursue the biological questions it opens up and create reagents of lasting value to the research community. This position offers an ideal opportunity for an enthusiastic, well-motivated individual with interest in tool and methodology development to work as part of a sociable multi-disciplinary team consisting of biochemists, structural biologists, cell biologists and proteomics researchers. We offer scientific ownership and mentoring tailored to your development towards independence. Inclusion, empathy and integrity underpin our culture. We welcome motivated and creative researchers who enjoy challenging problems and want to create tools that enable discovery across a field. The ideal candidate will have excellent experimental design, quantitative analysis and problem-solving skills, and will be able to work with minimal supervision. A rigorous and collaborative approach to research is essential for the position. Experience of protein biochemistry or molecular biology is required, together with a clear interest in ubiquitin signalling. Experience with protein engineering, studying protein-protein interactions or cellular imaging would be advantageous. What you will get in return: Fantastic market leading Pension scheme Excellent employee health and wellbeing services including an Employee Assistance Programme Exceptional starting annual leave entitlement, plus bank holidays Additional paid closure over the Christmas period Local and national discounts at a range of major retailers As an equal opportunities employer we welcome applicants from all sections of the community regardless of age, sex, gender (or gender identity), ethnicity, disability, sexual orientation and transgender status. All appointments are made on merit. Hybrid working arrangements may be considered. Please be aware that due to the number of applications we unfortunately may not able to provide individual feedback on your application. Please note that we are unable to respond to enquiries, accept CVs or applications from Recruitment Agencies. Any recruitment enquiries from recruitment agencies should be directed to recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Any CV’s submitted by a recruitment agency will be considered a gift. Enquiries about the vacancy, shortlisting and interviews: Name: Professor Yogesh Kulathu Email: yogesh.kulathu@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries: Email: recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical support: https://knowledge.jobtrain.co.uk/jobseeker [knowledge.jobtrain.co.uk] This vacancy will close for applications at midnight on the closing date. Please see the link below for the Further Particulars document which contains the person specification criteria. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46385": {
      "title": "DCF Building and Operations Manager",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for DCF Building and Operations Manager Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results DCF Building and Operations Manager Apply for job Job reference: SAE-032719 Salary: £48,337 to £59,390 per annum depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Science and Engineering Location: Dalton Cumbrian Facility, Cumbria Employment type: Permanent Division/Team: Not specified Hours Per Week: Full Time Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 20/11/2026 Contract Duration: Permanent School/Directorate: FSE Research Institutes Job Description DCF Building and Operations Manager Location: Dalton Cumbria Facility, Westlakes Science and Technology Park, near Whitehaven, Cumbria. The facility is geographically separate from the University’s main Manchester campus. The DCF Building and Operations Manager is responsible for the safe, secure and effective operation of the Dalton Cumbria Facility, overseeing the people, infrastructure and operational arrangements that support its research activities. You will lead and develop the local operational team, with responsibility for facilities, infrastructure, contractors, budgets, health and safety, compliance, business continuity and operational improvement. Working with researchers, technical teams, University services and external partners, you will create the well-managed environment needed to deliver specialist research and support the facility’s continued development. Working arrangements: This is a predominantly site-based role, reflecting its operational responsibilities. Some flexibility for occasional hybrid working may be available, subject to operational requirements. What you will get in return: Fantastic market leading Pension scheme Excellent employee health and wellbeing services including an Employee Assistance Programme Exceptional starting annual leave entitlement, plus bank holidays Additional paid closure over the Christmas period Local and national discounts at a range of major retailers As an equal opportunities employer we welcome applicants from all sections of the community regardless of age, sex, gender (or gender identity), ethnicity, disability, sexual orientation and transgender status. All appointments are made on merit. Our University is positive about flexible working – you can find out more here Hybrid working arrangements may be considered Please be aware that due to the number of applications we unfortunately may not able to provide individual feedback on your application. Please note that we are unable to respond to enquiries, accept CVs or applications from Recruitment Agencies. Any recruitment enquiries from recruitment agencies should be directed to recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Any CV’s submitted by a recruitment agency will be considered a gift. Enquiries about the vacancy, shortlisting and interviews: Name: Robert Jones Email: robert.jones-3@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries: Email: recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical support: https://knowledge.jobtrain.co.uk/jobseeker [knowledge.jobtrain.co.uk] This vacancy will close for applications at midnight on the closing date. Please see the link below for the Further Particulars document which contains the person specification criteria. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46406": {
      "title": "Research Associate",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Research Associate Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Research Associate Apply for job Job reference: BMH-032738 Salary: £38,448 - £46,970 per annum depending on experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Biology, Medicine Health Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Division of Molecular and Cellular Function Hours Per Week: Full time (1 FTE) Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 13/11/2026 Contract Duration: Fixed term for 36 months School/Directorate: School of Biological Sciences Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description We are seeking a Research Associate to join the Kulathu laboratory to establish, optimise and apply CRISPR-based genetic screening in human cells to discover novel components of quality control pathways regulated by ubiquitin signalling. You will work closely with Dr. Richard Timms’ laboratory and collaborate on mechanistic follow-up studies. This full-time post is fixed term for 36 months. You will be responsible for: Responsibility for establishing and performing human cell CRISPR screening, optimizing screen conditions, setting up new methods, validating hits and defining mechanisms, and documenting workflows and training colleagues. Conduct individual and collaborative research projects. Prepare research work for publication and dissemination. Collaborate with academic colleagues on areas of shared research interest. We welcome candidates who bring diverse perspectives, experiences, and approaches to their work. About You We encourage applications from individuals with a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. You should demonstrate: Essential Criteria: Have, or be about to obtain, a relevant PhD (or equivalent) At least one first author publication or preprint posted on BiorXiv or MedrXiv Specialist knowledge in the discipline including human cell genetics, CRISPR-based functional genomics and the principles of genetic screens, with an interest in ubiquitin biology, cellular quality control or stress responses. Excellent communication and interpersonal skills Ability to work independently and as part of a team Desirable Criteria: Previous experience establishing, optimising and analysing genetic screens is strongly desirable. We value transferable skills and real-world experience as much as formal qualifications. Our benefits include: • Generous employer contribution pension • 29 days annual leave plus bank holidays, along with Christmas closure • Ride to work and EV car scheme available For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Yogesh Kulathu Email Address: yogesh.kulathu@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46414": {
      "title": "Research Technician",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Research Technician Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Research Technician Apply for job Job reference: BMH-032746 Salary: £27,865 - £29,354 per annum, depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Biology, Medicine Health Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: FBMH Technical Services Hours Per Week: 35 hours per week (1 FTE) Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 13/11/2026 Contract Duration: Fixed term for 36 months School/Directorate: Faculty Office - Biology, Medicine and Health Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description We are seeking a motivated and collaborative individual to join the Kulathu Lab as a Research Technician in the School of Biological Sciences, Faculty of Biology, Medicine and Health. You will provide technical support across project-specific research activities, including running experiments, supporting research equipment, preparing reagents, and helping staff and students work safely and effectively in laboratories. The lab studies how ubiquitin and ubiquitin-like proteins regulate cellular pathways. You will be responsible for: Carrying out and assisting staff and students with standard experimental protocols, as directed by the supervisor. Supporting staff and students in the operation of laboratory equipment and the preparation of reagents. Assisting with the smooth running of laboratories, including general housekeeping duties. Recording, collating and preparing accurate data using paper-based and computer-based systems. We welcome candidates who bring diverse perspectives, experiences, and approaches to their work. About You We encourage applications from individuals with a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. You should demonstrate: Essential Criteria: Relevant experience of working in a research laboratory environment. Practical experience in one or several of the following laboratory techniques: recombinant protein expression and purification, chromatography (e.g. ÄKTA systems), SDS-PAGE and immunoblotting. Experience in mammalian cell culture and making stable cell lines. A qualification at QCF level 6, such as a bachelor’s degree, in a relevant biological science subject; or QCF level 2 passes, such as GCSEs, in English, Mathematics and at least one science subject, together with relevant experience of working in a research laboratory. Desirable Criteria: Experience of preparing ubiquitin chains or working with enzymes of the ubiquitin system or any other posttranslational modification (Desirable) We value transferable skills and real-world experience as much as formal qualifications. Our benefits include: Generous employer pension contribution Christmas closure across all departments outside of annual leave and bank holidays Purple Place employee discount platform For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Yogesh Kulathu Email Address: yogesh.kulathu@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home This role is not eligible for Skilled Worker visa sponsorship. Applicants must demonstrate the right to work in the UK. Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46254": {
      "title": "Teaching &amp; Learning Officer (Assessment &amp; Progression)",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Teaching &amp; Learning Officer (Assessment &amp; Progression) Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Teaching & Learning Officer (Assessment & Progression) Apply for job Job reference: BMH-032592 Salary: £37,694 to £46,049 per annum, depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Biology, Medicine Health Location: Manchester Employment type: Permanent Division/Team: School of Medical Sciences Admin Hours Per Week: Full Time Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 13/11/2026 Contract Duration: Permanent School/Directorate: School of Medical Sciences Job Description The Assessment & Progression Officer will lead a team and manage delivery of the School’s undergraduate and postgraduate taught assessment and progression activity. Through effective leadership, expert interpretation of policy and regulations, accurate management of student data and close collaboration with academic and Professional Services partners, the postholder will ensure that assessments, examination boards, progression decisions, external examining and complex student cases are managed consistently and effectively. The role will contribute to robust academic governance, regulatory compliance, service improvement and a positive experience for students and colleagues. The Assessment & Progression team sits within Teaching, Learning and Student Experience (TLSE) and is responsible for delivering a wide range of assessment, progression and student records activities that support undergraduate and postgraduate taught programmes across the School of Medical Sciences. Working in a complex and professionally regulated environment, the team collaborates closely with academic staff, professional services colleagues, external examiners, Faculty and University partners to ensure the effective administration of assessments, examination boards, student progression and academic governance processes. The role plays an important part in the wider University community by helping to maintain academic standards, support regulatory compliance, enhance the student experience and contribute to continuous improvement in teaching and learning services. What you will get in return: Fantastic market leading Pension scheme Excellent employee health and wellbeing services including an Employee Assistance Programme Exceptional starting annual leave entitlement, plus bank holidays Additional paid closure over the Christmas period Local and national discounts at a range of major retailers As an equal opportunities employer we welcome applicants from all sections of the community regardless of age, sex, gender (or gender identity), ethnicity, disability, sexual orientation and transgender status. All appointments are made on merit. Our University is positive about flexible working – you can find out more here Hybrid working arrangements may be considered. Please be aware that due to the number of applications we unfortunately may not able to provide individual feedback on your application. Please note that we are unable to respond to enquiries, accept CVs or applications from Recruitment Agencies. Any recruitment enquiries from recruitment agencies should be directed to recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Any CV’s submitted by a recruitment agency will be considered a gift. Enquiries about the vacancy, shortlisting and interviews: Name: Joel Loutfi Email: joel.loutfi@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries: Email: recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical support: 0161 850 2004 https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home This vacancy will close for applications at midnight on the closing date. Please see the link below for the Further Particulars document which contains the person specification criteria. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46352": {
      "title": "Research Associate in Multimodal Agentic AI for Cardiology",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Research Associate in Multimodal Agentic AI for Cardiology Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Research Associate in Multimodal Agentic AI for Cardiology Apply for job Job reference: BMH-032748 Salary: £38,448 to £41,885 per annum, depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Biology, Medicine Health Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Division of Informatics, Imaging & Data Science Hours Per Week: Full Time Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 19/11/2026 Contract Duration: 12 months School/Directorate: School of Health Sciences Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description The Division of Informatics, Imaging and Data Sciences within the Faculty of Biology, Medicine and Health invites applications for a Research Associate to develop multimodal agentic AI for clinical decision support in cardiology. The project will integrate cardiac imaging, ECGs, clinical reports and other patient information to develop AI systems that interpret clinical evidence, identify missing or conflicting information, and recommend appropriate next investigations for clinician review. In this role, you will curate multimodal cardiovascular datasets, develop methods to integrate imaging and clinical information, and design AI agents that gather evidence, use analytical tools and support clinical reasoning. Working closely with cardiologists, you will evaluate these systems for accuracy, reliability, interpretability and clinical relevance. You will also contribute to research publications, present findings at conferences and support the development of future funding applications. The successful candidate will work under the supervision of Dr Jinming Duan, collaborating with colleagues in the BHF Manchester Centre of Research Excellence and clinical teams at Wythenshawe Hospital, part of Manchester University NHS Foundation Trust. You should hold, or be nearing completion of, a PhD in computer science, artificial intelligence, medical image analysis, biomedical engineering, applied mathematics or a related discipline. You will have strong Python programming skills, practical experience in deep learning, and the ability to design and critically evaluate AI methods. Experience with multimodal learning, large language models, AI agents or medical imaging would be advantageous. Clear communication, effective collaboration and a commitment to rigorous, reproducible research are essential. British Heart Foundation Manchester Centre of Research Excellence The British Heart Foundation (BHF) has been awarded one of its prestigious Centres of Research Excellence (CRE) to The University of Manchester. This competitive award recognises the internationally leading position of cardiovascular science at our University and provides £4M over the next five years to further develop this excellence. The University has matched the BHF investment with £4M of its own money. This £8M total commitment represents the most significant initiative in cardiovascular research in Manchester this century. The Centre is interdisciplinary, involving not only basic cardiovascular scientists and clinicians, but colleagues in inflammation biology, data science, computer science and engineering. The BHF is one of the largest charitable funders of heart and circulatory research in the world. BHF funds around half of all the cardiovascular research performed in the UK and focuses its resources on scientifically excellent projects with a potential to benefit the health of people with, or at risk of, cardiovascular disease. BHF funded research has delivered many key advances for patients and their families, many of which are detailed on the BHF website. What you will get in return: Fantastic market leading Pension scheme Excellent employee health and wellbeing services including an Employee Assistance Programme Exceptional starting annual leave entitlement, plus bank holidays Additional paid closure over the Christmas period Local and national discounts at a range of major retailers As an equal opportunities employer we welcome applicants from all sections of the community regardless of age, sex, gender (or gender identity), ethnicity, disability, sexual orientation and transgender status. All appointments are made on merit. Our University is positive about flexible working – you can find out more here Hybrid working arrangements may be considered. Please be aware that due to the number of applications we unfortunately may not able to provide individual feedback on your application. Please note that we are unable to respond to enquiries, accept CVs or applications from Recruitment Agencies. Any recruitment enquiries from recruitment agencies should be directed to recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Any CV’s submitted by a recruitment agency will be considered a gift. Enquiries about the vacancy, shortlisting and interviews: Name: Dr Jinming Duan Email: Jinming.Duan@manchester.ac.uk Name: Ms Jennifer Carlson Email: BHFCRE@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries: Email: recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical support: https://knowledge.jobtrain.co.uk/jobseeker [knowledge.jobtrain.co.uk] This vacancy will close for applications at midnight on the closing date. Please see the link below for the Further Particulars document which contains the person specification criteria Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46408": {
      "title": "Research Assistant (Health Psychology)",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Research Assistant (Health Psychology) Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Research Assistant (Health Psychology) Apply for job Job reference: BMH-032740 Salary: £33,662 to £37,369 per annum, depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Biology, Medicine Health Location: Manchester Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Division of Psychology and Mental Health Hours Per Week: 35 hours per week Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 26/11/2026 Contract Duration: Until 17 January 2029 School/Directorate: School of Health Sciences Job Description The Research Assistant will support the delivery of a Diabetes UK-funded research project exploring the experiences and support needs of siblings of children and young people with Type 1 Diabetes and co-developing psychoeducational resources to support families. Working closely with the Research Fellow and under the supervision of the wider project team, the postholder will contribute to participant recruitment, survey administration, qualitative data collection, workshop delivery, data management and dissemination activities. This is an excellent opportunity to gain experience of mixed-methods health research, co-production approaches and research involving children, young people and families. The post will be based within the Manchester Centre for Health Psychology, part of the Division of Psychology and Mental Health at The University of Manchester. The successful candidate will work closely with Dr Jo Brooks, Dr Rhiannon Hawkes, Dr Daniela Ghio and project collaborators to deliver the study. They will join a multidisciplinary team working with healthcare professionals, public contributors, charities and community organisations to develop resources for siblings of children and young people living with Type 1 Diabetes. The project includes survey research, interviews, participatory workshops and resource development activities involving families across the UK. What you will get in return: Fantastic market leading Pension scheme Excellent employee health and wellbeing services including an Employee Assistance Programme Exceptional starting annual leave entitlement, plus bank holidays Additional paid closure over the Christmas period Local and national discounts at a range of major retailers As an equal opportunities employer we welcome applicants from all sections of the community regardless of age, sex, gender (or gender identity), ethnicity, disability, sexual orientation and transgender status. All appointments are made on merit. Our University is positive about flexible working – you can find out more here Hybrid working arrangements may be considered. Please be aware that due to the number of applications we unfortunately may not able to provide individual feedback on your application. Please note that we are unable to respond to enquiries, accept CVs or applications from Recruitment Agencies. Any recruitment enquiries from recruitment agencies should be directed to recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Any CV’s submitted by a recruitment agency will be considered a gift. Enquiries about the vacancy, shortlisting and interviews: Name: Dr Jo Brooks Email: joanna.brooks@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries: Email: recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical support: https://knowledge.jobtrain.co.uk/jobseeker [knowledge.jobtrain.co.uk] This vacancy will close for applications at midnight on the closing date. Please see the link below for the Further Particulars document which contains the person specification criteria. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46225": {
      "title": "Research Support Officer FSE",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Research Support Officer FSE Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Research Support Officer FSE Apply for job Job reference: SAE-032594 Salary: £33,662 - £37,369 per annum, depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Science and Engineering Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: FSE Research & Business Services Hours Per Week: 35 hours per week/ 1 FTE Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 19/11/2026 Contract Duration: 12 months School/Directorate: Faculty Office - Science and Engineering Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description We are seeking a motivated and collaborative individual to join our team as a Research Support Officer. This role offers an exciting opportunity to contribute to the Faculty of Science and Engineering’s Professional Services Research Services team, delivering high-quality support to academics and researchers throughout the funding lifecycle, from application development and award mobilisation to ongoing award management. Working collaboratively with academic and professional services colleagues, the postholder will manage a portfolio of research activity that supports the University in attracting research talent and income, delivering high-quality research, and maximising the impact of research outcomes, within a dynamic and inclusive environment. You will be responsible for: Managing a portfolio of research projects and providing a responsive support service to researchers. Preparing accurate project costings and budgets and advising researchers on funding requirements. Coordinating with external partners to obtain accurate and timely information for funding proposals. Supporting reporting, assurance and data-quality requirements. Working flexibly across the wider research support service to meet changing priorities and maintain service continuity. We welcome candidates who bring diverse perspectives, experiences, and approaches to their work. About You We encourage applications from individuals with a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. You should demonstrate: Essential Criteria: Broad understanding of the research administration lifecycle Experience of working within regulations and procedures Strong numeracy skills, with accuracy and attention to detail and the ability to complete complex calculations and present financial data Excellent written and verbal communication skills, with the ability to influence, build networks and communicate effectively with a wide range of internal and external contacts Excellent organisational skills, with the ability to plan and prioritise varied work in a busy environment and manage competing deadlines We value transferable skills and real-world experience as much as formal qualifications. Our benefits include: (Grades 1-5) Generous employer pension contribution Christmas closure across all departments outside of annual leave and bank holidays Purple Place employee discount platform For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Katie Powell Email Address: katie.powell@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home This role is not eligible for Skilled Worker visa sponsorship. Applicants must demonstrate the right to work in the UK. Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46423": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate in Proteomics (Microbial Competition and Type VI Secretion)",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Postdoctoral Research Associate in Proteomics (Microbial Competition and Type VI Secretion) Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Postdoctoral Research Associate in Proteomics (Microbial Competition and Type VI Secretion) Apply for job Job reference: BMH-032762 Salary: £38,448- £46,970 per annum, depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Biology, Medicine Health Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Division of Molecular and Cellular Function Hours Per Week: Full time (1 FTE) Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 17/11/2026 Contract Duration: Fixed term for 36 months (01/01/2027-31/12/2029) School/Directorate: School of Biological Sciences Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description We are seeking a motivated and collaborative individual to join our team as a Postdoctoral Research Associate in mass spectrometry-based proteomics . You will lead the proteomics work of a collaborative Wellcome Discovery Award on competitive interactions between microorganisms, working with colleagues at the Universities of Dundee, Newcastle, Edinburgh and Chicago. You will use data-independent acquisition (DIA) proteomics to characterise T6SS-secreted proteins and define their modes of action. You will be based in the Laboratory for Biomedical Mass Spectrometry under the supervision of Prof Matthias Trost, with hands-on access to modern instrumentation including the timsTOF HT and timsTOF Ultra AIP. You will be responsible for: design and run DIA proteomics experiments on complex microbial samples within the collaborative consortium; run and maintain one of the mass spectrometry and LC systems; apply data analysis pipelines in R and other bioinformatic analyses; plan your own experiments with the Principal Investigator and the wider award team, and publish your findings as lead or co-author. We welcome candidates who bring diverse perspectives, experiences, and approaches to their work. About You We encourage applications from individuals with a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. You should demonstrate: Essential Criteria: You will have a PhD (or be about to obtain one) and proven hands-on experience running and maintaining mass spectrometry systems, experience in proteomics, and good data analysis skills in R. You will collaborate closely with colleagues and also drive your own research questions. Desirable Criteria: Microbiology experience, particularly in bacterial or fungal biology, is an advantage but not essential. We value transferable skills and real-world experience as much as formal qualifications. Our benefits include: Generous employer contribution pension 29 days annual leave plus bank holidays, along with Christmas closure Ride to work and EV car scheme available For more information, please see here . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech here . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Prof Matthias Trost Email Address: matthias.trost@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support Jobtrain Jobseeker Support Hub Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46299": {
      "title": "Research Administrator",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Research Administrator Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Research Administrator Apply for job Job reference: BMH-032637 Salary: £27,865 to £29,354 pro rata per annum, depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Biology, Medicine Health Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Division of Musculoskeletal and Dermatological Science Hours Per Week: 0.4FTE (Part Time) Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 17/11/2026 Contract Duration: Until 31 March 2028 School/Directorate: School of Biological Sciences Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description This unique role is an exciting opportunity to work as part of a largescale research initiative, the UK Musculoskeletal Translational Research Collaboration (MSK TRC), which form part of the NIHR Manchester Biomedical Research Centre (BRC). The post holder will provide comprehensive and proactive administration support. They will be the key administrative point of contact within programmes across the MSK TRC, ensuring administrative enquiries and issues are dealt with effectively to support their smooth running. Working with multidisciplinary groups, at all levels, including senior academics and clinicians, the post holder will build working relationships to provide effective and professional liaison and communication to facilitate the efficient coordination and progress of day-to-day activities. The role is varied and the successful candidate will possess excellent organisational, communication and prioritisation skills, have proven administrative experience and be able to manage a demanding workload. What you will get in return: Fantastic market leading Pension scheme Excellent employee health and wellbeing services including an Employee Assistance Programme Exceptional starting annual leave entitlement, plus bank holidays Additional paid closure over the Christmas period Local and national discounts at a range of major retailers As an equal opportunities employer we welcome applicants from all sections of the community regardless of age, sex, gender (or gender identity), ethnicity, disability, sexual orientation and transgender status. All appointments are made on merit. Our University is positive about flexible working – you can find out more here Hybrid working arrangements may be considered. Please be aware that due to the number of applications we unfortunately may not able to provide individual feedback on your application. Please note that we are unable to respond to enquiries, accept CVs or applications from Recruitment Agencies. Any recruitment enquiries from recruitment agencies should be directed to People.Recruitment@manchester.ac.uk . Any CV’s submitted by a recruitment agency will be considered a gift. Please note this role is not eligible for sponsorship under the Skilled Worker route of the Points Based System. Candidates will need to be able to demonstrate their right to work in the UK in order to be eligible to take up the post. Enquiries about the vacancy, shortlisting and interviews: Name: Sheela Medahunsi Email: sheela.medahunsi@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries: Email: People.recruitment@manchester.ac.uk Technical support: Jobtrain: https://knowledge.jobtrain.co.uk/jobseeker [knowledge.jobtrain.co.uk] This vacancy will close for applications at midnight on the closing date. Please see the link below for the Further Particulars document which contains the person specification criteria. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46383": {
      "title": "Research Associate in Early Modern Global History",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Research Associate in Early Modern Global History Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Research Associate in Early Modern Global History Apply for job Job reference: HUM-032717 Salary: UoM Grade 6 £38,448 Faculty/Organisational Unit: Humanities Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: History Hours Per Week: Full time (1 FTE) Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 16/11/2026 Contract Duration: Fixed term for 20 months School/Directorate: School of Arts, Languages and Cultures Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description We are seeking a motivated and collaborative individual to join our team as a Research Associate in Early Modern Global History . This role offers an exciting opportunity to work with Prof Edmond Smith, conducting research for the ERC-selected, UKRI-funded project “Institutional Transformation and the Entangled Commercial Cultures of International Trade, 1450-1750” (INTRECCI). The project is based in the Department of History at The University of Manchester. You will be responsible for: Develop and pursue research for one or more of the principal case studies of the INTRECCI project, and contributing the connected analysis of institutional exchange between them. Pursue independent and collaborative research, at The University of Manchester and in archives and libraries elsewhere in support of this research. Contribute to collaborative publications (articles, monographs, special issues and conference proceedings). Communicate findings of the project at seminars and conferences. Contribute to the development of a shared project repository and the development of a public-facing database of global commercial actors. We welcome candidates who bring diverse perspectives, experiences, and approaches to their work. About You We encourage applications from individuals with a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. You should demonstrate: Essential Criteria: Educated to PhD level with appropriate specialism Sophisticated knowledge and research experience of relevant area of early modern global history Effective communication skills (speaking and in writing) Command of relevant languages, including experience working with primary sources in this language Collegiality and a willingness to work in a collaborative environment Desirable Criteria: Demonstrate clear potential for high quality research publications Experience of organising activities for scholarly or public audiences Familiarity with data entry and database systems We value transferable skills and real-world experience as much as formal qualifications. Our benefits include: Generous employer contribution pension 29 days annual leave plus bank holidays, along with Christmas closure Ride to work and EV car scheme available For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy. Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Prof Edmond Smith Email Address: edmond.smith@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support Jobtrain Jobseeker Support Hub Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46316": {
      "title": "TLSE Administrator (C&amp;P) x 2 Posts",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for TLSE Administrator (C&amp;P) x 2 Posts Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results TLSE Administrator (C&P) x 2 Posts Apply for job Job reference: BMH-032651 Salary: £30,180 - £32,722 per annum depending on experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Biology, Medicine Health Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Permanent Division/Team: School of Medical Sciences Admin Hours Per Week: 35 hours per week (1 FTE) Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 19/11/2026 Contract Duration: Permanent School/Directorate: School of Medical Sciences Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description We are seeking a motivated and collaborative individual to join our team as a TLSE Administrator in the Curriculum & Programmes Team, within the School of Medical Sciences. This role offers an exciting opportunity to contribute to deliver excellent service provision placing the student experience at the heart of what we do working flexibly both alone and as part of a team, across organisational boundaries. You will be a point of contact for teaching staff, both internal and external, key contacts within a dynamic and inclusive environment. You will be responsible for: Be responsible for the administration of Teaching, Learning and Student Experience activities for the assigned team. Supervise and co-ordinate the work of the team, acting as a point of reference. Maintain and update system records with reference to current data protection legislation and provide reports. Assist with the planning, organisation and delivery of events; organise and service formal committee meetings and groups. Support the administration of all required activities within the School Curriculum and Programmes team, including quality assurance processes, updating unit specification data, recording student change of circumstances, preparing materials for curriculum and programme reviews, supporting course unit selection, providing curriculum advice to students and supporting student registrations. We welcome candidates who bring diverse perspectives, experiences, and approaches to their work. About You We encourage applications from individuals with a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. You should demonstrate: Essential Criteria Relevant knowledge and experience, including teaching and learning and/or student administrative policies and procedures. Experience of working in a team environment within a large organisation, with the ability to be flexible and provide support for colleagues. Flexible and responsive to customer needs with excellent communication skills, able to provide information, advice and guidance whilst maintaining confidentiality. Good analytical and problem-solving skills with the ability to identify and resolve issues effectively. A high level of accuracy and attention to detail. We value transferable skills and real-world experience as much as formal qualifications. Our benefits include: Generous employer pension contribution Christmas closure across all departments outside of annual leave and bank holidays Purple Place employee discount platform For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Jackie Kan Email Address: jackie.kan@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://knowledge.jobtrain.co.uk/jobseeker [knowledge.jobtrain.co.uk] This role is not eligible for Skilled Worker visa sponsorship. Applicants must demonstrate the right to work in the UK. Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46317": {
      "title": "TLSE Assistant (C&amp;P)",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for TLSE Assistant (C&amp;P) Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results TLSE Assistant (C&P) Apply for job Job reference: BMH-032652 Salary: £27,865 - £29,354 per annum depending on experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Biology, Medicine Health Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Permanent Division/Team: School of Medical Sciences Admin Hours Per Week: 35 hours per week (1 FTE) Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 19/11/2026 Contract Duration: Permanent School/Directorate: School of Medical Sciences Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description We are seeking a motivated and collaborative individual to join our team as a TLSE Assistant in the Curriculum & Programmes Team, within the School of Medical Sciences. This role offers an exciting opportunity to contribute to work positively and respectfully with a range of students and colleagues from diverse backgrounds and be highly self-motivated with the capacity to work flexibly both alone and as part of a team, within a dynamic and inclusive environment. You will be responsible for: Provide administrative support for the Teaching and Learning team as directed by the assigned line manager. Generate bespoke correspondence, record replies and maintain accurate records as required. Be responsible for own data management, student record and data quality for all related areas of TLSE delivery. Assist the Curriculum and Programmes team with administration across the full academic calendar, including setting up and maintaining course unit records, supporting course unit selection, maintaining course unit information on online learning environments (e.g. Blackboard), providing curriculum advice to students and supporting student registrations. Support the Curriculum and Programmes team with quality assurance-related processes and specialist activities including placements, Online and Blended Learning, accreditation, OneMed and Languages for all Programmes; support activities within the School hub regarding specific curriculum and programme enquiries. We welcome candidates who bring diverse perspectives, experiences, and approaches to their work. About You We encourage applications from individuals with a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. You should demonstrate: Essential Criteria Experience of working with administrative processes. Digital literacy with familiarity with standard IT packages. Experience of working in a team environment with the ability to be flexible and provide support for colleagues. Good numeracy skills, attention to detail and ability to identify and resolve issues effectively. Flexible and responsive to customer needs, able to provide information and guidance whilst maintaining confidentiality. We value transferable skills and real-world experience as much as formal qualifications. Our benefits include: Generous employer pension contribution Christmas closure across all departments outside of annual leave and bank holidays Purple Place employee discount platform For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Jackie Kan Email Address: jackie.kan@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://knowledge.jobtrain.co.uk/jobseeker [knowledge.jobtrain.co.uk] This role is not eligible for Skilled Worker visa sponsorship. Applicants must demonstrate the right to work in the UK. Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46189": {
      "title": "Research Communications Assistant",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Research Communications Assistant Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Research Communications Assistant Apply for job Job reference: HUM-032540 Salary: UoM Grade 4 £30,180 - £32,722 per annum depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Humanities Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Global Development Institute Hours Per Week: 35 hours per week (1 FTE) Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 18/11/2026 Contract Duration: Fixed term for 9 months School/Directorate: School of Environment, Education & Development Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description The Global Development Institute (GDI) at The University of Manchester is where critical thinking meets social justice. The GDI Communications and Impact Team is looking for a Research Communications Assistant to help deliver communication activities that enhance the impact of our research. You will also maintain our alumni relations, as well as leading internal communications within the Institute as a means of building relationships and identifying opportunities with academic colleagues. This is a temporary role, covering a maternity leave. The University of Manchester values a diverse workforce and welcomes applications from all sections of the community. Job Specifications: We are seeking a collaborative individual to join the Global Development Institute (GDI) as a Research Communications Assistant. Working as part of the GDI Communications and Impact Team, you will deliver communications activities across digital, printed and face-to-face channels to enhance the impact of our research and raise the profile of GDI activity among academic and research audiences. You will be expected to: • Work with academics to develop strategies to best disseminate and amplify their latest research, project findings, media engagement, and public engagements by producing digital and printed content. • Produce engaging content to support research dissemination. This could involve editing and formatting blog posts, writing news releases, recording and editing podcast interviews, producing social media graphics and content. • Support the GDI working paper series including academic liaison, systems and processes, external copy-editing, publishing, scholarly communications and dissemination. • Lead on internal communications within GDI to ensure staff and students have a strong understanding of research activity and priorities. • Maintain regular communication channels to engage alumni, particularly through the regular e-newsletter. We welcome candidates who bring diverse perspectives, experiences and approaches to their work. About You We encourage applications from individuals with a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. You should demonstrate: Essential Criteria: • Possess a good standard of education, normally to degree level or equivalent. • Have experience of designing and delivering targeted communications campaigns and activities. • Have strong relevant experience gained in a communications role, ideally within a research or policy related environment. • Demonstrate a working understanding of key global development issues, stakeholders and debates. • Demonstrate a professional manner, working positively and collaboratively with a range of colleagues from diverse backgrounds. Desirable Criteria: • Have a persuasive and visible approach. • Ability to work on own initiative and to work on several projects at the same time. We value transferable skills and real-world experience as much as formal qualifications. Our benefits include: • Generous employer pension contribution • Christmas closure across all departments outside of annual leave and bank holidays • Purple Place employee discount platform For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid Working . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy. Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Skyla Baily Email Address: Skyla.baily@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support Jobtrain Jobseeker Support Hub This role is not eligible for Skilled Worker visa sponsorship. Applicants must demonstrate the right to work in the UK. Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Video From Manchester for the world - our new strategy to 2035 Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46202": {
      "title": "Integrated Clinical Academic Training Administrative Assistant",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Integrated Clinical Academic Training Administrative Assistant Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Integrated Clinical Academic Training Administrative Assistant Apply for job Job reference: BMH-032545 Salary: £27,865 - £29,354 per annum depending on experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Biology, Medicine Health Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Permanent Division/Team: FBMH Planning, Compliance, and Governance Hours Per Week: 35 hours per week (1 FTE) Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 17/11/2026 Contract Duration: Permanent School/Directorate: Faculty Office - Biology, Medicine and Health Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description We are seeking a motivated and collaborative individual to join our team as Integrated Clinical Academic Training (ICAT) Administrative Assistant. This varied and engaging role offers an exciting opportunity to contribute to the successful delivery of The University of Manchester’s Integrated Clinical Academic Training (ICAT) programme, within a dynamic and inclusive environment. You will support clinical academic trainees throughout their journey from recruitment to programme completion, while contributing to an established partnership with NHS England and the National Institute for Health and Care Research (NIHR). It is an excellent opportunity to build broad operational administrative experience and make a meaningful contribution to the development of future clinical and research leaders. You will be responsible for: - Provide administrative support to the ICAT programme, maintaining the professional image of ICAT at all times, and perform the duties of the role with a service-focused approach. Assisting with the coordination and delivery of ICAT conferences, events and other programme activities. Maintaining accurate trainee records and the ICAT database, including information required for monitoring and reporting. Supporting all ICAT meetings and governance, including scheduling meetings, preparing papers, taking minutes, recording actions and disseminating key information. Supporting wider programme activities, including programme funding bids, data collection, interview scheduling and preparation of interview documentation. We welcome candidates who bring diverse perspectives, experiences, and approaches to their work. About You We encourage applications from individuals with a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. You should demonstrate: Essential Criteria: Excellent organisational skills, and able to prioritise work in a busy environment and meet strict deadlines. Excellent communication skills (written and oral), demonstrating an ability to deal with a wide range of people. Numeracy, analytical and problem solving skills and the ability to identify problems and resolve issues effectively. Accuracy and attention to detail. Desirable Criteria: An understanding of clinical academic training. Experience of supporting events or conferences. We value transferable skills and real-world experience as much as formal qualifications. Our benefits include: Generous employer pension contribution Christmas closure across all departments outside of annual leave and bank holidays Purple Place employee discount platform For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Beth Taylor Thomas Email Address: beth.taylorthomas@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support Jobtrain Jobseeker Support Hub This role is not eligible for Skilled Worker visa sponsorship. Applicants must demonstrate the right to work in the UK. Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46248": {
      "title": "Financial Controller",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Financial Controller Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Financial Controller Apply for job Job reference: PSX-032588 Salary: UoM Grade 8 £59,966 - £71,566 pro rata depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Professional Services Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Permanent Division/Team: Not specified Hours Per Week: Full time (1 FTE) Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 17/11/2026 Contract Duration: Permanent School/Directorate: Directorate of Finance Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description We are seeking a motivated and collaborative individual to join our team as a Financial Controller. This role offers an exciting opportunity to lead the University’s financial control, statutory reporting and technical accounting arrangements. Working collaboratively across Finance and the wider University, you will ensure that financial information is accurate, complete and supported by proportionate, effective controls, while delivering high-quality financial statements, regulatory returns and authoritative accounting advice within a dynamic and inclusive environment. You will be responsible for: - Leading and continuously improving the University’s financial control framework, ensuring controls are proportionate, documented, consistently applied and effective across the University and its subsidiaries. - Overseeing the integrity of the accounting ledgers and balance sheet, including high-quality reconciliations, clear ownership of balances, timely resolution of outstanding items and meaningful reporting of risks and exceptions. - Delivering the University’s annual consolidated financial statements and complex accounting disclosures, ensuring compliance with applicable accounting standards and statutory requirements. - Leading the University’s relationships with internal and external auditors for non-research matters, coordinating audit planning and delivery, preparing colleagues and ensuring issues are addressed effectively. - Leading and developing the financial control, capital finance, subsidiary finance, central accounts and UMW finance business partnering teams, setting clear priorities and supporting high performance, collaboration and professional development. We welcome candidates who bring diverse perspectives, experiences, and approaches to their work. About You We encourage applications from individuals with a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. You should demonstrate: Essential Criteria: - A recognised professional accountancy qualification. - Educated to degree level. - Expert knowledge of statutory financial reporting, technical accounting and the preparation of consolidated financial statements. - Strong knowledge of financial control frameworks, balance sheet management, audit and financial governance in a large, complex organisation. - Leading a senior financial control or financial reporting function in a large, complex organisation with subsidiary entities and consolidation requirements. Desirable Criteria: - Delivering statutory financial statements and regulatory returns to demanding quality and timetable requirements. - Leading relationships with internal and external auditors and coordinating complex audits from planning through to completion. - Advising and influencing senior financial and non-financial stakeholders on complex, sensitive or high-risk matters. - Leading, motivating and developing multidisciplinary finance teams and delivering service and process improvement. - Advanced digital and data skills, including the ability to analyse large and complex datasets and use financial systems and spreadsheet tools effectively. Our benefits include: - Generous employer contribution pension - 29 days annual leave plus bank holidays, along with Christmas closure - Ride to work and EV car scheme available For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Gemma Lyons Email Address: gemma.Lyons@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://knowledge.jobtrain.co.uk/jobseeker [knowledge.jobtrain.co.uk] Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46273": {
      "title": "Curator of Methodist + Non-conformist Collections",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Curator of Methodist + Non-conformist Collections Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Curator of Methodist + Non-conformist Collections Apply for job Job reference: PSX-032609 Salary: £38,448 - £46,970 per annum depending on experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Professional Services Location: John Rylands Library Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Special Collections Hours Per Week: Full time (1 FTE) Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 16/11/2026 Contract Duration: 60 (ends 31.8.31) Fixed term for 60 months until 31st August 2031 School/Directorate: The University of Manchester Library Job Description We are seeking a motivated and collaborative individual to join our team as Curator – Methodist and Non-conformist Collections. This curatorial role is responsible for the stewardship of the Methodist and Non-conformist archival and printed collections held at the University of Manchester Library, primarily based at the John Rylands Library. You will be responsible for: To curate agreed Special Collections materials, print and digital, to exemplary standards, contributing to the development, management, care and security of the collections in line with Library policies and procedures To promote and actively deliver the teaching and learning agenda for Special Collections in collaboration with other colleagues across the library and to support all curators in teaching and learning activities To take a strategic approach to improving access to the collections, through cataloguing, digitisation, specialist enquiries and the reader service, liaising as appropriate with Library colleagues and academic staff To liaise with stakeholders, including existing and potential donors, creators and depositors, over the acquisition, development and use of Special Collections materials, in line with Library policies To contribute to the development and delivery of public engagement programmes, including curating exhibitions and displays, and to promote Special Collections materials and activities through tours, talks, publications and social media activities We welcome candidates who bring diverse perspectives, experiences, and approaches to their work. About You We encourage applications from individuals with a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. You should demonstrate: Essential Criteria Knowledge of curatorial practices for rare books and archive collections 3 years professional experience of curating Special Collections in a relevant environment Excellent interpersonal and teamworking skills to work in a highly collegiate environment Educated to degree level in a subject represented in the Special Collections of the University of Manchester Library Full professional qualification in librarianship, archive management or museum studies, and/or relevant experience at a comparable level Desirable Criteria Awareness of Methodist and Non-conformist history in the UK Knowledge of one or more metadata standards appropriate to Special Collections We value transferable skills and real-world experience as much as formal qualifications. Our benefits include: • Generous employer contribution pension • 29 days annual leave plus bank holidays, along with Christmas closure • Ride to work and EV car scheme available For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Jessica Smith Email Address: jessica.smith@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://knowledge.jobtrain.co.uk/jobseeker [knowledge.jobtrain.co.uk] Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Watch: The University of Manchester Strategy Launch Event Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46129": {
      "title": "Teaching Learning and Student Experience Coordinator (IAG)",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Teaching Learning and Student Experience Coordinator (IAG) Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Teaching Learning and Student Experience Coordinator (IAG) Apply for job Job reference: HUM-032493 Salary: £33,662 - £37,369 per annum depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Humanities Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: SEED Administration Hours Per Week: 35 Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 18/11/2026 Contract Duration: Expected start date 4th January 2027 for 12 months School/Directorate: School of Environment, Education & Development Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description We are seeking a motivated and collaborative individual to join our team as a Teaching Learning and Student Experience Coordinator to coordinate the delivery of high-quality teaching, learning and student experience administration within the Information, Advice and Guidance team, ensuring effective processes, accurate information, and excellent service provision. This is a student-facing role based within the IAG team. It requires on-campus presence for the majority of the working week, with flexibility required to work evenings on occasion depending on operational need. You will be responsible for: Coordinating the delivery of student-facing Information, Advice and Guidance services, including email, telephone and face-to-face enquiries, ensuring a responsive, accurate and student-centred service. Supervising and coordinating administrative colleagues and defined areas of activity, including work allocation, staff rotas, training and acting as a point of reference for the team. Coordinating student support, engagement and development initiatives, including peer mentoring, engagement with societies, student representatives, wellbeing activity and student voice work. Supporting the coordination and development of the School’s Academic Advisor scheme across undergraduate and postgraduate taught programmes, ensuring alignment with University policy. We welcome candidates who bring diverse perspectives, experiences, and approaches to their work. About You We encourage applications from individuals with a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. You should demonstrate: Essential Criteria: Relevant knowledge and experience, including teaching and learning, and/or student administrative policies and procedures. Experience of working in a customer service environment. Able to supervise staff and coordinate work activities. Flexible and responsive to customer needs with excellent communication skills and the ability to provide information, advice and guidance whilst maintaining confidentiality. Excellent organisational skills, able to prioritise a variety of work activities in a busy environment and meet strict deadlines. We value transferable skills and real-world experience as much as formal qualifications. Our benefits include: Generous employer pension contribution Christmas closure across all departments outside of annual leave and bank holidays Purple Place employee discount platform For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Shauna Rogers Email: shauna.rogers@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home This role is not eligible for Skilled Worker visa sponsorship. Applicants must demonstrate the right to work in the UK. Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46102": {
      "title": "Teaching, Learning and Student Experience Administrator (Assessment and Progression)",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Teaching, Learning and Student Experience Administrator (Assessment and Progression) Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Teaching, Learning and Student Experience Administrator (Assessment and Progression) Apply for job Job reference: HUM-032441 Salary: £29,588 - £32,080 per annum depending on experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Humanities Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Permanent Division/Team: Social Sciences Administration Hours Per Week: 35 hours per week (1 FTE) Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 19/11/2026 Contract Duration: Permanent School/Directorate: School of Social Sciences Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description We are seeking a proactive and organised Teaching, Learning and Student Experience Administrator to join the Assessment and Progression Team within the School of Social Sciences. This role will support the effective delivery of assessment-related administration across the academic cycle, contributing to a high-quality, student-focused service. Key Responsibilities You will be responsible for: Administering a range of assessment and progression processes, including marking, moderation, results preparation, appeals, mitigating circumstances and Examination Board activity. Maintain accurate records, tracking documents and data across relevant University systems. Support the set-up and maintenance of online assessment submission areas and related assessment processes. Prepare correspondence, documentation and meeting papers, and support panels, committees and boards where required. Work collaboratively across teams to provide a consistent, effective and student-focused service. Contribute to process improvement, standardisation and service excellence within assessment administration. We welcome candidates who bring diverse perspectives, experiences and approaches to their work. About You You will have strong administrative, organisational and communication skills, with the ability to work accurately and flexibly in a busy environment. You will be confident using standard IT packages and willing to learn University systems such as Campus Solutions and Canvas. You will also be comfortable working with confidential information and supporting colleagues, students and stakeholders in a professional and helpful manner. We encourage applications from individuals with a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. You should demonstrate: Essential criteria Relevant administrative experience, ideally within a customer service, student administration or busy office environment. Strong organisational skills, with the ability to prioritise competing tasks, manage deadlines and work accurately during busy periods. Excellent attention to detail and the ability to maintain accurate records, tracking documents and data. Excellent digital skills, including experience of Microsoft Office, particularly Word, Outlook and Excel. Advanced Microsoft Excel skills, with experience of managing, analysing, manipulating and maintaining complex datasets accurately. Excellent written and verbal communication skills, with the ability to communicate clearly and professionally with students, staff and stakeholders. Ability to work effectively as part of a team, support colleagues and work flexibly across processes and priorities. Ability to handle confidential information appropriately and work in line with data protection requirements. A proactive, student-focused approach and commitment to service excellence, equality, diversity and inclusion. Desirable criteria Experience of working in higher education or a teaching and learning/student support environment. Experience of assessment administration, examination boards, panels, appeals, mitigating circumstances or related student processes. Experience of using University systems such as Campus Solutions, Power BI, Canvas or similar student record and virtual learning environment systems. Experience of supporting committees, panels or meetings, including preparing papers, recording actions or taking minutes.# Our benefits include: Generous employer pension contribution Christmas closure across all departments outside of annual leave and bank holidays Purple Place employee discount platform For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Amanda Bridgeman Email Address: amanda.bridgeman@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home This role is not eligible for Skilled Worker visa sponsorship. Applicants must demonstrate the right to work in the UK. Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=45196": {
      "title": "Campus Parking Supervisor",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Campus Parking Supervisor Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Campus Parking Supervisor Apply for job Job reference: PSX-031570 Salary: £25,804 - £27,319 per annum depending on experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Professional Services Location: Manchester Employment type: Permanent Division/Team: Car Park Services Hours Per Week: 35 hours per week (1 FTE) Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 19/11/2026 Contract Duration: Permanent School/Directorate: Directorate of Estates and Facilities Job Description We are seeking a motivated and collaborative individual to join our team as a Campus Parking Supervisor. This role offers an exciting opportunity to contribute to the day-to-day operational requirements of the service within a dynamic and inclusive environment. You will be responsible for: Prepare rota information and collation of annual leave requests for Car Park Attendants and ensure that all cabins are staffed, and that cover is available for sickness and holidays. To monitor the barriers, pay on foot machines, bicycle shelters, and other car park equipment and to carry out simple maintenance and repairs where necessary. Daily enforcement of appropriate parking across campus, and resolution of inappropriate parking, maintaining records of repeat offenders, to be raised with the Campus Parking Manager if required. To raise helpdesk maintenance requests and liaise with contractors to arrange for maintenance and repairs of equipment when required. Supervision of Car Park Attendants, including regular visits to all locations to ensure that University car parks and access points are managed efficiently. We welcome candidates who bring diverse perspectives, experiences, and approaches to their work. About You We encourage applications from individuals with a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. You should demonstrate: Essential Criteria: A working knowledge of Health and Health and Safety relevant to the role The postholder will have at least 12 months experience in a supervisory role within a frontline service or other relevant experience. Experience of administration in a busy working environment The postholder must have experience of using online systems The postholder will have experience of training and motivating staff Desirable Criteria: The postholder will need to demonstrate team building skills and a positive approach to customer service. The postholder must demonstrate the ability to work using initiative and with a minimum of supervision. The postholder will have good communication skills, both written and oral and must have a working knowledge of Microsoft Office and preferably Microsoft 365. We value transferable skills and real-world experience as much as formal qualifications. Our benefits include: Generous employer pension contribution Christmas closure across all departments outside of annual leave and bank holidays Purple Place employee discount platform For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Phillip Lord Email Address: philip.lord@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home This role is not eligible for Skilled Worker visa sponsorship. Applicants must demonstrate the right to work in the UK. Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=45975": {
      "title": "Director of the Photon Science Institute",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Director of the Photon Science Institute Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Director of the Photon Science Institute Apply for job Job reference: SAE-032530 Salary: Competitive Faculty/Organisational Unit: Science and Engineering Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Permanent Division/Team: Photon Science Institute Hours Per Week: 1FTE Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 07/01/2027 Contract Duration: N/A School/Directorate: FSE Research Institutes Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description Director of the Photon Science Institute Shape our research vision. Strengthen collaboration. Lead world-class photon science. The University of Manchester is seeking an outstanding academic leader to become Director of the Photon Science Institute. Our Manchester 2035 strategy, From Manchester for the World, sets a bold direction for the University. This is an opportunity to lead a distinctive interdisciplinary research community, build on its international reputation and ensure that our facilities, expertise and partnerships continue to enable outstanding science with real-world impact. The opportunity As Director, you will provide strategic academic leadership for the Photon Science Institute, shaping its future direction and leading the development and delivery of its strategy. Working closely with colleagues across the Faculty of Science and Engineering and the wider University, you will bring together researchers, professional services and technical teams around a shared vision for the Institute. You will lead the evolution of the PSI to achieve greater research success, including the quality and impact of its outputs, research income, industrial collaboration and international reputation. You will support the development of ambitious multidisciplinary funding proposals and strengthen partnerships with UKRI, the EU, government, industry and other external organisations. The PSI brings together world-leading facilities and expertise across quantum devices, advanced characterisation and the electromagnetic spectrum, enabling researchers to investigate everything from ultrafast processes to nanoscale material interactions. Its mission is delivered through three core themes: Quantum Technology, Net Zero and Sustainability, and Healthcare. A central priority will be ensuring that the Institute’s facilities, resources and expertise are used effectively and sustainably. You will help shape investment and research priorities, support financially responsible access to facilities for internal and external users and ensure that the PSI operates safely and in line with University policies and regulatory requirements. Alongside this, you will build an inclusive and engaged community in which colleagues and students can thrive. You will support researcher development at every career stage, strengthen succession planning and encourage collaboration across disciplinary and organisational boundaries. About you You will bring an outstanding academic track record in an area relevant to the PSI, together with substantial experience of leading research activity in a complex academic environment. You will have a strong portfolio of research funding and evidence of developing successful strategic partnerships, including collaboration with industry. You will be an experienced and collegiate leader, able to engage and motivate academics, researchers, technical specialists and professional services colleagues. You will have led cross-disciplinary activity and know how to create the conditions in which ambitious research and innovation can succeed. An effective communicator and relationship builder, you will be able to establish credibility, influence across organisational boundaries and navigate competing priorities. You will combine strategic thinking with a practical approach to delivery and be comfortable leading change in consultation with the communities affected by it. You will also bring a strong understanding of higher education governance, academic structures and regulatory requirements, together with a clear commitment to inclusion, academic freedom, sustainability and social responsibility. Why Manchester? You will join a University with a globally recognised research profile, a strong commitment to interdisciplinary working and an ambition to tackle major societal challenges through research, innovation and partnership. For an academic leader with vision, credibility and a commitment to collaborative research, this is an opportunity to shape the future of the Photon Science Institute, strengthen its national and international influence and support scientific discovery with impact within and beyond the University. Find out more Please see the link to the Candidate Brochure and job description, attached below, for further information about the role and selection criteria. We welcome applications from people of all backgrounds and experiences. If you require reasonable adjustments at any stage of the recruitment process, please contact talent.attraction@manchester.ac.uk so that we can discuss how best to support you. For technical support please call 0161 850 2004 or visit https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home This vacancy will close for applications at midnight on the closing date. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46413": {
      "title": "Research Fellow in Health and Care Evaluation",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Research Fellow in Health and Care Evaluation Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Research Fellow in Health and Care Evaluation Apply for job Job reference: BMH-032745 Salary: £48,337 to £59,390 per annum depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Biology, Medicine Health Location: Manchester Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Division of Population Health, Health Services Research And Primary Care Hours Per Week: Full Time Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 20/11/2026 Contract Duration: until 31 December 2027 School/Directorate: School of Health Sciences Job Description We wish to appoint an exceptional Research Fellow in Health and Care Evaluation to join the Prevention and Early Intervention research theme of the NIHR Applied Research Collaboration for Greater Manchester (NIHR ARC-GM) based at the University of Manchester. NIHR ARC-GM aims to undertake timely, needs-driven, transformative applied health and social care research to benefit communities locally and nationally. The postholder will conduct robust and timely service evaluation in response to the needs of the funder and other stakeholders. You will develop, undertake and report high quality research and evaluation using your expertise and experience in qualitative and/or mixed methods methodologies and approaches. At ARC-GM, co-production is at the heart of our approach, and you will work with our partners to translate their strategic priorities into answerable research questions, ensuring that our research is relevant, useful and applicable to service needs and achieves impact on policy and practice. This post offers the opportunity to work as part of a multi-disciplinary, supportive team to build relationships with decision makers. The post holder will provide timely insights, helping guide implementation, scaling, or de-implementation. We also hope that the postholder can contribute to methodological development, including approaches for short-timescale evaluations. The successful candidate will work with Professor Pete Bower, Dr Christine Camacho and other members of the NIHR ARC team and will be based in the Division of Population Health, Health Services Research & Primary Care in the Faculty of Biology, Medicine and Health. This is a full-time post based in Manchester and hybrid working arrangements are possible (with agreement from your line manager). What you will get in return: Fantastic market leading Pension scheme Excellent employee health and wellbeing services including an Employee Assistance Programme Exceptional starting annual leave entitlement, plus bank holidays Additional paid closure over the Christmas period Local and national discounts at a range of major retailers As an equal opportunities employer we welcome applicants from all sections of the community regardless of age, sex, gender (or gender identity), ethnicity, disability, sexual orientation and transgender status. All appointments are made on merit. Our University is positive about flexible working – you can find out more here Hybrid working arrangements may be considered. Please be aware that due to the number of applications we unfortunately may not able to provide individual feedback on your application. Please note that we are unable to respond to enquiries, accept CVs or applications from Recruitment Agencies. Any recruitment enquiries from recruitment agencies should be directed to recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Any CV’s submitted by a recruitment agency will be considered a gift. Enquiries about the vacancy, shortlisting and interviews: Name: Pete Bower Email: peter.bower@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries: Email: recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical support: https://knowledge.jobtrain.co.uk/jobseeker [knowledge.jobtrain.co.uk] This vacancy will close for applications at midnight on the closing date. Please see the link below for the Further Particulars document which contains the person specification criteria Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46449": {
      "title": "Research Associate in Developmental Cell Biology",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Research Associate in Developmental Cell Biology Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Research Associate in Developmental Cell Biology Apply for job Job reference: BMH-032783 Salary: UoM Grade 6 £38,448 - £46,970 pro rata depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Biology, Medicine Health Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Division of Developmental Biology and Medicine Hours Per Week: Full time (1 FTE) Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 07/12/2026 Contract Duration: From 01/04/2027 for 36 months School/Directorate: School of Medical Sciences Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description Join the Manning Lab at the University of Manchester for a three-year, Wellcome-funded post investigating how cytonemes (specialised signalling filopodia) transmit Notch signals. Working within a collaborative Discovery Award with the University of Exeter, you will combine cell culture, live imaging, mouse embryo experiments and two-dimensional correlative light and electron microscopy to explore signalling during development. Facilities for animal research are first rate and are validated by all the appropriate Home Office licences. However, if you have any medical, social or ethical reasons for avoiding work on experimental animals, then do not apply for this post. You will be responsible for: Undertaking a programme of research to determine how cytonemes mediate signalling during developmental patterning and cell-fate decisions. Developing robust image-analysis and quantitative workflows to extract reproducible biological measurements from microscopy data. Managing your own research activity, identifying technical challenges and working with collaborators to develop practical solutions. Communicating findings through project meetings, conference presentations and high-quality research publications. We welcome candidates who bring diverse perspectives, experiences, and approaches to their work. About You We encourage applications from individuals with a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. You should demonstrate: Essential Criteria: Hold a PhD or due to complete PhD within 6 months in developmental or cell biology Strong knowledge of developmental biology or cell biology Significant experience in live-cell or live-tissue fluorescence imaging and the quantitative analysis of microscopy data. Experience in cell and molecular biology techniques such as CRISPR gene editing, immunofluorescence, qPCR, PCR, western blotting Willingness to work with mouse embryos and to complete all required training and competency assessment. Desirable Criteria: Experience with mouse embryonic stem cell differentiations We value transferable skills and real-world experience as much as formal qualifications. Our benefits include: Generous employer contribution pension 29 days annual leave plus bank holidays, along with Christmas closure Ride to work and EV car scheme available For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Cerys Manning Email Address: cerys.manning@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support Jobtrain Jobseeker Support Hub This role is not eligible for Skilled Worker visa sponsorship. Applicants must demonstrate the right to work in the UK. Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46461": {
      "title": "Research Technician in the Physiology of Appetite and Body Weight Research",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Research Technician in the Physiology of Appetite and Body Weight Research Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Research Technician in the Physiology of Appetite and Body Weight Research Apply for job Job reference: BMH-032796 Salary: £30,180 - £32,722 per annum, according to relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Biology, Medicine Health Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: FBMH Technical Services Hours Per Week: 35 hours per week (1 FTE) Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 23/11/2026 Contract Duration: Fixed term for 20 months School/Directorate: Faculty Office - Biology, Medicine and Health Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description We seek a research technician to join our European Research Council funded team extending the optogenetic potential of animal opsins by protein engineering and experimental validation. Applicants must have experience of experimental techniques relevant for in vivo or ex vivo neurophysiology. They should ideally hold a Home Office Personal License for research on laboratory rodents. The successful applicant will spend most of their time on project specific research tasks but will also be asked to contribute to the general management of the lab. Familiarity with general health and safety processes and with legislation regarding genetically modified organisms would be an advantage. The successful candidate will join a dynamic and ambitious research group in Manchester, working in close collaboration with our ERC Synergy award partners in Switzerland (Gebhard Schertler) and Germany (Peter Hegemann). Facilities for animal research are first rate and are validated by all the appropriate Home Office licences. However, if you have any medical, social or ethical reasons for avoiding work on experimental animals, then do not apply for this post. You will be responsible for: Collecting and collating data from neurophysiological experiments Maintaining accurate and comprehensive records Contributing to safe and effective running of the laboratory Maintaining effective communication with lab members and our internal and external collaborators We welcome candidates who bring diverse perspectives, experiences, and approaches to their work. We value transferable skills and real-world experience as much as formal qualifications. Our benefits include: Generous employer pension contribution Christmas closure across all departments outside of annual leave and bank holidays Purple Place employee discount platform For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits. You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here. Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Rachel Watkins Email: rachel.watkins@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home This role is not eligible for Skilled Worker visa sponsorship. Applicants must demonstrate the right to work in the UK. Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46407": {
      "title": "Research Fellow (Health Psychology/ Qualitative and Mixed Methods Research)",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Research Fellow (Health Psychology/ Qualitative and Mixed Methods Research) Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Research Fellow (Health Psychology/ Qualitative and Mixed Methods Research) Apply for job Job reference: BMH-032739 Salary: £48,337 - £59,390 per annum, depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Biology, Medicine Health Location: Manchester Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Division of Psychology and Mental Health Hours Per Week: 1 FTE Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 26/11/2026 Contract Duration: available until 17 January 2029 School/Directorate: School of Health Sciences Job Description The Research Fellow will lead on the delivery of a Diabetes UK-funded research project exploring the experiences and support needs of siblings of children and young people with Type 1 Diabetes and co-developing psychoeducational resources to support families. Working closely with the Research Assistant and the wider project team, the postholder will undertake survey and qualitative research, coordinate recruitment, conduct interviews, focus groups and participatory workshops, undertake analysis, and support co-production of psychoeducational resources. Working with young people and families, healthcare professionals, public contributors, charities and academic partners, you will help co-develop psychoeducational resources and contribute to publications, dissemination and future funding opportunities. The post will be based within the Manchester Centre for Health Psychology, part of the Division of Psychology and Mental Health at The University of Manchester. The successful candidate will work closely with Dr Jo Brooks, Dr Rhiannon Hawkes, Dr Daniela Ghio and project collaborators to deliver the study. They will join a multidisciplinary team working with healthcare professionals, public contributors, charities and community organisations to develop resources for siblings of children and young people living with Type 1 Diabetes. The project includes survey research, interviews, participatory workshops and resource development activities involving families across the UK. We are seeking a motivated and collaborative individual to join our team as a Research Fellow. This role offers an exciting opportunity to lead the day-to-day delivery of a Diabetes UK-funded study focused on the siblings of young people with Type 1 Diabetes within a dynamic and inclusive environment. About You We encourage applications from individuals with a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. You should demonstrate: Essential Criteria: • a PhD or equivalent research experience • Strong expertise in qualitative health research and project delivery Desirable Criteria: • Experience of mixed methods, co-production, or research involving children, young people or families would be advantageous. We value transferable skills and real-world experience as much as formal qualifications. Our benefits include: • Generous employer contribution pension • 29 days annual leave plus bank holidays, along with Christmas closure • Ride to work and EV car scheme available For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits. You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here. We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy. Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Dr Jo Brooks Email Address: Joanna.brooks@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home This role is not eligible for Skilled Worker visa sponsorship. Applicants must demonstrate the right to work in the UK. Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46390": {
      "title": "Lecturer in Urban Planning (Fixed-term maternity cover)",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Lecturer in Urban Planning (Fixed-term maternity cover) Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Lecturer in Urban Planning (Fixed-term maternity cover) Apply for job Job reference: HUM-032724 Salary: UoM Grade 6/7 £38,448 - £59,390 Faculty/Organisational Unit: Humanities Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Planning, Property and Environmental Management Hours Per Week: Full time (1 FTE) Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 23/11/2026 Contract Duration: Fixed term for 6 months (January - July 2027) School/Directorate: School of Environment, Education & Development Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description We are seeking a motivated and collaborative individual to join our team as a Lecturer in Urban Planning (Fixed-term 6-month contract), Grade 6 - Grade 7 (depending on experience), in Planning Property & Environmental Management (PPEM), School of Environment, Education and Development (SEED). The role covers undergraduate and postgraduate teaching for six months, including PLAN20172 Applied Research for Real Estate with Field Trip (with a field class to Portugal), PLAN30000 Dissertation and marking for URBN70011 Policy making in Greater Manchester. Teaching is on campus at Oxford Road or on location for field trips. You will be responsible for: - Provide research-informed teaching relevant to planning and environmental management and/or associated relevant areas such as sustainability and climate change. - Undertake teaching, including programme and curriculum review and development, to ensure our teaching continues to be innovative, research-led and of high quality at undergraduate and postgraduate levels. - Teach in a variety of settings, including tutorials, seminars, workshops, fieldtrips and via both online and on-campus delivery. - Supervise undergraduate and postgraduate dissertations or similar research projects. - Undertake such administrative or academic service, pastoral, and other duties appropriate to the post of lectureship, as may be assigned. We welcome candidates who bring diverse perspectives, experiences, and approaches to their work. About You We encourage applications from individuals with a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. You should demonstrate: Essential Criteria: - A PhD in a relevant academic discipline (e.g. Planning, Geography, Ur-ban Studies, relevant social sciences etc) - Previous experience of teaching and supervision of undergraduate/postgraduate students in urban planning in a higher education setting. - Demonstrable evidence of the ability to deliver high-quality research-led teaching. - Excellent oral and written communication skills. The ability to work as part of multidisciplinary academic teams. Desirable Criteria: - Strong administrative skills - A commitment to collegiality and team working We value transferable skills and real-world experience as much as formal qualifications. Our benefits include: • Generous employer contribution pension • 29 days annual leave plus bank holidays, along with Christmas closure • Ride to work and EV car scheme available For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Prof. Ian Mell Email Address: ian.mell@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support Jobtrain Jobseeker Support Hub Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46274": {
      "title": "Research Associate in Improving Pollutant Deposition Estimates to Upland Ecosystems (UplandDep)",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Research Associate in Improving Pollutant Deposition Estimates to Upland Ecosystems (UplandDep) Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Research Associate in Improving Pollutant Deposition Estimates to Upland Ecosystems (UplandDep) Apply for job Job reference: SAE-032610 Salary: £38,448 - £46,970 per annum, depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Science and Engineering Location: Manchester Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Department of Earth & Environmental Sciences Hours Per Week: 1 FTE Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 23/11/2026 Contract Duration: 2 years School/Directorate: School of Natural Sciences Job Description We are seeking a motivated and collaborative individual to join our team as a Post-Doctoral Research Associate on the Improving pollutant deposition estimates to upland ecosystems. This role offers an exciting opportunity to contribute to a major research project aiming to make a significant step forward in our understanding of the processes that alter the deposition of nitrogen in complex upland terrain, and in our measurement and modelling tools to quantify this deposition. The work will involve field research at locations in both Scotland and the north of England. The research project is a partnership between CEH Edinburgh, University of Manchester, University of Exeter, Environment Agency and the MetOffice, providing a dynamic and inclusive environment. You will be responsible for: Play a coordinating role in the set up and delivery of the field experiments. Analyse and interpret the measurement data collected during the field experiments and publish key scientific outcomes. Use initiative and creativity to identify new research questions, assessment methods and opportunities for collaboration. Collaborate with interdisciplinary researchers on broader air quality research challenges and play a role in building the collaborative team during and after field experiments. Build effective relationships with project partners, the Environment Agency and the Met Office, and wider research networks. We welcome candidates who bring diverse perspectives, experiences, and approaches to their work. About You: We encourage applications from individuals with a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. You should demonstrate: Essential Criteria: Specialist knowledge of instrumentation for measurement of air pollution that can be readily transferable to other techniques and methods. Experience of conducting field measurements and/or organisation of multi-partner experiments to tackle an air pollution research problem. Ability to interpret complex technical findings and translate them into actionable conclusions. Evidence of the ability to prepare high-quality research publications or equivalent technical outputs. Willingness and ability to work closely with colleagues from other research institutes and ability to establish effective working relationships with others, including external partners. Desirable Criteria: Experience of coordinating a field experiment to study air pollution. Experience of working in a research environment with multiple partners and a demonstrable ability to engage with and work synergistically alongside others in a multi-disciplinary team. Experience of working with government partners. Experience of coordinating interdisciplinary collaboration involving air pollution. Evidence of contributing to research proposals or securing external funding. We value transferable skills and real-world experience as much as formal qualifications. (Grades 6-9) Our benefits include: • Generous employer contribution pension • 29 days annual leave plus bank holidays, along with Christmas closure • Ride to work and EV car scheme available For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Professor Hugh Coe Email Address: hugh.coe@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home This role is not eligible for Skilled Worker visa sponsorship. Applicants must demonstrate the right to work in the UK. Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46320": {
      "title": "Stakeholder and Partnerships Lead",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Stakeholder and Partnerships Lead Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Stakeholder and Partnerships Lead Apply for job Job reference: PSX-032716 Salary: £48,337 - £59,390 per annum depending on experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Professional Services Location: Manchester Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Unit M Hours Per Week: 35 hours per week (1 FTE) Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 06/12/2026 Contract Duration: Fixed term for 12 months School/Directorate: Unit M Job Description Build partnerships that drive innovation. Help shape Greater Manchester’s AI future. The University of Manchester is seeking a Stakeholder and Partnerships Lead to join Turing Innovation Catalyst Manchester (TIC) for a 12-month maternity cover appointment. This is a varied leadership opportunity combining strategic relationship management with responsibility for performance, impact and coordinated delivery across TIC. You will build connections with businesses, funders, universities and public and third sector organisations, helping turn shared ambitions into partnerships that support AI innovation and inclusive economic growth. The role As a member of TIC’s Senior Leadership Team, you will develop our partner engagement strategy, strengthen existing relationships and attract new long-term partners. Working across TIC, Unit M and the wider University, you will ensure engagement is coordinated, responsive and delivers clear value. Your contribution will extend across TIC’s activities. You will support the Director with planning and organisational priorities, connect workstreams and help colleagues translate strategic objectives into effective delivery. You will bring sound judgement and a practical approach to emerging opportunities and challenges. You will also lead the monitoring of programme budgets, performance and funding commitments, working with colleagues to forecast expenditure, manage risks and demonstrate impact. Clear reporting and effective processes will be central to ensuring we deliver on our commitments to funders and partners. Alongside this, you will line manage the Events and Communications Officer and provide matrix management to the Finance Officer, supporting their development and effective collaboration across the team. About you You will bring experience of securing, managing and growing strategic partnerships, with the credibility to build trusted relationships and represent an organisation confidently. A strategic thinker with strong organisational skills, you will be comfortable leading work that spans teams and priorities. You will have experience of managing budgets, resources and programme performance, alongside a track record of leading and developing people. You will understand academic and business environments and what motivates partners to collaborate. You will also bring an understanding of AI and its potential, with enthusiasm to deepen your knowledge. Experience in deep tech, innovation communities or securing external funding would be beneficial. Above all, you will be a collaborative, values-led leader who combines clear communication with sound judgement and cares about how results are achieved. Why join us? TIC is an ambitious non-profit organisation hosted by The University of Manchester, working to strengthen Greater Manchester’s AI innovation community. Its activities span founder development, startup and scale-up support, collaborative research and development, and international connections. As part of Unit M, the University’s gateway to innovation, TIC brings together expertise, research and resources to help businesses grow and deliver economic and societal benefit. This role offers the opportunity to make a meaningful contribution across the organisation while building relationships at the intersection of technology, enterprise and research. Find out more Please see the job description, attached below, for further information about the role and selection criteria. We welcome applications from people of all backgrounds and experiences. If you require reasonable adjustments at any stage of the recruitment process, please contact talent.attraction@manchester.ac.uk so that we can discuss how best to support you. For technical support please call 0161 850 2004 or visit https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home This vacancy will close for applications at midnight on the closing date. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46322": {
      "title": "Mentoring Network Manager",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Mentoring Network Manager Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Mentoring Network Manager Apply for job Job reference: PSX-032657 Salary: UoM Grade 7 £48,337 - £59,390 Faculty/Organisational Unit: Professional Services Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Fixed Term Division/Team: Unit M Hours Per Week: 35 Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 06/12/2026 Contract Duration: 12 months School/Directorate: Unit M Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description Connect expertise with ambition. Help the next generation of founders turn ideas into thriving ventures. The University of Manchester is seeking a Mentoring Network Manager to lead the design, development and delivery of our startup mentoring network, supporting students, staff and recent graduates to develop entrepreneurial ideas, startups, social ventures and scalable businesses. Based within the Masood Entrepreneurship Centre, part of Unit M, you will help make entrepreneurship accessible to people across the University. You will bring together experienced mentors, specialist advisers and ecosystem partners to give founders the guidance, connections and confidence they need to move forward. The role This is an opportunity to shape and lead a University-wide mentoring network, with the autonomy to develop its operating model and take responsibility for its quality, inclusivity and impact. You will recruit and develop a diverse community of mentors, establish effective matching and support arrangements, and ensure founders can access expertise appropriate to their venture’s stage, sector and ambitions. Working with colleagues across enterprise programmes, competitions and accelerators, you will embed mentoring throughout the founder journey. Building trusted relationships will be central to your success. You will engage with entrepreneurs, alumni, investors, professional advisers and corporate partners, representing the University and developing partnerships that strengthen our startup support. You will also work closely with the University of Manchester Innovation Factory and colleagues across the wider innovation ecosystem. Alongside this relationship-focused work, you will establish clear governance, use data to demonstrate outcomes and continually improve the experience for founders and mentors. About you You will bring substantial experience of supporting entrepreneurs, founders, startups, social enterprises or innovation-led projects, including providing credible one-to-one advice. You will have a track record of designing, launching and leading mentoring frameworks, adviser networks or similar initiatives, alongside a strong understanding of what makes mentoring relationships effective. An accomplished relationship builder, you will be confident engaging senior external stakeholders and exercising independent judgement. You will combine excellent communication and project management skills with experience of delivering engaging events or programmes and using evidence to improve their impact. Above all, you will share our commitment to entrepreneurship for all, with experience of making opportunities accessible to people from different backgrounds, disciplines and levels of experience. Why join us? Unit M is the University’s dedicated innovation arm, bringing together expertise, partnerships and new approaches to unlock Manchester’s innovation potential. Within it, the Masood Entrepreneurship Centre combines enterprise education, practical venture creation and community building. Joining this team offers the opportunity to shape a network with lasting value: helping founders develop their capabilities, strengthening connections across Manchester’s entrepreneurial community, and supporting ventures that benefit society and the economy. Occasional evening or weekend work will be required for events and ecosystem activity, with arrangements to support an appropriate balance between working and non-working time. Find out more Please see the job description, attached below, for further information about the role and selection criteria. For informal enquiries, please contact Andrew Dixon at talent.attraction@manchester.ac.uk . We welcome applications from people of all backgrounds and experiences. If you require reasonable adjustments at any stage of the recruitment process, please contact talent.attraction@manchester.ac.uk so that we can discuss how best to support you. For technical support please call 0161 850 2004 or visit https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home This vacancy will close for applications at midnight on the closing date. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46255": {
      "title": "Data &amp; Performance Information Analyst",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Data &amp; Performance Information Analyst Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Data & Performance Information Analyst Apply for job Job reference: BMH-032616 Salary: £38,448 - £46,970 per annum depending on experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Biology, Medicine Health Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Permanent Division/Team: FBMH Planning, Compliance, and Governance Hours Per Week: Full time (1 FTE) Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 20/11/2026 Contract Duration: Permanent School/Directorate: Faculty Office - Biology, Medicine and Health Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description We are seeking a motivated and collaborative individual to join our team as a Data & Performance Information Analyst. You will play a key role in strengthening how the Faculty uses data and insight to support effective performance management and data-driven decision making at a Faculty and School level, as well as supporting planning processes that underpin elements of the strategic performance ambitions, all of which contributes to the delivery of the University’s Manchester 2035 strategy. You will be responsible for: Applying appropriate techniques to deliver detailed analysis that informs strategic and operational planning, benchmarking and performance monitoring across the Faculty and Schools. Using a range of IT systems and databases to source, combine and analyse diverse datasets, working creatively and with minimal supervision, to produce, visualise and present management information and KPI reports for, and to, senior leaders at both School and Faculty level. Building and maintaining effective professional relationships with stakeholders in the Faculty, in particular with Faculty Executive colleagues, to support with the use and interpretation of data. Developing and maintaining scenario models to support forecasting and performance tracking. Apply good data governance, security and quality-assurance practices to ensure robust and reliable management information products. We welcome candidates who bring diverse perspectives, experiences, and approaches to their work. About You We encourage applications from individuals with a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. You should demonstrate: Essential Criteria A degree, or equivalent qualification, or relevant professional experience in producing management information and performance reporting. Advanced skills in Excel and data visualisation tools such as Power BI. Experience of analysing, and interpreting large and complex datasets to support performance management and planning processes. Experience of supporting leadership teams in monitoring performance against strategic and operational priorities. Strong analytical and problem-solving skills including being able to present complex data clearly, including reports and dashboards. Desirable Criteria Experience working in a Higher Education environment, ideally with performance data and management information. We value transferable skills and real-world experience as much as formal qualifications. Our benefits include: • Generous employer contribution pension • 29 days annual leave plus bank holidays, along with Christmas closure • Ride to work and EV car scheme available For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Fiona Fraser Email Address: Fiona.fraser@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://knowledge.jobtrain.co.uk/jobseeker [knowledge.jobtrain.co.uk] Applications close at midnight on the closing date. Further particulars (with person specification) linked below. Watch: The University of Manchester – our new strategy to 2035 Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=46026": {
      "title": "Teaching, Learning &amp; Student Experience Assistant",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Teaching, Learning &amp; Student Experience Assistant Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Teaching, Learning & Student Experience Assistant Apply for job Job reference: PSX-032386 Salary: £27,319 - £28,778 per annum, depending on relevant experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Professional Services Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Permanent Division/Team: Student and Academic Services Hours Per Week: 35 hours per week (1 FTE) Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 23/11/2026 Contract Duration: open ended/permanent School/Directorate: Directorate for the Student Experience Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description We are recruiting for a permanent, full-time Teaching, Learning and Student Experience Assistant (Student Support) to join the Course Delivery Team in the Education Development department. The role is 35 hours per week. This role involves assisting with admissions and providing ongoing student support to online and blended learners both in the UK and overseas. Our learners may be studying part time for a postgraduate qualification or completing a short course/micro credential as part of their career development. We are looking for candidates with experience of working in an administrative/customer support environment, answering queries by phone, email or web-conferencing and taking ownership of problems while maintaining appropriate levels of confidentiality. Experience of working with customers in support of online services and/or students within an educational environment would be an advantage, but is not essential. Candidates will need to work effectively as part of a team and have good general computing skills, particularly in the use of MS Word, MS Excel, MS Outlook and online meeting software, and be prepared to undertake further technical training where necessary. You will be responsible for: To act as the first point of contact for students and learners for the portfolio of courses, providing information, advice, and guidance on routine matters and knowing when to escalate to others as appropriate To assist and support students and learners with activities to support their studies which could include registration and enrolment, engagement and interaction with Canvas (virtual learning environment), unit selection and creation and maintenance of student records To assist with the processing of applications and maintaining admissions records in line with specified criteria To provide administrative support for the team as directed by the Teaching, Learning and Student Experience Coordinator and/or Officer To generate bespoke correspondence, record replies and maintain accurate records as required We welcome candidates who bring diverse perspectives, experiences, and approaches to their work. About You Candidates are expected to have experience of working in an administrative/customer support environment, answering queries by phone, email or web-conferencing and taking ownership of problems while maintaining appropriate levels of confidentiality. Experience of working with customers in support of online services and/or students within an educational environment would be an advantage, but not essential. Candidates will need to work effectively as part of a team and have good general computing skills, particularly in the use of MS Word, MS Excel, MS Outlook, and online meeting software, and be prepared to undertake further technical training where necessary. Essential Criteria: Experience of working in a team environment with the ability to be flexible and provide support for colleagues Experience of working with administrative processes Strong customer service experience, with the ability to be flexible and responsive to customer needs and the ability to provide information and guidance whilst maintaining confidentiality The ability to work independently, using own initiative, and to take responsibility for an area of work Good oral and written communication skills with an ability to deal with a wide range of people Desirable Criteria: Digital literacy, strong IT skills and an ability to use the Microsoft Office Suite Good organisational skills, with the ability to prioritise multiple demands in a busy environment and meet deadlines Close attention to detail and the ability to maintain this on an ongoing basis whilst performing routine and/or repetitive tasks Good numeracy skills The ability, and a willingness, to learn new technologies and adapt to new ways of working We value transferable skills and real-world experience as much as formal qualifications. Our benefits include: Generous employer pension contribution Christmas closure across all departments outside of annual leave and bank holidays Purple Place employee discount platform For more information, please see University of Manchester Benefits . You can also find information on our Flexible and Hybrid working here . We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Freedom of Speech Policy . Enquiries about the role, shortlisting and interviews Name: Kate Robinson Email Address: kate.robinson@manchester.ac.uk General enquiries and administrative support recruitmentservices.people@manchester.ac.uk Technical and job portal support https://jobseekersupport.jobtrain.co.uk/support/home This role is not eligible for Skilled Worker visa sponsorship. Applicants must demonstrate the right to work in the UK Applications close at midnight on the closing date Further particulars (with person specification) linked below Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.manchester.ac.uk/Job/JobDetail?JobId=45563": {
      "title": "Lecturer or Senior Lecturer in AI-augmented Software Engineering",
      "description": "The University of Manchester - Job Information  | Apply for Lecturer or Senior Lecturer in AI-augmented Software Engineering Skip to main content Accessibility Tools Jobs at The University of Manchester University home Jobs Current jobs Close menu University home Jobs Current jobs Register for job alerts Returning candidates List Vacancies (current) Sign in List Vacancies (current) Back to Search Results Lecturer or Senior Lecturer in AI-augmented Software Engineering Apply for job Job reference: SAE-031995 Salary: UoM Grade 7/8 £48,337–£72,997 per annum depending on experience Faculty/Organisational Unit: Science and Engineering Location: Oxford Road Employment type: Permanent Division/Team: Department of Computer Science Hours Per Week: Full Time (1 FTE) Closing date (DD/MM/YYYY): 08/01/2027 Contract Duration: Permanent School/Directorate: School of Engineering Where to find us location_on Get directions Job Description Lecturer or Senior Lecturer in AI-augmented Software Engineering This is an exceptional opportunity to join the Department of Computer Science at the University of Manchester. The University is investing heavily for the future, with focus on areas of national importance such as health, security, smart cities, robotics, and data-intensive sciences such as physics, astronomy and biology. The Department of Computer Science is investing for growth in a number of areas including Artificial Intelligence and Machine Learning. The Department of Computer Science is a leading research institution, and values exceptional researchers. You will publish to the highest standards, secure external research funding, pursue real-world impact, and contribute to the world-class PhD training programmes within the Department. This post focuses on AI-augmented software engineering, in both teaching and research. You will help shape our software engineering curriculum, including the MSc Software Engineering, and work with our groups in security, formal methods, autonomy and verification, machine learning and information management. The Department values exceptional teachers. You will play a key role in maintaining our reputation as an institute of learning – designing and delivering innovative UG/PG topics. Exceptional teachers are encouraged to demonstrate this in their application. We are an open place of enquiry and challenge. We embrace and celebrate difference, diversity and debate, and we pride ourselves on being a place of education, learning and community where we are able, within the law, to question and test received wisdom, express new ideas and explore controversial or unpopular topics and opinions. Find out more from our Policy on Freedom of Speech and Academic Freedom. The University actively fosters a culture of inclusion and diversity and will seek to achieve true equality of opportunity for all members of its community. Information about the Department of Computer Science can be found at Department of Computer Science - The University of Manchester . Informal discussions about the position are encouraged – contact Prof Andrew Stewart ( andrew.stewart@manchester.ac.uk ). Application forms and further particulars are available from our website Jobs | The University of Manchester Closing date: 8 January 2027 To apply, please upload the following: - A full academic CV - A 1-page description of their vision and future research plans with an indication of how they would contribute to research activities in the Department of Computer Science, including links with the research groups named in Further Particulars document which is available below. - A 1-page statement on teaching, listing technical areas to which you could contribute, at both the undergraduate and postgraduate level, highlighting experience and indicating the teaching methods that you think would be appropriate. Take a look around the company https://www.manchester.ac.uk/ Apply for job favorite_border Save this job Contact us +44 (0) 161 306 6000 Find us The University of Manchester Oxford Rd Manchester M13 9PL UK Connect with us Social media directory Disclaimer / Privacy / Copyright notice / Accessibility / Freedom of information / Charitable status / Royal Charter Number: RC000797"
    }
  },
